window.shHubContent.receive("questions",{"questions":[{"id":"dm-glut4","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Insulin clears glucose from the blood into skeletal muscle and fat by moving one transporter from inside the cell to the cell membrane. Which transporter is it?","choices":["GLUT4","GLUT1","GLUT2","SGLT2"],"answer":0,"ex":"GLUT4 is the one to know: it waits inside muscle and fat cells until the insulin receptor (a tyrosine kinase) signals it to the membrane. The other GLUTs work without insulin, which is why the brain gets glucose whether or not insulin is around.","src":"rec","why":["GLUT4 is the insulin-dependent transporter: the insulin receptor's signal translocates it from the cytoplasm to the membrane of muscle and fat cells.","GLUT1 handles basal glucose uptake and carries glucose across the blood-brain barrier without waiting for insulin.","GLUT2 is one of the insulin-independent transporters (with GLUT1, 3 and 5); it works whether or not insulin is present.","SGLT2 reabsorbs filtered glucose in the kidney; it is the target of the gliflozins, not the transporter insulin moves."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Diagnosis and physiology: A1C and glucose cutoffs, prediabetes, GLUT4, complications","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-1","dm-num-5","dm-tf-3","dm-a1c-why","dm-tf-8","dm-glut4","dm-ex-micro"]}},{"id":"dm-2s-5","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. GLUT1 is the insulin-dependent glucose transporter of skeletal muscle and fat.\n2. Insulin increases the uptake of potassium into cells by stimulating Na/K-ATPase.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Insulin moves GLUT4 (not GLUT1) to the membrane of muscle and fat, and it also drives potassium into cells through Na/K-ATPase, which is why insulin is used to bring down a high potassium.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: GLUT4 is the insulin-dependent transporter there; GLUT1 handles basal uptake and the blood-brain barrier without insulin.","Statement 2 is true, not false: increased cellular potassium uptake through Na/K-ATPase is one of insulin's listed actions.","Statement 1 is false, not true: GLUT4 is the insulin-dependent transporter there; GLUT1 handles basal uptake and the blood-brain barrier without insulin. Statement 2 is true, not false: increased cellular potassium uptake through Na/K-ATPase is one of insulin's listed actions.","Statement 1 is false: GLUT4 is the insulin-dependent transporter there; GLUT1 handles basal uptake and the blood-brain barrier without insulin. Statement 2 is true: increased cellular potassium uptake through Na/K-ATPase is one of insulin's listed actions."]},{"id":"dm-a1c-why","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does a hemoglobin A1C value reflect a patient's average blood glucose over roughly the past 3 months?","choices":["Glucose binds hemoglobin covalently and stays for the red cell's life","Glucose binds hemoglobin reversibly, so the value falls as soon as glucose does","Red cells take glucose in through GLUT4 and hold it until they are replaced","It measures the C-peptide released with each insulin molecule over months"],"answer":0,"ex":"HbA1c is hemoglobin A with a sugar attached to its first amino acid. The higher the glucose, the higher the percentage glycated, and because the bond is covalent the value covers the red cell's life span of about 3 to 4 months.","src":"rec","why":["The binding is irreversible and red cells live about 90 to 120 days, so the percentage glycated tracks glucose over that span.","If glucose came off when blood glucose fell, A1C would show only today's level; the professor stressed that A1C does not come down when glucose drops.","GLUT4 is the insulin-dependent transporter of muscle and fat; A1C is glucose bound to the hemoglobin molecule itself.","C-peptide is the marker of the patient's own insulin production, not of average glucose."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Diagnosis and physiology: A1C and glucose cutoffs, prediabetes, GLUT4, complications","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-1","dm-num-5","dm-tf-3","dm-a1c-why","dm-tf-8","dm-glut4","dm-ex-micro"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-3","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: An HbA1c of 6.2 percent is in the diabetic range.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Diabetes starts at an A1C of 6.5 percent. Normal is under 5.7, and 5.7 to 6.4 is prediabetes, so 6.2 percent is prediabetes.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["6.2 percent is below the diabetic cutoff of 6.5 percent; it falls in the prediabetes window of 5.7 to 6.4.","Diabetes begins at 6.5 percent; 6.2 sits in the prediabetes range of 5.7 to 6.4 percent."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Diagnosis and physiology: A1C and glucose cutoffs, prediabetes, GLUT4, complications","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-1","dm-num-5","dm-tf-3","dm-a1c-why","dm-tf-8","dm-glut4","dm-ex-micro"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-8","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Once someone has prediabetes, progressing to type 2 diabetes is a given.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Prediabetes is the window between normal and diabetic numbers (A1C 5.7 to 6.4 percent, fasting glucose 100 to 125 mg/dL). It has no symptoms and most people with it don't know, but it can be reversed.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["The professor said the opposite: it is not a given that prediabetes becomes diabetes.","Prediabetes can be reversed: diet, lifestyle change and weight loss bring many people back to normal numbers."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Diagnosis and physiology: A1C and glucose cutoffs, prediabetes, GLUT4, complications","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-1","dm-num-5","dm-tf-3","dm-a1c-why","dm-tf-8","dm-glut4","dm-ex-micro"]}},{"id":"dm-num-5","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Each result below comes from a different patient. Which results fall in the diabetic range?\n1. Fasting plasma glucose 118 mg/dL\n2. HbA1c 6.7%\n3. Glucose 215 mg/dL 2 hours into an oral glucose tolerance test\n4. HbA1c 5.9%","choices":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4","3 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"Diabetic range: A1C 6.5 percent or higher, fasting glucose 126 mg/dL or higher, a 2-hour oral glucose tolerance value of 200 or higher, or a random glucose above 200. A fasting glucose of 100 to 125 and an A1C of 5.7 to 6.4 are prediabetes.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: 118 mg/dL is in the prediabetes window of 100 to 125; the diabetic cutoff is 126. It leaves out 2, which is true: an A1C of 6.7 percent is above the 6.5 percent cutoff.","Statements 2 and 3 are true. Statement 1 is false: 118 mg/dL is in the prediabetes window of 100 to 125; the diabetic cutoff is 126. Statement 4 is false: 5.9 percent is prediabetes (5.7 to 6.4), not diabetes.","It includes 4, which is false: 5.9 percent is prediabetes (5.7 to 6.4), not diabetes.","It leaves out 2, which is true: an A1C of 6.7 percent is above the 6.5 percent cutoff."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Diagnosis and physiology: A1C and glucose cutoffs, prediabetes, GLUT4, complications","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-1","dm-num-5","dm-tf-3","dm-a1c-why","dm-tf-8","dm-glut4","dm-ex-micro"]}},{"id":"dm-2s-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Type 1 diabetes usually results from autoimmune destruction of the beta cells.\n2. In type 2 diabetes the tissues respond normally to insulin, but too little is made.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Type 1 is absolute insulin deficiency, so the treatment is insulin. Type 2 is peripheral insulin resistance plus an inadequate secretory response, and with time a person with type 2 can also stop making insulin.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: type 2 is peripheral insulin resistance with an inadequate secretory response: the tissues are not sensitive to insulin's action.","Statement 1 is true, not false: type 1 is absolute insulin deficiency, usually autoimmune (or after the pancreas is removed), so patients depend on injected insulin.","Statement 1 is true: type 1 is absolute insulin deficiency, usually autoimmune (or after the pancreas is removed), so patients depend on injected insulin. Statement 2 is false: type 2 is peripheral insulin resistance with an inadequate secretory response: the tissues are not sensitive to insulin's action.","Statement 1 is true, not false: type 1 is absolute insulin deficiency, usually autoimmune (or after the pancreas is removed), so patients depend on injected insulin. Statement 2 is false, not true: type 2 is peripheral insulin resistance with an inadequate secretory response: the tissues are not sensitive to insulin's action."]},{"id":"dm-ex-micro","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is a microvascular complication of diabetes EXCEPT:","choices":["Stroke","Neuropathy","Nephropathy","Retinopathy"],"answer":0,"ex":"Microvascular means small vessels: neuropathy, nephropathy and retinopathy. Macrovascular means big vessels: coronary artery disease, peripheral vascular disease and stroke.","src":"rec","fmt":"except","why":["Stroke is macrovascular, with coronary artery disease and peripheral vascular disease: it affects the big vessels.","Neuropathy is one of the three microvascular complications the professor called very important to know.","Nephropathy is microvascular; diabetes is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease.","Retinopathy is microvascular and is often how an optometrist first discovers that a patient has diabetes."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Diagnosis and physiology: A1C and glucose cutoffs, prediabetes, GLUT4, complications","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-1","dm-num-5","dm-tf-3","dm-a1c-why","dm-tf-8","dm-glut4","dm-ex-micro"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The non-ketotic hyperosmolar state carries a higher mortality than diabetic ketoacidosis.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are acute complications. DKA comes from cells starved of glucose burning fatty acids into ketone bodies; the non-ketotic hyperosmolar state comes from the sheer amount of glucose in the blood and can lead to stupor, coma and death.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["The professor said the hyperosmolar state is actually more deadly than DKA.","It is the other way around: the non-ketotic hyperosmolar state has the higher mortality."]},{"id":"dm-secondary","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with no history of diabetes develops hyperglycemia while being treated for Cushing syndrome. According to the lecture, treatment should focus on:","choices":["The underlying Cushing syndrome that is causing the hyperglycemia","Lifelong insulin, because new hyperglycemia means type 1 diabetes","Metformin, because it is first-line for any newly found hyperglycemia","A sulfonylurea, to squeeze more insulin out of the pancreas"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pregnancy (gestational diabetes), acromegaly, Cushing syndrome, thyrotoxicosis, chronic pancreatitis, cancer and several drugs can raise glucose. That is secondary diabetes: there is a primary cause, and treatment is aimed at it.","src":"rec","why":["Secondary diabetes has a primary cause, and the professor said you treat that cause rather than the diabetes.","Type 1 is autoimmune loss of beta cells; hyperglycemia caused by another disease is secondary, not type 1.","Metformin is first-line for type 2 diabetes in an otherwise healthy patient, not for hyperglycemia with a treatable cause.","Stimulating insulin release ignores the cause of the high glucose."]},{"id":"dm-match-druginduced","lec":"dmpharm","type":"match","stem":"Match each drug class to how the lecture says it raises blood glucose.","pairs":[["Corticosteroids","Peripheral insulin resistance plus more gluconeogenesis"],["Protease inhibitors","Block the conversion of proinsulin to insulin"],["Thiazide diuretics","Less insulin secretion because of low potassium"],["Antipsychotics","Altered receptor binding that raises insulin resistance"]],"ex":"Corticosteroids cause peripheral insulin resistance and gluconeogenesis; protease inhibitors block proinsulin's conversion to insulin; thiazides lower potassium, which reduces insulin secretion; antipsychotics alter receptor binding and increase insulin resistance. Beta-blockers (less insulin secretion) and fluoroquinolones are on the same list.","src":"slides"},{"id":"dm-tf-7","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Insulin secretion is greater after glucose given intravenously than after the same glucose taken by mouth.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"This is the incretin effect: oral glucose releases more insulin than IV glucose because the gut's incretins, GLP-1 and GIP, are secreted in proportion to the nutrient load and add to the signal. The GLP-1 drugs and DPP-4 inhibitors build on it.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["It is the reverse: oral glucose gives the bigger insulin response.","Oral glucose gives the bigger insulin response (the incretin effect), because gut hormones add to the glucose signal."]},{"id":"dm-pb-k","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A man without diabetes is treated for a dangerously high potassium (see the Patient Box). Why is dextrose given together with the insulin?","choices":["To prevent hypoglycemia while insulin drives potassium into cells","Because dextrose itself is what pumps potassium into the cells","To treat the undiagnosed diabetes that raised his potassium level","To replace glucose he is losing in the urine by osmotic diuresis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Insulin increases cellular potassium uptake through Na/K-ATPase, so it is a standard way to bring down a high potassium, even in people who don't have diabetes. It is given with glucose so the patient doesn't become hypoglycemic.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","67-year-old man in the emergency department with muscle weakness and an irregular heartbeat"],["Potassium","6.5 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5)"],["Diabetes","None"],["Orders","Regular insulin IV with dextrose"]],"why":["Insulin lowers potassium by stimulating Na/K-ATPase uptake into cells; the glucose gives insulin something to clear so blood sugar doesn't crash.","The potassium-lowering effect is insulin's, through Na/K-ATPase; the dextrose is there to protect the blood glucose.","He has no diabetes; here insulin is used only as a potassium-lowering drug.","Glucose lost in the urine is what SGLT2 inhibitors cause; it has nothing to do with this treatment."]},{"id":"dm-pb-dx-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A dental patient with repeated periodontal abscesses is sent for blood work (see the Patient Box). Which conclusion do the results best support?","choices":["He has diabetes: two criteria agree from the one blood draw","He has prediabetes, so diet and exercise are all he needs","Nothing can be concluded until the glucose is repeated next visit","His values are normal, so the oral findings have another cause"],"answer":0,"ex":"Diagnosis needs two of: A1C 6.5 percent or more, fasting glucose 126 mg/dL or more, a 2-hour glucose tolerance value of 200 or more, or a random glucose above 200. Periodontal disease, slow healing, oral infections and dry mouth are the dental signs on the slide, and the dentist may be the first to suspect diabetes.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","49-year-old man at a dental recall visit"],["Mouth","Two periodontal abscesses this year, a slow-healing extraction socket, dry mouth"],["History","Always thirsty; gets up at night to urinate"],["Labs (one blood draw)","Fasting glucose 138 mg/dL; HbA1c 7.2%"],["Other","eGFR 88 mL/min; no heart, liver or kidney disease; BMI 27"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case1","why":["An A1C of 6.5 percent or more plus a fasting glucose of 126 mg/dL or more are two agreeing criteria, enough for a diagnosis from a single draw.","Prediabetes is an A1C of 5.7 to 6.4 percent or a fasting glucose of 100 to 125; both of his values are above those ranges.","Before A1C, glucose had to be high on two visits; now an A1C and a fasting glucose from the same draw can confirm it.","Normal is an A1C under 5.7 percent and a fasting glucose under 100 mg/dL; his are well above both."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Diagnosis and physiology: A1C and glucose cutoffs, prediabetes, GLUT4, complications","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-1","dm-num-5","dm-tf-3","dm-a1c-why","dm-tf-8","dm-glut4","dm-ex-micro"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-dx-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"The same patient is otherwise healthy. Which drug is the standard first-line choice for him?","choices":["Metformin","Glipizide","Insulin glargine","Sitagliptin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Apart from comorbidities that point elsewhere (a weight problem toward a GLP-1 agonist, heart failure or cardiovascular disease toward an SGLT2 inhibitor or a GLP-1 agonist), metformin is the first-line drug for type 2 diabetes. Other drugs are added on when it is no longer enough.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","49-year-old man at a dental recall visit"],["Mouth","Two periodontal abscesses this year, a slow-healing extraction socket, dry mouth"],["History","Always thirsty; gets up at night to urinate"],["Labs (one blood draw)","Fasting glucose 138 mg/dL; HbA1c 7.2%"],["Other","eGFR 88 mL/min; no heart, liver or kidney disease; BMI 27"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case1","why":["Metformin is still the first-line drug for a newly diagnosed, otherwise healthy patient with type 2 diabetes.","A sulfonylurea lowers glucose but causes hypoglycemia and weight gain and loses effect over time; it is not first-line.","Insulin is the treatment for type 1 diabetes; he still makes insulin, and type 2 starts with metformin.","A DPP-4 inhibitor is an add-on: expensive, with only modest A1C lowering."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Metformin: first-line, mechanism, no effect at normal glucose, GI upset, B12, lactic acidosis, eGFR below 30","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-2","dm-met-moa","dm-num-1","dm-pb-renal-1","dm-2s-2","dm-met-hypo"]}},{"id":"dm-ins-meal","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with type 1 diabetes is about to sit down to a large, carbohydrate-heavy dinner and needs insulin to cover the rise in glucose after the meal. Which is the best choice?","choices":["Insulin aspart","NPH insulin","Insulin glargine","Insulin degludec"],"answer":0,"ex":"Mealtime (bolus) insulin has to start fast: the rapid analogs lispro, aspart and glulisine, or regular insulin. As the professor put it, once you eat you can't wait 2 to 4 hours for NPH to kick in.","src":"rec","why":["Aspart is rapid-acting (onset in about 5 to 15 minutes), so given just before the meal it covers the postprandial spike.","NPH takes 2 to 4 hours to start working, far too late for a meal that is about to be eaten.","Glargine is a basal insulin with a slow, steady release over more than 24 hours; it doesn't cover a meal spike.","Degludec is ultra-long-acting (more than 42 hours): basal coverage, not a mealtime dose."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Choosing an insulin: rapid or regular for a meal, long-acting as basal, and the onset, peak and duration of each preparation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-ins-meal","dm-ins-shortest","dm-ins-basal","dm-pb-chair-2","dm-2s-6","dm-ins-nph"]}},{"id":"dm-ins-shortest","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Of these insulins, which one's effect wears off soonest?","choices":["Insulin lispro","Regular insulin","NPH insulin","Insulin detemir"],"answer":0,"ex":"Shortest to longest: rapid analogs (lispro, aspart, glulisine), then regular, then NPH, then the long-acting glargine and detemir, then ultra-long degludec. These time profiles decide which insulin is basal and which is used as needed with meals.","src":"rec","why":["Lispro is rapid-acting: onset in 5 to 15 minutes and a total duration of about 4 hours, the shortest here.","Regular (short-acting) insulin peaks around 3 hours and is gone by about 8 hours, longer than the rapid analogs.","NPH is intermediate-acting: protamine slows its absorption so it lasts longer than regular insulin.","Detemir is a long-acting basal insulin."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Choosing an insulin: rapid or regular for a meal, long-acting as basal, and the onset, peak and duration of each preparation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-ins-meal","dm-ins-shortest","dm-ins-basal","dm-pb-chair-2","dm-2s-6","dm-ins-nph"]}},{"id":"dm-ins-basal","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"In a basal-bolus regimen, which insulin is typically injected once a day, for example at night, to mimic the body's own background insulin?","choices":["Insulin glargine","Insulin glulisine","Regular insulin","Inhaled insulin (Afrezza)"],"answer":0,"ex":"Basal insulin runs all day whether or not the patient eats; bolus insulin is taken as needed (PRN) before each meal and skipped if the meal is skipped.","src":"rec","why":["Glargine releases slowly and steadily, with no real peak, and lasts more than 24 hours: the classic once-daily basal insulin.","Glulisine is rapid-acting, a mealtime (bolus) insulin.","Regular insulin peaks around 3 hours and is gone by about 8; it is used before meals.","Afrezza starts working within a minute or two, an ultra-fast mealtime insulin."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Choosing an insulin: rapid or regular for a meal, long-acting as basal, and the onset, peak and duration of each preparation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-ins-meal","dm-ins-shortest","dm-ins-basal","dm-pb-chair-2","dm-2s-6","dm-ins-nph"]}},{"id":"dm-ins-nph","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"What does the protamine in NPH insulin do?","choices":["It slows absorption from the injection site, so insulin lasts longer","It speeds absorption so the insulin can be given right before meals","It protects insulin from DPP-4 so the insulin can be taken by mouth","It acidifies the solution so the insulin can be mixed with any other"],"answer":0,"ex":"NPH = neutral protamine Hagedorn: neutral pH, protamine to slow absorption, and Hagedorn, the researcher. It was the first change made to lengthen insulin's action, so patients needed fewer injections.","src":"rec","why":["Hagedorn added the protein protamine so injected insulin is released slowly, turning a short-acting insulin into an intermediate one.","Faster release is what the rapid analogs (lispro, aspart, glulisine) were modified for; protamine does the opposite.","DPP-4 breaks down incretins, not insulin, and no insulin is swallowed; the one non-injected insulin is inhaled Afrezza.","The N stands for neutral: the preparation is neither acidic nor basic."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Choosing an insulin: rapid or regular for a meal, long-acting as basal, and the onset, peak and duration of each preparation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-ins-meal","dm-ins-shortest","dm-ins-basal","dm-pb-chair-2","dm-2s-6","dm-ins-nph"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Regular insulin and NPH can be bought at a pharmacy without a prescription.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The professor stressed it: the two older human insulins, regular (Humulin R, Novolin R) and NPH (Humulin N, Novolin N), are sold without a prescription to patients who know how to use them. The analogs are not.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["These two older human insulins are sold without a prescription; the professor said you should know that.","They are the exception: regular and NPH are available without a prescription, though only these two."]},{"id":"dm-2s-6","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Afrezza is an inhaled insulin that starts acting within a few minutes.\n2. Insulin glargine has a sharp peak about 3 hours after injection.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Afrezza, inhaled, is the fastest insulin (a minute or two). The sharp peak around 3 hours belongs to regular insulin; glargine releases slowly and evenly with no real peak, which is what makes it a basal insulin.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: the peak around 3 hours (gone by about 8) is regular insulin; glargine has a slow, flat release lasting more than 24 hours.","Statement 1 is true, not false: it is given by inhalation and its action sets in within one to two minutes.","Statement 1 is true: it is given by inhalation and its action sets in within one to two minutes. Statement 2 is false: the peak around 3 hours (gone by about 8) is regular insulin; glargine has a slow, flat release lasting more than 24 hours.","Statement 1 is true, not false: it is given by inhalation and its action sets in within one to two minutes. Statement 2 is false, not true: the peak around 3 hours (gone by about 8) is regular insulin; glargine has a slow, flat release lasting more than 24 hours."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Choosing an insulin: rapid or regular for a meal, long-acting as basal, and the onset, peak and duration of each preparation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-ins-meal","dm-ins-shortest","dm-ins-basal","dm-pb-chair-2","dm-2s-6","dm-ins-nph"]}},{"id":"dm-ex-insae","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is an adverse effect of insulin EXCEPT:","choices":["Weight loss","Hypoglycemia","Lipohypertrophy at the injection site","Lipoatrophy at the injection site"],"answer":0,"ex":"Insulin's adverse effects: hypoglycemia, weight gain, transient worsening of retinopathy, and lipoatrophy or lipohypertrophy at the injection site.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Insulin is anabolic, so it causes weight gain, not weight loss.","Hypoglycemia is insulin's main adverse effect, shared by every drug that raises insulin release.","Fat can build up at a repeatedly used injection site; it is on the list.","Fat can also waste away at an injection site; it is on the list too."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Which drugs cause hypoglycemia (insulin and the secretagogues) and which rarely do","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-hypo-class","dm-num-2","dm-met-hypo","dm-glucose-dep","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-ex-insae"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-9","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Starting insulin can cause a temporary worsening of diabetic retinopathy before it improves.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Transient deterioration of retinopathy is a listed adverse effect: most people with diabetes already have some eye disease, and starting insulin may make it worse for a while before it gets better.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Transient worsening of retinopathy is on the list of insulin's adverse effects, with hypoglycemia, weight gain and injection-site changes.","It is a listed adverse effect: eye complications can worsen for a while after insulin is started, then improve."]},{"id":"dm-pb-chair-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"During a scaling and root planing appointment, a patient with type 1 diabetes becomes sweaty and shaky (see the Patient Box). What should the dental team do first?","choices":["Give her orange juice, regular soda or glucose tablets by mouth","Give her an extra dose of lispro to bring her glucose back to normal","Finish the scaling quickly, then recheck her glucose afterward","Give intranasal glucagon before trying any food or drink"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sweating, shaking, anxiety and confusion with a glucose of 52 mg/dL is hypoglycemia. A patient who is awake and can swallow gets fast sugar by mouth (orange juice, regular soda, glucose tablets); glucagon is for emergencies.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","24-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes, 9:00 am appointment for scaling and root planing"],["Insulin","Glargine 18 units at bedtime; lispro before each meal"],["This morning","Took her lispro at 7:30 am, then skipped breakfast to make the appointment"],["At 9:40 am","Sweaty, shaky and anxious, a little confused but answering questions; fingerstick glucose 52 mg/dL"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case2","why":["She is awake and can swallow, so fast sugar by mouth is the first step the lecture gives for hypoglycemia.","Insulin lowers glucose further; her symptoms come from too much insulin for the food she has eaten.","Hypoglycemia can progress to collapse; it is treated as soon as it is recognized, not after the procedure.","Glucagon is the emergency drug for a patient who can't take sugar by mouth; she is alert and can drink."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Hypoglycemia in the dental chair: fast sugar by mouth if awake, glucagon in an emergency","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-chair-1","dm-pb-chair-2","dm-pb-chair-3"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-chair-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which of her insulins most likely caused this episode?","choices":["Lispro, a rapid-acting insulin taken with no meal to cover","Glargine, because its effect peaks sharply about 10 hours after a dose","Glargine, because basal insulin should be skipped on any day she fasts","Neither: like metformin, insulin has no effect once glucose is normal"],"answer":0,"ex":"In basal-bolus therapy the basal insulin (glargine) runs all day regardless of meals, while the bolus (lispro) is taken only when eating. Taking the bolus and skipping the meal leaves insulin with no glucose to match.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","24-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes, 9:00 am appointment for scaling and root planing"],["Insulin","Glargine 18 units at bedtime; lispro before each meal"],["This morning","Took her lispro at 7:30 am, then skipped breakfast to make the appointment"],["At 9:40 am","Sweaty, shaky and anxious, a little confused but answering questions; fingerstick glucose 52 mg/dL"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case2","why":["Lispro starts in 5 to 15 minutes and lasts about 4 hours, so the 7:30 dose was fully active during her appointment, with no breakfast to match.","Glargine has a slow, steady release with no real peak; it mimics background insulin.","The basal dose is taken whether or not she eats; it is the mealtime (PRN) dose that depends on eating.","Having no effect at normal glucose is metformin's property; insulin lowers glucose whatever the starting level, which is why hypoglycemia is its main adverse effect."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Hypoglycemia in the dental chair: fast sugar by mouth if awake, glucagon in an emergency","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-chair-1","dm-pb-chair-2","dm-pb-chair-3"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-chair-3","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Before she can drink anything, she becomes unresponsive and can no longer swallow. Which is the drug of choice now?","choices":["Glucagon, by injection or as a nasal spray","Regular insulin given IV together with dextrose","Pramlintide injected under the skin before a meal","Acarbose, to slow the breakdown of carbohydrate"],"answer":0,"ex":"In an emergency, the drug of choice for hypoglycemia is glucagon, which raises glucose from the liver. Nasal glucagon is given like nasal naloxone, so anyone can give it quickly.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","24-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes, 9:00 am appointment for scaling and root planing"],["Insulin","Glargine 18 units at bedtime; lispro before each meal"],["This morning","Took her lispro at 7:30 am, then skipped breakfast to make the appointment"],["At 9:40 am","Sweaty, shaky and anxious, a little confused but answering questions; fingerstick glucose 52 mg/dL"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case2","why":["Glucagon is the emergency treatment; it comes as a powder kit mixed for injection, a stable liquid for injection, or a nasal form.","Insulin with glucose is the treatment for a high potassium, not for hypoglycemia; insulin would lower her glucose further.","Pramlintide is an amylin analog given before meals; with insulin it can cause hypoglycemia, not treat it.","Acarbose blocks carbohydrate digestion, the opposite of what she needs, and she can't swallow."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Hypoglycemia in the dental chair: fast sugar by mouth if awake, glucagon in an emergency","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-chair-1","dm-pb-chair-2","dm-pb-chair-3"]}},{"id":"dm-seq-su","lec":"dmpharm","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the steps of sulfonylurea action in order, from the drug to insulin release.","steps":["The drug binds the sulfonylurea receptor (SUR) on the beta cell","The ATP-sensitive potassium channel closes","Potassium is trapped inside and the membrane depolarizes","Voltage-gated calcium channels open and calcium flows in","Insulin already made in the beta cell is released by exocytosis"],"ex":"Sulfonylureas and meglitinides both close the beta cell's ATP-sensitive potassium channel. Trapped potassium depolarizes the cell, calcium channels open, and the calcium influx triggers exocytosis of stored insulin.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Secretagogues: the K-ATP channel mechanism, the need for working beta cells (C-peptide), low durability, sulfonylurea warnings","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-seq-su","dm-pb-cpep-1","dm-pb-cpep-2","dm-su-durability","dm-meg","dm-tf-4","dm-pb-etoh"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-cpep-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A man with long-standing type 2 diabetes has been on insulin for 6 years (see the Patient Box). What does his C-peptide result tell his physician?","choices":["His beta cells no longer make insulin; what is measured is injected","His beta cells make plenty of insulin, but his tissues resist it","His A1C is falsely high because C-peptide binds to hemoglobin","He makes insulin, but the test can't detect it while he injects"],"answer":0,"ex":"Beta cells make proinsulin, which is cleaved into insulin plus C-peptide in equimolar amounts. An insulin level can't separate injected from natural insulin; C-peptide can. With time, a person with type 2 diabetes can stop making insulin, as in type 1.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","61-year-old man, type 2 diabetes for 15 years"],["Drugs","Insulin glargine and insulin aspart for the last 6 years; metformin"],["HbA1c","8.3%"],["Serum insulin","30 µIU/mL, drawn 2 hours after his usual morning aspart"],["C-peptide","Undetectable (below 0.1 ng/mL; normal fasting about 0.5 to 2)"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case3","why":["Proinsulin is cleaved into insulin and C-peptide in equal amounts and injected insulin carries none, so no C-peptide means no insulin of his own.","If his beta cells were still making insulin, C-peptide would be present in amounts equal to that insulin.","C-peptide plays no part in A1C; it is glucose that binds hemoglobin covalently.","That is the point of the test: an insulin level can't tell his insulin from the injected kind, but C-peptide comes only from his own proinsulin."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Secretagogues: the K-ATP channel mechanism, the need for working beta cells (C-peptide), low durability, sulfonylurea warnings","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-seq-su","dm-pb-cpep-1","dm-pb-cpep-2","dm-su-durability","dm-meg","dm-tf-4","dm-pb-etoh"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-cpep-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"His physician considers adding another drug. Which would be expected to have essentially no effect in him?","choices":["Glipizide","Dapagliflozin","Acarbose","Pramlintide"],"answer":0,"ex":"Secretagogues (sulfonylureas and meglitinides) need a functional pancreas. In the professor's words, if they have lost their ability to make insulin, it doesn't matter how much you stimulate.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","61-year-old man, type 2 diabetes for 15 years"],["Drugs","Insulin glargine and insulin aspart for the last 6 years; metformin"],["HbA1c","8.3%"],["Serum insulin","30 µIU/mL, drawn 2 hours after his usual morning aspart"],["C-peptide","Undetectable (below 0.1 ng/mL; normal fasting about 0.5 to 2)"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case3","why":["Sulfonylureas only squeeze insulin out of working beta cells; with no insulin being made, there is nothing to release.","SGLT2 inhibitors make the kidney spill glucose into the urine; they work without the pancreas.","Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors block carbohydrate breakdown in the gut, whatever the beta cells are doing.","Pramlintide, an amylin analog, is used with insulin in type 1 as well as type 2; it lowers glucagon and slows gastric emptying."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Secretagogues: the K-ATP channel mechanism, the need for working beta cells (C-peptide), low durability, sulfonylurea warnings","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-seq-su","dm-pb-cpep-1","dm-pb-cpep-2","dm-su-durability","dm-meg","dm-tf-4","dm-pb-etoh"]}},{"id":"dm-su-durability","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"The lecture lists low durability as a drawback of sulfonylureas. What does that mean?","choices":["Their effect wanes over the years, since the pancreas can be squeezed only so far","They are broken down so quickly by DPP-4 that they must be taken several times a day","The tablets lose their potency on the shelf and must be replaced every few months","Their effect lasts only about an hour after each dose, so they can't cover a whole day"],"answer":0,"ex":"Low durability means the glucose-lowering effect doesn't last over the years. It is listed for sulfonylureas, meglitinides and the DPP-4 inhibitors (about 2 years).","src":"rec","why":["The professor: after one or two years the dose has to go up, and eventually it doesn't do anything, because there is a limit to how far a drug can squeeze the pancreas.","DPP-4 breaks down the incretins, not sulfonylureas; dosing several times a day is the meglitinides' drawback.","Durability here describes how long the drug keeps working in the patient, not shelf life.","A half-life of about 1 hour describes the meglitinides, repaglinide and nateglinide."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Secretagogues: the K-ATP channel mechanism, the need for working beta cells (C-peptide), low durability, sulfonylurea warnings","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-seq-su","dm-pb-cpep-1","dm-pb-cpep-2","dm-su-durability","dm-meg","dm-tf-4","dm-pb-etoh"]}},{"id":"dm-meg","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which feature belongs to repaglinide and nateglinide (the meglitinides) rather than to the sulfonylureas?","choices":["A peak and half-life of about 1 hour, dosed with meals for postprandial glucose","They close the beta cell's ATP-sensitive potassium channel to release insulin","They cause no hypoglycemia, because they act only when glucose is high","They are the long-acting first generation, now rarely used for that reason"],"answer":0,"ex":"Meglitinides are the quick squeeze: same mechanism and side effects as sulfonylureas (hypoglycemia, though less, weight gain, low durability), with flexible dosing at meals but frequent doses. Don't use them if a sulfonylurea has failed.","src":"slides","why":["Meglitinides are rapidly absorbed, peak and clear in about an hour, and give a quick squeeze on the pancreas at meals (Prandin is named for postprandial).","Both classes do this; the meglitinides share two binding sites with the sulfonylureas and have one of their own.","They do cause hypoglycemia, just less than sulfonylureas; glucose-dependent insulin release belongs to the incretin drugs.","That describes chlorpropamide, the first-generation sulfonylurea: long-acting, with a high risk of hypoglycemia."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Secretagogues: the K-ATP channel mechanism, the need for working beta cells (C-peptide), low durability, sulfonylurea warnings","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-seq-su","dm-pb-cpep-1","dm-pb-cpep-2","dm-su-durability","dm-meg","dm-tf-4","dm-pb-etoh"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-4","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: A patient whose sulfonylurea has stopped working is a good candidate for repaglinide.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Repaglinide and nateglinide work the same way as sulfonylureas, through the beta cell's ATP-sensitive potassium channel, so the slide says not to use a meglitinide once a sulfonylurea has failed.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Meglitinides share the sulfonylureas' mechanism, so the slide says not to use them after a sulfonylurea has failed.","The slide says do not use a meglitinide if a sulfonylurea has failed: same mechanism, same limit."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Secretagogues: the K-ATP channel mechanism, the need for working beta cells (C-peptide), low durability, sulfonylurea warnings","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-seq-su","dm-pb-cpep-1","dm-pb-cpep-2","dm-su-durability","dm-meg","dm-tf-4","dm-pb-etoh"]}},{"id":"dm-glucose-dep","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient's new diabetes pill raises her insulin release mainly when her glucose is high, so on its own it causes no hypoglycemia. Which drug is it most likely to be?","choices":["Sitagliptin","Glimepiride","Repaglinide","Pioglitazone"],"answer":0,"ex":"Two kinds of drugs raise insulin secretion: the secretagogues (sulfonylureas, meglitinides), which close the K-ATP channel whatever the glucose, and the incretin drugs (GLP-1 agonists, DPP-4 inhibitors), which raise it in a glucose-dependent way. That is the split on the summary slide.","src":"slides","why":["A DPP-4 inhibitor keeps her own GLP-1 and GIP active longer, and the incretins raise insulin release in a glucose-dependent way, so there is no hypoglycemia.","A sulfonylurea closes the K-ATP channel and releases insulin continuously, whatever the glucose level, which is why it causes hypoglycemia.","A meglitinide is a quick squeeze at meals through the same K-ATP channel; it is not glucose-dependent and does cause hypoglycemia, though less than a sulfonylurea.","A TZD sensitizes muscle and liver to insulin; it doesn't raise insulin release at all."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Which drugs cause hypoglycemia (insulin and the secretagogues) and which rarely do","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-hypo-class","dm-num-2","dm-met-hypo","dm-glucose-dep","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-ex-insae"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-etoh","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with type 2 diabetes reports a reaction at a party (see the Patient Box). Which best explains it?","choices":["A disulfiram-like reaction to alcohol, a sulfonylurea warning","Lactic acidosis, the rare serious adverse effect of glipizide","Hemolysis from G6PD deficiency, set off by the wine she drank","Pancreatitis, the rare adverse effect of the incretin drugs"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sulfonylurea warnings: hypoglycemia and weight gain, G6PD deficiency (hemolysis), hepatic disease, renal disease (mostly glyburide), agranulocytosis, jaundice, hypersensitivity, and a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","56-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes"],["Drugs","Glipizide 10 mg daily"],["Complaint","Flushing, pounding headache, nausea and palpitations within minutes of two glasses of wine"]],"why":["Sulfonylureas can cause a disulfiram-like reaction, the same warning as metronidazole, so patients should avoid alcohol.","Lactic acidosis is listed for metformin, not glipizide, and it isn't a flushing reaction minutes after a drink.","G6PD deficiency is a sulfonylurea warning, but it means red-cell breakdown from the drug, not a reaction to alcohol.","Rare pancreatitis is listed for the GLP-1 agonists, and she isn't on one."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Secretagogues: the K-ATP channel mechanism, the need for working beta cells (C-peptide), low durability, sulfonylurea warnings","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-seq-su","dm-pb-cpep-1","dm-pb-cpep-2","dm-su-durability","dm-meg","dm-tf-4","dm-pb-etoh"]}},{"id":"dm-met-moa","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"What is metformin's primary glucose-lowering effect?","choices":["It inhibits gluconeogenesis in the liver","It closes ATP-sensitive potassium channels on beta cells","It activates PPAR-gamma in adipose tissue","It blocks glucose reabsorption by SGLT2 in the kidney"],"answer":0,"ex":"Metformin's net effect: less glucose made by the liver (primary), better insulin sensitivity in the periphery (especially skeletal muscle), and slower, smaller glucose absorption from the gut.","src":"rec","why":["Inhibiting hepatic gluconeogenesis is the primary effect, with better peripheral insulin sensitivity and slower gut absorption on top.","That is how the secretagogues (sulfonylureas, meglitinides) release insulin.","PPAR-gamma is the nuclear receptor the thiazolidinediones bind.","That is the gliflozins' mechanism."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Metformin: first-line, mechanism, no effect at normal glucose, GI upset, B12, lactic acidosis, eGFR below 30","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-2","dm-met-moa","dm-num-1","dm-pb-renal-1","dm-2s-2","dm-met-hypo"]}},{"id":"dm-met-hypo","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient asks why metformin, unlike glyburide, is unlikely to make her blood sugar drop too low. Which explanation is best?","choices":["It has no effect when glucose is already normal, and it doesn't push out insulin","It raises glucagon release, which balances out its effect on insulin release","It works only after meals, by blocking carbohydrate breakdown in the gut","It is weight neutral, and only drugs that cause weight gain also cause lows"],"answer":0,"ex":"Insulin, sulfonylureas and meglitinides lower glucose whatever the starting level. Metformin doesn't: in the professor's words, if glucose is normal, metformin will not bring it lower.","src":"rec","why":["Metformin has no effect in normoglycemic states: if glucose is normal it won't bring it lower, so its hypoglycemia risk is very low.","Metformin doesn't raise glucagon; lowering glucagon is what the incretins and amylin do.","Blocking carbohydrate breakdown is how acarbose and miglitol work; metformin acts mainly on the liver.","Weight and hypoglycemia are separate effects; the reason is that metformin doesn't stimulate insulin release."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Metformin: first-line, mechanism, no effect at normal glucose, GI upset, B12, lactic acidosis, eGFR below 30","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-2","dm-met-moa","dm-num-1","dm-pb-renal-1","dm-2s-2","dm-met-hypo"]}},{"id":"dm-2s-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Metformin delays the absorption of food from the gut, which flattens the glucose spike after a meal.\n2. Metformin's GI side effects usually fade within one or two weeks.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Besides cutting the liver's glucose output, metformin slows absorption from the gut, so a big post-meal spike becomes a slow plateau. The same slowing explains its common GI effects (bloating, indigestion, cramps, diarrhea), which usually settle after a week or two.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: the professor listed delayed gut absorption as its third effect; it turns a big post-meal spike into a slow plateau, and it is why patients feel bloated. Statement 2 is true: the professor said most people tolerate the bloating, cramps and diarrhea for one or two weeks, and then they usually disappear.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the professor listed delayed gut absorption as its third effect; it turns a big post-meal spike into a slow plateau, and it is why patients feel bloated. Statement 2 is true, not false: the professor said most people tolerate the bloating, cramps and diarrhea for one or two weeks, and then they usually disappear.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the professor said most people tolerate the bloating, cramps and diarrhea for one or two weeks, and then they usually disappear.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the professor listed delayed gut absorption as its third effect; it turns a big post-meal spike into a slow plateau, and it is why patients feel bloated."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Metformin: first-line, mechanism, no effect at normal glucose, GI upset, B12, lactic acidosis, eGFR below 30","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-2","dm-met-moa","dm-num-1","dm-pb-renal-1","dm-2s-2","dm-met-hypo"]}},{"id":"dm-num-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about metformin are true?\n1. Its primary effect is to inhibit gluconeogenesis in the liver.\n2. It can decrease the absorption of vitamin B12.\n3. It commonly causes weight gain, as sulfonylureas do.\n4. It is contraindicated when the eGFR is below 30 mL/min.","choices":["1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Metformin lowers hepatic glucose output, lowers B12 absorption (some patients need B12 injections), is weight neutral or causes modest weight loss, and is absolutely contraindicated below an eGFR of 30.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: metformin is weight neutral or causes modest weight loss; weight gain belongs to insulin, the secretagogues and TZDs.","It includes 3, which is false: metformin is weight neutral or causes modest weight loss; weight gain belongs to insulin, the secretagogues and TZDs. It leaves out 2, which is true: decreased B12 absorption is a listed adverse effect; some patients need B12 injections.","It includes 3, which is false: metformin is weight neutral or causes modest weight loss; weight gain belongs to insulin, the secretagogues and TZDs. It leaves out 1, which is true: inhibiting hepatic gluconeogenesis is its primary effect.","It leaves out 1, which is true: inhibiting hepatic gluconeogenesis is its primary effect."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Metformin: first-line, mechanism, no effect at normal glucose, GI upset, B12, lactic acidosis, eGFR below 30","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-2","dm-met-moa","dm-num-1","dm-pb-renal-1","dm-2s-2","dm-met-hypo"]}},{"id":"dm-ex-met","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Metformin should be avoided, or used with caution, in each of these patients EXCEPT one with:","choices":["Obesity, with a BMI of 33","Heavy daily alcohol intake","Acute, unstable heart failure","Acute metabolic acidosis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Metformin's contraindications: eGFR below 30 and acute or chronic metabolic acidosis. Cautions: excessive alcohol, unstable or acute heart failure, and hepatic impairment.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Metformin is weight neutral or causes modest weight loss, so obesity is no reason to avoid it.","The slide says to avoid metformin with excessive acute or chronic alcohol use.","Unstable or acute heart failure is a listed caution.","Acute or chronic metabolic acidosis is a contraindication; lactic acidosis is one of metformin's adverse effects."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Weight effects: gain with insulin, sulfonylureas, meglitinides and TZDs; loss with GLP-1 agonists; a GLP-1 agonist first for a patient with a weight problem","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-weight","dm-pb-wt-1","dm-2s-3","dm-pb-thyroid","dm-ex-met"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-renal-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"An older patient's kidney function has fallen since his last visit (see the Patient Box). What should happen to his metformin?","choices":["Stop it: an eGFR below 30 is an absolute contraindication","Continue it: it can't cause lows, so it is safe at any eGFR","Double the dose, because his A1C is above the usual target","Continue it and add vitamin B12 to protect his kidneys"],"answer":0,"ex":"An eGFR below 30 mL/min is an absolute contraindication to metformin. His eGFR of 26 means it has to stop, and his glucose control has to come from another drug.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","72-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, here for extractions"],["Drugs","Metformin 1000 mg twice daily; glyburide 5 mg daily"],["Kidney function","eGFR 26 mL/min (48 a year ago)"],["HbA1c","7.4%"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case4","why":["Metformin is well tolerated, but the professor said that when the GFR is below 30 it is not given.","Its low hypoglycemia risk doesn't make it safe in kidney failure; below an eGFR of 30 it is contraindicated.","Raising the dose ignores the kidney contraindication.","B12 replacement treats metformin's effect on B12 absorption; it does nothing for the kidneys."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Metformin: first-line, mechanism, no effect at normal glucose, GI upset, B12, lactic acidosis, eGFR below 30","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-dx-2","dm-met-moa","dm-num-1","dm-pb-renal-1","dm-2s-2","dm-met-hypo"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-renal-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"His physician needs a drug to take the metformin's place. According to the lecture, which of these needs no renal dose adjustment?","choices":["Linagliptin","Canagliflozin","Dapagliflozin","Acarbose"],"answer":0,"ex":"For a patient with poor kidney function: metformin stops below an eGFR of 30, the gliflozins have renal cutoffs (45 or 60), acarbose and miglitol are contraindicated, glyburide carries the sulfonylurea renal warning, and linagliptin needs no renal adjustment.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","72-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, here for extractions"],["Drugs","Metformin 1000 mg twice daily; glyburide 5 mg daily"],["Kidney function","eGFR 26 mL/min (48 a year ago)"],["HbA1c","7.4%"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case4","why":["Linagliptin is the DPP-4 inhibitor singled out for needing no renal adjustment, and DPP-4 inhibitors can be used in kidney dysfunction.","Canagliflozin's renal cutoff is an eGFR below 45; he is at 26.","Dapagliflozin's renal cutoff is an eGFR below 60; he is at 26.","Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors are contraindicated with renal impairment; both are cleared by the kidney."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Situational choice with heart failure, heart disease or kidney disease: avoid TZDs (edema, macular edema), consider an SGLT2 inhibitor or semaglutide, sitagliptin the DPP-4 exception, linagliptin without renal adjustment","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-hf-1","dm-pb-hf-2","dm-dpp4-hf","dm-pb-renal-2","dm-tzd-eye","dm-tf-5","dm-tf-10"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-hf-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A man with heart failure has just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes (see the Patient Box). Which drug should be avoided in him?","choices":["Pioglitazone","Sitagliptin","Glipizide","Semaglutide"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pioglitazone (Actos) and rosiglitazone (Avandia) cause edema, the same peripheral and pulmonary edema that marks heart failure, so a patient at risk quickly develops heart failure. They are recommended only for patients without cardiovascular problems.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","64-year-old man, newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes"],["Heart","NYHA class III heart failure with ankle edema; takes furosemide"],["HbA1c","7.8%"],["Kidney and liver","eGFR 70 mL/min; ALT normal"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case5","why":["TZDs cause dose-related edema and new or worsened heart failure; NYHA class III or IV heart failure is a contraindication.","Sitagliptin is the one DPP-4 inhibitor that is the exception to avoiding heart failure.","Sulfonylureas bring hypoglycemia and weight gain, but the lecture gives them no heart failure warning; fluid retention is the TZDs' problem.","GLP-1 agonists such as semaglutide are now approved for people with cardiovascular disease and cause no fluid retention."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Situational choice with heart failure, heart disease or kidney disease: avoid TZDs (edema, macular edema), consider an SGLT2 inhibitor or semaglutide, sitagliptin the DPP-4 exception, linagliptin without renal adjustment","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-hf-1","dm-pb-hf-2","dm-dpp4-hf","dm-pb-renal-2","dm-tzd-eye","dm-tf-5","dm-tf-10"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-hf-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which drug would lower his glucose and also help his heart failure?","choices":["Dapagliflozin","Rosiglitazone","Saxagliptin","Glyburide"],"answer":0,"ex":"Situational choice: for a patient who already takes a diuretic for heart failure and then develops diabetes, an SGLT2 inhibitor lowers glucose by peeing it out and adds to the diuresis. This can make it the first choice instead of metformin.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","64-year-old man, newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes"],["Heart","NYHA class III heart failure with ankle edema; takes furosemide"],["HbA1c","7.8%"],["Kidney and liver","eGFR 70 mL/min; ALT normal"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case5","why":["Dapagliflozin is FDA approved to treat heart failure: its glucose-driven diuresis helps with edema, the reason the professor suggested Farxiga for a patient like him.","TZDs cause edema and worsen heart failure.","DPP-4 inhibitors other than sitagliptin should be avoided in heart failure.","A sulfonylurea offers no heart failure benefit and adds hypoglycemia and weight gain."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Situational choice with heart failure, heart disease or kidney disease: avoid TZDs (edema, macular edema), consider an SGLT2 inhibitor or semaglutide, sitagliptin the DPP-4 exception, linagliptin without renal adjustment","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-hf-1","dm-pb-hf-2","dm-dpp4-hf","dm-pb-renal-2","dm-tzd-eye","dm-tf-5","dm-tf-10"]}},{"id":"dm-dpp4-hf","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A DPP-4 inhibitor is needed for a patient with heart failure. Which one does the lecture name as the exception to the rule of avoiding DPP-4 inhibitors in heart failure?","choices":["Sitagliptin","Saxagliptin","Linagliptin","Alogliptin"],"answer":0,"ex":"DPP-4 inhibitors are oral, once daily, well tolerated and usable in kidney and liver dysfunction, but they are expensive, lower A1C only modestly, lose effect in about 2 years and should be avoided in heart failure, except sitagliptin.","src":"slides","why":["Sitagliptin (Januvia) is the exception: the slide says avoid DPP-4 inhibitors in heart failure, except sitagliptin.","Saxagliptin (Onglyza) falls under the rule to avoid in heart failure.","Linagliptin (Tradjenta) is known for needing no renal adjustment, not for being safe in heart failure.","Alogliptin (Nesina) falls under the rule to avoid in heart failure."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Situational choice with heart failure, heart disease or kidney disease: avoid TZDs (edema, macular edema), consider an SGLT2 inhibitor or semaglutide, sitagliptin the DPP-4 exception, linagliptin without renal adjustment","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-hf-1","dm-pb-hf-2","dm-dpp4-hf","dm-pb-renal-2","dm-tzd-eye","dm-tf-5","dm-tf-10"]}},{"id":"dm-2s-3","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Thiazolidinediones act on a cell-surface tyrosine kinase receptor.\n2. Thiazolidinediones cause weight loss.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"TZDs are PPAR-gamma agonists: PPAR-gamma is a nuclear receptor, mostly in adipose tissue, that switches on genes of glucose and lipid metabolism. They add fat cells and fat mass and cause weight gain and edema, while improving insulin sensitivity.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: they bind PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that regulates genes of glucose and lipid metabolism; the tyrosine kinase is the insulin receptor. Statement 2 is false, not true: they increase the number of fat cells and fat mass, and weight gain is a listed adverse effect.","Statement 1 is false: they bind PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that regulates genes of glucose and lipid metabolism; the tyrosine kinase is the insulin receptor. Statement 2 is false: they increase the number of fat cells and fat mass, and weight gain is a listed adverse effect.","Statement 1 is false, not true: they bind PPAR-gamma, a nuclear receptor that regulates genes of glucose and lipid metabolism; the tyrosine kinase is the insulin receptor.","Statement 2 is false, not true: they increase the number of fat cells and fat mass, and weight gain is a listed adverse effect."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Weight effects: gain with insulin, sulfonylureas, meglitinides and TZDs; loss with GLP-1 agonists; a GLP-1 agonist first for a patient with a weight problem","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-weight","dm-pb-wt-1","dm-2s-3","dm-pb-thyroid","dm-ex-met"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-5","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Pioglitazone should be avoided when ALT is more than 2.5 times the upper limit of normal.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"TZD cautions: NYHA class III or IV heart failure, and hepatic disease (avoid if ALT is more than 2.5 times the upper limit of normal). Their advantages are a low risk of hypoglycemia, preserved beta-cell function, higher HDL and lower triglycerides and blood pressure.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The slide's hepatic caution for TZDs is exactly this: avoid if ALT is over 2.5 times the upper limit of normal.","It is a listed caution: TZDs are avoided when ALT is above 2.5 times the upper limit of normal."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Situational choice with heart failure, heart disease or kidney disease: avoid TZDs (edema, macular edema), consider an SGLT2 inhibitor or semaglutide, sitagliptin the DPP-4 exception, linagliptin without renal adjustment","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-hf-1","dm-pb-hf-2","dm-dpp4-hf","dm-pb-renal-2","dm-tzd-eye","dm-tf-5","dm-tf-10"]}},{"id":"dm-tzd-eye","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient with type 2 diabetes had a drug added six months ago. Her optometrist now finds macular edema and asks her physician to review the change. Which added drug is the most likely culprit?","choices":["Pioglitazone","Saxagliptin","Glimepiride","Ertugliflozin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Edema is the TZDs' signature adverse effect: peripheral edema, new or worse heart failure, and macular edema. The professor said he skipped it in class, but it is right there in the slide.","src":"slides","why":["The fluid retention TZDs cause isn't only in the ankles: the lecture links them to macular edema, which is why retinal health is watched.","DPP-4 inhibitors are weight neutral and aren't linked to edema; their heart failure caution is a separate issue.","Sulfonylureas cause hypoglycemia and weight gain, not edema.","SGLT2 inhibitors cause a diuresis, lowering fluid volume rather than retaining it."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Situational choice with heart failure, heart disease or kidney disease: avoid TZDs (edema, macular edema), consider an SGLT2 inhibitor or semaglutide, sitagliptin the DPP-4 exception, linagliptin without renal adjustment","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-hf-1","dm-pb-hf-2","dm-dpp4-hf","dm-pb-renal-2","dm-tzd-eye","dm-tf-5","dm-tf-10"]}},{"id":"dm-mm-glp1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"multi","stem":"Which of the following does GLP-1 receptor activation do? Select all that apply.","choices":["Increases insulin secretion","Decreases glucagon release","Slows gastric emptying","Blocks glucose reabsorption in the kidney","Activates PPAR-gamma in fat cells"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"GLP-1 (and GIP) are incretins secreted in proportion to the nutrient load. GLP-1 increases insulin secretion, decreases glucagon release, slows gastric emptying and decreases food intake, which is why its drugs treat both hyperglycemia and weight.","src":"rec","why":["GLP-1 increases insulin secretion, in a glucose-dependent way.","Less glucagon means less new glucose from the liver.","Slower emptying makes people feel full and eat less, and it delays the absorption of other oral drugs.","Blocking renal glucose reabsorption is what SGLT2 inhibitors do.","PPAR-gamma activation is the thiazolidinedione mechanism."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors: incretin actions, exendin-4, boxed warning for thyroid tumors, delayed gastric emptying","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-glp1","dm-exendin","dm-num-4","dm-pb-wt-2","dm-dpp4-moa","dm-ex-dpp4","dm-tf-6","dm-2s-4"]}},{"id":"dm-exendin","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Exendin-4, from Gila monster saliva, led to the first GLP-1 drug (exenatide). What made it useful when the body's own GLP-1 was not?","choices":["It isn't broken down by DPP-4, so it lasts for hours","It closes beta-cell potassium channels more strongly than GLP-1","It can be swallowed as a tablet, unlike the body's GLP-1","It acts on the kidney to spill glucose into the urine"],"answer":0,"ex":"DPP-4 breaks down the body's own incretins, so natural GLP-1 is too short-lived to be a drug. Exendin-4 is a potent GLP-1 agonist that DPP-4 doesn't metabolize; it became exenatide (Byetta twice daily, Bydureon weekly), and later analogs last up to a week.","src":"rec","why":["The body's own GLP-1 is quickly broken down by DPP-4; exendin-4 resists DPP-4 and has a half-life of 2 to 3 hours.","Closing the potassium channel is the sulfonylurea mechanism; exendin-4 acts on the GLP-1 receptor.","Exenatide is injected (twice daily before meals as Byetta); the oral GLP-1 agonist is semaglutide.","Spilling glucose into the urine is what the SGLT2 inhibitors do."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors: incretin actions, exendin-4, boxed warning for thyroid tumors, delayed gastric emptying","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-glp1","dm-exendin","dm-num-4","dm-pb-wt-2","dm-dpp4-moa","dm-ex-dpp4","dm-tf-6","dm-2s-4"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-6","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Liraglutide is the GLP-1 agonist that is available as a once-daily tablet.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The tablet is oral semaglutide (Rybelsus), taken once daily; because its absorption is poor, the oral dose is far larger than the weekly injected dose. Liraglutide (Victoza) is a once-daily injection.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["The tablet is oral semaglutide (Rybelsus); liraglutide (Victoza) is injected once daily.","The oral GLP-1 agonist is semaglutide (Rybelsus); liraglutide is injected once daily."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors: incretin actions, exendin-4, boxed warning for thyroid tumors, delayed gastric emptying","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-glp1","dm-exendin","dm-num-4","dm-pb-wt-2","dm-dpp4-moa","dm-ex-dpp4","dm-tf-6","dm-2s-4"]}},{"id":"dm-num-4","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about GLP-1 receptor agonists are true?\n1. They carry a boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors seen in animal studies.\n2. Their nausea and vomiting are dose-dependent and fade with time.\n3. Dulaglutide and semaglutide are injected twice daily before meals.\n4. Rare pancreatitis and acute renal failure are among their listed risks.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"GLP-1 agonists: boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors, dose-dependent nausea that wanes, delayed gastric emptying, rare pancreatitis, acute renal failure. Twice daily before meals is exenatide (Byetta); dulaglutide (Trulicity) and semaglutide (Ozempic) are weekly.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: rare pancreatitis and acute renal failure are on the slide's list of side effects.","It includes 3, which is false: both last about a week (half-lives of about 5 and 7 days), so they are given once weekly; twice daily before meals is exenatide.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: both last about a week (half-lives of about 5 and 7 days), so they are given once weekly; twice daily before meals is exenatide.","It leaves out 1, which is true: thyroid C-cell tumors developed in animal studies, so this is a boxed warning for the whole class."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors: incretin actions, exendin-4, boxed warning for thyroid tumors, delayed gastric emptying","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-glp1","dm-exendin","dm-num-4","dm-pb-wt-2","dm-dpp4-moa","dm-ex-dpp4","dm-tf-6","dm-2s-4"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-thyroid","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient asks to start semaglutide (see the Patient Box). What is the main concern?","choices":["GLP-1 agonists carry a boxed warning for thyroid C-cell tumors","Semaglutide causes weight gain, which would make her obesity worse","Semaglutide carries a high risk of hypoglycemia when used alone","Semaglutide is contraindicated once eGFR is below 30 mL/min"],"answer":0,"ex":"The boxed warning applies to all GLP-1 agonists. Without it, she would be a natural candidate: a weight problem points to a GLP-1 agonist as the first-line drug.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","47-year-old woman, newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes, BMI 36"],["History","Thyroid cancer, treated 8 years ago"],["Request","The weekly weight-loss shot her friend uses (semaglutide)"]],"why":["Thyroid C-cell tumors developed in animal studies, so people with a history of thyroid cancer are advised not to try these drugs.","GLP-1 agonists cause weight loss; weight gain belongs to insulin, the secretagogues and the TZDs.","As monotherapy, GLP-1 agonists have a low risk of hypoglycemia.","The eGFR-below-30 contraindication belongs to metformin."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Weight effects: gain with insulin, sulfonylureas, meglitinides and TZDs; loss with GLP-1 agonists; a GLP-1 agonist first for a patient with a weight problem","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-weight","dm-pb-wt-1","dm-2s-3","dm-pb-thyroid","dm-ex-met"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-wt-1","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A young woman with obesity has just been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes (see the Patient Box). Given her weight, which drug class does the lecture say could be the first choice instead of metformin?","choices":["A GLP-1 agonist such as semaglutide","A sulfonylurea such as glimepiride","A thiazolidinedione such as pioglitazone","A basal insulin such as glargine"],"answer":0,"ex":"Weight is one of the two questions asked of every diabetes drug. Weight gain: insulin, sulfonylureas, meglitinides, TZDs. Weight loss: GLP-1 agonists (metformin is neutral or modest loss, DPP-4 inhibitors neutral).","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","33-year-old woman, newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes"],["Weight","BMI 38; wants to lose weight"],["HbA1c","7.1%"],["Other drugs","Combined oral contraceptive"],["History","No thyroid disease; normal kidney function"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case6","why":["For a patient with a weight problem, the professor said a GLP-1 agonist is the first-line choice: it treats the diabetes and causes weight loss.","Sulfonylureas cause weight gain.","TZDs cause weight gain and edema.","Insulin is anabolic and causes weight gain; she still makes insulin."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Weight effects: gain with insulin, sulfonylureas, meglitinides and TZDs; loss with GLP-1 agonists; a GLP-1 agonist first for a patient with a weight problem","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-weight","dm-pb-wt-1","dm-2s-3","dm-pb-thyroid","dm-ex-met"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-wt-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Two weeks after starting weekly semaglutide, she needs an antibiotic after a dental extraction. What should she know about the antibiotic and her oral contraceptive?","choices":["Delayed gastric emptying can slow their absorption","Semaglutide induces liver enzymes that break both drugs down","There is no concern, because semaglutide is injected, not swallowed","Both drugs raise her risk of the thyroid tumors seen with GLP-1 drugs"],"answer":0,"ex":"Delayed gastric emptying is both the GLP-1 agonists' most common effect and their drug interaction: drugs that need rapid absorption (oral contraceptives, antibiotics) may be absorbed more slowly.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","33-year-old woman, newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes"],["Weight","BMI 38; wants to lose weight"],["HbA1c","7.1%"],["Other drugs","Combined oral contraceptive"],["History","No thyroid disease; normal kidney function"]],"pcase":"dmpharm-case6","why":["GLP-1 agonists delay gastric emptying, which matters for drugs that need rapid GI absorption, such as oral contraceptives and antibiotics.","The interaction the lecture gives is in the gut, through slower gastric emptying, not through liver enzymes.","Being injected doesn't prevent the interaction: the drug slows gastric emptying for anything taken by mouth.","The thyroid C-cell warning comes from animal studies of the GLP-1 drugs themselves, not from an interaction."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors: incretin actions, exendin-4, boxed warning for thyroid tumors, delayed gastric emptying","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-glp1","dm-exendin","dm-num-4","dm-pb-wt-2","dm-dpp4-moa","dm-ex-dpp4","dm-tf-6","dm-2s-4"]}},{"id":"dm-dpp4-moa","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"How do DPP-4 inhibitors such as sitagliptin lower glucose?","choices":["They block breakdown of the body's own GLP-1 and GIP","They activate the GLP-1 receptor directly, as exenatide does","They block the enzyme that digests starch at the brush border","They inhibit the enzyme that cleaves proinsulin to insulin"],"answer":0,"ex":"In the professor's words, taking a DPP-4 inhibitor is like taking a GLP-1 drug, except that you make the GLP-1 yourself and the drug makes it last. The gliptins are oral and taken once daily.","src":"rec","why":["DPP-4 is the enzyme that breaks down the incretins; blocking it makes the incretins released after each meal last longer.","Direct receptor activation is what the GLP-1 agonists do; DPP-4 inhibitors preserve your own incretins.","That enzyme is alpha-glucosidase, the target of acarbose and miglitol.","Blocking proinsulin's conversion is how protease inhibitors raise glucose."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors: incretin actions, exendin-4, boxed warning for thyroid tumors, delayed gastric emptying","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-glp1","dm-exendin","dm-num-4","dm-pb-wt-2","dm-dpp4-moa","dm-ex-dpp4","dm-tf-6","dm-2s-4"]}},{"id":"dm-2s-4","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. DPP-4 inhibitors are given by subcutaneous injection once a week.\n2. DPP-4 inhibitors are weight neutral and don't cause hypoglycemia.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"The gliptins (sitagliptin, saxagliptin, linagliptin, alogliptin) are oral, once daily, weight neutral and free of hypoglycemia. Weekly injections are the long-acting GLP-1 agonists.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the gliptins are all taken by mouth once daily; weekly injections belong to GLP-1 agonists such as dulaglutide and semaglutide.","Statement 2 is true, not false: no hypoglycemia and weight neutrality are their listed advantages.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the gliptins are all taken by mouth once daily; weekly injections belong to GLP-1 agonists such as dulaglutide and semaglutide. Statement 2 is true, not false: no hypoglycemia and weight neutrality are their listed advantages.","Statement 1 is false: the gliptins are all taken by mouth once daily; weekly injections belong to GLP-1 agonists such as dulaglutide and semaglutide. Statement 2 is true: no hypoglycemia and weight neutrality are their listed advantages."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors: incretin actions, exendin-4, boxed warning for thyroid tumors, delayed gastric emptying","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-glp1","dm-exendin","dm-num-4","dm-pb-wt-2","dm-dpp4-moa","dm-ex-dpp4","dm-tf-6","dm-2s-4"]}},{"id":"dm-ex-dpp4","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is a listed advantage of DPP-4 inhibitors EXCEPT:","choices":["Strong, lasting A1C lowering","No hypoglycemia when used alone","Weight neutral","Usable in kidney and liver dysfunction"],"answer":0,"ex":"DPP-4 inhibitors: well tolerated, weight neutral, no hypoglycemia, usable in renal and hepatic dysfunction (no renal adjustment for linagliptin). Drawbacks: expensive, modest A1C lowering, low durability, avoid in heart failure except sitagliptin.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["The reverse is true: their A1C lowering is modest and their durability low (about 2 years).","No hypoglycemia is an advantage, as with the GLP-1 agonists.","They are weight neutral (some people lose a little).","The slide says they can be used in patients with renal and hepatic dysfunction."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"GLP-1 agonists and DPP-4 inhibitors: incretin actions, exendin-4, boxed warning for thyroid tumors, delayed gastric emptying","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-glp1","dm-exendin","dm-num-4","dm-pb-wt-2","dm-dpp4-moa","dm-ex-dpp4","dm-tf-6","dm-2s-4"]}},{"id":"dm-agi","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient starts acarbose. Which adverse effect best fits how it works?","choices":["Flatulence and bloating from carbohydrate left undigested in the gut","Genital yeast infections from glucose spilled into the urine","Ankle edema and weight gain from fluid retained in the tissues","Frequent hypoglycemia from extra insulin squeezed out of the pancreas"],"answer":0,"ex":"Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors (acarbose, miglitol) work in the gut: complex carbohydrates aren't broken down, so less is absorbed and the post-meal spike is smaller. Malabsorption, flatulence, bloating and diarrhea follow. They are most effective on a starchy, high-fiber diet low in glucose and sucrose.","src":"rec","why":["Acarbose blocks alpha-glucosidase at the brush border, so starch and disaccharides aren't broken down; the GI effects scale with the dose and the carbohydrate eaten.","Glucose in the urine and genital yeast infections come with SGLT2 inhibitors.","Edema and weight gain come with the thiazolidinediones.","Acarbose doesn't release insulin and causes no hypoglycemia on its own."]},{"id":"dm-amylin","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement about pramlintide is correct?","choices":["It is a synthetic amylin injected before meals, in type 1 or type 2 diabetes","It is an oral DPP-4 inhibitor that preserves the body's own GLP-1 and GIP","It is an inhaled insulin that starts working within one to two minutes","It raises glucagon release, which protects against hypoglycemia with insulin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Amylin is co-secreted with insulin by beta cells (islet amyloid polypeptide). It acts in the hindbrain to lower glucagon, slow gastric emptying and increase satiety, and it forms amyloid fibers in type 2. Pramlintide: half-life about 50 minutes, cleared by the kidney; nausea, and hypoglycemia with insulin.","src":"slides","why":["Pramlintide (Symlin) is synthetic amylin given subcutaneously before meals; it can be used in both types.","Preserving GLP-1 and GIP is what the gliptins do; pramlintide is injected.","The inhaled insulin is Afrezza.","Amylin lowers glucagon release, and pramlintide can cause hypoglycemia when used with insulin."]},{"id":"dm-match-target","lec":"dmpharm","type":"match","stem":"Match each action to the drug class that does it.","pairs":[["Blocks glucose reabsorption in the kidney","SGLT2 inhibitor"],["Mainly cuts the liver's glucose output","Metformin"],["PPAR-gamma agonist that adds fat cells","Thiazolidinedione"],["Blocks carbohydrate breakdown at the brush border","Alpha-glucosidase inhibitor"],["Closes the beta cell's ATP-sensitive potassium channel","Sulfonylurea"]],"ex":"The summary by target: secretagogues raise insulin secretion; metformin cuts hepatic glucose output; TZDs sensitize to insulin and decrease lipotoxicity; alpha-glucosidase inhibitors slow absorption; SGLT2 inhibitors increase renal glucose excretion; GLP-1 agonists, DPP-4 inhibitors and amylin lower glucagon.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"SGLT2 inhibitors: glucosuria, genital yeast infections, UTIs, hypotension, boxed warning for amputation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-sglt2","dm-sglt2-bbw","dm-num-3","dm-2s-7","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-match-target"]}},{"id":"dm-pb-sglt2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient added a new drug three months ago and now has several complaints (see the Patient Box). Which best explains her findings?","choices":["Glucose left in the urine feeds infections and pulls water out with it","Hypoglycemia from empagliflozin squeezing insulin out of the pancreas","Fluid retention from empagliflozin acting on PPAR-gamma in fat","Lactic acidosis from metformin building up in her bloodstream"],"answer":0,"ex":"SGLT2 inhibitors make the patient pee out glucose. Expect genital fungal infections (men and women), UTIs, polyuria, and dizziness and hypotension from the osmotic diuresis, which is worth remembering when a patient sits up after a long appointment.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypertension"],["Drugs","Metformin and lisinopril for years; empagliflozin added 3 months ago"],["Since then","Two vaginal yeast infections; urinating more often"],["In the chair","Lightheaded when the chair is brought upright after a long appointment; BP 98/60"]],"why":["Empagliflozin blocks renal glucose reabsorption: the glucosuria invites genital yeast infections and UTIs, and osmotic diuresis causes polyuria, dizziness and hypotension.","SGLT2 inhibitors don't release insulin, and hypoglycemia is rare with them.","PPAR-gamma is the thiazolidinedione receptor; TZDs cause edema and wouldn't explain yeast infections or frequent urination.","Lactic acidosis wouldn't explain yeast infections and frequent urination; those come from the glucosuria."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"SGLT2 inhibitors: glucosuria, genital yeast infections, UTIs, hypotension, boxed warning for amputation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-sglt2","dm-sglt2-bbw","dm-num-3","dm-2s-7","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-match-target"]}},{"id":"dm-sglt2-bbw","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"The lecture gives a boxed warning for two of the newer drug groups. Which is the boxed warning for the SGLT2 inhibitors canagliflozin and ertugliflozin?","choices":["Risk of lower-limb amputation","Thyroid C-cell tumors in animals","Lactic acidosis","New or worse heart failure"],"answer":0,"ex":"Two boxed warnings to keep apart: thyroid C-cell tumors for the GLP-1 agonists, and amputation for canagliflozin and ertugliflozin. The SGLT2 class also lists fracture risk and rare euglycemic DKA, which came from clinical trials.","src":"rec","why":["The professor pointed it out: the below-the-knee amputation risk was significant in clinical trials, so it is a boxed warning.","That is the boxed warning for every GLP-1 agonist, the other one in this lecture.","Lactic acidosis is one of metformin's listed adverse effects.","Edema with new or worsened heart failure belongs to the thiazolidinediones."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"SGLT2 inhibitors: glucosuria, genital yeast infections, UTIs, hypotension, boxed warning for amputation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-sglt2","dm-sglt2-bbw","dm-num-3","dm-2s-7","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-match-target"]}},{"id":"dm-2s-7","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. SGLT2 inhibitors induce glucosuria in a dose-dependent manner.\n2. SGLT2 inhibitors tend to lower the serum potassium.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"The gliflozins are highly selective for SGLT2 and induce glucosuria in proportion to the dose. The potassium effect on the slide is hyperkalemia: as the professor put it, when you lose sodium through these transporters, you tend to keep potassium.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: the listed effect is hyperkalemia, a high potassium, because losing sodium through these transporters tends to preserve potassium.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the slide says SGLT2 inhibition induces glucosuria in a dose-dependent manner.","Statement 1 is true: the slide says SGLT2 inhibition induces glucosuria in a dose-dependent manner. Statement 2 is false: the listed effect is hyperkalemia, a high potassium, because losing sodium through these transporters tends to preserve potassium.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the slide says SGLT2 inhibition induces glucosuria in a dose-dependent manner. Statement 2 is false, not true: the listed effect is hyperkalemia, a high potassium, because losing sodium through these transporters tends to preserve potassium."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"SGLT2 inhibitors: glucosuria, genital yeast infections, UTIs, hypotension, boxed warning for amputation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-sglt2","dm-sglt2-bbw","dm-num-3","dm-2s-7","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-match-target"]}},{"id":"dm-num-3","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about SGLT2 inhibitors are true?\n1. Their effect depends on beta cells that can still make insulin.\n2. They lower blood pressure.\n3. Rare euglycemic DKA is among their warnings.\n4. Empagliflozin and canagliflozin have shown positive cardiovascular outcomes.","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"SGLT2 inhibitors work in the kidney, independent of insulin, so they can be added to any other antidiabetic drug. They lower blood pressure through the osmotic diuresis, rarely cause hypoglycemia and, for empagliflozin and canagliflozin, improve cardiovascular outcomes; rare euglycemic DKA is among the warnings.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: they block glucose reabsorption in the kidney, so they work without the beta cells and can be added to any other agent. It leaves out 4, which is true: positive cardiovascular outcomes with empagliflozin and canagliflozin are a listed advantage.","It includes 1, which is false: they block glucose reabsorption in the kidney, so they work without the beta cells and can be added to any other agent. It leaves out 3, which is true: euglycemic DKA is on the slide as a rare warning.","It leaves out 4, which is true: positive cardiovascular outcomes with empagliflozin and canagliflozin are a listed advantage.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: they block glucose reabsorption in the kidney, so they work without the beta cells and can be added to any other agent."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"SGLT2 inhibitors: glucosuria, genital yeast infections, UTIs, hypotension, boxed warning for amputation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-sglt2","dm-sglt2-bbw","dm-num-3","dm-2s-7","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-match-target"]}},{"id":"dm-ex-sglt2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is NOT an expected effect of an SGLT2 inhibitor such as dapagliflozin?","choices":["Frequent hypoglycemia","Urinary tract infections","A fall in blood pressure","More frequent urination"],"answer":0,"ex":"The gliflozins (the name sounds like flushing the sugar away) make the kidney excrete glucose. Expect infections, polyuria and lower blood pressure; hypoglycemia is rare.","src":"rec","fmt":"except","why":["Hypoglycemia is rare with SGLT2 inhibitors; they lower glucose without touching insulin.","Glucose in the urine attracts infection; UTIs are an expected adverse effect.","Osmotic diuresis lowers circulating volume, so blood pressure falls, an advantage in hypertension.","Water follows the glucose into the urine (osmotic diuresis), so polyuria is expected."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"SGLT2 inhibitors: glucosuria, genital yeast infections, UTIs, hypotension, boxed warning for amputation","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-sglt2","dm-sglt2-bbw","dm-num-3","dm-2s-7","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-match-target"]}},{"id":"dm-tf-10","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Beyond diabetes and weight loss, semaglutide (Ozempic, Wegovy) is now also approved for patients with chronic kidney disease or cardiovascular disease.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The GLP-1 agonists keep gaining indications: semaglutide for chronic kidney disease and cardiovascular risk, and tirzepatide (Zepbound) this year for sleep apnea. So for a patient with diabetes and several cardiovascular problems, the professor called Wegovy or Ozempic the first-line choice.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["The professor said semaglutide has been approved for people with chronic kidney disease or cardiovascular issues, in addition to diabetes and weight loss.","It is true: semaglutide now carries kidney and cardiovascular indications too, which can make it the first choice in such a patient."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Situational choice with heart failure, heart disease or kidney disease: avoid TZDs (edema, macular edema), consider an SGLT2 inhibitor or semaglutide, sitagliptin the DPP-4 exception, linagliptin without renal adjustment","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-pb-hf-1","dm-pb-hf-2","dm-dpp4-hf","dm-pb-renal-2","dm-tzd-eye","dm-tf-5","dm-tf-10"]}},{"id":"dm-hypo-class","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which drug is most likely to cause hypoglycemia when used on its own in type 2 diabetes?","choices":["Glimepiride","Pioglitazone","Linagliptin","Empagliflozin"],"answer":0,"ex":"The rule the professor repeated: any drug that works by stimulating insulin release has insulin's side effect of hypoglycemia. Insulin, sulfonylureas and meglitinides cause it; metformin, TZDs, DPP-4 inhibitors, SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists (alone) rarely do.","src":"rec","why":["Glimepiride is a sulfonylurea: it stimulates insulin release whatever the glucose level, so it shares insulin's main adverse effect.","TZDs sensitize tissues to insulin without releasing it, so their hypoglycemia risk is low.","DPP-4 inhibitors cause no hypoglycemia; the incretins they preserve act in a glucose-dependent way.","SGLT2 inhibitors lower glucose through the kidney, and hypoglycemia is rare."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Which drugs cause hypoglycemia (insulin and the secretagogues) and which rarely do","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-hypo-class","dm-num-2","dm-met-hypo","dm-glucose-dep","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-ex-insae"]}},{"id":"dm-num-2","lec":"dmpharm","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about hypoglycemia risk are true?\n1. Any drug that works by stimulating insulin release shares insulin's risk of hypoglycemia.\n2. DPP-4 inhibitors cause hypoglycemia about as often as sulfonylureas do.\n3. GLP-1 agonists have a low risk of hypoglycemia when used alone.\n4. Pramlintide can cause hypoglycemia when it is used with insulin.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4","3 and 4 only","4 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"Hypoglycemia follows insulin itself and the drugs that release it (sulfonylureas, meglitinides), and pramlintide when added to insulin. DPP-4 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists raise insulin only in a glucose-dependent way, so on their own they rarely cause it.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: DPP-4 inhibitors cause no hypoglycemia on their own; they only prolong the body's own glucose-dependent incretins.","Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: DPP-4 inhibitors cause no hypoglycemia on their own; they only prolong the body's own glucose-dependent incretins.","It leaves out 1, which is true: the professor said every drug that stimulates insulin release has the same side effect of hypoglycemia.","It leaves out 1, which is true: the professor said every drug that stimulates insulin release has the same side effect of hypoglycemia. It leaves out 3, which is true: a low hypoglycemia risk as monotherapy is one of their listed advantages."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Which drugs cause hypoglycemia (insulin and the secretagogues) and which rarely do","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-hypo-class","dm-num-2","dm-met-hypo","dm-glucose-dep","dm-ex-sglt2","dm-ex-insae"]}},{"id":"dm-mm-weight","lec":"dmpharm","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these drugs are expected to cause weight gain? Select all that apply.","choices":["Insulin glargine","Glipizide","Pioglitazone","Semaglutide","Metformin"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"The second question to ask of every diabetes drug, after hypoglycemia: does it cause weight gain? Yes: insulin, sulfonylureas, meglitinides, TZDs. No: metformin (neutral or loss), DPP-4 inhibitors (neutral), GLP-1 agonists (loss).","src":"rec","why":["Insulin is anabolic, so weight gain is one of its adverse effects.","Sulfonylureas raise insulin release and share insulin's weight gain.","TZDs add fat cells and fat mass; weight gain and edema are listed adverse effects.","GLP-1 agonists cause weight loss, which is why so many people take them for weight.","Metformin is weight neutral or causes modest weight loss."],"likely":{"lec":"dmpharm","t":"Weight effects: gain with insulin, sulfonylureas, meglitinides and TZDs; loss with GLP-1 agonists; a GLP-1 agonist first for a patient with a weight problem","basis":"recording","ids":["dm-mm-weight","dm-pb-wt-1","dm-2s-3","dm-pb-thyroid","dm-ex-met"]}},{"id":"dm-mm-route","lec":"dmpharm","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these are given by injection? Select all that apply.","choices":["Insulin glargine","Pramlintide","Dulaglutide","Sitagliptin","Pioglitazone"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"The old name 'oral hypoglycemic agents' no longer fits: insulin, pramlintide and the GLP-1 agonists are injected (oral semaglutide is the exception). Sulfonylureas, meglitinides, metformin, TZDs, DPP-4 inhibitors, SGLT2 inhibitors and alpha-glucosidase inhibitors are oral.","src":"slides","why":["Insulin is parenteral (the one non-injected form is inhaled Afrezza).","Pramlintide is injected subcutaneously before meals.","Dulaglutide (Trulicity) is a GLP-1 agonist injected once weekly.","DPP-4 inhibitors are oral, once daily.","TZDs are oral."]},{"id":"p1-feedback","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Dr. Dababneh's example of a broken feedback loop: the pituitary stops responding to feedback inhibition and keeps releasing TSH. What is the most likely result?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism, since constant TSH keeps the thyroid making T3 and T4","Hypothyroidism, since the excess TSH feeds back and shuts off the thyroid","No change, since the hypothalamus cuts its TRH and restores the balance","Hyperthyroidism with a suppressed TSH, the pattern of a thyroid cause"],"answer":0,"ex":"In the loop TRH → TSH → T3 and T4, rising T3 and T4 normally tell the pituitary to stop sending TSH and the hypothalamus to stop sending TRH. A pituitary that ignores that message keeps driving the thyroid, so the patient becomes hyperthyroid with a high TSH: a secondary cause.","src":"rec","why":["With nothing switching TSH off, the thyroid keeps getting its signal and overproduces T3 and T4.","TSH stimulates the thyroid; it is T3 and T4 that feed back, and they act on the pituitary and hypothalamus.","The hypothalamus may cut TRH, but a pituitary that ignores feedback keeps releasing TSH anyway.","A suppressed TSH marks a primary (thyroid) cause; here TSH stays high, which marks a secondary (pituitary) cause."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Primary vs secondary lab patterns (TSH vs free T4; the same rule for ACTH)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-num-labs","p1-hashi-1","p1-sheehan-2","p1-graves-2","p1-tsh-adenoma","p1-corticotroph","p1-feedback"]}},{"id":"p1-2s-hormone","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Steroid hormones cross the cell membrane and can act on DNA in the nucleus.\n2. Polypeptide and amine hormones also act mainly inside the nucleus.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Steroid hormones are fat-soluble, so they pass through the mostly lipid cell membrane and translocate to the nucleus. Polypeptide (protein) and amine hormones interact with receptors on the cell surface only.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: polypeptide and amine hormones interact only with receptors on the cell surface.","Statement 1 is true, not false: being fat-soluble, they pass through the lipid membrane and can bind DNA in the nucleus.","Statement 1 is true: being fat-soluble, they pass through the lipid membrane and can bind DNA in the nucleus. Statement 2 is false: polypeptide and amine hormones interact only with receptors on the cell surface.","Statement 1 is true, not false: being fat-soluble, they pass through the lipid membrane and can bind DNA in the nucleus. Statement 2 is false, not true: polypeptide and amine hormones interact only with receptors on the cell surface."]},{"id":"p1-tf-lobes","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is made of secretory epithelial cells.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) is secretory epithelium, a true gland. The posterior lobe (neurohypophysis) is unmyelinated nerve fibers holding neurosecretory granules of stored hormones, supported by pituicytes, which are modified glial cells.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Secretory epithelial cells make up the anterior lobe, the adenohypophysis, not the posterior lobe.","The posterior lobe is unmyelinated nerve fibers with neurosecretory granules of stored hormones, plus pituicytes."]},{"id":"p1-num-cells","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the anterior pituitary are true?\n1. Thyrotrophs make both TSH and prolactin.\n2. Corticotrophs make both ACTH and MSH.\n3. It has 5 cell types that make 7 hormones.\n4. Gonadotrophs make both FSH and LH.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"Five cell types make seven hormones because two of them make a pair: corticotrophs (ACTH and MSH) and gonadotrophs (FSH and LH). TRH releases both TSH and prolactin, but from two different cells: thyrotrophs and mammotrophs.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: thyrotrophs make only TSH; prolactin comes from mammotrophs, even though TRH releases both.","It leaves out 4, which is true: gonadotrophs make FSH and LH.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: thyrotrophs make only TSH; prolactin comes from mammotrophs, even though TRH releases both.","It leaves out 3, which is true: somatotrophs, mammotrophs, corticotrophs, thyrotrophs and gonadotrophs make 7 hormones between them."]},{"id":"p1-match-hypoth","lec":"path1","type":"match","stem":"Match each hypothalamic hormone to its effect on the anterior pituitary.","pairs":[["TRH","Releases TSH and prolactin"],["GnRH","Releases FSH and LH"],["CRH","Releases ACTH"],["Somatostatin","Inhibits growth hormone release"],["Dopamine","Inhibits prolactin release"]],"ex":"Four releasing hormones and two inhibitors: TRH (TSH and prolactin), GnRH (FSH and LH), CRH (ACTH) and GHRH (growth hormone); somatostatin stops growth hormone and dopamine stops prolactin.","src":"slides"},{"id":"p1-tf-pitnet","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Pituitary adenomas are now called pituitary neuroendocrine tumors (PitNETs), though everyday practice still says adenoma.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The pituitary is a neuroendocrine organ whose cells stain for neuroendocrine markers, so its benign tumors were renamed PitNETs to match neuroendocrine tumors elsewhere in the body. Day to day, pathologists still say pituitary adenoma.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["The new name stresses the true neuroendocrine cell type; the old name is still used in practice.","Both parts hold: PitNET is the new name, and Dr. Dababneh said pathologists still say pituitary adenoma day to day."]},{"id":"p1-macro-mass","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A nonfunctioning pituitary adenoma measuring 18 mm is most likely to come to attention through which of the following?","choices":["Headaches, nausea and vomiting, and double vision from its mass effect","Amenorrhea and milky nipple discharge from the prolactin it secretes","An enlarging skull, vertebrae and organs from the growth hormone it secretes","Moon face, central obesity and diabetes from the ACTH it secretes"],"answer":0,"ex":"Microadenomas are under 10 mm; macroadenomas are over 10 mm. A large tumor shows itself through local mass effect: an enlarged sella turcica, raised intracranial pressure (nausea and vomiting), headache and vision problems. A nonfunctioning tumor has no hormone excess to give it away.","src":"rec","why":["Over 10 mm, it presses on nearby structures and raises intracranial pressure; with no hormone excess, mass effect is what brings it to attention.","That is a functioning prolactinoma, usually a microadenoma; a nonfunctioning tumor secretes no hormone.","That is acromegaly from a functioning somatotroph tumor; a nonfunctioning tumor secretes no hormone.","That is Cushing disease from a functioning corticotroph tumor; a nonfunctioning tumor secretes no hormone."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Pituitary adenomas: micro vs macro, mass effect, bitemporal hemianopsia, the functioning types","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-macro-mass","p1-hemianopsia","p1-somato","p1-gonado","p1-prl-1","p1-prl-2","p1-2s-gigant"]}},{"id":"p1-hemianopsia","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A macroadenoma grows up out of the sella turcica and presses on the optic chiasm. Which visual defect results, and why?","choices":["Bitemporal hemianopsia, from pressure on the crossing central fibers","Bitemporal hemianopsia, from pressure on the uncrossed lateral fibers","Loss of both nasal fields, from pressure on the uncrossed lateral fibers","Loss of one whole eye's field, from pressure on a single optic nerve"],"answer":0,"ex":"The adenoma presses on the crossing central fibers of the optic chiasm and spares the uncrossed lateral fibers, so the outer (temporal) half of each eye's field is lost: bitemporal hemianopsia, beginning in the upper temporal quadrants.","src":"slides","why":["The crossing central fibers of the chiasm are compressed, so both temporal fields are lost, starting in the upper temporal quadrants.","The uncrossed lateral fibers are the ones a chiasm lesion spares; the crossing central fibers are compressed.","The uncrossed lateral fibers are spared, and the fields lost are the temporal ones, not the nasal ones.","Pressing one optic nerve can affect that eye, but pressure on the chiasm itself hits the crossing fibers from both eyes."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Pituitary adenomas: micro vs macro, mass effect, bitemporal hemianopsia, the functioning types","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-macro-mass","p1-hemianopsia","p1-somato","p1-gonado","p1-prl-1","p1-prl-2","p1-2s-gigant"]}},{"id":"p1-tf-hyperpit","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Excess hormone production by the anterior pituitary almost always means a pituitary adenoma.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hyperpituitarism almost always implies an adenoma. The rare causes are carcinoma, nontumorous hyperplasia, hypothalamic lesions and visceral cancers.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The slide says hyperpituitarism almost always implies an adenoma.","Other causes exist (carcinoma, hyperplasia, hypothalamic lesions, visceral cancers), but they are rare; an adenoma is almost always the cause."]},{"id":"p1-somato","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A 50-year-old man's hat no longer fits. Imaging shows an enlarged skull and vertebrae and a 2.5 cm sellar mass, and he has new glucose intolerance and hypertension. Which cells make up the tumor?","choices":["Somatotrophs, making growth hormone","Corticotrophs, making ACTH and MSH","Gonadotrophs, making FSH and LH","Thyrotrophs, making thyroid-stimulating hormone"],"answer":0,"ex":"Somatotroph tumors are the largest pituitary adenomas and often show mass effects. Growth hormone excess causes gigantism in a child and acromegaly in an adult, with glucose intolerance, osteoporosis and hypertension.","src":"slides","why":["Growth hormone excess in an adult enlarges bones and organs (acromegaly) and brings glucose intolerance, osteoporosis and hypertension; somatotroph tumors are the largest adenomas.","ACTH excess (Cushing disease) also brings hypertension and diabetes, but with central obesity and a moon face, not an enlarging skull, and these tend to be microadenomas.","Gonadotroph adenomas are large but cause no clinical syndrome; they don't enlarge bones.","A TSH-secreting adenoma causes hyperthyroidism (weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance), not bone growth."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Pituitary adenomas: micro vs macro, mass effect, bitemporal hemianopsia, the functioning types","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-macro-mass","p1-hemianopsia","p1-somato","p1-gonado","p1-prl-1","p1-prl-2","p1-2s-gigant"]}},{"id":"p1-2s-gigant","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Gigantism is the result of growth hormone excess in an adult.\n2. Somatotroph adenomas are the largest pituitary adenomas.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Age decides the picture: growth hormone excess in a child causes gigantism (bigger and taller); in an adult it causes acromegaly (enlarged organs and bones, a bigger head). Somatotroph tumors are the largest adenomas, and mass effects are frequent.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: gigantism is growth hormone excess in a child; in an adult the same excess causes acromegaly.","Statement 2 is true, not false: somatotroph adenomas are the largest, and mass effects are frequent.","Statement 1 is false, not true: gigantism is growth hormone excess in a child; in an adult the same excess causes acromegaly. Statement 2 is true, not false: somatotroph adenomas are the largest, and mass effects are frequent.","Statement 1 is false: gigantism is growth hormone excess in a child; in an adult the same excess causes acromegaly. Statement 2 is true: somatotroph adenomas are the largest, and mass effects are frequent."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Pituitary adenomas: micro vs macro, mass effect, bitemporal hemianopsia, the functioning types","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-macro-mass","p1-hemianopsia","p1-somato","p1-gonado","p1-prl-1","p1-prl-2","p1-2s-gigant"]}},{"id":"p1-gonado","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A 48-year-old man is evaluated for headaches and blurred vision and found to have a 2 cm pituitary mass. He has no hormone-related symptoms, but his FSH and LH are elevated. Which kind of adenoma is this most likely to be?","choices":["A gonadotroph adenoma, which causes no clinical syndrome of its own","A null cell adenoma, since a tumor with no syndrome secretes nothing","A prolactinoma, the most common hyperfunctioning pituitary tumor","A corticotroph adenoma, which drives the adrenals to make excess steroids"],"answer":0,"ex":"Gonadotroph adenomas make FSH and LH but cause no clinical syndrome, so they come to attention through local mass effect and are then diagnosed by the elevated FSH and LH. They are 10 to 20 percent of macroadenomas in middle-aged men and women.","src":"slides","why":["Gonadotroph adenomas make FSH and LH without a syndrome, so they surface through mass effect and are diagnosed by the high FSH and LH.","Null cell adenomas make no hormone; this one is raising FSH and LH, so it is secreting.","A prolactinoma would raise prolactin and, in a woman, cause amenorrhea and galactorrhea; most are microadenomas.","That would raise ACTH and cause Cushing disease, and corticotroph tumors tend to be small."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Pituitary adenomas: micro vs macro, mass effect, bitemporal hemianopsia, the functioning types","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-macro-mass","p1-hemianopsia","p1-somato","p1-gonado","p1-prl-1","p1-prl-2","p1-2s-gigant"]}},{"id":"p1-prl-1","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Prolactinoma, a microadenoma of mammotrophs","Gonadotroph adenoma, a macroadenoma secreting FSH and LH","Sheehan syndrome, ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary","Corticotroph adenoma, a microadenoma secreting ACTH"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prolactinomas are the most common hyperfunctioning pituitary tumor, usually microadenomas in premenopausal women. Excess prolactin causes galactorrhea and, through negative feedback, shuts down the menstrual cycle: the amenorrhea-galactorrhea syndrome.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","26-year-old woman with no periods for 5 months and a milky discharge from both nipples"],["Pregnancy test","Negative"],["Prolactin","150 ng/mL (normal under 25)"],["MRI","6 mm mass in the sella turcica"]],"pcase":"path1-case1","why":["Amenorrhea plus galactorrhea with a high prolactin and a mass under 10 mm in a premenopausal woman is the classic prolactinoma.","Gonadotroph adenomas are large and cause no clinical syndrome; this mass is 6 mm and the prolactin is high.","Sheehan syndrome causes hypopituitarism with an inability to make milk; she is making milk without a pregnancy.","A corticotroph adenoma causes Cushing disease (central obesity, moon face), not milk production."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Pituitary adenomas: micro vs macro, mass effect, bitemporal hemianopsia, the functioning types","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-macro-mass","p1-hemianopsia","p1-somato","p1-gonado","p1-prl-1","p1-prl-2","p1-2s-gigant"]}},{"id":"p1-prl-2","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which hypothalamic hormone normally holds back the cells this tumor comes from?","choices":["Dopamine","Somatostatin","Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH)","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH)"],"answer":0,"ex":"A prolactinoma is a tumor of mammotrophs. Dopamine is prolactin's brake; TRH releases prolactin along with TSH.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","26-year-old woman with no periods for 5 months and a milky discharge from both nipples"],["Pregnancy test","Negative"],["Prolactin","150 ng/mL (normal under 25)"],["MRI","6 mm mass in the sella turcica"]],"pcase":"path1-case1","why":["Dopamine from the hypothalamus inhibits prolactin release from mammotrophs.","Somatostatin inhibits growth hormone, not prolactin.","TRH releases prolactin (and TSH), so it stimulates rather than holds back.","GnRH releases FSH and LH from gonadotrophs."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Pituitary adenomas: micro vs macro, mass effect, bitemporal hemianopsia, the functioning types","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-macro-mass","p1-hemianopsia","p1-somato","p1-gonado","p1-prl-1","p1-prl-2","p1-2s-gigant"]}},{"id":"p1-corticotroph","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A corticotroph adenoma causes Cushing disease. Using the lecture's primary versus secondary rule, which lab pattern fits?","choices":["ACTH high and cortisol high, with the cause upstream","ACTH low and cortisol high, with feedback suppressing ACTH","ACTH high and cortisol low, from a failing adrenal cortex","ACTH low and cortisol low, from a failing pituitary"],"answer":0,"ex":"A corticotroph adenoma makes ACTH, which stimulates excess adrenal cortical steroids. When the stimulating hormone and the gland's hormone are both high, the cause is secondary (upstream), the same rule as TSH and T4. Dr. Dababneh applied it to Cushing in the next hour too.","src":"rec","why":["The pituitary overproduces ACTH, which drives the adrenal cortex, so both rise: the cause is upstream of the adrenal, a secondary pattern.","A low ACTH with high cortisol means the adrenal makes cortisol on its own (primary) and feedback has shut ACTH off.","A rising ACTH with low cortisol is the pituitary responding to a failing adrenal, not an ACTH-secreting tumor.","Both low means the pituitary isn't making ACTH, the opposite of a corticotroph adenoma."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Primary vs secondary lab patterns (TSH vs free T4; the same rule for ACTH)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-num-labs","p1-hashi-1","p1-sheehan-2","p1-graves-2","p1-tsh-adenoma","p1-corticotroph","p1-feedback"]}},{"id":"p1-tf-hypopit75","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Hypopituitarism appears once about 25 percent of the anterior pituitary is lost.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The gland has a large reserve: hypopituitarism appears only after about 75 percent of the anterior pituitary parenchyma is lost. Less often, a hypothalamic disorder (such as a hypothalamic tumor) blocks delivery of the releasing factors.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The threshold is about 75 percent loss, not 25 percent.","It takes loss of about 75 percent of the anterior pituitary parenchyma."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypopituitarism: the 75 percent rule, Sheehan syndrome, null cell adenoma, empty sella, and its symptoms","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-sheehan-1","p1-mm-hypopit","p1-tf-hypopit75","p1-null-cell","p1-x-macro"]}},{"id":"p1-null-cell","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains how a \"null cell\" adenoma leads to hypopituitarism?","choices":["It makes no hormone itself, and as it grows it shuts down the normal pituitary","It cuts the pituitary's blood supply after childbirth, causing ischemic necrosis","It pushes the subarachnoid space into the sella through a defect in the diaphragm","Its hormone excess feeds back on the hypothalamus and stops every releasing hormone"],"answer":0,"ex":"Non-secretory (null cell) adenomas head the slide's list of causes of hypopituitarism: the tumor makes no hormone, and as a mass-like lesion it shuts down the pituitary around it.","src":"rec","why":["A non-secretory adenoma is just a mass: none of its cells make hormone, and it shuts the normal gland down.","That is Sheehan syndrome, postpartum ischemic necrosis, not an adenoma.","That describes empty sella syndrome, a chronic herniation through a defective diaphragma sellae.","A null cell adenoma has no hormone excess at all; that is what 'null cell' means."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypopituitarism: the 75 percent rule, Sheehan syndrome, null cell adenoma, empty sella, and its symptoms","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-sheehan-1","p1-mm-hypopit","p1-tf-hypopit75","p1-null-cell","p1-x-macro"]}},{"id":"p1-mm-hypopit","lec":"path1","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these are causes of hypopituitarism? Select all that apply.","choices":["Sheehan syndrome (postpartum ischemic necrosis)","A non-secretory (null cell) adenoma","Empty sella syndrome","A prolactin-secreting microadenoma (prolactinoma)","Ectopic ADH made by small cell carcinoma of the lung"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Hypopituitarism appears after about 75 percent of the anterior pituitary is lost. The lecture's causes: non-secretory (null cell) adenomas, ischemic necrosis (Sheehan syndrome), ablation, empty sella syndrome, inflammatory lesions, trauma and metastatic neoplasms.","src":"slides","why":["Ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary after delivery is a classic cause.","It makes no hormone and, as a mass, shuts down the normal gland.","A chronically herniated subarachnoid space leaves an enlarged, empty sella turcica.","A prolactinoma is the most common hyperfunctioning tumor, usually a microadenoma: hormone excess, not deficiency.","Ectopic ADH from a lung tumor is the most common cause of SIADH, a posterior pituitary syndrome, not hypopituitarism."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypopituitarism: the 75 percent rule, Sheehan syndrome, null cell adenoma, empty sella, and its symptoms","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-sheehan-1","p1-mm-hypopit","p1-tf-hypopit75","p1-null-cell","p1-x-macro"]}},{"id":"p1-x-macro","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A woman whose anterior pituitary has been largely destroyed could have each of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Milky discharge from the nipples (galactorrhea)","Sensitivity to cold, with fatigue","Irregular or absent periods and loss of pubic hair","Facial puffiness and anemia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hypopituitarism's symptoms depend on which hormones are missing: fatigue, weight loss, decreased sex drive, sensitivity to cold, decreased appetite, facial puffiness, anemia and infertility, and in women hot flashes, irregular or absent periods, loss of pubic hair and an inability to produce milk. Galactorrhea is the hormone excess of a prolactinoma.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Galactorrhea is prolactin excess, the prolactinoma picture; hypopituitarism brings the opposite, an inability to produce milk.","Fatigue and sensitivity to cold are on the slide's list of hypopituitarism symptoms.","Irregular or absent periods and loss of pubic hair are on the slide's list for women, since the pituitary hormones that run the cycle are missing.","Facial puffiness and anemia are both on the slide's list of hypopituitarism symptoms."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypopituitarism: the 75 percent rule, Sheehan syndrome, null cell adenoma, empty sella, and its symptoms","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-sheehan-1","p1-mm-hypopit","p1-tf-hypopit75","p1-null-cell","p1-x-macro"]}},{"id":"p1-sheehan-1","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sheehan syndrome: ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary","Empty sella syndrome: herniation of the subarachnoid space into the sella","Hashimoto thyroiditis: lymphocytes shutting down the thyroid follicles","Prolactinoma: a mammotroph microadenoma in a premenopausal woman"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sheehan syndrome is postpartum ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary: blood pressure falls around delivery and the gland's blood supply stops. The symptoms are those of hypopituitarism, including fatigue, cold sensitivity and an inability to produce milk for breastfeeding.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","31-year-old woman, 4 weeks after a delivery complicated by heavy bleeding and a sudden drop in blood pressure"],["Symptoms","Unable to produce milk for breastfeeding, fatigue, feels cold all the time"],["TSH","0.2 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0)"],["Free T4","0.5 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8)"]],"pcase":"path1-case2","why":["A sudden drop in blood pressure around delivery stops the anterior pituitary's blood supply; failure to make milk and a low TSH fit hypopituitarism.","Empty sella is a chronic herniation through a defective diaphragma sellae, classically in obese multiparous women, not an abrupt event after delivery.","Hashimoto thyroiditis is primary hypothyroidism, so her TSH would be high, not low.","A prolactinoma causes milk overproduction (galactorrhea); she cannot make milk at all."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypopituitarism: the 75 percent rule, Sheehan syndrome, null cell adenoma, empty sella, and its symptoms","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-sheehan-1","p1-mm-hypopit","p1-tf-hypopit75","p1-null-cell","p1-x-macro"]}},{"id":"p1-sheehan-2","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains why her TSH is low even though her free T4 is also low?","choices":["Her damaged pituitary can't make TSH, so her thyroid gets no signal: secondary hypothyroidism","Her thyroid has failed on its own, and its low output has turned TSH down: primary hypothyroidism","Her thyroid is overproducing T4, which has switched TSH off: primary hyperthyroidism","Antibodies are blocking her TSH receptors, which lowers TSH and T4 together: Hashimoto thyroiditis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Low thyroid hormone normally makes the pituitary raise TSH. When TSH is low too, the pituitary can't respond: secondary hypothyroidism, here from Sheehan syndrome.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","31-year-old woman, 4 weeks after a delivery complicated by heavy bleeding and a sudden drop in blood pressure"],["Symptoms","Unable to produce milk for breastfeeding, fatigue, feels cold all the time"],["TSH","0.2 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0)"],["Free T4","0.5 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8)"]],"pcase":"path1-case2","why":["When the pituitary is the problem, TSH and T4 fall together; that is the secondary pattern.","A failing thyroid makes the pituitary raise TSH by feedback, so primary hypothyroidism shows a high TSH.","Her free T4 is low, not high; a low TSH with a high T4 would be primary hyperthyroidism.","Blocking antibodies (Hashimoto) cause primary hypothyroidism, in which TSH rises."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Primary vs secondary lab patterns (TSH vs free T4; the same rule for ACTH)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-num-labs","p1-hashi-1","p1-sheehan-2","p1-graves-2","p1-tsh-adenoma","p1-corticotroph","p1-feedback"]}},{"id":"p1-2s-pp","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. ADH and oxytocin are made in the posterior pituitary.\n2. Diabetes insipidus is the clinical syndrome of a lack of oxytocin.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Both hormones are made in the hypothalamus (supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei) and only stored in the posterior pituitary. Diabetes insipidus is a lack of ADH action; the slide says no clinical syndromes are associated with oxytocin, which potentiates uterine contractions and milk ejection.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: they are made in the hypothalamus and only stored in the posterior pituitary. Statement 2 is false, not true: diabetes insipidus is a lack of ADH action; no clinical syndromes are associated with oxytocin.","Statement 1 is false: they are made in the hypothalamus and only stored in the posterior pituitary. Statement 2 is false: diabetes insipidus is a lack of ADH action; no clinical syndromes are associated with oxytocin.","Statement 1 is false, not true: they are made in the hypothalamus and only stored in the posterior pituitary.","Statement 2 is false, not true: diabetes insipidus is a lack of ADH action; no clinical syndromes are associated with oxytocin."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) vs SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-di-1","p1-di-2","p1-siadh","p1-tf-siadh","p1-tf-di","p1-num-di","p1-adh","p1-2s-pp"]}},{"id":"p1-adh","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pair of actions belongs to ADH?","choices":["Water reabsorption in the renal tubules and vasoconstriction","Potentiating uterine contractions and milk ejection","Milk production and control of the menstrual cycle","Helping bone take up calcium and inhibiting bone resorption"],"answer":0,"ex":"ADH maintains serum osmolality: it acts on the renal tubules to reabsorb water and also causes vasoconstriction. Its name is the hint: it works against diuresis.","src":"slides","why":["ADH maintains serum osmolality by having the tubules take back water, and it also constricts vessels.","Those are oxytocin's actions, the other posterior pituitary hormone.","Those belong to prolactin, from the anterior pituitary's mammotrophs.","That is calcitonin, from the thyroid's parafollicular C cells."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) vs SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-di-1","p1-di-2","p1-siadh","p1-tf-siadh","p1-tf-di","p1-num-di","p1-adh","p1-2s-pp"]}},{"id":"p1-tf-di","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Central diabetes insipidus causes polyuria with a low urine specific gravity and a low serum sodium.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Without ADH the tubules can't take back water, so a large volume of dilute urine (low specific gravity) leaves and the blood is concentrated: serum sodium and osmolality go up.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The serum sodium is high, not low: water is lost and the blood is concentrated.","Polyuria and dilute urine are right, but serum sodium and osmolality are high."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) vs SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-di-1","p1-di-2","p1-siadh","p1-tf-siadh","p1-tf-di","p1-num-di","p1-adh","p1-2s-pp"]}},{"id":"p1-tf-siadh","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The most common cause of SIADH is ectopic ADH made by small cell carcinoma of the lung.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"SIADH is continued ADH release without regard to serum osmolality. Water is retained and dilutes the blood, so these patients have hyponatremia; the most common cause is ectopic production by a nonendocrine tumor, small cell carcinoma of the lung.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Small cell carcinoma of the lung is the most common source of ectopic ADH.","The slide and the recording both name small cell lung carcinoma as the most common cause."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) vs SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-di-1","p1-di-2","p1-siadh","p1-tf-siadh","p1-tf-di","p1-num-di","p1-adh","p1-2s-pp"]}},{"id":"p1-num-di","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about posterior pituitary syndromes are true?\n1. Central diabetes insipidus is a deficiency of ADH.\n2. In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, ADH is normal but the kidneys don't respond to it.\n3. SIADH causes a high serum sodium.\n4. Diabetes insipidus is a disorder of blood sugar, like diabetes mellitus.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","2 and 4 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Diabetes insipidus is a lack of ADH action: central (no ADH) or nephrogenic (normal ADH, unresponsive kidneys). It has nothing to do with sugar; diabetes just means polyuria. SIADH is the opposite: water retention with hyponatremia.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1 and 2 are true. Statement 3 is false: SIADH retains water and dilutes the blood, so the sodium is low (hyponatremia). Statement 4 is false: diabetes insipidus is about ADH; diabetes means polyuria, and only diabetes mellitus is about sugar.","It includes 3, which is false: SIADH retains water and dilutes the blood, so the sodium is low (hyponatremia).","It includes 3, which is false: SIADH retains water and dilutes the blood, so the sodium is low (hyponatremia). It leaves out 2, which is true: nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is end-organ resistance: normal ADH, unresponsive kidneys.","It includes 4, which is false: diabetes insipidus is about ADH; diabetes means polyuria, and only diabetes mellitus is about sugar. It leaves out 1, which is true: central diabetes insipidus means the brain isn't producing enough ADH."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) vs SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-di-1","p1-di-2","p1-siadh","p1-tf-siadh","p1-tf-di","p1-num-di","p1-adh","p1-2s-pp"]}},{"id":"p1-di-1","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which diagnosis best fits?","choices":["Diabetes insipidus, from a lack of ADH action","SIADH, from ADH released without regard to osmolality","Diabetes mellitus, a disorder of glucose intolerance","Oxytocin deficiency from damage to the posterior pituitary"],"answer":0,"ex":"Without ADH, the renal tubules can't reabsorb water: polyuria, a low urine specific gravity, and a high serum sodium and osmolality. Head trauma, neoplasms, inflammatory disorders and surgery on the pituitary or hypothalamus cause the central form.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","23-year-old man, 3 days after a head injury in a car crash"],["Urine","Very large volumes; specific gravity 1.002 (low)"],["Serum sodium","151 mEq/L (normal 135-145)"],["Serum osmolality","312 mOsm/kg (normal 275-295)"]],"pcase":"path1-case3","why":["Polyuria with dilute urine and a high serum sodium and osmolality is the picture of missing ADH; head trauma is a listed cause.","SIADH retains water, so sodium falls (hyponatremia); his sodium is high.","Diabetes mellitus is about glucose; this picture comes from ADH, and 'diabetes' here just means polyuria.","Oxytocin has no associated clinical syndrome; the water problem comes from ADH."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) vs SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-di-1","p1-di-2","p1-siadh","p1-tf-siadh","p1-tf-di","p1-num-di","p1-adh","p1-2s-pp"]}},{"id":"p1-di-2","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which detail best supports the central form rather than the nephrogenic form?","choices":["The head injury, since trauma cuts ADH output while nephrogenic DI has normal ADH","The high serum sodium, since only the central form concentrates the blood","The low urine specific gravity, since the nephrogenic form makes concentrated urine","The large urine volume, since the nephrogenic form causes little polyuria"],"answer":0,"ex":"Central and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus look the same in the labs because in both, ADH has no effect on the tubules. The cause separates them: central is a lack of ADH (head trauma, tumors, inflammation, surgery); nephrogenic is normal ADH with kidneys that don't respond.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","23-year-old man, 3 days after a head injury in a car crash"],["Urine","Very large volumes; specific gravity 1.002 (low)"],["Serum sodium","151 mEq/L (normal 135-145)"],["Serum osmolality","312 mOsm/kg (normal 275-295)"]],"pcase":"path1-case3","why":["Head trauma is a listed cause of central diabetes insipidus; the nephrogenic form is a kidney that ignores normal ADH.","Both forms lose water and concentrate the blood, so a high sodium doesn't separate them.","Both forms make dilute urine, since ADH has no effect on the tubules in either.","Both forms cause polyuria; the difference is whether ADH is missing or ignored."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) vs SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-di-1","p1-di-2","p1-siadh","p1-tf-siadh","p1-tf-di","p1-num-di","p1-adh","p1-2s-pp"]}},{"id":"p1-siadh","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains his low sodium?","choices":["The tumor makes ADH ectopically, and the retained water dilutes his sodium","The tumor has spread to the posterior pituitary, so ADH is no longer released","His kidneys have stopped responding to ADH, so sodium is lost in the urine","The tumor makes oxytocin ectopically, and oxytocin drives water retention"],"answer":0,"ex":"SIADH is continued ADH release without regard to serum osmolality: water is retained, body fluids are diluted, and sodium falls. The most common cause is ectopic ADH from small cell carcinoma of the lung.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","67-year-old man who has smoked for 50 years, with a central lung mass and new confusion"],["Serum sodium","121 mEq/L (normal 135-145)"],["Serum osmolality","255 mOsm/kg (normal 275-295)"]],"why":["Small cell lung carcinoma is the most common source of ectopic ADH; retained water dilutes the blood (SIADH).","Losing ADH causes diabetes insipidus, which raises the sodium.","Kidneys that ignore ADH is nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, which loses water and raises the sodium.","Oxytocin acts on uterine contraction and milk ejection and has no clinical syndrome; ADH is the water hormone."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Diabetes insipidus (central vs nephrogenic) vs SIADH (small cell lung carcinoma)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-di-1","p1-di-2","p1-siadh","p1-tf-siadh","p1-tf-di","p1-num-di","p1-adh","p1-2s-pp"]}},{"id":"p1-lingual","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"During an oral exam, a dentist finds a smooth, solid mass in the midline at the base (posterior third) of a 17-year-old's tongue, and a biopsy shows normal thyroid follicles. Given how the thyroid develops, which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Lingual thyroid: tissue that never finished its descent from the tongue","Substernal thyroid: tissue that descended past the neck into the chest","Goiter: diffuse enlargement of a normally placed gland in the neck","Thyroglossal duct cyst: a remnant of the duct lower in the midline neck"],"answer":0,"ex":"The thyroid develops from an evagination of pharyngeal epithelium at the posterior tongue and descends to the neck. That downward migration explains the anomalies: tissue left in the tongue (lingual thyroid), a gland that goes too far (substernal), or a duct that stays (thyroglossal duct cyst).","src":"hub","why":["The thyroid starts as an evagination of pharyngeal epithelium at the back of the tongue; tissue left there is a lingual thyroid.","A substernal gland is the opposite error, too far down, and it sits in the chest, not the tongue.","A goiter enlarges the whole gland in its normal place below the larynx, not in the tongue.","A duct between tongue and thyroid that fails to go away gives a cyst along its path; this mass is solid thyroid tissue in the tongue base itself, a lingual thyroid."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Thyroid development, follicular adenoma and anaplastic carcinoma","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-lingual","p1-tf-adenoma","p1-ana-1","p1-ana-2","p1-tf-anaplastic"]}},{"id":"p1-ccell","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pairing of thyroid cell, product and action is correct?","choices":["Parafollicular C cells: calcitonin, acting on bone","Follicular cells: calcitonin, acting on bone","Parafollicular C cells: T3 and T4, regulating metabolism","Follicular cells: TSH, acting on the pituitary"],"answer":0,"ex":"Most thyroid cells are follicular cells (thyrocytes), which make T3 and T4 to regulate carbohydrate, lipid and protein metabolism. The parafollicular C cells make calcitonin, which acts on bone. Medullary carcinoma comes from the C cells.","src":"slides","why":["The C cells beside the follicles make calcitonin, which helps bone take up calcium and inhibits bone resorption.","Follicular cells (thyrocytes) make T3 and T4; calcitonin comes from the C cells beside the follicles.","T3 and T4 come from the follicular cells; the C cells make calcitonin.","TSH is made by pituitary thyrotrophs and acts on the thyroid, not the other way around."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Medullary carcinoma: C cells, RET and MEN 2, amyloid, calcitonin/chromogranin/CEA, sporadic vs familial","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mtc-1","p1-mtc-2","p1-x-mtc","p1-tf-mtc","p1-ccell"]}},{"id":"p1-goiter","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pairing of goiter type and cause is correct?","choices":["Endemic goiter: iodine deficiency","Sporadic goiter: iodine deficiency","Endemic goiter: hereditary enzyme defects","Endemic goiter: idiopathic, with no cause found"],"answer":0,"ex":"Goiter means enlargement of the thyroid. Endemic goiter comes from iodine deficiency (the most common cause); sporadic goiter comes from hereditary enzyme defects or is idiopathic.","src":"slides","why":["Endemic goiter is iodine deficiency, the most common cause of goiter.","Sporadic goiter comes from hereditary enzyme defects or is idiopathic; iodine deficiency is endemic.","Hereditary enzyme defects cause sporadic goiter, not endemic goiter.","Idiopathic goiter falls under sporadic; endemic goiter has a known cause, iodine deficiency."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypothyroid and hyperthyroid features: myxedema and cretinism (enlarged tongue), causes of hyperthyroidism","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mm-myxedema","p1-cretin","p1-x-hyper","p1-goiter"]}},{"id":"p1-num-labs","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about thyroid lab patterns are true?\n1. A low TSH with a high free T4 points to a cause in the thyroid itself.\n2. A high TSH with a high free T4 points to Graves disease.\n3. A high TSH with a low free T4 points to a cause in the thyroid itself.\n4. A low TSH with a low free T4 points to a cause in the thyroid itself.","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","3 and 4 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"Read TSH against T4. Opposite directions (one high, one low) mean the thyroid itself is the cause and feedback is working: primary. The same direction (both high or both low) means the pituitary is driving or failing: secondary. Graves is primary, with TSH suppressed.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: both high means the pituitary is overproducing TSH (secondary); Graves is primary, with a low TSH.","Statements 1 and 3 are true. Statement 2 is false: both high means the pituitary is overproducing TSH (secondary); Graves is primary, with a low TSH. Statement 4 is false: both low means the pituitary isn't making TSH: secondary hypothyroidism.","It includes 4, which is false: both low means the pituitary isn't making TSH: secondary hypothyroidism.","It includes 4, which is false: both low means the pituitary isn't making TSH: secondary hypothyroidism. It leaves out 1, which is true: high T4 made by the thyroid suppresses TSH by feedback: primary hyperthyroidism."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Primary vs secondary lab patterns (TSH vs free T4; the same rule for ACTH)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-num-labs","p1-hashi-1","p1-sheehan-2","p1-graves-2","p1-tsh-adenoma","p1-corticotroph","p1-feedback"]}},{"id":"p1-graves-1","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"What is driving her thyroid?","choices":["An IgG autoantibody that stimulates the TSH receptor","An autoantibody that blocks the TSH receptor's action","Excess TSH from a thyrotroph adenoma in the pituitary","A single hyperfunctional follicular adenoma in one lobe"],"answer":0,"ex":"Graves disease is the most common cause of endogenous hyperthyroidism, about 5 times more common in women. An IgG autoantibody stimulates the TSH receptor, so the whole gland undergoes diffuse hyperplasia (a goiter), and Graves ophthalmopathy affects the eyes.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","29-year-old woman in the dental chair for a cleaning"],["Vital signs","Pulse 118 and irregular; fine tremor of the hands; sweating"],["History","Lost 12 pounds without trying, can't stand the heat, nervous, frequent diarrhea"],["Neck and eyes","Diffusely enlarged thyroid; eyes appear to bulge"],["TSH","Under 0.01 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0)"],["Free T4","3.8 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8)"]],"pcase":"path1-case4","why":["Graves disease: IgG autoantibodies stimulate the TSH receptor, giving diffuse hyperplasia, goiter and eye disease.","Blocking antibodies are Hashimoto thyroiditis, which causes hypothyroidism.","A TSH-secreting adenoma would raise TSH; hers is suppressed.","A hyperfunctional adenoma is one nodule; her whole gland is enlarged and her eyes are involved."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Graves vs Hashimoto: stimulating vs blocking TSH-receptor antibodies, histology, epidemiology","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-graves-1","p1-2s-hashi","p1-hashi-2","p1-hashi-3","p1-2s-graves"]}},{"id":"p1-graves-2","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement about her lab pattern is correct?","choices":["It is primary: her thyroid makes the excess T4, and feedback has shut off TSH","It is secondary: her pituitary is making excess TSH, which then drives the thyroid","It is secondary: her hypothalamus has stopped making TRH, so TSH has fallen","It is primary: her thyroid is failing, so the pituitary has turned TSH down"],"answer":0,"ex":"High T4 with a low TSH is the primary pattern: the thyroid itself overproduces, and its hormone shuts off TSH. Dr. Dababneh called this a typical question: T3 and T4 high with TSH low is primary; both high is secondary.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","29-year-old woman in the dental chair for a cleaning"],["Vital signs","Pulse 118 and irregular; fine tremor of the hands; sweating"],["History","Lost 12 pounds without trying, can't stand the heat, nervous, frequent diarrhea"],["Neck and eyes","Diffusely enlarged thyroid; eyes appear to bulge"],["TSH","Under 0.01 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0)"],["Free T4","3.8 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8)"]],"pcase":"path1-case4","why":["A gland making hormone on its own suppresses TSH by negative feedback: high T4, low TSH, primary.","Secondary hyperthyroidism shows TSH high along with T4; hers is suppressed.","Losing TRH and TSH would lower T4, not raise it.","Her thyroid is overactive, not failing; a failing thyroid makes TSH rise."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Primary vs secondary lab patterns (TSH vs free T4; the same rule for ACTH)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-num-labs","p1-hashi-1","p1-sheehan-2","p1-graves-2","p1-tsh-adenoma","p1-corticotroph","p1-feedback"]}},{"id":"p1-2s-graves","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Graves disease is the most common cause of endogenous hyperthyroidism.\n2. Graves disease affects women about 5 times as often as men.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Graves disease is the most common cause of endogenous hyperthyroidism and is about 5 times more common in women. An IgG autoantibody stimulates the TSH receptor, causing diffuse hyperplasia with a goiter and Graves ophthalmopathy.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: it is the most common endogenous cause. Statement 2 is true: the slide gives a female-to-male ratio of 5 to 1.","Statement 1 is true, not false: it is the most common endogenous cause. Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide gives a female-to-male ratio of 5 to 1.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide gives a female-to-male ratio of 5 to 1.","Statement 1 is true, not false: it is the most common endogenous cause."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Graves vs Hashimoto: stimulating vs blocking TSH-receptor antibodies, histology, epidemiology","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-graves-1","p1-2s-hashi","p1-hashi-2","p1-hashi-3","p1-2s-graves"]}},{"id":"p1-tsh-adenoma","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Where is the cause most likely to be?","choices":["A TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma: secondary hyperthyroidism","Graves disease, with antibodies stimulating the thyroid: primary","Too many thyroid hormone pills: exogenous hyperthyroidism","A hyperfunctional multinodular goiter in the thyroid (Plummer syndrome)"],"answer":0,"ex":"When TSH and T4 are both high, the pituitary is the source: secondary hyperthyroidism, here from a TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma (one of the less common causes). Causes that start in the thyroid suppress TSH.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","45-year-old man with weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance and new headaches"],["TSH","7.2 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0)"],["Free T4","3.1 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8)"]],"why":["TSH and T4 both high means the pituitary is driving the thyroid; new headaches fit a pituitary mass.","Graves makes the thyroid overproduce on its own, so TSH would be suppressed.","Thyroid hormone from pills feeds back on the pituitary and suppresses TSH.","A hyperfunctional multinodular goiter is a thyroid cause, so TSH would be suppressed."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Primary vs secondary lab patterns (TSH vs free T4; the same rule for ACTH)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-num-labs","p1-hashi-1","p1-sheehan-2","p1-graves-2","p1-tsh-adenoma","p1-corticotroph","p1-feedback"]}},{"id":"p1-x-hyper","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is a cause of hyperthyroidism EXCEPT:","choices":["Sheehan syndrome after delivery","Struma ovarii","Hyperfunctional multinodular goiter (Plummer syndrome)","A TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma"],"answer":0,"ex":"Causes of hyperthyroidism: Graves disease (diffuse hyperplasia), exogenous thyroid hormone, hyperfunctional multinodular goiter (Plummer syndrome) and a hyperfunctional adenoma; less often thyroiditis, TSH-secreting pituitary adenomas and struma ovarii. Sheehan syndrome is hypopituitarism.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Sheehan syndrome destroys the anterior pituitary, so TSH falls and the result is hypothyroidism.","Struma ovarii is listed among the less common causes of hyperthyroidism.","A multinodular goiter can be hyperfunctioning; the slide calls it Plummer syndrome.","A TSH-secreting adenoma is a less common, secondary cause of hyperthyroidism."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypothyroid and hyperthyroid features: myxedema and cretinism (enlarged tongue), causes of hyperthyroidism","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mm-myxedema","p1-cretin","p1-x-hyper","p1-goiter"]}},{"id":"p1-hashi-1","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Where is the problem in her thyroid axis?","choices":["In the thyroid itself: primary hypothyroidism","In the pituitary: secondary hypothyroidism","In the thyroid itself: primary hyperthyroidism","In the pituitary: secondary hyperthyroidism"],"answer":0,"ex":"Low T4 with a high TSH is primary hypothyroidism: the gland has failed and the pituitary is pushing harder. Her enlarged tongue, puffy hands and coarse features are myxedema findings a dentist can see.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","46-year-old woman, born and raised in the US, at a new-patient dental visit"],["Oral and facial findings","Enlarged tongue, puffy face and hands, coarse facial features"],["History","Always cold, has gained weight, feels sluggish and apathetic"],["TSH","18.5 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0)"],["Free T4","0.5 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8)"]],"pcase":"path1-case5","why":["A low free T4 with a high TSH means the thyroid is failing and the pituitary is responding by feedback.","Pituitary failure would give a low TSH alongside the low T4.","Her free T4 is low and her symptoms (cold, weight gain, sluggish) are hypothyroid.","Secondary hyperthyroidism shows TSH and T4 both high; her T4 is low."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Primary vs secondary lab patterns (TSH vs free T4; the same rule for ACTH)","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-num-labs","p1-hashi-1","p1-sheehan-2","p1-graves-2","p1-tsh-adenoma","p1-corticotroph","p1-feedback"]}},{"id":"p1-hashi-2","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most likely cause of her condition?","choices":["Hashimoto thyroiditis, an autoimmune lymphocytic thyroiditis","Graves disease, an autoimmune disease of the TSH receptor in women","Sheehan syndrome, a postpartum necrosis of the anterior pituitary","Endemic goiter from a lack of iodine in the diet"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hashimoto thyroiditis is a chronic autoimmune thyroiditis and the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the US. In this lecture its antibodies are anti-TSH receptor antibodies that block TSH, the opposite of the stimulating antibodies in Graves.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","46-year-old woman, born and raised in the US, at a new-patient dental visit"],["Oral and facial findings","Enlarged tongue, puffy face and hands, coarse facial features"],["History","Always cold, has gained weight, feels sluggish and apathetic"],["TSH","18.5 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0)"],["Free T4","0.5 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8)"]],"pcase":"path1-case5","why":["Hashimoto thyroiditis (chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis) is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the US.","Graves antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor and cause hyperthyroidism, with TSH suppressed.","Sheehan syndrome is pituitary failure, so her TSH would be low, not high.","Iodine deficiency causes endemic goiter, but in a US-born adult the most common cause of hypothyroidism is Hashimoto thyroiditis."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Graves vs Hashimoto: stimulating vs blocking TSH-receptor antibodies, histology, epidemiology","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-graves-1","p1-2s-hashi","p1-hashi-2","p1-hashi-3","p1-2s-graves"]}},{"id":"p1-hashi-3","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"If her thyroid were biopsied, which description would fit best?","choices":["Many lymphocytes; follicular cells with larger, irregular nuclei and pinker cytoplasm","Diffuse hyperplasia of all the follicular cells throughout an enlarged, overactive gland","Papillae on fibrovascular cores, lined by crowded cells with pale, grooved nuclei","Nests of spindled neuroendocrine cells with no colloid and deposits of amyloid"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hashimoto's proper name, chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis, describes the slide: lymphocytes infiltrate the gland and shut down the follicles. Compared with normal thyroid, the follicular cells have larger, irregular nuclei and pinker cytoplasm.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","46-year-old woman, born and raised in the US, at a new-patient dental visit"],["Oral and facial findings","Enlarged tongue, puffy face and hands, coarse facial features"],["History","Always cold, has gained weight, feels sluggish and apathetic"],["TSH","18.5 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4.0)"],["Free T4","0.5 ng/dL (normal 0.8-1.8)"]],"pcase":"path1-case5","why":["Chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis: lymphocytes shut down the follicles, whose cells get larger, irregular nuclei and pinker cytoplasm.","Diffuse hyperplasia of all the follicular cells is Graves disease, which overproduces hormone.","Papillae with pale, grooved nuclei are papillary thyroid carcinoma.","Spindled nests without colloid, with amyloid, are medullary carcinoma."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Graves vs Hashimoto: stimulating vs blocking TSH-receptor antibodies, histology, epidemiology","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-graves-1","p1-2s-hashi","p1-hashi-2","p1-hashi-3","p1-2s-graves"]}},{"id":"p1-2s-hashi","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. In Hashimoto thyroiditis, antibodies against the TSH receptor stimulate it.\n2. Hashimoto thyroiditis is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the US.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"In this lecture both diseases involve antibodies against the TSH receptor: in Graves they stimulate it (hyperthyroidism), in Hashimoto they block it (hypothyroidism). Hashimoto is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the US.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: Hashimoto antibodies block the action of TSH; it is Graves antibodies that stimulate the receptor.","Statement 2 is true, not false: it is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the US.","Statement 1 is false, not true: Hashimoto antibodies block the action of TSH; it is Graves antibodies that stimulate the receptor. Statement 2 is true, not false: it is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the US.","Statement 1 is false: Hashimoto antibodies block the action of TSH; it is Graves antibodies that stimulate the receptor. Statement 2 is true: it is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the US."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Graves vs Hashimoto: stimulating vs blocking TSH-receptor antibodies, histology, epidemiology","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-graves-1","p1-2s-hashi","p1-hashi-2","p1-hashi-3","p1-2s-graves"]}},{"id":"p1-mm-myxedema","lec":"path1","type":"multi","stem":"A patient has long-standing primary hypothyroidism (myxedema). Which findings fit? Select all that apply.","choices":["Enlargement of the tongue","Coarsening of the facial features","Heat intolerance","Weight loss with diarrhea","Apathy and mental sluggishness"],"correct":[0,1,4],"ex":"Myxedema: apathy and mental sluggishness, weight gain, cold intolerance, lethargy, coarse facial features, an enlarged tongue and puffy hands. Everything slows down, the mirror image of hyperthyroidism.","src":"slides","why":["An enlarged tongue is on the myxedema list, and it is a finding a dentist may see first.","Coarse facial features are on the myxedema list.","Heat intolerance is hyperthyroid; hypothyroid patients are cold intolerant.","Weight loss and diarrhea are hyperthyroid findings; myxedema brings weight gain.","Apathy and mental sluggishness lead the myxedema list."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypothyroid and hyperthyroid features: myxedema and cretinism (enlarged tongue), causes of hyperthyroidism","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mm-myxedema","p1-cretin","p1-x-hyper","p1-goiter"]}},{"id":"p1-cretin","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A 3-year-old has impaired mental development, short stature, a large head and a large tongue. Which pairing of condition and cause fits?","choices":["Cretinism, from agenesis of the thyroid","Gigantism, from a somatotroph adenoma in childhood","Cretinism, from a TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma","Acromegaly, from growth hormone excess before adulthood"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cretinism is hypothyroidism in childhood: impaired mental development, short stature, a large head and a large tongue. Its causes are iodine deficiency, enzyme deficiency and agenesis of the thyroid.","src":"slides","why":["Cretinism is childhood hypothyroidism; iodine deficiency, enzyme deficiency and thyroid agenesis are its listed causes.","Gigantism makes a child bigger and taller; this child is short.","A TSH-secreting adenoma causes hyperthyroidism, not childhood hypothyroidism.","Acromegaly is growth hormone excess in an adult, and it enlarges rather than stunts."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Hypothyroid and hyperthyroid features: myxedema and cretinism (enlarged tongue), causes of hyperthyroidism","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mm-myxedema","p1-cretin","p1-x-hyper","p1-goiter"]}},{"id":"p1-tf-adenoma","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Follicular adenoma usually presents as a painless, solitary cold nodule.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Follicular adenoma is encapsulated, normal-appearing tissue that usually forms a single painless cold nodule (rarely hyperthyroid), sometimes with difficulty swallowing.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Painless, usually solitary and cold (rarely hyperthyroid) is the slide's description.","The slide describes exactly that: a painless cold nodule, usually solitary."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Thyroid development, follicular adenoma and anaplastic carcinoma","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-lingual","p1-tf-adenoma","p1-ana-1","p1-ana-2","p1-tf-anaplastic"]}},{"id":"p1-adenoma-vs-ca","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A resected solitary thyroid nodule is made of follicles. Which finding makes it a follicular carcinoma rather than a follicular adenoma?","choices":["Tumor pushing through its capsule or into a blood vessel","An intact fibrous capsule surrounding the whole nodule","Follicles that look like normal thyroid tissue","Nuclear grooves and pseudoinclusions in the tumor cells"],"answer":0,"ex":"Follicular adenoma and follicular carcinoma are both encapsulated follicular tumors. The adenoma's capsule is intact; the carcinoma invades through the capsule (a mushroom-like push) or into blood vessels. Invasion makes the diagnosis.","src":"rec","why":["Invasion of the capsule or vessels is what defines follicular carcinoma; the adenoma stays inside its capsule.","An intact capsule with no invasion is the follicular adenoma.","Normal-appearing tissue in a capsule is the adenoma's description; it is invasion that marks the carcinoma.","Grooves and pseudoinclusions define papillary carcinoma, not follicular carcinoma."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary vs follicular carcinoma: BRAF/lymphatics/multifocal/total thyroidectomy vs RAS/blood/invasion/hemithyroidectomy","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-2s-ptc","p1-ptc-2","p1-fc-mets","p1-mm-fc","p1-match-ca","p1-adenoma-vs-ca","p1-num-fc"]}},{"id":"p1-num-fc","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about follicular thyroid carcinoma are true?\n1. It is more common in women.\n2. The mean age at diagnosis is about 50 years.\n3. A minimally invasive tumor shows capsular invasion only.\n4. A widely invasive tumor has invaded one or two blood vessels.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":1,"ex":"Follicular carcinoma is more common in women, mean age about 50. It is classified by invasion: minimally invasive (capsule only), encapsulated angioinvasive (vessels) and widely invasive (four or more vessels plus the adjacent thyroid tissue).","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 3, which is true: minimally invasive means grossly encapsulated, with capsular invasion on histology.","Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: widely invasive means four or more blood vessels plus the adjacent thyroid tissue.","It includes 4, which is false: widely invasive means four or more blood vessels plus the adjacent thyroid tissue.","It includes 4, which is false: widely invasive means four or more blood vessels plus the adjacent thyroid tissue. It leaves out 1, which is true: the slide says it is more common in women."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary vs follicular carcinoma: BRAF/lymphatics/multifocal/total thyroidectomy vs RAS/blood/invasion/hemithyroidectomy","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-2s-ptc","p1-ptc-2","p1-fc-mets","p1-mm-fc","p1-match-ca","p1-adenoma-vs-ca","p1-num-fc"]}},{"id":"p1-fc-mets","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A 55-year-old woman with widely invasive follicular carcinoma develops a metastasis. Which pairing of site and route fits this tumor?","choices":["Bone, such as the skull or sternum, via the blood","The cervical lymph nodes, carried there through the lymphatics","The lung, after spreading there through the lymphatic vessels","The cervical lymph nodes, carried there through the blood"],"answer":0,"ex":"Follicular carcinoma invades capsule and vessels, so its metastases are blood-borne: lung and bones (shoulder girdle, sternum, skull, iliac bones), most often from widely invasive tumors. Papillary carcinoma is the one that goes to lymph nodes.","src":"slides","why":["Follicular carcinoma spreads by angioinvasion, so its metastases are blood-borne, to lung and bones.","Lymphatic spread to the neck nodes is papillary carcinoma's route.","The lung is a target, but follicular carcinoma reaches it through blood vessels, not lymphatics.","Follicular carcinoma's blood-borne spread goes to distant organs rather than the neck nodes."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary vs follicular carcinoma: BRAF/lymphatics/multifocal/total thyroidectomy vs RAS/blood/invasion/hemithyroidectomy","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-2s-ptc","p1-ptc-2","p1-fc-mets","p1-mm-fc","p1-match-ca","p1-adenoma-vs-ca","p1-num-fc"]}},{"id":"p1-mm-fc","lec":"path1","type":"multi","stem":"Which findings point to follicular carcinoma rather than papillary carcinoma? Select all that apply.","choices":["Tumor invading through its capsule","Metastasis to the lung or bones through the blood","Psammoma bodies","Separate tumor foci in both lobes","Nuclear grooves and pseudoinclusions"],"correct":[0,1],"ex":"Follicular carcinoma (RAS-like) is defined by invasion of its capsule or vessels and spreads through blood to lung and bone. Papillary carcinoma (BRAF) has the distinctive nuclei and psammoma bodies, can be multifocal and spreads to lymph nodes.","src":"rec","why":["Capsular invasion is one of the two kinds of invasion that define follicular carcinoma.","Follicular carcinoma spreads by angioinvasion, giving blood-borne metastases to lung and bone.","Psammoma bodies, calcified dead papillae, point to papillary carcinoma.","Multifocal disease is a papillary carcinoma feature, which is why it gets a total thyroidectomy.","Grooves and pseudoinclusions are the BRAF-driven nuclear features of papillary carcinoma."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary vs follicular carcinoma: BRAF/lymphatics/multifocal/total thyroidectomy vs RAS/blood/invasion/hemithyroidectomy","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-2s-ptc","p1-ptc-2","p1-fc-mets","p1-mm-fc","p1-match-ca","p1-adenoma-vs-ca","p1-num-fc"]}},{"id":"p1-2s-ptc","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Papillary thyroid carcinoma is linked to radiation exposure.\n2. Papillary thyroid carcinoma spreads mainly through blood vessels.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Papillary carcinoma is usually linked to radiation exposure rather than iodine deficiency, and it spreads through lymphatics to the lymph nodes. Spread through blood vessels is follicular carcinoma's route.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: it spreads through lymphatics to the lymph nodes; blood-vessel spread is follicular carcinoma's route.","Statement 1 is true, not false: radiation exposure, rather than iodine deficiency, is its usual background.","Statement 1 is true: radiation exposure, rather than iodine deficiency, is its usual background. Statement 2 is false: it spreads through lymphatics to the lymph nodes; blood-vessel spread is follicular carcinoma's route.","Statement 1 is true, not false: radiation exposure, rather than iodine deficiency, is its usual background. Statement 2 is false, not true: it spreads through lymphatics to the lymph nodes; blood-vessel spread is follicular carcinoma's route."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary vs follicular carcinoma: BRAF/lymphatics/multifocal/total thyroidectomy vs RAS/blood/invasion/hemithyroidectomy","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-2s-ptc","p1-ptc-2","p1-fc-mets","p1-mm-fc","p1-match-ca","p1-adenoma-vs-ca","p1-num-fc"]}},{"id":"p1-ptc-braf","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A pathologist describes a thyroid tumor: papillae with thin fibrovascular cores, lined by overcrowded cells with enlarged, pale nuclei, chromatin pushed to the edge, nuclear grooves and intranuclear inclusions of cytoplasm. Which mutation most likely drives it?","choices":["BRAF","RAS (such as NRAS or HRAS)","RET","p53"],"answer":0,"ex":"This is papillary thyroid carcinoma, which Dr. Dababneh tied to BRAF. In his sorting scheme, RAS goes with follicular tumors and RET with medullary carcinoma.","src":"rec","why":["These are papillary carcinoma's features, and its grooves and pseudoinclusions are features of the BRAF mutation that defines it.","RAS mutations drive follicular tumors, which lack these nuclear changes and spread through blood vessels.","RET drives medullary carcinoma, a C-cell tumor of spindled nests with amyloid.","p53 is among the many mutations of anaplastic carcinoma, which is too undifferentiated to show these features."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary carcinoma histology: pale grooved nuclei, pseudoinclusions, papillae, psammoma bodies","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-ptc-braf","p1-pseudoincl","p1-seq-psammoma","p1-tf-psammoma","p1-x-ptc","p1-ptc-1"]}},{"id":"p1-pseudoincl","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"In papillary thyroid carcinoma, what is a nuclear pseudoinclusion?","choices":["Cytoplasm trapped inside the nucleus by a deep fold of its membrane","A line across the nucleus where its membrane dips inward only slightly","A laminated calcification that is left behind when a papilla has died","A clump of amyloid taken up into the nucleus of a neuroendocrine cell"],"answer":0,"ex":"Papillary carcinoma's nuclear membranes are irregular. A shallow dip, seen edge-on on a flat slide, looks like a line: the groove. When the dip deepens until the membrane meets itself, a lobule of cytoplasm is trapped inside the nucleus: the pseudoinclusion.","src":"rec","why":["When the nuclear membrane folds in until it meets itself, a lobule of cytoplasm ends up inside the nucleus.","A shallow dip seen edge-on is a nuclear groove, the step before a pseudoinclusion.","That is a psammoma body, found in about half of papillary carcinomas.","Amyloid is a medullary carcinoma feature, not part of papillary carcinoma's nuclei."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary carcinoma histology: pale grooved nuclei, pseudoinclusions, papillae, psammoma bodies","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-ptc-braf","p1-pseudoincl","p1-seq-psammoma","p1-tf-psammoma","p1-x-ptc","p1-ptc-1"]}},{"id":"p1-tf-psammoma","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Psammoma bodies are found in nearly all papillary thyroid carcinomas.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Psammoma bodies are in about half (50 percent) of papillary carcinomas. When a true psammoma body is seen in the thyroid, the presumption is papillary carcinoma.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["They are found in about 50 percent of cases, not nearly all.","The slide gives psammoma bodies in 50 percent of cases."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary carcinoma histology: pale grooved nuclei, pseudoinclusions, papillae, psammoma bodies","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-ptc-braf","p1-pseudoincl","p1-seq-psammoma","p1-tf-psammoma","p1-x-ptc","p1-ptc-1"]}},{"id":"p1-seq-psammoma","lec":"path1","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the steps in the formation of a psammoma body in order.","steps":["A papilla grows into the lumen on a thin fibrovascular core","Its small capillary can't keep the floating papilla supplied","The papilla dies","The dead papilla calcifies in laminated layers"],"ex":"Papillae float on a small capillary supply, so some die out; when they die they calcify in laminated (layered) rings. Those rings are psammoma bodies, and in the thyroid the presumption is papillary carcinoma.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary carcinoma histology: pale grooved nuclei, pseudoinclusions, papillae, psammoma bodies","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-ptc-braf","p1-pseudoincl","p1-seq-psammoma","p1-tf-psammoma","p1-x-ptc","p1-ptc-1"]}},{"id":"p1-num-ptc","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about papillary thyroid carcinoma are true?\n1. It makes up about 10 percent of thyroid cancers.\n2. It usually occurs before age 40.\n3. Its 10-year survival is greater than 80 percent.\n4. Its nuclei are darker than normal (hyperchromatic).","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only"],"answer":3,"ex":"Papillary carcinoma is 60 to 80 percent of thyroid cancers, usually under 40, slow growing, with a 10-year survival greater than 80 percent. Its nuclei are pale (hypochromatic), the Orphan Annie eye look. Ten percent is the share of anaplastic carcinoma.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: it is 60 to 80 percent of thyroid cancers; 10 percent is anaplastic carcinoma. It leaves out 3, which is true: it is slow growing, with a 10-year survival greater than 80 percent.","It includes 1, which is false: it is 60 to 80 percent of thyroid cancers; 10 percent is anaplastic carcinoma.","It includes 1, which is false: it is 60 to 80 percent of thyroid cancers; 10 percent is anaplastic carcinoma. It leaves out 2, which is true: it usually occurs under 40.","Statements 2 and 3 are true. Statement 1 is false: it is 60 to 80 percent of thyroid cancers; 10 percent is anaplastic carcinoma. Statement 4 is false: its nuclei are pale (hypochromatic), with the chromatin pushed to the edge: Orphan Annie eye nuclei."]},{"id":"p1-x-ptc","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma typically shows each of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Deposits of amyloid between the tumor cells","Nuclear grooves and pseudoinclusions","Laminated calcifications, called psammoma bodies","Papillae built on fibrovascular cores"],"answer":0,"ex":"Papillary carcinoma: papillae on fibrovascular cores, enlarged pale overcrowded nuclei with grooves and pseudoinclusions, and psammoma bodies (laminated calcifications) in about half. Amyloid belongs to medullary carcinoma: in the thyroid, a laminated calcification points to papillary, amyloid to medullary.","src":"rec","fmt":"except","why":["Amyloid, with its apple-green birefringence, is a hint to medullary carcinoma, the C-cell tumor; it is not a papillary carcinoma feature.","Grooves and pseudoinclusions are the defining nuclear features of papillary carcinoma.","About half of papillary carcinomas have psammoma bodies, the calcified remains of dead papillae.","Fronds with a small vessel in the middle give papillary carcinoma its name."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary carcinoma histology: pale grooved nuclei, pseudoinclusions, papillae, psammoma bodies","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-ptc-braf","p1-pseudoincl","p1-seq-psammoma","p1-tf-psammoma","p1-x-ptc","p1-ptc-1"]}},{"id":"p1-ptc-1","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Which diagnosis and route of spread best fit?","choices":["Papillary carcinoma, which spreads through the lymphatics","Follicular carcinoma, which spreads through the lymphatics","Papillary carcinoma, which spreads through blood vessels","Medullary carcinoma, which arises from the C cells"],"answer":0,"ex":"Papillary carcinoma: usually under 40, linked to radiation, defined by its nuclei (enlarged, overcrowded, pale, grooved, with pseudoinclusions) and spread through lymphatics to the neck nodes.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman whose neck was treated with radiation as a child"],["Exam","Firm 2 cm thyroid nodule and an enlarged lymph node on the same side of the neck"],["Fine-needle aspirate","Crowded cells with enlarged, pale nuclei, nuclear grooves and pseudoinclusions"]],"pcase":"path1-case6","why":["A radiation history, pale grooved nuclei with pseudoinclusions and a neck node all point to papillary carcinoma, which travels by lymphatics.","Follicular carcinoma spreads through blood vessels and lacks these nuclear features.","It is papillary carcinoma, but it spreads by lymphatics, which is why the neck node is involved.","Medullary carcinoma is spindled neuroendocrine cells with amyloid, not pale, grooved nuclei."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary carcinoma histology: pale grooved nuclei, pseudoinclusions, papillae, psammoma bodies","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-ptc-braf","p1-pseudoincl","p1-seq-psammoma","p1-tf-psammoma","p1-x-ptc","p1-ptc-1"]}},{"id":"p1-ptc-2","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Her surgeon plans to remove the whole thyroid rather than one lobe. Why?","choices":["Papillary carcinoma can be multifocal, so the other lobe may hold a second focus","Papillary carcinoma spreads through blood vessels, so a lobe left behind risks metastasis","Every thyroid carcinoma, follicular included, needs the whole gland removed","Its 10-year survival is under 50 percent, so the widest surgery is needed"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Dababneh stressed this: a follicular carcinoma can be treated by removing the lobe it is in (hemithyroidectomy), but papillary carcinoma can be multifocal, so the entire gland is taken.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman whose neck was treated with radiation as a child"],["Exam","Firm 2 cm thyroid nodule and an enlarged lymph node on the same side of the neck"],["Fine-needle aspirate","Crowded cells with enlarged, pale nuclei, nuclear grooves and pseudoinclusions"]],"pcase":"path1-case6","why":["Papillary carcinoma can be multifocal, so a total thyroidectomy removes a possible focus in the other lobe.","Papillary carcinoma spreads through lymphatics, not blood vessels.","For follicular carcinoma a hemithyroidectomy is enough; the whole gland is papillary's because of multifocality.","Papillary carcinoma is slow growing, with a 10-year survival greater than 80 percent."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary vs follicular carcinoma: BRAF/lymphatics/multifocal/total thyroidectomy vs RAS/blood/invasion/hemithyroidectomy","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-2s-ptc","p1-ptc-2","p1-fc-mets","p1-mm-fc","p1-match-ca","p1-adenoma-vs-ca","p1-num-fc"]}},{"id":"p1-mtc-1","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A thyroid tumor is made of nests of spindled cells with no colloid, and deposits that show apple-green birefringence. Which tumor is it, and which stains would confirm it?","choices":["Medullary carcinoma, positive for calcitonin, chromogranin and CEA","Anaplastic carcinoma, negative even for its own thyroid markers","Papillary carcinoma, with psammoma bodies and pale, grooved nuclei","Follicular carcinoma, invading its capsule and nearby blood vessels"],"answer":0,"ex":"Medullary carcinoma comes from the C cells, so it makes no thyroid hormone or colloid. It looks like a neuroendocrine tumor (nested, somewhat spindled cells), produces amyloid with apple-green birefringence, and stains for neuroendocrine markers (chromogranin, synaptophysin), calcitonin and CEA.","src":"rec","why":["C-cell tumors look neuroendocrine, make amyloid and stain for calcitonin (their product), chromogranin and CEA.","Anaplastic carcinoma is high-grade and resembles no tissue; spindled nests with amyloid are medullary carcinoma.","Papillary carcinoma forms papillae with distinctive nuclei; the deposits here are amyloid, not psammoma bodies.","Follicular carcinoma keeps a follicular pattern; these cells make no colloid and lay down amyloid."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Medullary carcinoma: C cells, RET and MEN 2, amyloid, calcitonin/chromogranin/CEA, sporadic vs familial","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mtc-1","p1-mtc-2","p1-x-mtc","p1-tf-mtc","p1-ccell"]}},{"id":"p1-mtc-2","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Compared with sporadic medullary thyroid carcinoma, the familial form is more likely to be which of the following?","choices":["Multiple and bilateral with C-cell hyperplasia, at a mean age of 35","A solitary lesion in an older patient, at a mean age of about 45 years","Driven by a BRAF mutation and spread mainly through the lymphatics","Arising from follicular cells in a region where goiter is endemic"],"answer":0,"ex":"Medullary carcinoma is sporadic in about 80 percent: a solitary lesion, mean age 45. The familial form strikes younger patients (mean age 35), is often multiple and bilateral, arises with C-cell hyperplasia, and is part of MEN 2A and MEN 2B through RET.","src":"slides","why":["The familial form strikes younger patients, is often multiple and bilateral, and arises with C-cell hyperplasia.","That describes the sporadic form, about 80 percent of cases.","BRAF and lymphatic spread describe papillary carcinoma; familial medullary carcinoma carries a germline RET mutation.","Areas of endemic goiter go with anaplastic carcinoma; medullary carcinoma comes from C cells."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Medullary carcinoma: C cells, RET and MEN 2, amyloid, calcitonin/chromogranin/CEA, sporadic vs familial","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mtc-1","p1-mtc-2","p1-x-mtc","p1-tf-mtc","p1-ccell"]}},{"id":"p1-tf-mtc","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Most medullary thyroid carcinomas are sporadic rather than familial.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"About 80 percent of medullary carcinomas are sporadic (solitary, mean age 45). The familial ones (mean age 35) are often multiple and bilateral and belong to MEN 2A or MEN 2B through a RET mutation.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["About 80 percent are sporadic.","The slide gives sporadic cases as 80 percent; familial cases are the minority."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Medullary carcinoma: C cells, RET and MEN 2, amyloid, calcitonin/chromogranin/CEA, sporadic vs familial","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mtc-1","p1-mtc-2","p1-x-mtc","p1-tf-mtc","p1-ccell"]}},{"id":"p1-x-mtc","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma is associated with each of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Origin from the follicular cells that make T3 and T4","A RET mutation and MEN 2A or MEN 2B","Amyloid deposits within the tumor","Staining for calcitonin, chromogranin and CEA"],"answer":0,"ex":"Medullary carcinoma is the C-cell (parafollicular) tumor: RET-driven, part of MEN 2A and 2B when familial, with amyloid, and positive for calcitonin, chromogranin and CEA.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Medullary carcinoma comes from the parafollicular C cells, which make calcitonin.","RET mutations and the MEN 2 syndromes go with familial medullary carcinoma.","Amyloid production is a hint to medullary carcinoma.","These are the slide's markers: calcitonin (its product), chromogranin (neuroendocrine) and CEA."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Medullary carcinoma: C cells, RET and MEN 2, amyloid, calcitonin/chromogranin/CEA, sporadic vs familial","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-mtc-1","p1-mtc-2","p1-x-mtc","p1-tf-mtc","p1-ccell"]}},{"id":"p1-ana-1","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"A 74-year-old woman from a region where goiter is endemic has a neck mass that has grown over two months into the thyroid cartilage and skin. The biopsy shows high-grade cells with a high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio that resemble no particular tissue. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":["Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma","Widely invasive follicular carcinoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Papillary thyroid carcinoma"],"answer":0,"ex":"Anaplastic carcinoma: about 10 percent of thyroid cancers, ages 60 to 80, often in areas of endemic goiter, a rapidly fatal neck mass that invades cartilage, skin and muscle. It has lost its differentiation and may even lose its thyroid markers.","src":"slides","why":["Older age, endemic goiter, a mass growing over weeks to months and undifferentiated cells are anaplastic carcinoma.","It can invade the thyroid cartilage, but it keeps its follicular differentiation and has a mean age of about 50.","Medullary carcinoma forms neuroendocrine nests with amyloid and stains for calcitonin.","Papillary carcinoma is slow growing, in younger patients, with recognizable papillae and nuclei."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Thyroid development, follicular adenoma and anaplastic carcinoma","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-lingual","p1-tf-adenoma","p1-ana-1","p1-ana-2","p1-tf-anaplastic"]}},{"id":"p1-ana-2","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does Dr. Dababneh treat a new biopsy diagnosis of anaplastic thyroid carcinoma as an emergency?","choices":["Without a targetable mutation such as BRAF it is rapidly fatal, so molecular testing must start at once","It is often multifocal, so the opposite lobe has to be removed before the tumor can seed it","It arises from the C cells and may signal an inherited RET syndrome that the family needs to know about","It is well differentiated, so a hemithyroidectomy done right away will cure it before it spreads"],"answer":0,"ex":"Most patients with anaplastic carcinoma die quickly unless the tumor carries a mutation a targeted drug can hit, such as BRAF. So the pathologist tells the clinicians at once and sends the tissue straight for molecular testing.","src":"rec","why":["Most patients die unless the tumor has a mutation a drug can target, so he calls the clinicians and sends it for molecular testing right away.","Multifocality is papillary carcinoma's issue, handled by a total thyroidectomy, not an emergency.","That is medullary carcinoma; anaplastic carcinoma is undifferentiated.","It is the opposite, undifferentiated; a hemithyroidectomy is the operation for follicular carcinoma."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Thyroid development, follicular adenoma and anaplastic carcinoma","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-lingual","p1-tf-adenoma","p1-ana-1","p1-ana-2","p1-tf-anaplastic"]}},{"id":"p1-tf-anaplastic","lec":"path1","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Anaplastic thyroid carcinoma typically arises in patients under 40.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Anaplastic carcinoma is a disease of older patients, 60 to 80 years old; under 40 is the typical age for papillary carcinoma.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Under 40 is papillary carcinoma's age group; anaplastic carcinoma arises at 60 to 80.","Anaplastic carcinoma typically arises at 60 to 80 years of age."],"likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Thyroid development, follicular adenoma and anaplastic carcinoma","basis":"slides","ids":["p1-lingual","p1-tf-adenoma","p1-ana-1","p1-ana-2","p1-tf-anaplastic"]}},{"id":"p1-match-ca","lec":"path1","type":"match","stem":"Match each thyroid carcinoma to the feature that best identifies it.","pairs":[["Papillary carcinoma","BRAF mutation; spreads through lymphatics"],["Follicular carcinoma","RAS mutation; spreads through blood vessels"],["Medullary carcinoma","RET mutation; arises from C cells"],["Anaplastic carcinoma","Undifferentiated; a rapidly fatal neck mass"]],"ex":"The lecture's sorting rule: BRAF-like papillary cancers spread by lymphatics, RAS-like follicular cancers by blood vessels, medullary carcinoma is the RET-driven C-cell tumor, and anaplastic carcinoma has lost its differentiation and kills quickly.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"path1","t":"Papillary vs follicular carcinoma: BRAF/lymphatics/multifocal/total thyroidectomy vs RAS/blood/invasion/hemithyroidectomy","basis":"recording","ids":["p1-2s-ptc","p1-ptc-2","p1-fc-mets","p1-mm-fc","p1-match-ca","p1-adenoma-vs-ca","p1-num-fc"]}},{"id":"p2-pth-kidney","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Low serum calcium triggers PTH release. Which set of actions does PTH have on the kidney?","choices":["More calcium reabsorbed, less phosphate reabsorbed, more 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D made","More calcium reabsorbed, more phosphate reabsorbed, less 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D made","Less calcium reabsorbed, less phosphate reabsorbed, more 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D made","More sodium reabsorbed, more potassium excreted, more 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D made"],"answer":0,"ex":"PTH raises serum calcium through the kidney (keep calcium, dump phosphate, make 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D) and the bone (more turnover). The rising calcium then switches PTH off: negative feedback.","src":"slides","why":["PTH holds on to calcium, dumps phosphate and turns on active vitamin D, which all push serum calcium up.","PTH makes the kidney dump phosphate, not keep it, and it increases active vitamin D rather than lowering it.","Losing calcium in the urine would lower serum calcium, the opposite of what PTH is released to do.","Keeping sodium and dumping potassium is aldosterone's action on the kidney, not PTH's."]},{"id":"p2-pth-fragments","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement about the fragments of PTH in the blood is accurate?","choices":["N-terminal fragments are biologically active, last minutes and are broken down by the liver and kidney","C-terminal fragments are biologically active, last minutes and are broken down by the liver and kidney","N-terminal fragments are biologically inactive, last several hours and are cleared by glomerular filtration","C-terminal fragments are biologically active, last several hours and are cleared by glomerular filtration"],"answer":0,"ex":"Intact PTH and its N-terminal fragments do the work and last only minutes; C-terminal fragments are inactive, last hours and are cleared by glomerular filtration. That short half-life is why PTH can be measured during surgery to confirm the gland is out.","src":"rec","why":["The active end of PTH is short-lived and is metabolized by the liver and kidney.","The C-terminal fragments are the inactive ones; the N-terminal end is the part that acts.","That describes the C-terminal fragments: inactive, long-lived and filtered by the kidney.","C-terminal fragments do last hours and are filtered, but they are biologically inactive."]},{"id":"p2-fat-age","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Dr. Dababneh's rule of thumb: subtract a person's age from 100 to get the expected percent of cells in a normal parathyroid, the rest being fat. A gland from a 65-year-old is about 80 percent cells with very little fat. How is it best read?","choices":["Hypercellular for the patient's age, which fits a hyperfunctioning gland","Normal for the patient's age, because parathyroid fat shrinks as people age","Hypocellular for the patient's age, which fits hypoparathyroidism","Normal at any age, since the ratio of fat to cells stays the same"],"answer":0,"ex":"A young adult's parathyroid is mostly cells; a 70-year-old's is about 70 percent fat and 30 percent cells. A middle-aged or older gland at 70 to 80 percent cells is hypercellular and may be hyperfunctioning.","src":"rec","why":["At 65 the gland should be about 35 percent cells; 80 percent is far too cellular, the picture of an adenoma or hyperplasia.","Parathyroid fat increases with age; an older gland should be mostly fat, not mostly cells.","Hypocellular would mean fewer cells than expected; 80 percent is many more, not fewer.","The ratio changes with age: a young gland is mostly cells, a 70-year-old's is about 70 percent fat."]},{"id":"p2-phpt-def","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best defines primary hyperparathyroidism?","choices":["PTH secretion that is inappropriately high for the serum calcium, with no known stimulus driving it","PTH secretion raised in response to chronically low serum calcium, most often from renal failure","Normal or high PTH secretion with target organs that fail to respond, so serum calcium stays low","PTH secretion too low for the serum calcium, most often after the glands are removed with the thyroid"],"answer":0,"ex":"Primary means the parathyroid itself is the problem (adenoma, hyperplasia or carcinoma), so PTH and calcium are both high. Secondary means something else, such as renal failure or a lack of calcium or vitamin D, is driving the glands.","src":"slides","why":["That is the slide's definition: the gland itself is overactive.","That is secondary hyperparathyroidism: the glands are responding to a disease that lowers calcium.","That is pseudohypoparathyroidism, end-organ resistance to PTH.","That is hypoparathyroidism, which causes low calcium."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism: renal failure, all glands, fix the cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-shpt-dialysis","p2-shpt-fix","p2-2s-2","p2-phpt-def"]}},{"id":"p2-phpt-cause","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism, and about how often is it the cause?","choices":["A solitary parathyroid adenoma, in about 80 to 90 percent of cases","Chief cell hyperplasia of all four glands, in about 80 to 90 percent of cases","A solitary parathyroid adenoma, in about 10 to 15 percent of cases","Parathyroid carcinoma, in about 10 to 15 percent of cases"],"answer":0,"ex":"Adenoma 80 to 90 percent, chief cell hyperplasia 10 to 15 percent, carcinoma rare. All three raise PTH and calcium.","src":"rec","why":["Adenoma is the most common cause by far, 80 to 90 percent.","Hyperplasia is the second cause, about 10 to 15 percent of cases.","Adenoma is the right lesion but the wrong share; 10 to 15 percent is hyperplasia's share.","Carcinoma is very rare; Dr. Dababneh said he had probably seen one."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: adenoma versus hyperplasia versus carcinoma, and how they are told apart","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-phpt-cause","p2-adhyp-dx","p2-pca-features","p2-num-1","p2-tf-3","p2-case1-a","p2-2s-3","p2-surg-classic"]}},{"id":"p2-triad","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"The classic triad of primary hyperparathyroidism, which Dr. Dababneh remembered as \"stones, bones and groans,\" is best listed as which?","choices":["Renal calculi, osteitis fibrosa cystica and neuromuscular abnormalities","Renal calculi, basal ganglia calcification and tetany with convulsions","Cataracts, dental root hypoplasia and laryngospasm","Short metacarpals and metatarsals, short stature and developmental anomalies"],"answer":0,"ex":"Too much PTH means too much calcium: it is pulled out of bone (osteitis fibrosa cystica), forms kidney stones and disturbs nerve and muscle function. Most patients today have none of these and are found on routine labs.","src":"rec","why":["Kidney stones from the extra calcium, bone broken down for its calcium, and nerve and muscle complaints.","Basal ganglia calcification, tetany and convulsions belong to hypoparathyroidism, the low-calcium disease.","These are findings of hypoparathyroidism and low calcium.","These are the features of pseudohypoparathyroidism."]},{"id":"p2-num-1","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about parathyroid adenoma are true?\n1. It is a benign neoplasm of chief or oxyphil cells.\n2. It is about three times more common in women than in men.\n3. The upper parathyroid glands are involved more often than the lower ones.\n4. It usually involves all four glands at once.\n5. It usually weighs between 0.3 and 1.0 g.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2, 3 and 5","1, 2 and 5","2, 4 and 5"],"answer":2,"ex":"Parathyroid adenoma: a solitary benign neoplasm of chief or oxyphil cells, 80 to 90 percent of primary hyperparathyroidism, three times more common in women, usually in the 4th to 5th decade, more often in a lower gland, 0.3 to 1.0 g.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 5, which is true: the slide gives a weight range of 0.3 to 1.0 g.","It includes 3, which is false: the lower glands are the ones involved more often.","Statements 1, 2 and 5 are true. Statement 3 is false: the lower glands are the ones involved more often. Statement 4 is false: an adenoma is a solitary lesion; four enlarged glands points to hyperplasia.","It includes 4, which is false: an adenoma is a solitary lesion; four enlarged glands points to hyperplasia. It leaves out 1, which is true: it is a benign neoplasm of either parathyroid cell type."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: adenoma versus hyperplasia versus carcinoma, and how they are told apart","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-phpt-cause","p2-adhyp-dx","p2-pca-features","p2-num-1","p2-tf-3","p2-case1-a","p2-2s-3","p2-surg-classic"]}},{"id":"p2-adhyp-dx","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient has high calcium and high PTH. How is a parathyroid adenoma told apart from primary chief cell hyperplasia?","choices":["By anatomy: one enlarged gland means adenoma, several enlarged glands mean hyperplasia","By labs: hyperplasia raises PTH and calcium, while an adenoma raises PTH alone","By labs: an adenoma pushes calcium over 13, while hyperplasia keeps it under 13","By histology alone: an adenoma is full of fat, while hyperplasia has little fat"],"answer":0,"ex":"Adenoma and hyperplasia both cause high PTH and hypercalcemia, so the diagnosis is anatomic: a single enlarged gland versus several. Adenomas in all four glands are very rare, so four enlarged glands is most likely hyperplasia.","src":"rec","why":["Labs can't separate them, so the number of enlarged glands decides; an adenoma is a solitary lesion.","Both raise PTH and calcium; the slide says lab testing can't tell them apart.","Calcium over 13 is the clue for parathyroid carcinoma, not for adenoma.","Both have more cells and less fat; the adenoma is the cellular, fat-poor area beside normal gland."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: adenoma versus hyperplasia versus carcinoma, and how they are told apart","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-phpt-cause","p2-adhyp-dx","p2-pca-features","p2-num-1","p2-tf-3","p2-case1-a","p2-2s-3","p2-surg-classic"]}},{"id":"p2-pca-features","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which finding most strongly suggests parathyroid carcinoma rather than an adenoma?","choices":["Calcium over 13 with an ill-defined neck mass stuck to the thyroid and esophagus","A single well-defined lower gland weighing 0.6 g, rimmed by normal fatty parathyroid","All four glands enlarged, with a combined weight of about 2 g","Mildly high calcium found on routine labs in a patient who feels well"],"answer":0,"ex":"Parathyroid carcinoma is very rare: calcium tends to run over 13, and it is an ill-defined, invasive mass (mean 3 cm, 6.7 g) adherent to soft tissue, thyroid or the tissue around the esophagus, with pleomorphic nuclei and tumor necrosis.","src":"rec","why":["Very high, symptomatic hypercalcemia and a mass adherent to nearby tissue are the carcinoma picture.","That is a typical adenoma: solitary, in a lower gland, 0.3 to 1.0 g.","Four enlarged glands weighing 1 to 3 g together is primary chief cell hyperplasia.","That is how most primary hyperparathyroidism is found now; carcinoma is very symptomatic."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: adenoma versus hyperplasia versus carcinoma, and how they are told apart","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-phpt-cause","p2-adhyp-dx","p2-pca-features","p2-num-1","p2-tf-3","p2-case1-a","p2-2s-3","p2-surg-classic"]}},{"id":"p2-2s-3","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Parathyroid carcinoma usually forms a well-circumscribed mass that separates easily from the thyroid.\n2. Tumor necrosis in a parathyroid tumor points toward a benign process.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Parathyroid carcinoma is an ill-defined mass adherent to soft tissue, the thyroid or the tissue around the esophagus, with larger pleomorphic nuclei and necrosis. Dr. Dababneh: when cells die out inside a neoplasm, it is usually a malignant process.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: carcinoma is ill-defined and adherent to the soft tissue, thyroid or tissue around the esophagus. Statement 2 is false, not true: necrosis inside a neoplasm usually signals a malignant process, as in parathyroid carcinoma.","Statement 1 is false: carcinoma is ill-defined and adherent to the soft tissue, thyroid or tissue around the esophagus. Statement 2 is false: necrosis inside a neoplasm usually signals a malignant process, as in parathyroid carcinoma.","Statement 1 is false, not true: carcinoma is ill-defined and adherent to the soft tissue, thyroid or tissue around the esophagus.","Statement 2 is false, not true: necrosis inside a neoplasm usually signals a malignant process, as in parathyroid carcinoma."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: adenoma versus hyperplasia versus carcinoma, and how they are told apart","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-phpt-cause","p2-adhyp-dx","p2-pca-features","p2-num-1","p2-tf-3","p2-case1-a","p2-2s-3","p2-surg-classic"]}},{"id":"p2-2s-1","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Parathyroid chief cells look clear to bluish, while oxyphil cells look pink.\n2. The parathyroid glands sit on the front surface of the thyroid.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"The parathyroid has two cell types, clear to bluish chief cells and pink oxyphil cells, growing in nests between fat with salt and pepper chromatin. The four glands sit behind the thyroid; Dr. Dababneh repeated \"behind.\"","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: the four glands sit behind the thyroid, not in front of it.","Statement 1 is true, not false: on his slides the chief cells were the clear to bluish cells and the oxyphils the pink ones.","Statement 1 is true: on his slides the chief cells were the clear to bluish cells and the oxyphils the pink ones. Statement 2 is false: the four glands sit behind the thyroid, not in front of it.","Statement 1 is true, not false: on his slides the chief cells were the clear to bluish cells and the oxyphils the pink ones. Statement 2 is false, not true: the four glands sit behind the thyroid, not in front of it."]},{"id":"p2-tf-1","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In a healthy 70-year-old, about 70 percent of the parathyroid is fat.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Dababneh's rule of thumb: the expected percent of cells is about 100 minus the age, so at 70 the gland is about 30 percent cells and 70 percent fat. A gland far more cellular than that is hypercellular.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["At 70, about 70 percent fat and 30 percent cells is the expected normal gland.","The statement is true: parathyroid fat rises with age, to about 70 percent at age 70."]},{"id":"p2-tf-2","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Most patients with primary hyperparathyroidism today come to attention because of kidney stones or bone disease.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Stones, bones and groans is the classic picture, but most patients now have no symptoms: the high calcium turns up on a routine blood chemistry panel.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The classic triad is now the exception; most patients are found on routine labs.","Most patients are asymptomatic and found by a high calcium on routine chemistry."]},{"id":"p2-tf-3","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In primary chief cell hyperplasia, the enlarged parathyroid glands contain more stromal fat than normal.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Hyperplasia means increased parenchymal cell mass with decreased stromal fat: sheets or nodules of chief cells, oncocytic cells or both, with little fat between them, in all four glands (1 to 3 g together).","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The fat goes down, not up: the cells crowd out the stromal fat.","Hyperplastic glands have more cells and less stromal fat, not more fat."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: adenoma versus hyperplasia versus carcinoma, and how they are told apart","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-phpt-cause","p2-adhyp-dx","p2-pca-features","p2-num-1","p2-tf-3","p2-case1-a","p2-2s-3","p2-surg-classic"]}},{"id":"p2-surg-classic","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"In a classic bilateral neck exploration, the surgeon finds all four parathyroid glands enlarged. What is the usual next step?","choices":["Remove three and a half glands and leave half a gland to keep calcium in balance","Remove the largest gland, send it to pathology and biopsy the other three","Remove all four glands, since every enlarged gland is a neoplasm","Remove only the two lower glands, since the lower glands are the ones involved most often"],"answer":0,"ex":"One enlarged gland: presumed adenoma, remove it and biopsy a grossly normal gland. Several enlarged: presumed hyperplasia, remove three and a half. Removed tissue can even be saved and re-implanted elsewhere in the body, where it still works.","src":"rec","why":["Several enlarged glands means presumed hyperplasia, so three and a half come out and half stays.","That is the plan for a single enlarged gland, a presumed adenoma: remove it and biopsy a normal one.","Hyperplasia is not necessarily neoplastic, and removing all four would leave no PTH at all.","Lower glands are favored by adenoma, a single-gland disease; four enlarged glands is hyperplasia."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: adenoma versus hyperplasia versus carcinoma, and how they are told apart","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-phpt-cause","p2-adhyp-dx","p2-pca-features","p2-num-1","p2-tf-3","p2-case1-a","p2-2s-3","p2-surg-classic"]}},{"id":"p2-seq-mips","lec":"path2","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the steps of minimally invasive parathyroid surgery in order.","steps":["Localize the abnormal gland before surgery with a sestamibi scan","Measure a preoperative PTH level","Remove the abnormal gland through a small, localized incision","Measure PTH during the operation and look for a drop of more than 50 percent"],"ex":"It works because most primary hyperparathyroidism is a single adenoma, so it can be found ahead of time, and because active PTH lasts only minutes, so the drop shows within 15 to 20 minutes.","src":"slides"},{"id":"p2-case1-a","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most likely cause of her findings?","choices":["A single parathyroid adenoma","Hyperplasia of all four parathyroid glands","Parathyroid carcinoma","Secondary hyperparathyroidism from renal failure"],"answer":0,"ex":"High calcium, low phosphate and high PTH: primary hyperparathyroidism. A well patient found on routine chemistry is now the usual presentation, typically a woman near the mean age of 58. Labs can't separate adenoma from hyperplasia, but the odds favor adenoma.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","58-year-old woman, new patient at the dental clinic; feels well"],["History","High calcium flagged on routine bloodwork by her physician; no kidney stones"],["Calcium","11.4 mg/dL (8.5-10.5)"],["Phosphate","2.1 mg/dL (2.5-4.5)"],["PTH","128 pg/mL (10-65)"],["Creatinine","0.8 mg/dL (normal)"]],"pcase":"path2-case1","why":["High calcium with a PTH that is high instead of suppressed is primary disease, and 80 to 90 percent of it is a solitary adenoma.","Hyperplasia gives the same labs but causes only 10 to 15 percent of primary cases.","Carcinoma is rare, very symptomatic and pushes calcium over 13; she feels well at 11.4.","Her kidney function is normal and her calcium is high; secondary disease is driven by low calcium."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Primary hyperparathyroidism: adenoma versus hyperplasia versus carcinoma, and how they are told apart","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-phpt-cause","p2-adhyp-dx","p2-pca-features","p2-num-1","p2-tf-3","p2-case1-a","p2-2s-3","p2-surg-classic"]}},{"id":"p2-case1-b","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Before surgery she has a sestamibi scan. How is the scan best read?","choices":["One hyperfunctioning gland, most likely an adenoma, located before surgery","Hyperplasia of all four glands, since uptake was seen everywhere at first","Parathyroid carcinoma, since the tracer stayed in the gland for 2 hours","A failed scan, since the tracer should have cleared from every gland by 2 hours"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sestamibi is the preoperative localization study: at 15 minutes everything lights up; at 2 hours only the hyperfunctioning tissue still holds the tracer. Finding the single adenoma ahead of time is what allows a small, focused incision.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","58-year-old woman with primary hyperparathyroidism"],["Calcium","11.4 mg/dL (8.5-10.5)"],["PTH","128 pg/mL (10-65)"],["Sestamibi, 15 minutes","Uptake throughout the thyroid and parathyroid region"],["Sestamibi, 2 hours","One bright spot remains, below the left lobe of the thyroid"]],"pcase":"path2-case1","why":["Only hyperfunctioning tissue holds the tracer at 2 hours, so one lit spot is a single overactive gland.","Uptake everywhere is the normal early image at 15 minutes; only one spot persisted at 2 hours.","Holding tracer marks a hyperfunctioning gland, not invasion; carcinoma is judged by calcium, size and invasion.","Tracer clearing from normal tissue while one spot stays lit is exactly the expected positive result."]},{"id":"p2-case1-c","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"The surgeon removes the left lower gland through a small incision and checks PTH. How should the result be read?","choices":["The operation worked: PTH fell by more than half after the gland came out","The operation failed: PTH is still above the upper limit of normal","The result can't be read yet: PTH takes several hours to fall after removal","The operation failed: the surgeon must have removed fat, so the other three glands must come out"],"answer":0,"ex":"Effective surgery means PTH falls more than 50 percent after the enlarged gland is removed, with results in 15 to 20 minutes. This works only because intact PTH has a half-life of minutes.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","58-year-old woman, single adenoma localized by sestamibi"],["Surgery","Focused removal of the left lower parathyroid gland"],["PTH just before removal","150 pg/mL (10-65)"],["PTH 15 minutes after removal","70 pg/mL"]],"pcase":"path2-case1","why":["A drop of more than 50 percent within 15 to 20 minutes confirms the hyperfunctioning gland is out.","The test asks for a fall of more than 50 percent, not a normal value; 150 to 70 is about a 53 percent drop.","Active PTH lasts only minutes; the hours-long half-life belongs to the inactive C-terminal fragments.","If PTH had not dropped, the surgeon may have taken fat; here it did drop by more than half."]},{"id":"p2-shpt-dialysis","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"A 61-year-old man on dialysis for chronic renal failure has calcium 8.1 mg/dL (8.5-10.5) and PTH 420 pg/mL (10-65). At surgery, all four parathyroids are enlarged. Which best explains his glands?","choices":["Secondary hyperparathyroidism: chronic low calcium from renal failure drives all four glands to grow","Primary hyperparathyroidism from chief cell hyperplasia, which enlarges all four glands without a stimulus","Pseudohypoparathyroidism: his kidneys resist PTH, so the glands make more and more of it","Parathyroid carcinoma, since PTH is more than six times the upper limit"],"answer":0,"ex":"Renal failure is the most common cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism: chronic hypocalcemia keeps the glands stimulated until they are all hyperplastic. Its features resemble primary disease but are less severe; surgery, when needed, is a three-and-a-half gland parathyroidectomy.","src":"hub","why":["Renal failure is the most common cause, and the glands enlarge because low calcium keeps stimulating them.","Primary disease raises calcium; his is low, and his renal failure is a known stimulus.","Pseudohypoparathyroidism is diagnosed only after renal failure is ruled out, and he has renal failure.","Carcinoma brings very high calcium, over 13, not low calcium."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism: renal failure, all glands, fix the cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-shpt-dialysis","p2-shpt-fix","p2-2s-2","p2-phpt-def"]}},{"id":"p2-shpt-fix","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient's secondary hyperparathyroidism is traced to vitamin D deficiency. According to Dr. Dababneh, what matters most?","choices":["Treating the vitamin D deficiency, after which calcium and PTH return to normal on their own","Finding the one enlarged gland on a sestamibi scan and removing it through a small incision","Giving glucocorticoids so that negative feedback switches the overactive parathyroids off","Removing all four parathyroid glands right away so that no more PTH can be made"],"answer":0,"ex":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism is a response to disease (renal failure, calcium or vitamin D deficiency). Surgery, if needed, is a three-and-a-half gland parathyroidectomy, but the most important step is fixing the cause.","src":"rec","why":["He stressed solving the primary issue: once it is fixed, calcium and the parathyroids return to normal.","A single hyperfunctioning gland is the adenoma of primary disease; secondary disease involves all the glands.","Glucocorticoid feedback is the treatment for congenital adrenal hyperplasia, working through ACTH.","Even when surgery is done, half a gland is left behind; and the first job is the cause."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism: renal failure, all glands, fix the cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-shpt-dialysis","p2-shpt-fix","p2-2s-2","p2-phpt-def"]}},{"id":"p2-2s-2","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Secondary hyperparathyroidism usually makes all of the parathyroid glands hyperplastic.\n2. When secondary hyperparathyroidism is treated surgically, three and a half glands are removed.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Chronic low calcium stimulates every gland, so secondary disease is hyperplasia of all of them. Surgery removes three and a half glands and leaves half to keep calcium in balance, but fixing the cause comes first.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: chronic low calcium stimulates every gland, so they all become hyperplastic. Statement 2 is true: the slide's operation is a three-and-a-half gland parathyroidectomy, leaving half a gland behind.","Statement 1 is true, not false: chronic low calcium stimulates every gland, so they all become hyperplastic. Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide's operation is a three-and-a-half gland parathyroidectomy, leaving half a gland behind.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide's operation is a three-and-a-half gland parathyroidectomy, leaving half a gland behind.","Statement 1 is true, not false: chronic low calcium stimulates every gland, so they all become hyperplastic."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism: renal failure, all glands, fix the cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-shpt-dialysis","p2-shpt-fix","p2-2s-2","p2-phpt-def"]}},{"id":"p2-mm-hypo","lec":"path2","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these are causes of hypoparathyroidism given in the lecture? Select all that apply.","choices":["Removal or damage of the glands during a thyroidectomy","Thymic aplasia in DiGeorge syndrome","Autoimmune hereditary polyglandular deficiency syndrome","Chronic renal failure","A solitary parathyroid adenoma"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Hypoparathyroidism has three listed causes: surgical (thyroidectomy), congenital absence (thymic aplasia, DiGeorge syndrome) and autoimmune (hereditary polyglandular deficiency syndrome). Renal failure and adenoma both raise PTH.","src":"slides","why":["Surgery is the classic cause, since the parathyroids sit right behind the thyroid.","The glands are congenitally absent along with the thymus.","Autoimmune attack on the glands is a listed cause, much as Hashimoto disease attacks the thyroid.","Renal failure lowers calcium and causes secondary hyperparathyroidism: more PTH, not less.","An adenoma overproduces PTH; it is the main cause of primary hyperparathyroidism."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Hypoparathyroidism: causes, low calcium with high phosphate, Chvostek and Trousseau, dental root hypoplasia","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case2-a","p2-case2-b","p2-mm-hypo","p2-hypo-except","p2-tf-4","p2-num-2"]}},{"id":"p2-hypo-except","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"All of the following are findings of hypoparathyroidism EXCEPT:","choices":["Recurrent kidney stones","Cataracts and calcification of the basal ganglia","Dental root hypoplasia","Spotty alopecia and fragile nails"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hypoparathyroidism means low calcium: tetany, convulsions, laryngospasm, paresthesias, Chvostek and Trousseau signs, cataracts, dental root hypoplasia, spotty alopecia, nail fragility and basal ganglia calcification. Stones are a high-calcium finding.","src":"rec","fmt":"except","why":["Kidney stones come from too much calcium, so they belong to hyperparathyroidism.","Both are listed findings of hypoparathyroidism.","Low calcium stunts the roots; Dr. Dababneh flagged this one as important.","Both are listed among the features of hypoparathyroidism."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Hypoparathyroidism: causes, low calcium with high phosphate, Chvostek and Trousseau, dental root hypoplasia","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case2-a","p2-case2-b","p2-mm-hypo","p2-hypo-except","p2-tf-4","p2-num-2"]}},{"id":"p2-tf-4","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Dental root hypoplasia is a finding of hypoparathyroidism.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Without enough calcium the roots form poorly; Dr. Dababneh singled it out (\"important, no calcium\"), along with cataracts, spotty alopecia, fragile nails and basal ganglia calcification.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Low calcium leaves the roots underformed, and he called this finding important.","It is on the lecture's list of hypoparathyroid findings, and he flagged it as important."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Hypoparathyroidism: causes, low calcium with high phosphate, Chvostek and Trousseau, dental root hypoplasia","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case2-a","p2-case2-b","p2-mm-hypo","p2-hypo-except","p2-tf-4","p2-num-2"]}},{"id":"p2-num-2","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about low-calcium disorders of the parathyroid are true?\n1. Hypoparathyroidism causes low calcium with high phosphate.\n2. Chvostek sign is a twitch of the facial muscles when the cheek is tapped.\n3. Pseudohypoparathyroidism is caused by underproduction of PTH.\n4. Hypocalcemia can cause laryngospasm and convulsions.","choices":["1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Without PTH the kidney dumps calcium and keeps phosphate, so calcium is low and phosphate high; low calcium brings tetany, convulsions, laryngospasm, paresthesias and the Chvostek and Trousseau signs. In pseudohypoparathyroidism PTH is high and the target organs resist it.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: in pseudohypoparathyroidism PTH is made normally and is in fact high; the target organs resist it.","It includes 3, which is false: in pseudohypoparathyroidism PTH is made normally and is in fact high; the target organs resist it. It leaves out 2, which is true: tapping the cheek makes the facial muscles twitch when calcium is low.","It includes 3, which is false: in pseudohypoparathyroidism PTH is made normally and is in fact high; the target organs resist it. It leaves out 1, which is true: without PTH the kidney loses calcium and holds on to phosphate.","It leaves out 1, which is true: without PTH the kidney loses calcium and holds on to phosphate."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Hypoparathyroidism: causes, low calcium with high phosphate, Chvostek and Trousseau, dental root hypoplasia","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case2-a","p2-case2-b","p2-mm-hypo","p2-hypo-except","p2-tf-4","p2-num-2"]}},{"id":"p2-case2-a","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"How is the twitch she shows best named and explained?","choices":["Chvostek sign, from low calcium","Trousseau sign, from low calcium","Chvostek sign, from high calcium","Trousseau sign, from high calcium"],"answer":0,"ex":"Chvostek: tap the cheek, the face twitches. Trousseau: compress the arm, the hand spasms. Both are signs of the muscle irritability of low calcium, and both turn up in hypoparathyroidism.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","46-year-old woman at an emergency dental visit for a cracked filling"],["History","Total thyroidectomy for thyroid cancer 3 days ago"],["Symptoms","Tingling around the lips and in the fingertips; muscle cramps"],["Exam","Tapping the cheek in front of the ear makes the corner of her mouth twitch"]],"pcase":"path2-case2","why":["Tapping over the cheek makes the facial muscles twitch when calcium is low.","Trousseau sign is the hand spasm when the arm is compressed with a cuff or tourniquet.","The name fits, but the twitch comes from low calcium; high calcium gives stones, bones and groans.","Both parts miss: Trousseau is the hand spasm, and both signs come from low calcium."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Hypoparathyroidism: causes, low calcium with high phosphate, Chvostek and Trousseau, dental root hypoplasia","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case2-a","p2-case2-b","p2-mm-hypo","p2-hypo-except","p2-tf-4","p2-num-2"]}},{"id":"p2-case2-b","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Her surgeon's labs from yesterday are now available. Which best explains them?","choices":["Her parathyroids were removed or injured during the thyroidectomy","Pseudohypoparathyroidism: her kidneys resist PTH, so calcium falls","Secondary hyperparathyroidism: low calcium is driving her glands to overwork","DiGeorge syndrome: her parathyroids and thymus never formed before birth"],"answer":0,"ex":"Low PTH means the kidney dumps calcium and keeps phosphate: low calcium, high phosphate. Thyroidectomy is the classic cause because the glands sit right behind the thyroid; the other low-calcium sign is Trousseau's (cuff on the arm, spasm of the hand).","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","46-year-old woman, 3 days after total thyroidectomy"],["Exam","Positive Chvostek sign; tingling around the lips"],["Calcium","6.9 mg/dL (8.5-10.5)"],["Phosphate","5.6 mg/dL (2.5-4.5)"],["PTH","6 pg/mL (10-65)"]],"pcase":"path2-case2","why":["The glands sit behind the thyroid, so surgery is the classic cause of low PTH and low calcium.","In pseudohypoparathyroidism PTH is high; hers is below normal.","Secondary disease has high PTH; hers is below normal.","DiGeorge is congenital and would have shown up long ago; her low calcium began after surgery."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Hypoparathyroidism: causes, low calcium with high phosphate, Chvostek and Trousseau, dental root hypoplasia","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case2-a","p2-case2-b","p2-mm-hypo","p2-hypo-except","p2-tf-4","p2-num-2"]}},{"id":"p2-pseudo-mech","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the underlying problem in pseudohypoparathyroidism?","choices":["Target organs resist PTH, even though the glands make plenty of it","The parathyroid glands never formed, as in DiGeorge syndrome","The glands were removed or damaged during a thyroidectomy","Renal failure lowers calcium and drives all four glands to enlarge"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism looks like hypoparathyroidism (low calcium, high phosphate), but PTH is high because the end organs don't respond. Patients also have short stature, short metacarpals and metatarsals, developmental anomalies and intellectual disability.","src":"rec","why":["Pseudo means false: the glands work, but the end organs don't respond.","Congenital absence of the glands is true hypoparathyroidism, with low PTH.","Surgical loss is the classic cause of true hypoparathyroidism, with low PTH.","That is secondary hyperparathyroidism; renal failure must be ruled out before calling it pseudo."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism: end-organ resistance with high PTH","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pseudo-mech","p2-case3-a","p2-case3-b","p2-tf-5","p2-match-labs"]}},{"id":"p2-tf-5","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In pseudohypoparathyroidism, the PTH level is low.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Pseudo means false: calcium is low and phosphate high, as in hypoparathyroidism, but PTH is high because the target organs don't respond to it.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["PTH is high: the glands work fine and keep pushing against resistant target organs.","The glands make plenty of PTH; it is the target organs that resist it."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism: end-organ resistance with high PTH","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pseudo-mech","p2-case3-a","p2-case3-b","p2-tf-5","p2-match-labs"]}},{"id":"p2-case3-a","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pseudohypoparathyroidism","Hypoparathyroidism from DiGeorge syndrome","Secondary hyperparathyroidism from renal failure","Primary hyperparathyroidism from an adenoma"],"answer":0,"ex":"Low calcium and high phosphate look like hypoparathyroidism, but PTH is high: the end organs resist it. Short stature, short metacarpals and developmental delay complete the picture.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","10-year-old boy brought in for a dental exam"],["History","Short for his age; learning delays; short fingers from short metacarpals"],["Calcium","7.5 mg/dL (8.8-10.8 for age)"],["Phosphate","6.9 mg/dL (3.7-5.6 for age)"],["PTH","240 pg/mL (10-65)"],["Kidney function and vitamin D","Normal"]],"pcase":"path2-case3","why":["High PTH that fails to raise calcium, with short metacarpals and short stature, is end-organ resistance to PTH.","True hypoparathyroidism has low PTH; his is high.","His kidney function is normal, so renal failure has been ruled out.","Primary disease raises calcium and lowers phosphate; his calcium is low."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism: end-organ resistance with high PTH","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pseudo-mech","p2-case3-a","p2-case3-b","p2-tf-5","p2-match-labs"]}},{"id":"p2-case3-b","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Why did his doctors check his kidney function and vitamin D before making the diagnosis?","choices":["Renal failure and vitamin D deficiency can also cause low calcium with a high PTH","Renal failure and vitamin D deficiency are the usual causes of short metacarpals and short stature","Vitamin D deficiency is the end-organ resistance that defines the disease","Normal kidneys rule out a parathyroid adenoma as the cause"],"answer":0,"ex":"Low calcium with high PTH also happens when the kidneys fail or calcium and vitamin D are lacking (secondary hyperparathyroidism). Only when renal failure, malabsorption and vitamin D deficiency are ruled out does end-organ resistance remain.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","10-year-old boy, short stature, short metacarpals, learning delays"],["Calcium","7.5 mg/dL (8.8-10.8 for age)"],["Phosphate","6.9 mg/dL (3.7-5.6 for age)"],["PTH","240 pg/mL (10-65)"],["Kidney function and vitamin D","Normal"]],"pcase":"path2-case3","why":["The slide says to rule out renal failure, malabsorption and vitamin D deficiency, which look alike on labs.","Short metacarpals and short stature are part of pseudohypoparathyroidism itself.","The resistance is to PTH in the target organs; vitamin D deficiency is a look-alike to rule out.","His low calcium already rules out an adenoma; the kidney test is for a look-alike."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism: end-organ resistance with high PTH","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pseudo-mech","p2-case3-a","p2-case3-b","p2-tf-5","p2-match-labs"]}},{"id":"p2-match-labs","lec":"path2","type":"match","stem":"Match each disorder with its lab pattern.","pairs":[["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Calcium high, PTH high"],["Hypoparathyroidism","Calcium low, PTH low"],["Pseudohypoparathyroidism","Calcium low, PTH high"],["Conn syndrome","Aldosterone high, renin low"],["Addison disease","Cortisol low, ACTH high"]],"ex":"Read each hormone with its driver. Primary hyperparathyroidism: the gland overproduces, so both are high. Hypoparathyroidism: too little PTH, so calcium falls. Pseudo: PTH high but ignored. Conn: the adrenal makes aldosterone on its own and renin shuts off. Addison: the cortex fails and ACTH climbs.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pseudohypoparathyroidism: end-organ resistance with high PTH","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pseudo-mech","p2-case3-a","p2-case3-b","p2-tf-5","p2-match-labs"]}},{"id":"p2-match-zones","lec":"path2","type":"match","stem":"Match each part of the adrenal gland with its main product.","pairs":[["Zona glomerulosa","Aldosterone (mineralocorticoid)"],["Zona fasciculata","Cortisol (glucocorticoid)"],["Zona reticularis","Androgens (sex steroids)"],["Adrenal medulla","Catecholamines"]],"ex":"From the outside in: glomerulosa (salt), fasciculata (sugar), reticularis (sex), then the medulla, a neuroendocrine tissue making catecholamines. The slide lists glucocorticoids and androgens in both inner zones, but fasciculata is mainly cortisol and reticularis mainly androgens.","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Adrenal cortex zones: salt, sugar, sex","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-match-zones","p2-zones-apply","p2-tf-10"]}},{"id":"p2-zones-apply","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Using \"salt, sugar, sex,\" a hormone-making lesion confined to the innermost zone of the adrenal cortex would most likely cause which picture?","choices":["Hirsutism and male features from excess androgens","Hypertension and low potassium from excess aldosterone","Moon face and hyperglycemia from excess cortisol","Sweating and palpitations from excess catecholamines"],"answer":0,"ex":"From the outside in: glomerulosa (mineralocorticoids, salt), fasciculata (glucocorticoids, sugar), reticularis (androgens, sex); then the medulla (catecholamines). \"It gets sweeter as you go from outside to inside.\"","src":"rec","why":["The zona reticularis, the innermost cortical zone, mainly makes androgens: sex.","Aldosterone (salt) comes from the zona glomerulosa, the outermost zone.","Cortisol (sugar) comes mainly from the zona fasciculata, the middle zone.","Catecholamines come from the medulla, which lies inside the cortex and is not part of it."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Adrenal cortex zones: salt, sugar, sex","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-match-zones","p2-zones-apply","p2-tf-10"]}},{"id":"p2-tf-10","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The zona glomerulosa is the innermost zone of the adrenal cortex.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"From the outside in: glomerulosa (salt), fasciculata (sugar), reticularis (sex), then the medulla. The glomerulosa is the outermost zone of the cortex.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["The glomerulosa is the outermost zone; the reticularis is the innermost.","Salt, sugar, sex runs from the outside in, so the glomerulosa is outermost."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Adrenal cortex zones: salt, sugar, sex","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-match-zones","p2-zones-apply","p2-tf-10"]}},{"id":"p2-cortisol-except","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is true of cortisol EXCEPT:","choices":["It increases glucose uptake by cells","Its secretion is stimulated by ACTH","It increases gluconeogenesis in the liver","It promotes fat deposition"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cortisol, released under ACTH, inhibits glucose uptake by cells, promotes fat deposition and increases hepatic gluconeogenesis, which is why its excess brings hyperglycemia and central obesity.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Cortisol inhibits glucose uptake by cells, one reason it raises blood glucose.","ACTH from the pituitary drives the adrenal cortex to release cortisol.","Making new glucose in the liver is one of cortisol's listed actions.","Fat deposition is a listed action, behind the central obesity of Cushing syndrome."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Cushing syndrome lab logic: cortisol read with ACTH (pituitary, ectopic, adrenal)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case4-a","p2-cush-ectopic","p2-tf-7","p2-case4-b","p2-mm-cushing","p2-cortisol-except"]}},{"id":"p2-mm-cushing","lec":"path2","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these are manifestations of Cushing syndrome? Select all that apply.","choices":["Moon face and a buffalo hump","Osteoporosis","Hyperglycemia","Weight loss","Low blood pressure"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Cushing manifestations on the slide: central obesity, buffalo hump, moon face, hyperglycemia, hirsutism and excess androgens, osteoporosis, psychiatric changes, hypertension and acne. Weight loss and low blood pressure are Addison disease.","src":"rec","why":["Cortisol's fat deposition rounds the face and builds a fat pad on the upper back.","Osteoporosis is on the lecture's list of Cushing manifestations.","Cortisol raises glucose by blocking uptake and driving gluconeogenesis.","Cushing brings central obesity; weight loss is a feature of Addison disease.","Cushing syndrome causes hypertension; low pressure belongs to Addison disease."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Cushing syndrome lab logic: cortisol read with ACTH (pituitary, ectopic, adrenal)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case4-a","p2-cush-ectopic","p2-tf-7","p2-case4-b","p2-mm-cushing","p2-cortisol-except"]}},{"id":"p2-case4-a","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Where is her excess cortisol most likely coming from?","choices":["A tumor of the adrenal cortex","An ACTH-secreting pituitary tumor","An ACTH-secreting lung tumor","Long-term oral steroid pills"],"answer":0,"ex":"Adrenal Cushing: cortisol high, ACTH low, because cortisol feeds back on the pituitary. Pituitary and ectopic tumors: both high. Same logic as primary hyperthyroidism, with T4 high and TSH low.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","41-year-old woman at a dental recall visit"],["History","A year of weight gain around the middle; rounder face; fat pad at the back of the neck; new acne"],["Medications","None; no steroid use"],["Blood pressure","154/96 mm Hg"],["Fasting glucose","164 mg/dL (70-99)"],["Morning cortisol","32 µg/dL (5-23)"],["ACTH","Under 5 pg/mL (10-60)"]],"pcase":"path2-case4","why":["Cortisol high with ACTH low means the adrenal makes cortisol on its own and has switched the pituitary off.","A pituitary source raises ACTH as well as cortisol; hers is suppressed.","Ectopic ACTH also raises ACTH; hers is under 5.","Steroid pills can cause Cushing syndrome, but she takes none."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Cushing syndrome lab logic: cortisol read with ACTH (pituitary, ectopic, adrenal)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case4-a","p2-cush-ectopic","p2-tf-7","p2-case4-b","p2-mm-cushing","p2-cortisol-except"]}},{"id":"p2-case4-b","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Her dentist updates her problem list. Which other manifestation of her disease should be watched for?","choices":["Osteoporosis","Darkening of the skin and gums","Low blood glucose","Low blood pressure"],"answer":0,"ex":"Excess cortisol: central obesity, buffalo hump, moon face, hyperglycemia, hypertension, acne, hirsutism, psychiatric changes and osteoporosis. The other three choices are Addison disease, the opposite state.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","41-year-old woman with Cushing syndrome from an adrenal tumor"],["Blood pressure","154/96 mm Hg"],["Fasting glucose","164 mg/dL (70-99)"],["Morning cortisol","32 µg/dL (5-23)"],["ACTH","Under 5 pg/mL (10-60)"]],"pcase":"path2-case4","why":["Osteoporosis is on the lecture's list of Cushing manifestations, along with hypertension and hyperglycemia.","Hyperpigmentation is listed for Addison disease, where ACTH is high; hers is suppressed.","Cortisol raises glucose through gluconeogenesis; hers is already high.","Cushing syndrome raises blood pressure; low pressure belongs to Addison disease."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Cushing syndrome lab logic: cortisol read with ACTH (pituitary, ectopic, adrenal)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case4-a","p2-cush-ectopic","p2-tf-7","p2-case4-b","p2-mm-cushing","p2-cortisol-except"]}},{"id":"p2-cush-ectopic","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"A 63-year-old man who has smoked for 40 years has a lung mass, new central obesity, hypertension and hyperglycemia. His cortisol is high and his ACTH is high. What best explains his cortisol?","choices":["The lung tumor is secreting ACTH, which drives his adrenal cortex to make cortisol","An adrenal adenoma is making cortisol on its own, which has switched off his pituitary","The lung tumor is secreting cortisol itself, and ACTH has risen to match","Long-term oral steroids have taken over from his own adrenal glands"],"answer":0,"ex":"High cortisol with high ACTH means the drive is ACTH: a pituitary tumor or an ectopic tumor such as a lung cancer (paraneoplastic, like the lung cancers that make ADH). With a lung mass, ectopic is the best fit. An adrenal tumor gives high cortisol with low ACTH.","src":"hub","why":["High cortisol with high ACTH means ACTH is the driver, and a lung cancer can make ACTH as a paraneoplastic effect.","An adrenal source would suppress ACTH; his ACTH is high.","Cortisol excess suppresses ACTH rather than raising it; the tumor makes ACTH.","Nothing in his history mentions steroid pills, and they would not raise his ACTH."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Cushing syndrome lab logic: cortisol read with ACTH (pituitary, ectopic, adrenal)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case4-a","p2-cush-ectopic","p2-tf-7","p2-case4-b","p2-mm-cushing","p2-cortisol-except"]}},{"id":"p2-tf-7","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: On the lecture's lab table, a pituitary ACTH-secreting tumor and an ectopic ACTH-secreting tumor both show high cortisol with high ACTH.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"In both, ACTH is the driver, so both are high; only an adrenal source shows high cortisol with low ACTH. The clinical story, such as a lung mass, points to the ectopic source.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Both are ACTH-driven, so ACTH and cortisol are both high.","Both are driven by ACTH and share the same high-high pattern; it is the adrenal source that has low ACTH."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Cushing syndrome lab logic: cortisol read with ACTH (pituitary, ectopic, adrenal)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case4-a","p2-cush-ectopic","p2-tf-7","p2-case4-b","p2-mm-cushing","p2-cortisol-except"]}},{"id":"p2-2s-4","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Conn syndrome is secondary hyperaldosteronism caused by renal artery stenosis.\n2. Long-term oral steroid pills can cause Cushing syndrome.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Conn syndrome is primary hyperaldosteronism from an adrenal adenoma (aldosterone high, renin low); renal artery stenosis causes secondary hyperaldosteronism (both high). Cushing syndrome, cortisol excess, can come from a pituitary tumor, an adrenal tumor, an ectopic ACTH-making tumor or steroid pills taken for other conditions.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: Conn syndrome is primary: an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma, with renin low.","Statement 2 is true, not false: Dr. Dababneh listed steroid pills, taken for many conditions, among the causes of Cushing syndrome.","Statement 1 is false, not true: Conn syndrome is primary: an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma, with renin low. Statement 2 is true, not false: Dr. Dababneh listed steroid pills, taken for many conditions, among the causes of Cushing syndrome.","Statement 1 is false: Conn syndrome is primary: an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma, with renin low. Statement 2 is true: Dr. Dababneh listed steroid pills, taken for many conditions, among the causes of Cushing syndrome."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Conn syndrome (renin low) versus secondary hyperaldosteronism (renin high)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case5-a","p2-case5-b","p2-mm-renin","p2-hyperaldo-except","p2-2s-4","p2-tf-6","p2-seq-raas"]}},{"id":"p2-seq-raas","lec":"path2","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the steps of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system in order.","steps":["Renin acts on angiotensinogen in the plasma","Angiotensin I is formed","ACE converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II","Angiotensin II stimulates aldosterone secretion","The kidney retains sodium and excretes potassium"],"ex":"Renin turns angiotensinogen into angiotensin I, ACE makes angiotensin II, and angiotensin II drives aldosterone, which retains sodium and excretes potassium: the major regulator of fluid volume.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Conn syndrome (renin low) versus secondary hyperaldosteronism (renin high)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case5-a","p2-case5-b","p2-mm-renin","p2-hyperaldo-except","p2-2s-4","p2-tf-6","p2-seq-raas"]}},{"id":"p2-tf-6","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Aldosterone makes the kidney retain potassium and excrete sodium.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It is the reverse: aldosterone takes sodium back and dumps potassium, as PTH takes back calcium and dumps phosphate. Excess aldosterone means high sodium, low potassium and hypertension.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["The pair is swapped: aldosterone retains sodium and excretes potassium.","Aldosterone keeps sodium and dumps potassium, the reverse of this statement."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Conn syndrome (renin low) versus secondary hyperaldosteronism (renin high)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case5-a","p2-case5-b","p2-mm-renin","p2-hyperaldo-except","p2-2s-4","p2-tf-6","p2-seq-raas"]}},{"id":"p2-case5-a","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Conn syndrome: an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma","Secondary hyperaldosteronism from renal artery stenosis","Cushing syndrome from a cortisol-secreting adrenal tumor","Addison disease from autoimmune adrenal destruction"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hypertension, fatigue, high sodium and low potassium are aldosterone excess. Aldosterone high with renin low means the adrenal is the source: primary hyperaldosteronism, from an adenoma (Conn syndrome) or adrenocortical hyperplasia.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","49-year-old man"],["Complaint","Fatigue; blood pressure still high on three medications"],["Blood pressure","168/102 mm Hg"],["Sodium","146 mmol/L (135-145)"],["Potassium","2.9 mmol/L (3.5-5.0)"],["Aldosterone","38 ng/dL (4-31)"],["Renin activity","0.2 ng/mL/h (0.5-4.0)"]],"pcase":"path2-case5","why":["High aldosterone with low renin is primary hyperaldosteronism, and an adrenal adenoma is Conn syndrome.","Renal artery stenosis raises renin along with aldosterone; his renin is low.","Cushing is cortisol excess; his labs point to aldosterone, with no cortisol picture.","Addison disease lowers aldosterone, so sodium falls and potassium rises: the opposite of his labs."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Conn syndrome (renin low) versus secondary hyperaldosteronism (renin high)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case5-a","p2-case5-b","p2-mm-renin","p2-hyperaldo-except","p2-2s-4","p2-tf-6","p2-seq-raas"]}},{"id":"p2-case5-b","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which result best separates his condition from renal artery stenosis?","choices":["Renin: shut off in his condition, driven high in renal artery stenosis","Aldosterone: high in his condition, low in renal artery stenosis","Potassium: low in his condition, high in renal artery stenosis","Sodium: high in his condition, low in renal artery stenosis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both forms have high aldosterone; renin tells them apart. Primary (Conn adenoma or adrenocortical hyperplasia): the adrenal makes aldosterone on its own and negative feedback shuts renin off. Secondary (CHF, renal artery stenosis): poor renal perfusion drives renin up, and renin drives aldosterone.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","49-year-old man with resistant hypertension and fatigue"],["Potassium","2.9 mmol/L (3.5-5.0)"],["Aldosterone","38 ng/dL (4-31)"],["Renin activity","0.2 ng/mL/h (0.5-4.0)"],["CT","1.5 cm nodule in the left adrenal gland"]],"pcase":"path2-case5","why":["Primary disease shuts renin off by feedback; poor renal perfusion drives renin up.","Aldosterone is high in both, which is why it can't separate them.","Both are aldosterone excess, so both tend toward low potassium.","Both are aldosterone excess, so both retain sodium."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Conn syndrome (renin low) versus secondary hyperaldosteronism (renin high)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case5-a","p2-case5-b","p2-mm-renin","p2-hyperaldo-except","p2-2s-4","p2-tf-6","p2-seq-raas"]}},{"id":"p2-mm-renin","lec":"path2","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these raise aldosterone together with a high renin? Select all that apply.","choices":["Congestive heart failure with reduced renal perfusion","Renal artery stenosis","A renin-secreting tumor","An aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma (Conn syndrome)","Primary adrenocortical hyperplasia"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Secondary hyperaldosteronism starts at the kidney (CHF, renal artery stenosis, a renin-secreting tumor): renin high, aldosterone high. Primary starts at the adrenal (Conn adenoma, adrenocortical hyperplasia): aldosterone high, renin low.","src":"rec","why":["A poorly perfused kidney releases more renin, which drives aldosterone up.","The narrowed artery starves the kidney of blood, so renin and aldosterone both rise.","Dr. Dababneh named it: the tumor's renin pushes aldosterone up.","Conn syndrome is primary: aldosterone high, renin low.","Adrenocortical hyperplasia is listed under primary hyperaldosteronism, with renin low."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Conn syndrome (renin low) versus secondary hyperaldosteronism (renin high)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case5-a","p2-case5-b","p2-mm-renin","p2-hyperaldo-except","p2-2s-4","p2-tf-6","p2-seq-raas"]}},{"id":"p2-hyperaldo-except","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"All of the following fit primary hyperaldosteronism EXCEPT:","choices":["High serum potassium","Hypertension","Low plasma renin","Fatigue"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hyperaldosteronism: hypertension, fatigue, high sodium and low potassium. Primary: aldosterone high, renin low.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Aldosterone dumps potassium, so it runs low; high potassium fits aldosterone deficiency, as in Addison disease.","Retained sodium and water raise blood pressure, the main sign.","In primary disease, aldosterone from the adrenal shuts renin off.","Fatigue is on the lecture's list of clinical features."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Conn syndrome (renin low) versus secondary hyperaldosteronism (renin high)","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case5-a","p2-case5-b","p2-mm-renin","p2-hyperaldo-except","p2-2s-4","p2-tf-6","p2-seq-raas"]}},{"id":"p2-cah-21-why","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does 21-hydroxylase deficiency lead to androgen excess?","choices":["Blocked paths to aldosterone and cortisol shunt the pathway into androgens","21-hydroxylase normally breaks down testosterone in the peripheral tissues","The block makes precursors with mineralocorticoid activity pile up instead","It triggers an androgen-secreting carcinoma of the adrenal cortex"],"answer":0,"ex":"In 21-hydroxylase deficiency the paths to aldosterone and cortisol are blocked, so the pathway shifts into androgens: androgen excess, and in about a third, sodium wasting from aldosterone deficiency.","src":"rec","why":["With the routes to mineralocorticoids and glucocorticoids shut, the precursors go to sex steroids.","21-hydroxylase is an adrenal enzyme on the way to aldosterone and cortisol, not a breakdown enzyme.","Mineralocorticoid precursors piling up, with sodium retention and hypertension, is 17-hydroxylase deficiency.","Virilizing adrenal tumors are mostly carcinomas, but CAH is an enzyme defect, not a tumor."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: 21-hydroxylase versus 17-hydroxylase, treated with glucocorticoids","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-cah-21-why","p2-cah-newborn","p2-cah-17","p2-cah-tx","p2-2s-5","p2-num-5","p2-tf-12"]}},{"id":"p2-cah-tx","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Why are glucocorticoids the treatment for congenital adrenal hyperplasia?","choices":["Their negative feedback lowers ACTH, quieting the pathway and the extra androgens","They directly inhibit the defective enzyme so fewer precursors build up in the gland","They are converted into aldosterone in the cortex and so correct the sodium wasting","They stimulate ACTH so the blocked enzyme works harder"],"answer":0,"ex":"CAH leaves the patient short of cortisol. Giving glucocorticoid uses negative feedback: ACTH falls, the whole adrenal cortical pathway slows, and the androgen excess eases. It is the treatment for all forms.","src":"rec","why":["Giving glucocorticoid tells the pituitary to cut ACTH, which drives the whole adrenal pathway.","The enzyme is already defective; the problem is the ACTH drive, which feedback removes.","Glucocorticoids are not converted to aldosterone; the treatment works through feedback on ACTH.","Stimulating ACTH would worsen the androgen excess; the goal is to lower ACTH."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: 21-hydroxylase versus 17-hydroxylase, treated with glucocorticoids","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-cah-21-why","p2-cah-newborn","p2-cah-17","p2-cah-tx","p2-2s-5","p2-num-5","p2-tf-12"]}},{"id":"p2-2s-5","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. 21-hydroxylase deficiency causes androgen excess.\n2. 17-hydroxylase deficiency causes sodium wasting and low blood pressure.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"21-hydroxylase: androgen excess, and a third waste sodium. 17-hydroxylase: androgen deficiency, with precursors that have mineralocorticoid activity, so sodium is retained and blood pressure rises. Glucocorticoids treat both.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: 17-hydroxylase deficiency builds up precursors with mineralocorticoid activity, so sodium is retained and blood pressure rises.","Statement 1 is true, not false: blocking the paths to aldosterone and cortisol shifts the pathway into androgens.","Statement 1 is true: blocking the paths to aldosterone and cortisol shifts the pathway into androgens. Statement 2 is false: 17-hydroxylase deficiency builds up precursors with mineralocorticoid activity, so sodium is retained and blood pressure rises.","Statement 1 is true, not false: blocking the paths to aldosterone and cortisol shifts the pathway into androgens. Statement 2 is false, not true: 17-hydroxylase deficiency builds up precursors with mineralocorticoid activity, so sodium is retained and blood pressure rises."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: 21-hydroxylase versus 17-hydroxylase, treated with glucocorticoids","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-cah-21-why","p2-cah-newborn","p2-cah-17","p2-cah-tx","p2-2s-5","p2-num-5","p2-tf-12"]}},{"id":"p2-tf-12","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: About one third of patients with 21-hydroxylase deficiency waste sodium because they lack aldosterone.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The slide: 21-hydroxylase deficiency has several variants, all with androgen excess, and about a third have sodium wasting from aldosterone deficiency.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["One third is the slide's figure for sodium wasting from aldosterone deficiency.","This is the slide's own figure: a third have sodium wasting."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: 21-hydroxylase versus 17-hydroxylase, treated with glucocorticoids","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-cah-21-why","p2-cah-newborn","p2-cah-17","p2-cah-tx","p2-2s-5","p2-num-5","p2-tf-12"]}},{"id":"p2-num-5","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about congenital adrenal hyperplasia are true?\n1. 17-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common form.\n2. Each form is a defect in an enzyme involved in cortisol biosynthesis.\n3. It is a group of autosomal recessive disorders.\n4. Glucocorticoids treat it through negative feedback that lowers ACTH.","choices":["1, 2 and 3","2, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only","3 and 4 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"CAH is a group of autosomal recessive defects in an enzyme of cortisol biosynthesis. 21-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common form (androgen excess); 17-hydroxylase deficiency is less common (androgen deficiency, sodium retention, hypertension). All forms are treated with glucocorticoids.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: 21-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common form; 17-hydroxylase deficiency is less common. It leaves out 4, which is true: giving glucocorticoid lowers ACTH and quiets the androgen excess.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: 21-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common form; 17-hydroxylase deficiency is less common.","It leaves out 3, which is true: each form is inherited recessively, with a defective copy from each parent.","It leaves out 2, which is true: the defect is in an enzyme on the way to cortisol, which is why these patients are short of cortisol."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: 21-hydroxylase versus 17-hydroxylase, treated with glucocorticoids","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-cah-21-why","p2-cah-newborn","p2-cah-17","p2-cah-tx","p2-2s-5","p2-num-5","p2-tf-12"]}},{"id":"p2-cah-newborn","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"A 10-day-old girl had an enlarged clitoris at birth and is now vomiting and feeding poorly. Her sodium is 126 and potassium 6.6 mmol/L. Which best explains both findings?","choices":["21-hydroxylase deficiency of the salt-wasting type","17-hydroxylase deficiency with mineralocorticoid excess","Addison disease from autoimmune destruction of the cortex","Primary hyperaldosteronism from an adrenal adenoma"],"answer":0,"ex":"21-hydroxylase deficiency is the most common CAH: androgen excess (clitoral hypertrophy, pseudohermaphroditism) and, in about a third, sodium wasting from aldosterone deficiency. Treatment is glucocorticoids.","src":"hub","why":["Androgen excess virilized her, and the blocked aldosterone path wastes sodium, as in about a third of cases.","17-hydroxylase deficiency causes androgen deficiency and sodium retention, the opposite picture.","Addison disease could explain the salt loss, but not the androgen excess present at birth.","Aldosterone excess would raise sodium and lower potassium."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: 21-hydroxylase versus 17-hydroxylase, treated with glucocorticoids","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-cah-21-why","p2-cah-newborn","p2-cah-17","p2-cah-tx","p2-2s-5","p2-num-5","p2-tf-12"]}},{"id":"p2-cah-17","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"A 16-year-old girl has never developed breasts or other secondary sex characteristics. Her blood pressure is 158/98 mm Hg and her potassium is low. Which enzyme deficiency best fits?","choices":["17-hydroxylase deficiency","21-hydroxylase deficiency","Conn syndrome from an aldosterone-secreting adenoma","Addison disease from autoimmune adrenal destruction"],"answer":0,"ex":"17-hydroxylase deficiency: androgen deficiency (absent secondary sex characteristics in females, pseudohermaphroditism in males) and a buildup of precursors with mineralocorticoid activity, so sodium is retained and blood pressure rises. Treatment, as for all CAH, is glucocorticoids.","src":"hub","why":["Androgen deficiency (no secondary sex characteristics) plus precursors with mineralocorticoid activity (sodium retention, hypertension).","21-hydroxylase deficiency causes androgen excess and, in a third, sodium wasting: the opposite picture.","Conn syndrome explains the blood pressure but not the missing puberty.","Addison disease lowers blood pressure and raises potassium."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: 21-hydroxylase versus 17-hydroxylase, treated with glucocorticoids","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-cah-21-why","p2-cah-newborn","p2-cah-17","p2-cah-tx","p2-2s-5","p2-num-5","p2-tf-12"]}},{"id":"p2-case6-a","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the diagnosis?","choices":["Addison disease: primary failure of the adrenal cortex","Secondary hypoadrenalism: the pituitary has stopped making ACTH","Conn syndrome: an adrenal adenoma making excess aldosterone","An ACTH-secreting pituitary tumor overdriving the adrenals"],"answer":0,"ex":"Addison disease is chronic primary hypoadrenalism: weakness, fatigue, weight loss, nausea and vomiting, hyperpigmentation, hypotension, low glucose, low sodium, high potassium and high ACTH, with an inability to withstand stress.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","44-year-old man at a dental visit"],["Complaint","Months of fatigue, weakness, nausea and a 6 kg weight loss"],["Oral exam","Brown patches on the gingiva and buccal mucosa; skin darker than usual"],["Blood pressure","88/56 mm Hg"],["Sodium","128 mmol/L (135-145)"],["Potassium","5.9 mmol/L (3.5-5.0)"],["Glucose","62 mg/dL (70-99)"],["Morning cortisol","2 µg/dL (5-23)"],["ACTH","480 pg/mL (10-60)"]],"pcase":"path2-case6","why":["Low cortisol with high ACTH is primary failure of the cortex, and the low sodium and high potassium show aldosterone is gone too.","Secondary disease has low ACTH along with low cortisol; his ACTH is very high.","Aldosterone excess would raise sodium, lower potassium and raise blood pressure: the opposite of his labs.","A pituitary ACTH tumor would push cortisol up, not down."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Addison disease: features, high ACTH, autoimmune cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case6-a","p2-case6-b","p2-addison-except","p2-num-3","p2-tf-8"]}},{"id":"p2-case6-b","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most common cause of his condition?","choices":["Autoimmune attack on the adrenal cortex","Tuberculosis of both adrenal glands","Lung cancer metastatic to both adrenals","Amyloid deposits in the adrenal cortex"],"answer":0,"ex":"Addison disease causes: autoimmune (60 to 70 percent), viral (herpes, CMV, AIDS), chronic infection (fungal, TB), metastatic cancer (lung, breast, melanoma) and systemic disease (amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis).","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","44-year-old man with primary adrenal insufficiency"],["Oral exam","Brown patches on the gingiva and buccal mucosa"],["Morning cortisol","2 µg/dL (5-23)"],["ACTH","480 pg/mL (10-60)"],["History","No TB exposure, no cancer history, no steroid use"]],"pcase":"path2-case6","why":["Autoimmune disease causes 60 to 70 percent of Addison disease.","TB and fungal infection are listed causes, but much less common than autoimmune disease.","Metastases from the lung, breast or melanoma are a listed but less common cause.","Amyloidosis is one of the systemic diseases listed, not the most common cause."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Addison disease: features, high ACTH, autoimmune cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case6-a","p2-case6-b","p2-addison-except","p2-num-3","p2-tf-8"]}},{"id":"p2-addison-except","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"All of the following are expected in Addison disease EXCEPT:","choices":["Low serum potassium","Hyperpigmentation","Low blood glucose","High ACTH"],"answer":0,"ex":"Addison disease loses every cortical hormone: no aldosterone (low sodium, high potassium, low blood pressure), no cortisol (low glucose, fatigue, weakness), and ACTH rises because feedback is lost.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Without aldosterone the kidney holds on to potassium, so it runs high.","Darkening of the skin is on the slide's list of Addison features.","With cortisol gone, glucose falls.","The failing cortex no longer feeds back on the pituitary, so ACTH climbs."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Addison disease: features, high ACTH, autoimmune cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case6-a","p2-case6-b","p2-addison-except","p2-num-3","p2-tf-8"]}},{"id":"p2-num-3","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about Addison disease are true?\n1. Its most common cause is tuberculosis of the adrenal glands.\n2. Cancer metastatic from the lung, breast or melanoma can destroy the adrenal cortex.\n3. Low sodium and high potassium follow from the loss of aldosterone.\n4. ACTH is high, because the failing cortex no longer feeds back on the pituitary.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Addison disease is mostly autoimmune (60 to 70 percent); TB, fungal and viral infections, metastatic cancer and systemic diseases are the rest. Losing aldosterone gives low sodium and high potassium; losing cortisol feedback gives high ACTH.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: autoimmune disease is the most common cause, 60 to 70 percent; TB is a less common one.","It includes 1, which is false: autoimmune disease is the most common cause, 60 to 70 percent; TB is a less common one. It leaves out 2, which is true: metastatic lung cancer, breast cancer and melanoma are listed causes.","It leaves out 4, which is true: in primary failure feedback is lost, so the pituitary pushes ACTH up.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: autoimmune disease is the most common cause, 60 to 70 percent; TB is a less common one."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Addison disease: features, high ACTH, autoimmune cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case6-a","p2-case6-b","p2-addison-except","p2-num-3","p2-tf-8"]}},{"id":"p2-tf-8","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In secondary hypoadrenalism, ACTH is high while cortisol is low.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"High ACTH with low cortisol is primary failure of the cortex, Addison disease. In secondary hypoadrenalism the pituitary is the problem, so ACTH and the adrenal cortical hormones are both low.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["That pattern is primary failure (Addison disease); secondary has both low.","In secondary disease the pituitary fails, so ACTH is low along with cortisol."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Addison disease: features, high ACTH, autoimmune cause","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-case6-a","p2-case6-b","p2-addison-except","p2-num-3","p2-tf-8"]}},{"id":"p2-cortex-adenoma","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement about adrenal cortical adenomas is accurate?","choices":["Most secrete nothing; of those that do, most secrete aldosterone","Most secrete cortisol, which makes them the main cause of Cushing syndrome","Most secrete androgens, which makes them the usual virilizing adrenal tumor","About half are functional, and they are highly malignant"],"answer":0,"ex":"Adrenal cortical adenoma: mostly nonsecreting; the commonest functional type secretes aldosterone (high sodium, low potassium, hypertension). Carcinoma: large, over 2 cm, half functional, highly malignant, and the usual virilizing tumor.","src":"slides","why":["The slide: most adenomas are nonsecreting, and the most common functional ones make aldosterone.","Most adenomas are nonsecreting, and the functional ones mostly make aldosterone.","Most virilizing adrenal neoplasms are carcinomas, not adenomas.","Half functional and highly malignant describes adrenal cortical carcinoma."]},{"id":"p2-2s-6","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Adrenal cortical carcinomas are usually small, under 2 cm.\n2. About half of adrenal cortical carcinomas are functional.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Adrenal cortical carcinoma is large (over 2 cm on the slide), about 50 percent functional and highly malignant; most virilizing adrenal tumors are carcinomas. Most adenomas, by contrast, secrete nothing.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the slide describes them as large, over 2 cm.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide gives 50 percent functional, and they are highly malignant.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the slide describes them as large, over 2 cm. Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide gives 50 percent functional, and they are highly malignant.","Statement 1 is false: the slide describes them as large, over 2 cm. Statement 2 is true: the slide gives 50 percent functional, and they are highly malignant."]},{"id":"p2-tf-9","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Most virilizing adrenal neoplasms are carcinomas.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The slide lists it under adrenal cortical carcinoma: most virilizing adrenal neoplasms are carcinomas, while adenomas are mostly nonsecreting or make aldosterone.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["An adrenal tumor that makes a patient look more male is most often a carcinoma.","This is the slide's statement: virilization from an adrenal tumor points to carcinoma."]},{"id":"p2-pheo-cell","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of which cells?","choices":["Neural crest chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla","Zona glomerulosa cells of the adrenal cortex","Primitive neuroblasts of the sympathetic chain in young children","Chief cells of a lower parathyroid gland"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pheochromocytoma: a tumor of neural crest chromaffin cells in the adrenal medulla that secretes catecholamines, causing sweating, palpitations, paroxysmal attacks and hypertension.","src":"rec","why":["The medulla's chromaffin cells are neuroendocrine cells of neural crest origin that make catecholamines.","Glomerulosa cells make aldosterone; their adenoma is Conn syndrome.","That is neuroblastoma, the aggressive childhood tumor.","A chief cell tumor of a lower parathyroid is the usual parathyroid adenoma."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pheochromocytoma (rule of 10s, Zellballen) and neuroblastoma","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pheo-dental","p2-num-4","p2-pheo-cell","p2-nb","p2-2s-7","p2-tf-11"]}},{"id":"p2-pheo-dental","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"During a scaling appointment, a 39-year-old has a sudden spell of pounding heartbeat, drenching sweat and a blood pressure of 212/118 mm Hg that settles after 20 minutes. She has had similar attacks before. Workup shows high catecholamines and a 4 cm adrenal mass. Which tumor is most likely, and how would it look under the microscope?","choices":["Pheochromocytoma, with its cells grouped in nests or balls called Zellballen","Neuroblastoma, with primitive blasts in a pink fibrillary background","Conn syndrome adenoma, with steady hypertension and low potassium","Adrenal cortical carcinoma, a large tumor that often makes steroid hormones"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pheochromocytoma secretes catecholamines: sweating, palpitations, paroxysmal attacks and hypertension. Grossly a reddish mass pushes the golden cortex aside; microscopically the cells form nests or balls, Zellballen (German for cell balls).","src":"hub","why":["Catecholamine attacks from an adrenal mass are pheochromocytoma, and its cells sit in balls: Zellballen.","Neuroblastoma is a tumor of young children, 80 percent under age 5.","Aldosterone excess gives steady hypertension, not attacks of sweating and palpitations.","Cortical carcinoma makes steroids, not catecholamines, so it doesn't cause these attacks."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pheochromocytoma (rule of 10s, Zellballen) and neuroblastoma","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pheo-dental","p2-num-4","p2-pheo-cell","p2-nb","p2-2s-7","p2-tf-11"]}},{"id":"p2-num-4","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about pheochromocytoma are true?\n1. About 10 percent occur in familial syndromes such as MEN, neurofibromatosis, von Hippel-Lindau and Sturge-Weber.\n2. About 50 percent are bilateral.\n3. Extra-adrenal tumors, called paragangliomas, can arise in the organ of Zuckerkandl or the carotid body.\n4. The tumor cells grow in nests or balls called Zellballen.\n5. It is a tumor of the adrenal cortex.","choices":["1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","1, 3, 4 and 5","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":1,"ex":"The rule of 10s: 10 percent familial, 10 percent extra-adrenal (paragangliomas: organ of Zuckerkandl, carotid body), 10 percent bilateral, 10 percent malignant. Dr. Dababneh warned the numbers are old and approximate. The cells form Zellballen; the tumor is medullary and makes catecholamines.","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: Zellballen, German for cell balls, is the classic pattern.","Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: the rule of 10s gives 10 percent bilateral. Statement 5 is false: pheochromocytoma arises from the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, not the cortex, and secretes catecholamines.","It includes 5, which is false: pheochromocytoma arises from the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, not the cortex, and secretes catecholamines.","It includes 2, which is false: the rule of 10s gives 10 percent bilateral. It leaves out 1, which is true: 10 percent are familial, in MEN, neurofibromatosis, von Hippel-Lindau and Sturge-Weber."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pheochromocytoma (rule of 10s, Zellballen) and neuroblastoma","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pheo-dental","p2-num-4","p2-pheo-cell","p2-nb","p2-2s-7","p2-tf-11"]}},{"id":"p2-nb","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"A 3-year-old has a large abdominal mass arising from the adrenal, with lesions in the bones and liver. Biopsy shows sheets of small, primitive cells with pink fibrillary material between them. What is the diagnosis?","choices":["Neuroblastoma","Pheochromocytoma","Adrenal cortical carcinoma","Olfactory neuroblastoma"],"answer":0,"ex":"Neuroblastoma: very aggressive, 80 percent under age 5, anywhere in the sympathetic nervous system but most often the abdomen (adrenal medulla or retroperitoneal ganglia), spreading to bone, liver, lung and lymph nodes. \"Blast\" means immature; the pink fibrillary material looks like glial brain tissue.","src":"hub","why":["A very aggressive childhood tumor of primitive neural cells, in the adrenal medulla, spreading to bone and liver.","Pheochromocytoma grows as nests of chromaffin cells and is malignant only about 10 percent of the time.","Cortical carcinoma arises from the steroid-making cortex, not from primitive neural cells.","This is the adult form, arising in the olfactory cleft in the roof of the nose."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pheochromocytoma (rule of 10s, Zellballen) and neuroblastoma","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pheo-dental","p2-num-4","p2-pheo-cell","p2-nb","p2-2s-7","p2-tf-11"]}},{"id":"p2-2s-7","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Neuroblastoma rarely spreads beyond the site where it starts.\n2. In children, neuroblastoma most often arises in the head and neck.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Neuroblastoma is very aggressive and spreads to bone, liver, lung and lymph nodes. It can arise anywhere in the sympathetic nervous system, most often in the abdomen from the adrenal medulla or the retroperitoneal sympathetic ganglia; the head and neck form, olfactory neuroblastoma, is an adult tumor.","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: it is very aggressive and spreads to bone, liver, lung and lymph nodes. Statement 2 is false, not true: it can arise anywhere in the sympathetic nervous system but most often in the abdomen, from the adrenal medulla or the retroperitoneal ganglia.","Statement 1 is false: it is very aggressive and spreads to bone, liver, lung and lymph nodes. Statement 2 is false: it can arise anywhere in the sympathetic nervous system but most often in the abdomen, from the adrenal medulla or the retroperitoneal ganglia.","Statement 1 is false, not true: it is very aggressive and spreads to bone, liver, lung and lymph nodes.","Statement 2 is false, not true: it can arise anywhere in the sympathetic nervous system but most often in the abdomen, from the adrenal medulla or the retroperitoneal ganglia."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pheochromocytoma (rule of 10s, Zellballen) and neuroblastoma","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pheo-dental","p2-num-4","p2-pheo-cell","p2-nb","p2-2s-7","p2-tf-11"]}},{"id":"p2-tf-11","lec":"path2","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Adults can develop a form of neuroblastoma in the olfactory cleft, in the roof of the nose.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Olfactory neuroblastoma arises in the olfactory cleft at the skull base in adults; like the childhood tumor it is very aggressive, with primitive cells in a glial-like matrix.","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Dr. Dababneh described this adult form in the roof of the nose.","He described it: an aggressive adult neuroblastoma of the olfactory cleft."],"likely":{"lec":"path2","t":"Pheochromocytoma (rule of 10s, Zellballen) and neuroblastoma","basis":"recording","ids":["p2-pheo-dental","p2-num-4","p2-pheo-cell","p2-nb","p2-2s-7","p2-tf-11"]}},{"id":"dc-classes","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Dr. Taylor gives one example hormone for each chemical class. Which pairing matches his slide?","choices":["Peptide hormone: insulin","Steroid hormone: epinephrine","Amine hormone: estrogen","Peptide hormone: estrogen"],"answer":0,"ex":"Three classes, one example each: amine = epinephrine, peptide = insulin, steroid = estrogen.","src":"slides","why":["Insulin is his example of a peptide hormone.","Epinephrine is his amine example; his steroid example is estrogen.","Estrogen is his steroid example; his amine example is epinephrine.","Estrogen is a steroid; his peptide example is insulin."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Pituitary hormones and the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, hormone classes and the adrenal medulla","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-pit-match","dc-adh","dc-2s-pit","dc-tsh","dc-classes","dc-medulla-dental","dc-tf-calcitonin"]}},{"id":"dc-2s-class","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Estrogen, a steroid hormone, acts through intracellular receptors.\n2. Insulin, a peptide hormone, binds receptors inside the cell.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"His mechanism slide lists cell-surface receptors (often working through second messengers) and intracellular receptors. Steroids such as estrogen bind receptors inside the cell; peptides such as insulin bind on the cell surface.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: peptides such as insulin bind receptors on the cell surface; the intracellular receptors are the steroids'.","Statement 1 is true, not false: steroids such as estrogen bind receptors inside the cell.","Statement 1 is true: steroids such as estrogen bind receptors inside the cell. Statement 2 is false: peptides such as insulin bind receptors on the cell surface; the intracellular receptors are the steroids'.","Statement 1 is true, not false: steroids such as estrogen bind receptors inside the cell. Statement 2 is false, not true: peptides such as insulin bind receptors on the cell surface; the intracellular receptors are the steroids'."]},{"id":"dc-tf-endo-nerv","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Compared with the nervous system, the endocrine system responds more slowly, but its effects last longer.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"His slide names three points of contrast (speed of response, duration of action, signaling mechanism) without the answers. The standard contrast: hormones travel in the blood, so they act more slowly than nerve signals, and their effects last longer.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Hormones are slower to act than nerve signals, and they act for longer.","The statement has both directions right: slower onset, longer duration."]},{"id":"dc-pit-match","lec":"dental","type":"match","stem":"Match each anterior pituitary hormone to its major function on Dr. Taylor's table.","pairs":[["ACTH","Adrenal cortex stimulation"],["TSH","Thyroid stimulation"],["GH","Growth regulation"],["FSH and LH","Reproductive function"],["Prolactin","Lactation"]],"ex":"The anterior pituitary table: ACTH drives the adrenal cortex, TSH the thyroid, GH growth, FSH and LH reproduction, prolactin lactation. ADH and oxytocin are the posterior pituitary hormones.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Pituitary hormones and the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, hormone classes and the adrenal medulla","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-pit-match","dc-adh","dc-2s-pit","dc-tsh","dc-classes","dc-medulla-dental","dc-tf-calcitonin"]}},{"id":"dc-adh","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Diabetes insipidus is the disorder Dr. Taylor lists under which hormone?","choices":["ADH, the posterior pituitary hormone that conserves water","Insulin, the beta-cell hormone that moves glucose into cells","Oxytocin, the other posterior pituitary hormone","Aldosterone, the adrenal cortex hormone that retains sodium"],"answer":0,"ex":"Diabetes insipidus goes with ADH (water conservation, posterior pituitary). Diabetes mellitus goes with insulin (pancreatic beta cells).","src":"slides","why":["ADH conserves water, and diabetes insipidus is listed under it.","Insulin's disorder is diabetes mellitus; the shared word \"diabetes\" is the trap.","Oxytocin is on the same slide as ADH, but diabetes insipidus is listed under ADH.","Aldosterone handles sodium and potassium; diabetes insipidus is listed under ADH."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Pituitary hormones and the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, hormone classes and the adrenal medulla","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-pit-match","dc-adh","dc-2s-pit","dc-tsh","dc-classes","dc-medulla-dental","dc-tf-calcitonin"]}},{"id":"dc-2s-pit","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. ADH and oxytocin are posterior pituitary hormones.\n2. Prolactin is a posterior pituitary hormone.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"The posterior pituitary slide has ADH and oxytocin. Prolactin (lactation) is on the anterior pituitary table with ACTH, TSH, GH, FSH and LH.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: prolactin is on the anterior pituitary table.","Statement 1 is true, not false: ADH and oxytocin are the two posterior pituitary hormones on his slide.","Statement 1 is true: ADH and oxytocin are the two posterior pituitary hormones on his slide. Statement 2 is false: prolactin is on the anterior pituitary table.","Statement 1 is true, not false: ADH and oxytocin are the two posterior pituitary hormones on his slide. Statement 2 is false, not true: prolactin is on the anterior pituitary table."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Pituitary hormones and the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, hormone classes and the adrenal medulla","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-pit-match","dc-adh","dc-2s-pit","dc-tsh","dc-classes","dc-medulla-dental","dc-tf-calcitonin"]}},{"id":"dc-tsh","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"T3 and T4 synthesis is regulated by TSH. Where does TSH come from?","choices":["The anterior pituitary","The posterior pituitary","The thyroid gland itself","The parathyroid glands"],"answer":0,"ex":"TSH comes from the anterior pituitary, part of the hypothalamic-pituitary master control system, and stimulates the thyroid to make T3 and T4, which act on metabolism.","src":"slides","why":["TSH is on the anterior pituitary table, with the function \"thyroid stimulation\".","The posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin, not TSH.","The thyroid makes T3 and T4 (and calcitonin); TSH reaches it from the pituitary.","The parathyroids make PTH, the calcium hormone."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Pituitary hormones and the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, hormone classes and the adrenal medulla","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-pit-match","dc-adh","dc-2s-pit","dc-tsh","dc-classes","dc-medulla-dental","dc-tf-calcitonin"]}},{"id":"dc-acro","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"What best explains this patient's findings?","choices":["Growth hormone excess (acromegaly)","Hypothyroidism with macroglossia","Hyperparathyroidism with bone resorption","Cushing syndrome with hyperglycemia"],"answer":0,"ex":"GH excess (acromegaly) shows in the mouth as macroglossia, mandibular prognathism, diastemas and malocclusion. An enlarged tongue alone also fits hypothyroidism, so the jaw and spacing changes decide it.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","47-year-old man, routine exam"],["Complaint","New gaps between his teeth; his bite feels different"],["Exam","Enlarged tongue, lower jaw jutting forward, diastemas, malocclusion"],["Vitals","BP 132/84, pulse 76"]],"why":["Macroglossia, mandibular prognathism, diastemas and malocclusion are Dr. Taylor's oral signs of GH excess.","Hypothyroidism can enlarge the tongue, but its dental list is delayed eruption, macroglossia and delayed healing, not prognathism or diastemas.","Excess PTH resorbs bone (loss of lamina dura, brown tumors, mobility); it doesn't enlarge the tongue or push the jaw forward.","Cushing syndrome brings a moon face, buffalo hump and hyperglycemia, not jaw changes or a large tongue."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Acromegaly in the mouth; macroglossia in two conditions","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-acro","dc-macro","dc-hpt-except","dc-hypo-child"]}},{"id":"dc-macro","lec":"dental","type":"multi","stem":"Macroglossia appears on Dr. Taylor's slides for which conditions? Select all that apply.","choices":["Growth hormone excess (acromegaly)","Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Addison disease","Hyperparathyroidism"],"correct":[0,1],"ex":"An enlarged tongue shows up twice: GH excess and hypothyroidism. Tell them apart by the rest of the picture: prognathism and diastemas (GH) vs delayed eruption, bradycardia and weight gain (hypothyroid).","src":"slides","why":["Macroglossia is listed with prognathism, diastemas and malocclusion.","Macroglossia is listed with delayed eruption and delayed wound healing.","Hyperthyroidism's dental list is epinephrine sensitivity and accelerated eruption, not macroglossia.","Addison disease shows in the mouth as diffuse oral melanosis.","Hyperparathyroidism shows as loss of lamina dura, brown tumors and tooth mobility."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Acromegaly in the mouth; macroglossia in two conditions","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-acro","dc-macro","dc-hpt-except","dc-hypo-child"]}},{"id":"dc-hyperthy-except","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is a systemic finding of hyperthyroidism EXCEPT:","choices":["Bradycardia","Weight loss","Tachycardia","Heat intolerance"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hyperthyroidism: weight loss, tachycardia, heat intolerance. Hypothyroidism is the mirror image: fatigue, weight gain, bradycardia.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["A slow heart rate is on the hypothyroidism slide; hyperthyroidism speeds the heart.","Weight loss is on the hyperthyroidism slide (hypothyroidism brings weight gain).","A fast heart rate is on the hyperthyroidism slide.","Heat intolerance is on the hyperthyroidism slide."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroid vs hypothyroid dental considerations (eruption, macroglossia, healing)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-2s-thy","dc-thy-num","dc-hypo-child","dc-hyperthy-except","dc-2s-ff","dc-c1-oral"]}},{"id":"dc-2s-thy","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Hyperthyroidism in a child can accelerate tooth eruption.\n2. Delayed wound healing is a dental consideration in hypothyroidism.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Too much thyroid hormone accelerates eruption in children; too little delays eruption and wound healing and can enlarge the tongue.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: accelerated tooth eruption in children is on the hyperthyroidism slide. Statement 2 is true: delayed wound healing is on the hypothyroidism slide, with delayed eruption and macroglossia.","Statement 1 is true, not false: accelerated tooth eruption in children is on the hyperthyroidism slide. Statement 2 is true, not false: delayed wound healing is on the hypothyroidism slide, with delayed eruption and macroglossia.","Statement 2 is true, not false: delayed wound healing is on the hypothyroidism slide, with delayed eruption and macroglossia.","Statement 1 is true, not false: accelerated tooth eruption in children is on the hyperthyroidism slide."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroid vs hypothyroid dental considerations (eruption, macroglossia, healing)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-2s-thy","dc-thy-num","dc-hypo-child","dc-hyperthy-except","dc-2s-ff","dc-c1-oral"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-hypo-epi","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The caution about increased sensitivity to epinephrine applies to patients with uncontrolled hypothyroidism.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Increased sensitivity to epinephrine, and avoiding excessive vasoconstrictor in uncontrolled disease, are on the hyperthyroidism slide. Hypothyroidism's dental list is delayed eruption, macroglossia and delayed wound healing.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["It is hyperthyroidism, not hypothyroidism, that makes patients overly sensitive to epinephrine.","The epinephrine caution belongs to hyperthyroidism; the statement swaps hyper for hypo."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease (his first worked case): epinephrine sensitivity and thyroid storm","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c1-epi","dc-c1-storm","dc-c1-lab","dc-storm-num","dc-storm-case","dc-storm-vs-hypo","dc-tf-hypo-epi"]}},{"id":"dc-thy-num","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pairings of thyroid condition and dental consideration are on Dr. Taylor's slides?\n1. Hyperthyroidism: delayed wound healing\n2. Hyperthyroidism: accelerated tooth eruption in children\n3. Hypothyroidism: delayed eruption\n4. Hypothyroidism: macroglossia","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Hyperthyroidism: increased sensitivity to epinephrine, avoid excessive vasoconstrictor when uncontrolled, accelerated eruption in children. Hypothyroidism: delayed eruption, macroglossia, delayed wound healing.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: delayed wound healing is listed for hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism. It leaves out 4, which is true: macroglossia is on the hypothyroidism slide.","It includes 1, which is false: delayed wound healing is listed for hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism. It leaves out 2, which is true: accelerated eruption in children is on the hyperthyroidism slide.","It leaves out 4, which is true: macroglossia is on the hypothyroidism slide.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: delayed wound healing is listed for hypothyroidism, not hyperthyroidism."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroid vs hypothyroid dental considerations (eruption, macroglossia, healing)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-2s-thy","dc-thy-num","dc-hypo-child","dc-hyperthy-except","dc-2s-ff","dc-c1-oral"]}},{"id":"dc-storm-num","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about thyroid storm are true?\n1. It is also called thyroid crisis or thyrotoxic crisis.\n2. It occurs when the gland releases a large amount of hormone in a short time.\n3. The heart rate can exceed 140 beats per minute.\n4. A low body temperature is one of its signs.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":1,"ex":"Thyroid storm (thyroid crisis, thyrotoxic crisis) is a life-threatening release of a large amount of thyroid hormone in a short time: high fever, a heart rate that can exceed 140, agitation or anxiety, shakiness, nausea and delirium.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 3, which is true: the slide says the rate can exceed 140 beats per minute.","Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: thyroid storm brings a high fever, not a low temperature.","It includes 4, which is false: thyroid storm brings a high fever, not a low temperature. It leaves out 2, which is true: a large release in a short time is how the slide defines it.","It includes 4, which is false: thyroid storm brings a high fever, not a low temperature. It leaves out 1, which is true: the slide gives both other names."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease (his first worked case): epinephrine sensitivity and thyroid storm","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c1-epi","dc-c1-storm","dc-c1-lab","dc-storm-num","dc-storm-case","dc-storm-vs-hypo","dc-tf-hypo-epi"]}},{"id":"dc-c1-lab","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which hormone finding fits this patient's condition?","choices":["Elevated T3 and T4 from the thyroid","Elevated cortisol from the adrenal cortex","Elevated PTH from the parathyroids","Low T3 and T4 from the thyroid"],"answer":0,"ex":"The gland is the thyroid, and the elevated hormones are thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3): Graves disease is hyperthyroidism.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","39-year-old woman, here for two fillings under local anesthesia"],["Chief complaint","\"My heart feels like it's pounding.\""],["Medical history","Graves disease, diagnosed 2 months ago; takes methimazole"],["Signs","Losing weight despite a bigger appetite, always feels hot, hand tremor, anxious, visibly enlarged thyroid"],["Vitals","BP 146/90, pulse 116"]],"pcase":"dental-case1","why":["Graves disease is hyperthyroidism: T4 and T3 are elevated, which fits her weight loss, heat intolerance and fast pulse.","High cortisol is the Cushing picture (moon face, buffalo hump, hyperglycemia), not heat intolerance and weight loss.","High PTH is hyperparathyroidism: bone resorption, loss of lamina dura and brown tumors.","Low thyroid hormone is hypothyroidism: fatigue, weight gain and bradycardia, the opposite of her picture."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease (his first worked case): epinephrine sensitivity and thyroid storm","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c1-epi","dc-c1-storm","dc-c1-lab","dc-storm-num","dc-storm-case","dc-storm-vs-hypo","dc-tf-hypo-epi"]}},{"id":"dc-c1-epi","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"How should the dentist approach her local anesthetic, and why?","choices":["Use epinephrine cautiously: she may be overly sensitive to it, and it raises heart rate and blood pressure","Add extra epinephrine: her thyroid disease slows the heart, so a stronger vasoconstrictor will keep her pulse up","Confirm she has eaten and taken her medication first, and keep a fast-acting carbohydrate ready in case her glucose drops","Avoid epinephrine completely, because a vasoconstrictor in thyroid disease can set off an adrenal crisis in the chair"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hyperthyroid patients may be excessively sensitive to sympathomimetic drugs, and excess epinephrine raises heart rate and blood pressure; hers are already 116 and 146 over 90. So use epinephrine-containing anesthetics cautiously and avoid excessive vasoconstrictor while her disease is uncontrolled.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","39-year-old woman, here for two fillings under local anesthesia"],["Chief complaint","\"My heart feels like it's pounding.\""],["Medical history","Graves disease, diagnosed 2 months ago; takes methimazole"],["Signs","Losing weight despite a bigger appetite, always feels hot, hand tremor, anxious, visibly enlarged thyroid"],["Vitals","BP 146/90, pulse 116"]],"pcase":"dental-case1","why":["Hyperthyroid patients may be excessively sensitive to sympathomimetic drugs, so excessive vasoconstrictor is avoided while the disease is uncontrolled.","A slow heart is hypothyroidism; she is already tachycardic, and epinephrine would push her rate higher.","That is the diabetes and hypoglycemia plan; her problem is thyroid hormone excess, not glucose.","Adrenal crisis comes from cortisol deficiency (Addison disease); the thyroid concern is heart rate and blood pressure."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease (his first worked case): epinephrine sensitivity and thyroid storm","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c1-epi","dc-c1-storm","dc-c1-lab","dc-storm-num","dc-storm-case","dc-storm-vs-hypo","dc-tf-hypo-epi"]}},{"id":"dc-c1-oral","lec":"dental","type":"multi","stem":"Which oral findings or treatment problems does Dr. Taylor list for a hyperthyroid patient like her? Select all that apply.","choices":["Increased susceptibility to caries","Burning mouth symptoms","A tremor that can complicate treatment","Macroglossia","Delayed tooth eruption"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"His Graves case lists accelerated eruption (younger patients), increased caries susceptibility, burning mouth symptoms and a tremor that may complicate treatment. Macroglossia and delayed eruption are the hypothyroid picture.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","39-year-old woman, here for two fillings under local anesthesia"],["Chief complaint","\"My heart feels like it's pounding.\""],["Medical history","Graves disease, diagnosed 2 months ago; takes methimazole"],["Signs","Losing weight despite a bigger appetite, always feels hot, hand tremor, anxious, visibly enlarged thyroid"],["Vitals","BP 146/90, pulse 116"]],"pcase":"dental-case1","why":["His Graves case lists increased caries susceptibility among the oral manifestations.","His Graves case lists burning mouth symptoms among the oral manifestations.","His Graves case notes that the tremor may complicate dental treatment.","An enlarged tongue goes with hypothyroidism and GH excess, not hyperthyroidism.","Delayed eruption is hypothyroidism; hyperthyroidism accelerates eruption in younger patients."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroid vs hypothyroid dental considerations (eruption, macroglossia, healing)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-2s-thy","dc-thy-num","dc-hypo-child","dc-hyperthy-except","dc-2s-ff","dc-c1-oral"]}},{"id":"dc-c1-storm","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"If her hyperthyroidism stays uncontrolled, which emergency could occur, and how would it look?","choices":["Thyroid storm: fever, severe tachycardia, hypertension and altered mental status","Adrenal crisis: severe hypotension, weakness, confusion, loss of consciousness and shock","Hypoglycemia: sweating, tremors, confusion and tachycardia that respond to fast-acting carbohydrate","Tetany: neuromuscular irritability from the low calcium of hypoparathyroidism"],"answer":0,"ex":"Uncontrolled hyperthyroidism can tip into thyroid storm (thyroid crisis): a large release of hormone in a short time with high fever, severe tachycardia (it can exceed 140), hypertension, agitation and delirium. It is life-threatening.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","39-year-old woman, here for two fillings under local anesthesia"],["Chief complaint","\"My heart feels like it's pounding.\""],["Medical history","Graves disease, diagnosed 2 months ago; takes methimazole"],["Signs","Losing weight despite a bigger appetite, always feels hot, hand tremor, anxious, visibly enlarged thyroid"],["Vitals","BP 146/90, pulse 116"]],"pcase":"dental-case1","why":["This is the emergency his Graves case names for uncontrolled hyperthyroidism.","Adrenal crisis comes from cortisol deficiency (Addison disease), and the blood pressure falls rather than rises.","These are the low-glucose signs of a diabetic patient; they overlap with hers, but they bring no fever and the cause is glucose.","Tetany is a parathyroid and calcium problem, not thyroid hormone excess."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease (his first worked case): epinephrine sensitivity and thyroid storm","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c1-epi","dc-c1-storm","dc-c1-lab","dc-storm-num","dc-storm-case","dc-storm-vs-hypo","dc-tf-hypo-epi"]}},{"id":"dc-storm-case","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most likely emergency?","choices":["Thyroid storm","Hypoglycemia","Adrenal crisis","Hypocalcemic tetany"],"answer":0,"ex":"Thyroid storm is the life-threatening emergency of uncontrolled hyperthyroidism: high fever, a heart rate that can exceed 140, hypertension, agitation, shakiness, nausea and delirium. The fever and the high blood pressure set it apart from hypoglycemia and adrenal crisis. (Exam 1's thyroid lecture named stopping the antithyroid drug, and surgery, among its triggers.)","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","45-year-old woman with Graves disease, in the chair for an extraction"],["History","Ran out of her methimazole weeks ago; no diabetes"],["During treatment","Becomes agitated, shaky, nauseated and delirious"],["Vitals","Temperature 103.6 °F, pulse 152, BP 168/96"]],"why":["High fever, a pulse above 140, hypertension, agitation and delirium in a patient with uncontrolled Graves disease is thyroid storm.","Shakiness, confusion and a fast pulse overlap, but hypoglycemia doesn't explain a fever of 103.6, and she has no diabetes.","Adrenal crisis brings severe hypotension and shock; her blood pressure is high.","Tetany is the neuromuscular irritability of low calcium; it doesn't explain her fever and racing pulse."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease (his first worked case): epinephrine sensitivity and thyroid storm","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c1-epi","dc-c1-storm","dc-c1-lab","dc-storm-num","dc-storm-case","dc-storm-vs-hypo","dc-tf-hypo-epi"]}},{"id":"dc-storm-vs-hypo","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient in the chair turns shaky, confused and tachycardic. Which additional finding points to thyroid storm rather than hypoglycemia?","choices":["A high fever","Sweating","Tremors","A pulse over 100"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both bring tremor, confusion or delirium, and a racing pulse. Thyroid storm adds a high fever, with hypertension, nausea and agitation. Sweating is on his hypoglycemia list, but a hyperthyroid patient sweats too, so the fever is what separates them.","src":"hub","why":["High fever is a thyroid storm sign; it isn't on the hypoglycemia list.","Sweating is on his hypoglycemia list, but hyperthyroid patients sweat too (Exam 1), so it doesn't separate them.","Shakiness and tremors appear on both lists.","A racing heart appears on both lists, so it doesn't separate them."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroidism and Graves disease (his first worked case): epinephrine sensitivity and thyroid storm","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c1-epi","dc-c1-storm","dc-c1-lab","dc-storm-num","dc-storm-case","dc-storm-vs-hypo","dc-tf-hypo-epi"]}},{"id":"dc-hypo-child","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which condition best explains her findings, and which other dental concern goes with it?","choices":["Hypothyroidism, which also delays her wound healing","Hyperthyroidism, which also raises epinephrine sensitivity","Growth hormone excess, which also causes diastemas","Hypoparathyroidism, which also causes brown tumors"],"answer":0,"ex":"Fatigue, weight gain and a slow pulse with delayed eruption and macroglossia point to hypothyroidism, whose dental list also includes delayed wound healing. Hyperthyroidism is the mirror image: weight loss, tachycardia and accelerated eruption.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","9-year-old girl, new patient exam"],["Parent's report","Tired all the time and gaining weight"],["Exam","Several teeth erupting late for her age; enlarged tongue"],["Vitals","Pulse 58, slow for her age"]],"why":["Fatigue, weight gain, bradycardia, delayed eruption and macroglossia are the hypothyroid picture, and delayed wound healing is on the same list.","Hyperthyroidism accelerates eruption and brings weight loss and tachycardia, the opposite of her picture.","GH excess shares the large tongue, but not the late eruption, fatigue, weight gain and slow pulse.","Hypoparathyroidism brings hypocalcemia and tetany; brown tumors belong to hyperparathyroidism."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Acromegaly in the mouth; macroglossia in two conditions","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-acro","dc-macro","dc-hpt-except","dc-hypo-child"]}},{"id":"dc-ca-except","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Blood calcium falls and PTH is released. Each of the following is part of PTH's response EXCEPT:","choices":["Release of calcitonin from the thyroid","Stimulation of osteoclasts to resorb bone","Increased calcium reabsorption by the kidneys","Calcitriol from the kidneys, so the gut absorbs more calcium"],"answer":0,"ex":"PTH (fast acting, under negative feedback) raises calcium three ways: bone (osteoclasts), kidney reabsorption, and calcitriol for gut absorption. Calcitonin is the opposite signal, released by the thyroid when calcium is high.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Calcitonin is released when blood calcium rises, and it reverses PTH's effects on bone cells.","PTH's net effect on bone is osteoclast resorption, so bone gives up calcium (Exam 1: it works through receptors on the osteoblasts, which activate the osteoclasts).","PTH has the kidneys reabsorb calcium.","PTH has the kidneys release calcitriol (active vitamin D), which makes the GI tract absorb calcium."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperparathyroidism in the jaws and the calcium regulators (PTH, vitamin D, calcitonin)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-hpt-except","dc-c5-gland","dc-c5-mech","dc-ca-except","dc-2s-ca","dc-ca-seq","dc-tf-hypopara"]}},{"id":"dc-2s-ca","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Vitamin D is the fast-acting regulator of blood calcium, and PTH is the long-term regulator.\n2. A rise in blood calcium stimulates the thyroid to release calcitonin.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"PTH is the fast-acting regulator; vitamin D, activated in the kidney, is the long-term regulator that helps the gut absorb calcium. High calcium brings out calcitonin from the thyroid.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the roles are swapped: PTH is fast acting and vitamin D is the long-term regulator.","Statement 2 is true, not false: increased blood calcium stimulates the thyroid to release calcitonin.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the roles are swapped: PTH is fast acting and vitamin D is the long-term regulator. Statement 2 is true, not false: increased blood calcium stimulates the thyroid to release calcitonin.","Statement 1 is false: the roles are swapped: PTH is fast acting and vitamin D is the long-term regulator. Statement 2 is true: increased blood calcium stimulates the thyroid to release calcitonin."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperparathyroidism in the jaws and the calcium regulators (PTH, vitamin D, calcitonin)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-hpt-except","dc-c5-gland","dc-c5-mech","dc-ca-except","dc-2s-ca","dc-ca-seq","dc-tf-hypopara"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-vitd","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Vitamin D, the long-term regulator of blood calcium, is activated in the kidney.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Vitamin D is activated in the kidney (the kidneys release calcitriol) and helps the GI tract absorb calcium; PTH is the fast-acting regulator.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His slide says vitamin D is the long-term regulator and is activated in the kidney.","Both details are on the slide: long-term regulator, activated in the kidney."]},{"id":"dc-tf-calcitonin","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The hypothalamus steers the anterior pituitary with releasing and inhibiting hormones.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"His slide calls the hypothalamic-pituitary axis the master control system: the hypothalamus sends releasing and inhibiting hormones to the anterior pituitary, whose ACTH, TSH, GH, FSH and LH, and prolactin then drive other glands.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Releasing and inhibiting hormones from the hypothalamus are how the master control system runs the anterior pituitary.","The statement matches his slide: the hypothalamus controls the pituitary through releasing and inhibiting hormones."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Pituitary hormones and the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, hormone classes and the adrenal medulla","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-pit-match","dc-adh","dc-2s-pit","dc-tsh","dc-classes","dc-medulla-dental","dc-tf-calcitonin"]}},{"id":"dc-ca-seq","lec":"dental","type":"sequence","stem":"Put PTH's route for raising blood calcium through the gut in order.","steps":["Blood calcium falls","The parathyroid glands release PTH","The kidneys release calcitriol (active vitamin D)","The GI tract absorbs more calcium"],"ex":"Low calcium brings out PTH; PTH has the kidneys release calcitriol, and calcitriol makes the gut absorb calcium. As calcium rises, negative feedback turns PTH down.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperparathyroidism in the jaws and the calcium regulators (PTH, vitamin D, calcitonin)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-hpt-except","dc-c5-gland","dc-c5-mech","dc-ca-except","dc-2s-ca","dc-ca-seq","dc-tf-hypopara"]}},{"id":"dc-hpt-except","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is an oral finding of hyperparathyroidism EXCEPT:","choices":["Mandibular prognathism","Loss of the lamina dura","Brown tumors","Tooth mobility"],"answer":0,"ex":"Excess PTH resorbs bone (the skeletal effects are bone resorption and osteoporosis), which shows in the jaws as loss of the lamina dura, brown tumors and tooth mobility. Prognathism belongs to acromegaly.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["A jutting mandible is an oral sign of growth hormone excess (acromegaly).","Loss of the lamina dura is an oral finding of hyperparathyroidism.","Brown tumors are an oral finding of hyperparathyroidism.","Tooth mobility is an oral finding of hyperparathyroidism."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Acromegaly in the mouth; macroglossia in two conditions","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-acro","dc-macro","dc-hpt-except","dc-hypo-child"]}},{"id":"dc-c5-gland","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which gland is most likely overactive?","choices":["The parathyroid glands","The thyroid gland","The anterior pituitary","The adrenal cortex"],"answer":0,"ex":"High blood calcium with bone resorption in the jaws is hyperparathyroidism: excess PTH drives bone resorption, so the jaws lose lamina dura and develop brown tumors, and the teeth loosen. (Exam 1's calcium lecture gave the labs: high calcium with low phosphate is primary hyperparathyroidism.)","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","58-year-old woman, recall exam"],["Complaint","Several lower teeth feel loose"],["Radiographs","Loss of the lamina dura around several teeth; a radiolucent lesion in the mandible that the biopsy report calls a brown tumor"],["Labs","Serum calcium 11.8 mg/dL (reference 8.5 to 10.5); phosphate low"]],"pcase":"dental-case5","why":["High calcium with loss of lamina dura, a brown tumor and loose teeth is hyperparathyroidism.","The thyroid's calcium hormone is calcitonin, which reverses bone resorption rather than driving it.","Pituitary GH excess changes the jaw (prognathism, diastemas), not the lamina dura.","Cortisol excess (Cushing syndrome) brings infection risk and delayed healing, not brown tumors."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperparathyroidism in the jaws and the calcium regulators (PTH, vitamin D, calcitonin)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-hpt-except","dc-c5-gland","dc-c5-mech","dc-ca-except","dc-2s-ca","dc-ca-seq","dc-tf-hypopara"]}},{"id":"dc-c5-mech","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which mechanism best explains why her teeth are loosening?","choices":["Excess PTH drives osteoclasts to resorb alveolar bone and release calcium","Excess calcitonin drives osteoclasts to resorb alveolar bone and release calcium","Hyperglycemia increases inflammation and impairs immune function in the gingiva","Growth hormone enlarges the mandible and spreads the teeth apart into diastemas"],"answer":0,"ex":"PTH raises blood calcium partly through bone resorption. His slide puts it as \"inhibits osteoblasts, stimulates osteoclasts\"; Exam 1's calcium lecture gave the mechanism: PTH binds receptors on the osteoblasts, which then activate the osteoclasts. Either way, osteoclasts resorb the bone, and in the jaws that means loss of the lamina dura, brown tumors and tooth mobility.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","58-year-old woman, recall exam"],["Complaint","Several lower teeth feel loose"],["Radiographs","Loss of the lamina dura around several teeth; a radiolucent lesion in the mandible that the biopsy report calls a brown tumor"],["Labs","Serum calcium 11.8 mg/dL (reference 8.5 to 10.5); phosphate low"]],"pcase":"dental-case5","why":["Excess PTH resorbs bone; around the teeth that shows as lost lamina dura and mobility.","Calcitonin does the opposite: it is released when calcium is high and reverses PTH's effect on bone.","That is the diabetes route to periodontal destruction; her labs point to PTH.","That is acromegaly; it spaces the teeth but doesn't resorb the lamina dura."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperparathyroidism in the jaws and the calcium regulators (PTH, vitamin D, calcitonin)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-hpt-except","dc-c5-gland","dc-c5-mech","dc-ca-except","dc-2s-ca","dc-ca-seq","dc-tf-hypopara"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-hypopara","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Hypoparathyroidism causes hypercalcemia, which shows up as neuromuscular irritability and tetany.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Too little PTH means too little calcium: hypoparathyroidism causes hypocalcemia, and low calcium brings neuromuscular irritability and tetany.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Hypoparathyroidism lowers calcium (hypocalcemia); it doesn't raise it.","The statement flips the calcium level: it is hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperparathyroidism in the jaws and the calcium regulators (PTH, vitamin D, calcitonin)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-hpt-except","dc-c5-gland","dc-c5-mech","dc-ca-except","dc-2s-ca","dc-ca-seq","dc-tf-hypopara"]}},{"id":"dc-hormone-match","lec":"dental","type":"match","stem":"Match each hormone to its action on Dr. Taylor's slides.","pairs":[["Cortisol","Stress response, immune regulation and glucose metabolism"],["Aldosterone","Sodium retention and potassium regulation"],["Glucagon","Glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis"],["Insulin","Moves glucose into cells; the storage hormone"],["Calcitonin","Reverses PTH's effects on bone cells when calcium is high"]],"ex":"Adrenal cortex: cortisol (stress, immunity, glucose) and aldosterone (sodium, potassium). Pancreas: glucagon from alpha cells and insulin from beta cells. Thyroid: calcitonin, released when calcium is high.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes; alpha vs beta cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-dm-types","dc-2s-dm","dc-glucagon","dc-tf-insulin","dc-hormone-match"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-medulla","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Aldosterone is made by the adrenal medulla.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The adrenal cortex makes cortisol and aldosterone; the adrenal medulla makes epinephrine and norepinephrine.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Aldosterone is a cortex hormone; the medulla makes epinephrine and norepinephrine.","It names the wrong layer: aldosterone comes from the adrenal cortex."]},{"id":"dc-2s-adr","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Addison disease presents with hypotension, fatigue and hyperpigmentation.\n2. Cushing syndrome presents with a moon face, a buffalo hump and hyperglycemia.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Addison disease is a cortisol deficiency: low blood pressure, fatigue, darkened skin and diffuse oral melanosis. Cushing syndrome brings a moon face, buffalo hump and hyperglycemia, with more infections and slower healing in the mouth.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: hypotension, fatigue and hyperpigmentation are its clinical features. Statement 2 is true: moon face, buffalo hump and hyperglycemia are its clinical features.","Statement 1 is true, not false: hypotension, fatigue and hyperpigmentation are its clinical features. Statement 2 is true, not false: moon face, buffalo hump and hyperglycemia are its clinical features.","Statement 2 is true, not false: moon face, buffalo hump and hyperglycemia are its clinical features.","Statement 1 is true, not false: hypotension, fatigue and hyperpigmentation are its clinical features."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Addison disease (his third worked case): oral melanosis from high ACTH, adrenal crisis triggers and signs; Cushing syndrome","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c3-pig","dc-c3-trigger","dc-c3-crisis","dc-addison-seq","dc-tf-addison-pig","dc-2s-adr","dc-cushing"]}},{"id":"dc-medulla-dental","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Dr. Taylor ties the adrenal medulla's hormones to dentistry through which two points?","choices":["Vasoconstrictor use and stress management during treatment","Diffuse oral melanosis and the risk of adrenal crisis during surgery","Loss of the lamina dura, brown tumors and tooth mobility","Increased infection risk and delayed healing after surgery"],"answer":0,"ex":"The medulla makes epinephrine and norepinephrine. In dentistry that matters for vasoconstrictor use (the epinephrine in local anesthetics) and for managing the patient's stress during treatment.","src":"slides","why":["Epinephrine and norepinephrine are the medulla's hormones; his dental points are vasoconstrictors and managing stress in the chair.","Those belong to the adrenal cortex (Addison disease), not the medulla.","Those are hyperparathyroidism's oral findings.","Those are the oral considerations for Cushing syndrome."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Pituitary hormones and the hypothalamic-pituitary axis, hormone classes and the adrenal medulla","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-pit-match","dc-adh","dc-2s-pit","dc-tsh","dc-classes","dc-medulla-dental","dc-tf-calcitonin"]}},{"id":"dc-c3-pig","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"What best explains the pigmentation of his buccal mucosa and gingiva?","choices":["Low cortisol removes negative feedback, so ACTH rises and stimulates melanocytes","High cortisol from an overactive adrenal cortex directly stimulates the oral melanocytes","Low ACTH leaves the melanocytes unchecked, so they overproduce pigment","Epinephrine from the adrenal medulla stimulates melanocytes in the gingiva"],"answer":0,"ex":"His adrenal cortex makes too little cortisol, so negative feedback no longer holds ACTH down. The increased ACTH stimulates melanocytes, producing hyperpigmentation of the skin and diffuse oral melanosis.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","27-year-old man asking about brown patches in his mouth"],["Medical history","Addison disease for 4 years; takes hydrocortisone every day; no other conditions"],["Symptoms","Chronic fatigue, weight loss, dizzy when he stands up, muscle weakness"],["Exam","Diffuse brown pigmentation of the buccal mucosa and gingiva"],["Vitals","BP 88/58"]],"pcase":"dental-case3","why":["Increased ACTH production stimulates melanocytes, darkening the skin and oral mucosa.","Cortisol is low in Addison disease, and his slide credits ACTH, not cortisol, with the pigment.","ACTH is high, not low, in Addison disease, and it is the rise that stimulates melanocytes.","The medulla's epinephrine matters for vasoconstrictors and stress, not pigment."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Addison disease (his third worked case): oral melanosis from high ACTH, adrenal crisis triggers and signs; Cushing syndrome","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c3-pig","dc-c3-trigger","dc-c3-crisis","dc-addison-seq","dc-tf-addison-pig","dc-2s-adr","dc-cushing"]}},{"id":"dc-c3-trigger","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"He needs a long surgical extraction of an infected molar. Each of the following could precipitate an adrenal crisis in him EXCEPT:","choices":["Taking his usual steroid dose that morning","A stressful, lengthy surgical procedure","A significant infection or trauma","Missing his steroid medication"],"answer":0,"ex":"Crisis triggers on his slide: stressful or lengthy surgical procedures, significant infection or trauma, and missed steroid medication. Taking the steroid as prescribed is the opposite of a trigger.","src":"hub","fmt":"except","pbox":[["Patient","27-year-old man asking about brown patches in his mouth"],["Medical history","Addison disease for 4 years; takes hydrocortisone every day; no other conditions"],["Symptoms","Chronic fatigue, weight loss, dizzy when he stands up, muscle weakness"],["Exam","Diffuse brown pigmentation of the buccal mucosa and gingiva"],["Vitals","BP 88/58"]],"pcase":"dental-case3","why":["Taking his dose is what he should do; it is a missed steroid dose that can set off a crisis.","Stressful or lengthy surgery is on his list of crisis triggers.","Significant infection or trauma is on his list of crisis triggers.","A missed steroid dose is on his list of crisis triggers."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Addison disease (his third worked case): oral melanosis from high ACTH, adrenal crisis triggers and signs; Cushing syndrome","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c3-pig","dc-c3-trigger","dc-c3-crisis","dc-addison-seq","dc-tf-addison-pig","dc-2s-adr","dc-cushing"]}},{"id":"dc-c3-crisis","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Forty minutes into a long surgical extraction, he becomes weak and confused, and his blood pressure drops to 70/40. What is most likely happening?","choices":["Adrenal crisis","Thyroid storm","Hypoglycemia","Hypocalcemic tetany"],"answer":0,"ex":"Adrenal crisis signs: severe hypotension, weakness, confusion, loss of consciousness and shock. A long, stressful surgery is one of the procedures that can set it off in a patient with Addison disease.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","27-year-old man asking about brown patches in his mouth"],["Medical history","Addison disease for 4 years; takes hydrocortisone every day; no other conditions"],["Symptoms","Chronic fatigue, weight loss, dizzy when he stands up, muscle weakness"],["Exam","Diffuse brown pigmentation of the buccal mucosa and gingiva"],["Vitals","BP 88/58"]],"pcase":"dental-case3","why":["Severe hypotension, weakness and confusion in a patient with Addison disease under surgical stress is adrenal crisis; loss of consciousness and shock can follow.","Thyroid storm brings fever, severe tachycardia and hypertension; his pressure is falling.","Confusion can occur, but he has no diabetes, and hypoglycemia's list is sweating, tremors and tachycardia, not a pressure of 70 over 40.","Tetany is the neuromuscular irritability of low calcium, not a collapse of blood pressure."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Addison disease (his third worked case): oral melanosis from high ACTH, adrenal crisis triggers and signs; Cushing syndrome","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c3-pig","dc-c3-trigger","dc-c3-crisis","dc-addison-seq","dc-tf-addison-pig","dc-2s-adr","dc-cushing"]}},{"id":"dc-addison-seq","lec":"dental","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the chain that darkens the oral mucosa in Addison disease in order.","steps":["The adrenal cortex makes too little cortisol","Negative feedback on the pituitary weakens","ACTH production increases","ACTH stimulates melanocytes, and the mucosa darkens"],"ex":"Low cortisol takes the brake (negative feedback) off ACTH. The extra ACTH stimulates melanocytes, so the skin and oral mucosa darken: diffuse oral melanosis.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Addison disease (his third worked case): oral melanosis from high ACTH, adrenal crisis triggers and signs; Cushing syndrome","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c3-pig","dc-c3-trigger","dc-c3-crisis","dc-addison-seq","dc-tf-addison-pig","dc-2s-adr","dc-cushing"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-addison-pig","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In Addison disease, the hormone primarily deficient is aldosterone.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"His Addison case answers: the deficient hormone is cortisol, often accompanied by reduced aldosterone levels. The low cortisol is also what lets ACTH rise and darken the mouth.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Cortisol is the hormone primarily deficient; aldosterone is often reduced along with it.","The statement swaps the two: cortisol is primarily deficient, often with reduced aldosterone."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Addison disease (his third worked case): oral melanosis from high ACTH, adrenal crisis triggers and signs; Cushing syndrome","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c3-pig","dc-c3-trigger","dc-c3-crisis","dc-addison-seq","dc-tf-addison-pig","dc-2s-adr","dc-cushing"]}},{"id":"dc-cushing","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which oral considerations should the dentist plan for?","choices":["Increased infection risk and delayed healing","Diffuse oral melanosis and severe hypotension","Loss of the lamina dura and brown tumors","Accelerated eruption and epinephrine sensitivity"],"answer":0,"ex":"Moon face, buffalo hump and hyperglycemia are Cushing syndrome. Its oral considerations are an increased risk of infection and delayed healing, so plan for both around the extractions.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","61-year-old woman, scheduled for extraction of two teeth"],["Appearance","Rounded \"moon\" face; fat pad between the shoulders (buffalo hump)"],["Labs","Blood glucose elevated"]],"why":["These are Dr. Taylor's oral considerations for Cushing syndrome, which her moon face, buffalo hump and hyperglycemia point to.","Those go with Addison disease, not the moon-face picture.","Those are hyperparathyroidism's oral findings.","Those are hyperthyroidism's dental considerations."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Addison disease (his third worked case): oral melanosis from high ACTH, adrenal crisis triggers and signs; Cushing syndrome","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-c3-pig","dc-c3-trigger","dc-c3-crisis","dc-addison-seq","dc-tf-addison-pig","dc-2s-adr","dc-cushing"]}},{"id":"dc-glucagon","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which hormone, made where, breaks down glycogen and drives gluconeogenesis?","choices":["Glucagon from alpha cells","Insulin from beta cells","Glucagon from beta cells","Insulin from alpha cells"],"answer":0,"ex":"Alpha cells make glucagon (glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis). Beta cells make insulin (moves glucose into cells, the storage hormone).","src":"slides","why":["Alpha cells make glucagon, which breaks down glycogen and drives gluconeogenesis.","Insulin moves glucose into cells and stores it, the opposite job.","Glucagon is the right hormone, but it comes from alpha cells; beta cells make insulin.","Insulin comes from beta cells, and it stores glucose rather than releasing it."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes; alpha vs beta cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-dm-types","dc-2s-dm","dc-glucagon","dc-tf-insulin","dc-hormone-match"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-insulin","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Insulin is a storage hormone that moves glucose into cells.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Beta cells make insulin, which moves glucose into cells and is the storage hormone; glucagon from alpha cells does the reverse.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His pancreas slide describes insulin exactly this way.","The statement matches the slide: insulin moves glucose into cells and stores it."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes; alpha vs beta cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-dm-types","dc-2s-dm","dc-glucagon","dc-tf-insulin","dc-hormone-match"]}},{"id":"dc-dm-types","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which feature points to type 1 rather than type 2 diabetes?","choices":["Autoimmune destruction of the beta cells","Insulin resistance with high insulin levels (hyperinsulinemia)","Hyperglycemia found on a fasting blood glucose","An elevated HbA1c on the diabetes workup"],"answer":0,"ex":"Type 1: autoimmune destruction of the beta cells. Type 2: insulin resistance with hyperinsulinemia, and insulin production may decline over time. Hyperglycemia and HbA1c are how both are diagnosed and followed.","src":"slides","why":["Type 1 is autoimmune destruction of the beta cells, the insulin makers.","Insulin resistance and hyperinsulinemia define type 2.","High glucose is a diagnostic concept for diabetes in general, not a way to tell the types apart.","HbA1c is a diagnostic concept for both types."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes; alpha vs beta cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-dm-types","dc-2s-dm","dc-glucagon","dc-tf-insulin","dc-hormone-match"]}},{"id":"dc-2s-dm","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Type 2 diabetes begins with autoimmune destruction of the beta cells.\n2. In type 2 diabetes, insulin production may decline over time.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Type 1 is autoimmune destruction of the beta cells. Type 2 begins as insulin resistance with hyperinsulinemia, and his diabetes case adds that insulin production may decline over time.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: autoimmune beta-cell destruction is type 1; type 2 begins as insulin resistance with hyperinsulinemia.","Statement 2 is true, not false: his diabetes case says insulin production may decline over time in type 2.","Statement 1 is false, not true: autoimmune beta-cell destruction is type 1; type 2 begins as insulin resistance with hyperinsulinemia. Statement 2 is true, not false: his diabetes case says insulin production may decline over time in type 2.","Statement 1 is false: autoimmune beta-cell destruction is type 1; type 2 begins as insulin resistance with hyperinsulinemia. Statement 2 is true: his diabetes case says insulin production may decline over time in type 2."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes; alpha vs beta cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-dm-types","dc-2s-dm","dc-glucagon","dc-tf-insulin","dc-hormone-match"]}},{"id":"dc-dm-oral-except","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is an oral manifestation of diabetes mellitus EXCEPT:","choices":["Diffuse oral melanosis","Candidiasis","Burning mouth syndrome","Xerostomia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Diabetes in the mouth: xerostomia, candidiasis, burning mouth syndrome, delayed healing, and more periodontal disease and caries. Diffuse oral melanosis is Addison disease.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Diffuse oral melanosis is the oral finding of Addison disease (high ACTH).","Candidiasis is on the diabetes list.","Burning mouth syndrome is on the diabetes list.","Dry mouth is on the diabetes list."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Oral manifestations of diabetes, the two-way periodontal link and poor healing","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-oral-except","dc-perio-mech","dc-tf-perio","dc-c2-heal","dc-c2-organ","dc-tf-morning"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-perio","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Periodontal inflammation can worsen a diabetic patient's glycemic control.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The link runs both ways: diabetes worsens periodontal disease, and periodontal inflammation worsens glycemic control.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["It is the second half of the bidirectional relationship on his slide.","The relationship is bidirectional, so periodontal inflammation does affect glycemic control."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Oral manifestations of diabetes, the two-way periodontal link and poor healing","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-oral-except","dc-perio-mech","dc-tf-perio","dc-c2-heal","dc-c2-organ","dc-tf-morning"]}},{"id":"dc-perio-mech","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is periodontal disease often more severe in patients with diabetes?","choices":["Hyperglycemia increases inflammation and impairs immune function, so infection destroys more tissue","Excess PTH stimulates osteoclasts, so the lamina dura and alveolar bone around the teeth are resorbed","Hyperglycemia suppresses inflammation, so periodontal infection spreads unnoticed before it is treated","Hormone surges like those of puberty and pregnancy raise gingival inflammation, and diabetes adds to it"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hyperglycemia increases inflammation and impairs immune function, so the patient is more susceptible to the infection that destroys periodontal tissue. The relationship is bidirectional: periodontal inflammation in turn worsens glycemic control.","src":"slides","why":["This is the explanation in his diabetes case: more inflammation and weaker defenses mean more destruction.","That is hyperparathyroidism's route to tooth mobility, not diabetes.","The direction is flipped: hyperglycemia increases inflammation.","Hormonal gingival inflammation belongs to puberty and pregnancy; diabetes works through hyperglycemia."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Oral manifestations of diabetes, the two-way periodontal link and poor healing","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-oral-except","dc-perio-mech","dc-tf-perio","dc-c2-heal","dc-c2-organ","dc-tf-morning"]}},{"id":"dc-dm-mgmt-num","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which steps are on Dr. Taylor's dental management list for a patient with diabetes?\n1. Before treatment, review glycemic control and assess the HbA1c.\n2. Before treatment, confirm the patient has eaten and taken their medication.\n3. Schedule late-afternoon appointments when possible.\n4. During treatment, monitor for hypoglycemia.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":2,"ex":"Before treatment: review glycemic control, assess HbA1c, confirm meals and medication compliance. During treatment: morning appointments are preferred, and monitor for hypoglycemia.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: watching for hypoglycemia is the during-treatment step.","It includes 3, which is false: he prefers morning appointments, not late-afternoon ones.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: he prefers morning appointments, not late-afternoon ones.","It includes 3, which is false: he prefers morning appointments, not late-afternoon ones. It leaves out 1, which is true: reviewing glycemic control and the HbA1c come first on his list."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"The diabetic patient in the chair (his second worked case and management slide): before-treatment checks, morning appointments, recognizing and treating hypoglycemia","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-mgmt-num","dc-hypo-signs","dc-c4-what","dc-c4-do","dc-c4-unc","dc-c4-prevent","dc-c2-before"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-morning","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Delayed healing is a dental concern in hypothyroidism, Cushing syndrome and diabetes alike.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Delayed healing shows up on three of his slides: hypothyroidism (delayed wound healing), Cushing syndrome (with increased infection risk) and diabetes (decreased neutrophil function, poor blood supply, impaired collagen synthesis).","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["All three slides list delayed healing, so the statement is true.","Each of the three conditions lists delayed healing; none of them is the odd one out."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Oral manifestations of diabetes, the two-way periodontal link and poor healing","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-oral-except","dc-perio-mech","dc-tf-perio","dc-c2-heal","dc-c2-organ","dc-tf-morning"]}},{"id":"dc-hypo-signs","lec":"dental","type":"multi","stem":"Which signs does Dr. Taylor list for recognizing hypoglycemia in the chair? Select all that apply.","choices":["Sweating","Tremors","Confusion","Bradycardia","High fever"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Hypoglycemia: sweating, tremors, confusion and tachycardia. Treat it with a fast-acting carbohydrate, and activate emergency protocols if the patient is unconscious.","src":"slides","why":["Sweating is on his recognition list.","Tremors are on his recognition list.","Confusion is on his recognition list, with tachycardia.","Hypoglycemia brings tachycardia, a fast pulse; bradycardia is a sign of hypothyroidism.","A high fever points to thyroid storm, not low glucose."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"The diabetic patient in the chair (his second worked case and management slide): before-treatment checks, morning appointments, recognizing and treating hypoglycemia","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-mgmt-num","dc-hypo-signs","dc-c4-what","dc-c4-do","dc-c4-unc","dc-c4-prevent","dc-c2-before"]}},{"id":"dc-2s-ff","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Brown tumors are an oral finding of hypoparathyroidism.\n2. Accelerated tooth eruption in children is a dental consideration in hypothyroidism.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Brown tumors come from bone resorption in hyperparathyroidism (too much PTH). Accelerated eruption is hyperthyroidism; hypothyroidism delays eruption.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: brown tumors belong to hyperparathyroidism, the excess, not the deficiency. Statement 2 is false, not true: hypothyroidism delays eruption; it is hyperthyroidism that accelerates it.","Statement 1 is false: brown tumors belong to hyperparathyroidism, the excess, not the deficiency. Statement 2 is false: hypothyroidism delays eruption; it is hyperthyroidism that accelerates it.","Statement 1 is false, not true: brown tumors belong to hyperparathyroidism, the excess, not the deficiency.","Statement 2 is false, not true: hypothyroidism delays eruption; it is hyperthyroidism that accelerates it."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Hyperthyroid vs hypothyroid dental considerations (eruption, macroglossia, healing)","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-2s-thy","dc-thy-num","dc-hypo-child","dc-hyperthy-except","dc-2s-ff","dc-c1-oral"]}},{"id":"dc-c2-organ","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which hormone, and which cells that make it, are at the center of his disease?","choices":["Insulin, from the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans","Glucagon, from the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans","Cortisol, from the adrenal cortex, raising glucose under stress","Insulin, from the alpha cells of the islets of Langerhans"],"answer":0,"ex":"The organ is the pancreas (the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans), and the hormone is insulin. In type 2 diabetes insulin resistance is present, and insulin production may decline over time.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","66-year-old man, here for extraction of a non-restorable mandibular first molar"],["Medical history","Type 2 diabetes for 20 years; hypertension; takes his metformin only now and then; smokes a pack a day"],["Symptoms","Always thirsty; urinating often"],["Vitals","BP 150/94, pulse 86"],["Dental findings","Generalized moderate periodontal disease, dry mouth, several carious lesions; a previous extraction site healed slowly"],["Labs","Fasting blood glucose 231 mg/dL; HbA1c 10.1%"]],"pcase":"dental-case2","why":["In type 2 diabetes the tissues resist insulin, and the beta cells' production may decline over time.","Glucagon raises glucose, but the defect in diabetes is insulin's, not glucagon's.","Cortisol has a role in glucose metabolism, but his diagnosis is diabetes, an insulin disease.","Insulin is the right hormone, but beta cells make it; alpha cells make glucagon."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Oral manifestations of diabetes, the two-way periodontal link and poor healing","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-oral-except","dc-perio-mech","dc-tf-perio","dc-c2-heal","dc-c2-organ","dc-tf-morning"]}},{"id":"dc-c2-before","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"His labs are in the box. What should the dentist do about the extraction?","choices":["Hold the extraction until his glycemic control is adequate, then time it after his meal and metformin","Proceed today, since an extraction is short, and recheck his HbA1c at his next recall visit","Use an anesthetic without epinephrine, since diabetes makes his heart overly sensitive to any vasoconstrictor","Book a late-afternoon visit so he has the whole day to take his medication before the extraction"],"answer":0,"ex":"Thirst, frequent urination, irregular metformin, a fasting glucose of 231 and an HbA1c of 10.1% all say his diabetes is poorly controlled (Lecture 11: diabetes is diagnosed at an HbA1c of 6.5% or a fasting glucose of 126, and an HbA1c of 8 or 9% means glucose has run high for months). Dr. Taylor's concerns are delayed healing and postoperative infection, so he verifies adequate glycemic control before treatment and times the procedure after the patient's meal and medication.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","66-year-old man, here for extraction of a non-restorable mandibular first molar"],["Medical history","Type 2 diabetes for 20 years; hypertension; takes his metformin only now and then; smokes a pack a day"],["Symptoms","Always thirsty; urinating often"],["Vitals","BP 150/94, pulse 86"],["Dental findings","Generalized moderate periodontal disease, dry mouth, several carious lesions; a previous extraction site healed slowly"],["Labs","Fasting blood glucose 231 mg/dL; HbA1c 10.1%"]],"pcase":"dental-case2","why":["Dr. Taylor verifies adequate glycemic control before treatment; these numbers show it isn't adequate yet.","Poorly controlled diabetes raises the risk of delayed healing and infection; control is checked before treatment, not after.","The epinephrine caution is for hyperthyroidism; his slides give no vasoconstrictor caution for diabetes.","Morning appointments are preferred, with the procedure timed after his meal and medication."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"The diabetic patient in the chair (his second worked case and management slide): before-treatment checks, morning appointments, recognizing and treating hypoglycemia","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-mgmt-num","dc-hypo-signs","dc-c4-what","dc-c4-do","dc-c4-unc","dc-c4-prevent","dc-c2-before"]}},{"id":"dc-c2-heal","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements explain why his extraction site may heal poorly?\n1. Neutrophil function is decreased.\n2. Collagen synthesis is impaired.\n3. Blood supply to the site is increased.\n4. Hyperglycemia dampens inflammation in the tissues.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","2 and 3 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Uncontrolled diabetes slows healing through decreased neutrophil function, poor blood supply and impaired collagen synthesis. Hyperglycemia also increases inflammation and impairs immune function, which raises the risk of postoperative infection.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","66-year-old man, here for extraction of a non-restorable mandibular first molar"],["Medical history","Type 2 diabetes for 20 years; hypertension; takes his metformin only now and then; smokes a pack a day"],["Symptoms","Always thirsty; urinating often"],["Vitals","BP 150/94, pulse 86"],["Dental findings","Generalized moderate periodontal disease, dry mouth, several carious lesions; a previous extraction site healed slowly"],["Labs","Fasting blood glucose 231 mg/dL; HbA1c 10.1%"]],"pcase":"dental-case2","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1 and 2 are true. Statement 3 is false: the blood supply is poor, not increased. Statement 4 is false: hyperglycemia increases inflammation; it doesn't dampen it.","It includes 3, which is false: the blood supply is poor, not increased.","It includes 4, which is false: hyperglycemia increases inflammation; it doesn't dampen it.","It includes 3, which is false: the blood supply is poor, not increased. It leaves out 1, which is true: decreased neutrophil function is one of his three wound-healing reasons."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Oral manifestations of diabetes, the two-way periodontal link and poor healing","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-oral-except","dc-perio-mech","dc-tf-perio","dc-c2-heal","dc-c2-organ","dc-tf-morning"]}},{"id":"dc-c4-what","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the most likely cause of her change?","choices":["Hypoglycemia: she took her insulin but never ate breakfast","Hyperglycemia: without breakfast, her insulin had no glucose to act on","Thyroid storm from the stress of the appointment","Adrenal crisis from the stress of the appointment"],"answer":0,"ex":"Insulin moves glucose into cells. Taken without the meal, it drops her glucose: sweating, tremors, confusion and tachycardia are Dr. Taylor's hypoglycemia signs. That is why he confirms meals and medication before treatment.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes, mid-morning scaling and root planing"],["Medications","Insulin; she took her usual morning dose"],["Today","Skipped breakfast because she was running late"],["30 minutes in","Sweating, shaky, confused, slow to answer"],["Vitals","Pulse 118 (78 at check-in)"]],"pcase":"dental-case4","why":["Insulin moved glucose into her cells with no meal behind it; sweating, tremor, confusion and tachycardia are the signs.","Taking insulin and skipping the meal drives glucose down, not up.","Thyroid storm brings a high fever and hypertension and needs uncontrolled hyperthyroidism; she has type 1 diabetes.","Adrenal crisis brings severe hypotension and shock in a patient with adrenal insufficiency, which she doesn't have."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"The diabetic patient in the chair (his second worked case and management slide): before-treatment checks, morning appointments, recognizing and treating hypoglycemia","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-mgmt-num","dc-hypo-signs","dc-c4-what","dc-c4-do","dc-c4-unc","dc-c4-prevent","dc-c2-before"]}},{"id":"dc-c4-do","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"She is still awake and responding. What should the dentist do now?","choices":["Stop treatment and give her a fast-acting carbohydrate now","Give a small extra dose of her insulin to steady her glucose","Activate emergency protocols before giving her anything by mouth","Finish the quadrant quickly, then offer a snack at checkout"],"answer":0,"ex":"Management on his slide: a fast-acting carbohydrate, and activate emergency protocols if the patient is unconscious. She is conscious, so stop and give carbohydrate.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes, mid-morning scaling and root planing"],["Medications","Insulin; she took her usual morning dose"],["Today","Skipped breakfast because she was running late"],["30 minutes in","Sweating, shaky, confused, slow to answer"],["Vitals","Pulse 118 (78 at check-in)"]],"pcase":"dental-case4","why":["A conscious patient with hypoglycemia gets a fast-acting carbohydrate.","Insulin lowers glucose further; it would make hypoglycemia worse.","Emergency protocols are for the patient who is unconscious; she is awake, so give carbohydrate first.","Hypoglycemia is treated when it is recognized, not after the procedure."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"The diabetic patient in the chair (his second worked case and management slide): before-treatment checks, morning appointments, recognizing and treating hypoglycemia","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-mgmt-num","dc-hypo-signs","dc-c4-what","dc-c4-do","dc-c4-unc","dc-c4-prevent","dc-c2-before"]}},{"id":"dc-c4-unc","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: If she becomes unconscious, the next step on Dr. Taylor's slide is to activate emergency protocols.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"A fast-acting carbohydrate is for the conscious patient; if hypoglycemia leaves the patient unconscious, activate emergency protocols.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes, mid-morning scaling and root planing"],["Medications","Insulin; she took her usual morning dose"],["Today","Skipped breakfast because she was running late"],["30 minutes in","Sweating, shaky, confused, slow to answer"],["Vitals","Pulse 118 (78 at check-in)"]],"pcase":"dental-case4","fmt":"tf","why":["Unconsciousness is the point where his slide says to activate emergency protocols.","The slide does say to activate emergency protocols for the unconscious patient."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"The diabetic patient in the chair (his second worked case and management slide): before-treatment checks, morning appointments, recognizing and treating hypoglycemia","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-mgmt-num","dc-hypo-signs","dc-c4-what","dc-c4-do","dc-c4-unc","dc-c4-prevent","dc-c2-before"]}},{"id":"dc-c4-prevent","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which step before treatment would most likely have prevented this episode?","choices":["Confirming that she had eaten and taken her medication","Reviewing her HbA1c from her last physician visit","Choosing an anesthetic with no epinephrine in it","Moving her appointment from the morning to the afternoon"],"answer":0,"ex":"Before treatment: review glycemic control and HbA1c, and confirm meals and medication compliance. Only the last would have caught insulin taken without breakfast.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes, mid-morning scaling and root planing"],["Medications","Insulin; she took her usual morning dose"],["Today","Skipped breakfast because she was running late"],["30 minutes in","Sweating, shaky, confused, slow to answer"],["Vitals","Pulse 118 (78 at check-in)"]],"pcase":"dental-case4","why":["She took insulin without eating; asking about meals and medication catches exactly that.","HbA1c shows her overall glycemic control; it wouldn't show that she skipped breakfast today.","The epinephrine caution is for hyperthyroidism; her problem is low glucose.","He prefers morning appointments for patients with diabetes."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"The diabetic patient in the chair (his second worked case and management slide): before-treatment checks, morning appointments, recognizing and treating hypoglycemia","basis":"slides","ids":["dc-dm-mgmt-num","dc-hypo-signs","dc-c4-what","dc-c4-do","dc-c4-unc","dc-c4-prevent","dc-c2-before"]}},{"id":"dc-preg","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pair of diagnoses best fits her findings?","choices":["Pregnancy gingivitis and a pyogenic granuloma (\"pregnancy tumor\")","Burning mouth syndrome and candidiasis from uncontrolled hyperglycemia","Loss of the lamina dura and a brown tumor from excess PTH","Diffuse oral melanosis and gingival pigment from excess ACTH"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pregnancy brings pregnancy gingivitis and the pyogenic granuloma, the \"pregnancy tumor\". Puberty increases gingival inflammation; menopause brings xerostomia, altered taste and osteoporosis implications.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","29-year-old woman, 24 weeks pregnant"],["Medical history","No other conditions"],["Complaint","Gums bleed when she brushes; a lump on her gum"],["Exam","Red, swollen gingiva; a raised gingival mass between two lower teeth"]],"why":["Both are the oral effects of pregnancy on Dr. Taylor's reproductive slide.","Those are diabetes findings, and she has no diabetes.","A brown tumor is a bone lesion of hyperparathyroidism, not a gingival mass of pregnancy.","Melanosis is brown pigmentation in Addison disease, not swollen, bleeding gingiva."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Sex hormones and the mouth: puberty, pregnancy, menopause","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-preg","dc-repro-num","dc-tf-puberty","dc-meno"]}},{"id":"dc-repro-num","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pairings of life stage and oral effect are on Dr. Taylor's reproductive endocrinology slide?\n1. Menopause: pyogenic granuloma\n2. Puberty: increased gingival inflammation\n3. Menopause: xerostomia and altered taste\n4. Menopause: osteoporosis implications","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Puberty: more gingival inflammation. Pregnancy: pregnancy gingivitis and pyogenic granuloma (the pregnancy tumor). Menopause: xerostomia, altered taste and osteoporosis implications.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: the pyogenic granuloma, the pregnancy tumor, goes with pregnancy. It leaves out 4, which is true: osteoporosis implications are listed for menopause.","It includes 1, which is false: the pyogenic granuloma, the pregnancy tumor, goes with pregnancy.","It leaves out 4, which is true: osteoporosis implications are listed for menopause.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: the pyogenic granuloma, the pregnancy tumor, goes with pregnancy."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Sex hormones and the mouth: puberty, pregnancy, menopause","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-preg","dc-repro-num","dc-tf-puberty","dc-meno"]}},{"id":"dc-tf-puberty","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Xerostomia and altered taste are the oral effects Dr. Taylor links to puberty.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Xerostomia and altered taste are menopause effects (with osteoporosis implications). Puberty's oral effect is increased gingival inflammation.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Puberty's listed effect is increased gingival inflammation; dry mouth and altered taste are menopause.","The statement names the wrong life stage: these are menopause effects."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Sex hormones and the mouth: puberty, pregnancy, menopause","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-preg","dc-repro-num","dc-tf-puberty","dc-meno"]}},{"id":"dc-meno","lec":"dental","type":"mcq","stem":"Her symptoms fit the oral effects of menopause. Which other implication does Dr. Taylor tie to the same stage?","choices":["Osteoporosis implications for her bone","A rising risk of pyogenic granuloma","Increased gingival inflammation, as in puberty","Accelerated eruption of her remaining teeth"],"answer":0,"ex":"Menopause: xerostomia, altered taste and osteoporosis implications. Puberty raises gingival inflammation; pregnancy brings pregnancy gingivitis and the pyogenic granuloma.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","57-year-old woman, postmenopausal for 5 years"],["Complaint","Dry mouth; food tastes different than it used to"],["Medical history","No diabetes; no other conditions"]],"why":["Menopause's list is xerostomia, altered taste and osteoporosis implications.","The pyogenic granuloma is the pregnancy tumor.","That is the oral effect listed for puberty.","Accelerated eruption is a hyperthyroid effect in children."],"likely":{"lec":"dental","t":"Sex hormones and the mouth: puberty, pregnancy, menopause","basis":"objectives","ids":["dc-preg","dc-repro-num","dc-tf-puberty","dc-meno"]}},{"id":"mh-lobules","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"The testis is divided into about 250 pyramidal lobules. What does each lobule contain?","choices":["Several blind-ended, coiled seminiferous tubules in loose vascular connective tissue holding Leydig cells","One straight tubule that opens directly into the epididymis, wrapped in dense connective tissue and smooth muscle","A network of channels lined by cuboidal epithelium that collects sperm on their way to the efferent ductules","Several coiled tubules lined by pseudostratified columnar cells with stereocilia, with Sertoli cells between them"],"answer":0,"ex":"Testis → about 250 lobules → several blind-ended, highly coiled seminiferous tubules in each. The loose, vascular connective tissue around the tubules holds the Leydig cells, outside the tubules.","src":"slides","why":["Each lobule packs several coiled, blind-ended tubules, and the loose vascular connective tissue between them holds the Leydig cells.","The tubules are several per lobule and highly coiled, and they drain through straight tubules and the rete testis, not straight into the epididymis.","That is the rete testis, a single network in the mediastinum that receives the straight tubules; it is not what fills each lobule.","Pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia is the epididymis lining, and Sertoli cells sit inside the tubules, not between them."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermiogenesis and spermiation; testis organization and temperature","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-organelles","mh-spermiation","mh-tf-head","mh-lobules","mh-stages","mh-tf-temp","mh-case-temp"]}},{"id":"mh-wall","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"In a cross-section of a seminiferous tubule, which cells sit in the tubule's outer wall, in its connective tissue, outside the seminiferous epithelium?","choices":["Peritubular myoid cells","Leydig (interstitial) cells","Sertoli cells","Dark type A spermatogonia"],"answer":0,"ex":"From outside in: interstitium with Leydig cells → tubule wall (connective tissue and peritubular myoid cells) → seminiferous epithelium (Sertoli cells and spermatogenic cells) → lumen.","src":"slides","why":["His labeled section marks M, the peritubular myoid cells, in the outer wall of the tubule along with its connective tissue.","Leydig cells are outside the tubule too, but out in the loose connective tissue between tubules, not in the tubule's own wall.","Sertoli cells are inside the seminiferous epithelium, standing on the basement membrane and reaching the lumen.","Spermatogonia sit on the basement membrane but inside the epithelium, in the basal compartment."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cell functions (ABP, AMH, inhibin, phagocytosis) and Leydig cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-sertoli","mh-amh","mh-tf-leydig","mh-wall","mh-match-secretions"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-temp","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: On Dr. Herr's histology slides, the testes descend into the scrotum during fetal development, and normal spermatogenesis needs about 35°C rather than the body's 37°C.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dr. Herr's slide: the testes descend during fetal development, and a lower temperature (about 35°C vs 37°C) is necessary for normal spermatogenesis. Dr. Madison's physiology lecture gives about 34°C; both put it a few degrees below body temperature.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Both halves are on Dr. Herr's slide: the testes descend during fetal development, and spermatogenesis needs about 35°C rather than 37°C.","Nothing is changed from Dr. Herr's slide, which gives about 35°C; the 34°C figure is from Dr. Madison's physiology lecture, and this statement names Dr. Herr's."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermiogenesis and spermiation; testis organization and temperature","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-organelles","mh-spermiation","mh-tf-head","mh-lobules","mh-stages","mh-tf-temp","mh-case-temp"]}},{"id":"mh-lh","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"LH from the anterior pituitary acts on which testicular cell, and what does that cell make in response?","choices":["Leydig cells, which make testosterone","Sertoli cells, which make androgen-binding protein","Sertoli cells, which make testosterone","Leydig cells, which make inhibin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pair them: LH → Leydig cells → testosterone; FSH → Sertoli cells → androgen-binding protein (and inhibin back to the pituitary). Testosterone then lowers LH secretion by negative feedback.","src":"slides","why":["LH binds receptors on Leydig cells and starts testosterone production, which stimulates sperm development.","That is FSH's target and product: FSH stimulates Sertoli cells to make ABP.","Sertoli cells answer to FSH and do not make testosterone; the Leydig cells do.","Leydig cells are LH's target, but inhibin comes from Sertoli cells and blocks FSH release."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"LH vs FSH: Leydig and testosterone vs Sertoli, ABP and inhibin","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-lh","mh-abp","mh-2s-1","mh-mm-gonadotropin","mh-case-t-1","mh-case-t-2"]}},{"id":"mh-abp","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A drug blocks the FSH receptors in the testis. Which effect follows most directly from Dr. Herr's description of FSH?","choices":["Sertoli cells make less androgen-binding protein, so less testosterone stays concentrated in the tubules","Leydig cells lose their main stimulus and stop making testosterone, so spermatogonia cannot enter the cell cycle","Sertoli cells make more inhibin, which then blocks the release of LH from the anterior pituitary","Peritubular myoid cells stop making androgen-binding protein, so the tubule wall loosens"],"answer":0,"ex":"FSH → Sertoli cells → androgen-binding protein → testosterone held at a high concentration inside the tubules. LH → Leydig cells → testosterone is a separate pathway.","src":"slides","why":["FSH stimulates ABP production by Sertoli cells, and ABP is what helps keep testosterone concentrated in the seminiferous tubules.","Leydig cells are driven by LH, not FSH, so blocking FSH receptors leaves their testosterone production running.","Inhibin blocks FSH, not LH, and losing the FSH signal would not make Sertoli cells more active.","ABP is a Sertoli cell product; the myoid cells in the tubule wall are not FSH's target."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"LH vs FSH: Leydig and testosterone vs Sertoli, ABP and inhibin","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-lh","mh-abp","mh-2s-1","mh-mm-gonadotropin","mh-case-t-1","mh-case-t-2"]}},{"id":"mh-2s-1","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Testosterone lowers LH secretion through a negative feedback loop.\n2. Inhibin made by Sertoli cells blocks the release of FSH by the anterior pituitary.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Two brakes, one for each gonadotropin: testosterone (from Leydig cells) lowers LH, and inhibin (from Sertoli cells) blocks FSH.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: his slide says release of LH is inhibited by a feedback loop in which testosterone lowers LH secretion. Statement 2 is true: inhibin is the Sertoli cell product that blocks FSH release by the anterior pituitary.","Statement 1 is true, not false: his slide says release of LH is inhibited by a feedback loop in which testosterone lowers LH secretion. Statement 2 is true, not false: inhibin is the Sertoli cell product that blocks FSH release by the anterior pituitary.","Statement 2 is true, not false: inhibin is the Sertoli cell product that blocks FSH release by the anterior pituitary.","Statement 1 is true, not false: his slide says release of LH is inhibited by a feedback loop in which testosterone lowers LH secretion."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"LH vs FSH: Leydig and testosterone vs Sertoli, ABP and inhibin","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-lh","mh-abp","mh-2s-1","mh-mm-gonadotropin","mh-case-t-1","mh-case-t-2"]}},{"id":"mh-mm-gonadotropin","lec":"mhisto","type":"multi","stem":"Which cells respond directly to a pituitary gonadotropin (LH or FSH), according to Dr. Herr's slides? Select all that apply.","choices":["Leydig cells","Sertoli cells","Peritubular myoid cells","Spermatids","Columnar cells of the epididymis"],"correct":[0,1],"ex":"Two targets, one each: LH → Leydig cells (testosterone); FSH → Sertoli cells (androgen-binding protein), which answer back with inhibin. The myoid cells, germ cells and epididymis are not direct targets.","src":"slides","why":["They carry the LH receptors; LH starts their testosterone production.","They respond to FSH, which stimulates them to make androgen-binding protein.","Myoid cells sit in the tubule wall; his slides give them no LH or FSH role.","Spermatids depend on Sertoli support and testosterone, not on LH or FSH directly.","These absorb fluid and secrete glycerophosphocholine; they are not LH or FSH targets on his slides."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"LH vs FSH: Leydig and testosterone vs Sertoli, ABP and inhibin","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-lh","mh-abp","mh-2s-1","mh-mm-gonadotropin","mh-case-t-1","mh-case-t-2"]}},{"id":"mh-sertoli-id","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"In a seminiferous tubule, a tall columnar cell stretches from the basement membrane all the way to the lumen, and its nucleus is generally ovoid. Which cell is it?","choices":["Sertoli cell","Leydig (interstitial) cell","Primary spermatocyte","Pale type A spermatogonium"],"answer":0,"ex":"Only the Sertoli cell reaches from the basement membrane to the lumen; the germ cells develop against and between Sertoli cells, and the Leydig cells are outside the tubule.","src":"slides","why":["Columnar, basement membrane to lumen, ovoid nucleus: that is his description of the Sertoli (sustentacular) cell.","Leydig cells are outside the tubules, in the connective tissue between them.","Primary spermatocytes are germ cells in the adluminal compartment; they do not span the whole epithelium.","Spermatogonia are small cells at the base of the tubule, not tall cells that reach the lumen."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cells: shape, the blood-testis barrier and its two compartments","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sertoli-id","mh-btb","mh-btb-why","mh-mm-adluminal","mh-num-sertoli","mh-case-rad","mh-tf-sertoli-divide"]}},{"id":"mh-btb","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"What forms the blood-testis barrier?","choices":["Tight (occluding) junctions between neighboring Sertoli cells, near their bases","Tight junctions between Leydig cells and the capillaries of the interstitium","A continuous sheet of peritubular myoid cells in the tubule's outer wall","Desmosomes that bind the spermatogonia firmly to the basement membrane of the tubule"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sertoli-Sertoli tight junctions divide the tubule into a basal compartment (spermatogonia) and an adluminal compartment (spermatocytes and spermatids), protecting the developing gametes from the immune system.","src":"slides","why":["Occluding junctions in the basolateral region of adjacent Sertoli cells form the barrier and split the tubule into two compartments.","Leydig cells lie outside the tubule and form no barrier; the barrier is inside the seminiferous epithelium.","Myoid cells sit in the tubule wall, but the barrier is made by the junctions between Sertoli cells.","Spermatogonia lie below the barrier, in the basal compartment; they do not form it."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cells: shape, the blood-testis barrier and its two compartments","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sertoli-id","mh-btb","mh-btb-why","mh-mm-adluminal","mh-num-sertoli","mh-case-rad","mh-tf-sertoli-divide"]}},{"id":"mh-btb-why","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"If the junctions between neighboring Sertoli cells were broken, which cells would lose the protection the barrier normally gives them?","choices":["Primary spermatocytes and spermatids in the adluminal compartment","Dark and pale type A spermatogonia in the basal compartment","Leydig cells in the loose connective tissue between the tubules","Peritubular myoid cells in the connective tissue of the tubule wall"],"answer":0,"ex":"Below the junctions (basal compartment): the spermatogonia. Type B spermatogonia become primary spermatocytes, which move straight into the adluminal compartment; from then on the germ cells develop behind the barrier, hidden from the immune system.","src":"slides","why":["The barrier shields the developing gametes above it, in the adluminal compartment, from the immune system.","Spermatogonia sit below the junctions, on the blood side, so the barrier never shielded them.","Leydig cells are outside the tubule altogether, so the barrier never covered them.","Myoid cells are in the wall, outside the epithelium and outside the barrier."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cells: shape, the blood-testis barrier and its two compartments","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sertoli-id","mh-btb","mh-btb-why","mh-mm-adluminal","mh-num-sertoli","mh-case-rad","mh-tf-sertoli-divide"]}},{"id":"mh-mm-adluminal","lec":"mhisto","type":"multi","stem":"Which cells are found in the adluminal compartment, above the tight junctions between Sertoli cells? Select all that apply.","choices":["Primary spermatocytes","Secondary spermatocytes","Early and late spermatids","Dark type A spermatogonia","Type B spermatogonia"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Basal compartment: the spermatogonia (dark A, pale A and type B). Adluminal compartment: primary and secondary spermatocytes and spermatids, protected from the immune system by the blood-testis barrier.","src":"slides","why":["Type B spermatogonia make primary spermatocytes, which move straight into the adluminal compartment.","They come from primary spermatocytes, so they are already behind the barrier.","Spermatids form by meiosis behind the barrier and are remodeled into sperm there.","Spermatogonia are small, basally located cells, below the junctions in the basal compartment.","Type B cells are still spermatogonia at the base of the tubule; only their products, the primary spermatocytes, move up."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cells: shape, the blood-testis barrier and its two compartments","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sertoli-id","mh-btb","mh-btb-why","mh-mm-adluminal","mh-num-sertoli","mh-case-rad","mh-tf-sertoli-divide"]}},{"id":"mh-x-sertoli","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of these is a function of Sertoli cells EXCEPT:","choices":["Secreting testosterone in response to LH","Phagocytosing cytoplasm shed during sperm development","Making androgen-binding protein in response to FSH","Making inhibin, which blocks FSH release"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sertoli cells: support and nourish the germ cells, phagocytose shed cytoplasm, make ABP (under FSH), anti-Müllerian hormone and inhibin, and form the blood-testis barrier. Testosterone is the Leydig cell's job.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["That is the Leydig cell, outside the tubule, which carries the LH receptors.","Sertoli cells do phagocytose the cytoplasm discarded as sperm develop.","ABP is a Sertoli product, made under FSH, that helps keep testosterone high in the tubules.","Inhibin is a Sertoli product that blocks FSH release by the anterior pituitary."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cell functions (ABP, AMH, inhibin, phagocytosis) and Leydig cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-sertoli","mh-amh","mh-tf-leydig","mh-wall","mh-match-secretions"]}},{"id":"mh-amh","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"In a male embryo, the Sertoli cells cannot make anti-Müllerian hormone. Based on that hormone's job on Dr. Herr's slide, what would you expect?","choices":["The precursor duct of the female reproductive tract is not suppressed","The Leydig cells cannot start making testosterone during embryonic development","The blood-testis barrier never forms between the neighboring Sertoli cells","The spermatogonia enter the cell cycle years early, long before puberty"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sertoli cells make anti-Müllerian hormone, which prevents development of the Müllerian duct (the precursor of the female reproductive system). Without it, that duct is not suppressed.","src":"slides","why":["Anti-Müllerian hormone from Sertoli cells prevents development of the Müllerian duct, the precursor of the female reproductive system, so without it that duct is free to develop.","Leydig cell testosterone in the embryo does not depend on AMH; AMH's job on his slide is blocking the Müllerian duct.","The barrier is made by tight junctions between Sertoli cells, not by AMH.","Spermatogonia start dividing at puberty under testosterone; AMH plays no part in that on his slides."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cell functions (ABP, AMH, inhibin, phagocytosis) and Leydig cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-sertoli","mh-amh","mh-tf-leydig","mh-wall","mh-match-secretions"]}},{"id":"mh-num-sertoli","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about Sertoli cells are true?\n1. They respond to LH by making androgen-binding protein.\n2. Tight junctions between neighboring Sertoli cells divide the tubule into basal and adluminal compartments.\n3. They are columnar, reach from the basement membrane to the lumen, and have a generally ovoid nucleus.\n4. They make anti-Müllerian hormone, which prevents development of the Müllerian duct.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Sertoli cells: tall columnar with an ovoid nucleus, joined by tight junctions (the blood-testis barrier), respond to FSH (ABP), make AMH and inhibin, phagocytose shed cytoplasm, and stop dividing in the mature testis.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: Sertoli cells respond to FSH, which drives ABP; LH acts on the Leydig cells.","It includes 1, which is false: Sertoli cells respond to FSH, which drives ABP; LH acts on the Leydig cells. It leaves out 3, which is true: that is his description of their shape and nucleus.","It leaves out 4, which is true: AMH from Sertoli cells blocks the Müllerian duct, the precursor of the female tract.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: Sertoli cells respond to FSH, which drives ABP; LH acts on the Leydig cells."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cells: shape, the blood-testis barrier and its two compartments","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sertoli-id","mh-btb","mh-btb-why","mh-mm-adluminal","mh-num-sertoli","mh-case-rad","mh-tf-sertoli-divide"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-sertoli-divide","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Sertoli cells keep dividing throughout adult life to keep pace with the germ cells they support.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Sertoli cells do not divide in the mature testis, and they are resistant to heat, ionizing radiation and various toxic agents, so they often outlast the germ cells.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His slide says the opposite: Sertoli cells do not divide in the mature testis.","Sertoli cells stop dividing in the mature testis, and they resist heat, ionizing radiation and toxic agents."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cells: shape, the blood-testis barrier and its two compartments","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sertoli-id","mh-btb","mh-btb-why","mh-mm-adluminal","mh-num-sertoli","mh-case-rad","mh-tf-sertoli-divide"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-leydig","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Leydig cells first begin to secrete testosterone at puberty.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Leydig (interstitial) cells, outside the tubules, secrete testosterone during embryonic development, at sexual maturity and to maintain spermatogenesis, so they start long before puberty.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His slide gives three times, starting with embryonic development, then sexual maturity and the maintenance of spermatogenesis; puberty is not the start.","Leydig cells already secrete testosterone in the embryo, before they do so again at sexual maturity and to maintain spermatogenesis."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cell functions (ABP, AMH, inhibin, phagocytosis) and Leydig cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-sertoli","mh-amh","mh-tf-leydig","mh-wall","mh-match-secretions"]}},{"id":"mh-terms","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which term names the transformation of spermatids into spermatozoa?","choices":["Spermiogenesis","Spermatocytogenesis","Spermiation","Capacitation"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spermatogenesis is the whole process (germ cell → sperm). Inside it: spermatocytogenesis (spermatogonia → primary spermatocytes), meiosis (primary spermatocytes → haploid spermatids) and spermiogenesis (spermatids → spermatozoa).","src":"slides","why":["Spermiogenesis is the remodeling step, spermatid to spermatozoon, with no further cell division.","That is an earlier step: spermatogonia differentiating into primary spermatocytes.","Spermiation is the release of the finished sperm from the Sertoli cell, at the very end.","Capacitation is the conditioning sperm need in the female tract before they can fertilize an ovum."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermatogenesis terms, spermatogonia types and meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-terms","mh-meiosis","mh-dark-a","mh-2s-3","mh-seq-cells","mh-num-spg","mh-tf-puberty"]}},{"id":"mh-meiosis","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which cell enters meiosis as a diploid cell and, through meiosis, gives rise to haploid spermatids?","choices":["Primary spermatocyte","Type B spermatogonium","Secondary spermatocyte","Pale type A spermatogonium"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spermatogonia (dark A, pale A, type B) → primary spermatocyte (diploid; enters meiosis) → secondary spermatocyte → spermatids (haploid) → spermiogenesis → spermatozoa.","src":"slides","why":["Diploid primary spermatocytes undergo meiosis and reduce their chromosome complement, forming haploid spermatids.","Type B spermatogonia differentiate into primary spermatocytes; they come before meiosis.","The secondary spermatocyte is already partway through meiosis, made by the first division of a primary spermatocyte.","Pale type A cells are progenitors that make more pale A and type B cells; they do not enter meiosis."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermatogenesis terms, spermatogonia types and meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-terms","mh-meiosis","mh-dark-a","mh-2s-3","mh-seq-cells","mh-num-spg","mh-tf-puberty"]}},{"id":"mh-stages","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"On one slide of the testis, one tubule cross-section is full of late spermatids while the tubule beside it holds mostly spermatocytes. What best explains the difference?","choices":["Spermatogenesis is at a different stage at each point along a tubule","Each lobule makes only one kind of germ cell, so neighboring tubules specialize","The tubule full of spermatids is really rete testis, where finished sperm wait","The second tubule lost its blood-testis barrier, so immune cells cleared its spermatids"],"answer":0,"ex":"All stages occur in the seminiferous tubules, but at any one spot the tubule is at one stage of a process that takes about 74 days on his slide. Cut across many tubules and you see many different mixes of cells.","src":"slides","why":["His slide says it directly: the stages vary along the tubule, so cross-sections show different numbers of cells at each stage.","Every tubule runs the whole process; it is the stage at that spot, not a specialty, that differs.","The rete testis is a cuboidal-lined network in the mediastinum, not a tubule full of developing spermatids.","Nothing points to damage; a normal testis shows different stages from one tubule cut to the next."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermiogenesis and spermiation; testis organization and temperature","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-organelles","mh-spermiation","mh-tf-head","mh-lobules","mh-stages","mh-tf-temp","mh-case-temp"]}},{"id":"mh-num-spg","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"On Dr. Herr's slides, which statements about spermatogenesis are true?\n1. Spermiogenesis is the division in which diploid primary spermatocytes become haploid spermatids.\n2. One complete round of spermatogenesis takes about 74 days.\n3. Different tubule cross-sections show different mixes of cells because the stages vary along the tubule.\n4. Sperm leaving the testis are fully mature and ready to fertilize an ovum.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","2 and 3 only","3 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"About 74 days per round, all stages in the tubules but at different stages along their length. The division that makes haploid spermatids is meiosis; spermiogenesis remodels them afterward. Sperm finish maturing in the epididymis and are capacitated in the female tract.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: that division is meiosis; spermiogenesis is the later remodeling of spermatids into spermatozoa. It leaves out 3, which is true: spermatogenesis runs at different stages along the tubule, so each cut shows a different mix.","It includes 1, which is false: that division is meiosis; spermiogenesis is the later remodeling of spermatids into spermatozoa.","Statements 2 and 3 are true. Statement 1 is false: that division is meiosis; spermiogenesis is the later remodeling of spermatids into spermatozoa. Statement 4 is false: maturation is completed in the epididymis, and capacitation happens later still, in the female tract.","It leaves out 2, which is true: his slide estimates 74 days for spermatogenesis to be completed (Dr. Madison's physiology lecture says about two months)."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermatogenesis terms, spermatogonia types and meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-terms","mh-meiosis","mh-dark-a","mh-2s-3","mh-seq-cells","mh-num-spg","mh-tf-puberty"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-puberty","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: At puberty, spermatogonia enter the cell cycle under the influence of testosterone.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spermatogonia are small, basally located cells that enter the cell cycle at puberty under the influence of testosterone, made by the Leydig cells under LH.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["That is his spermatogonia slide: small basal cells that enter the cell cycle at puberty, under testosterone.","Nothing is changed: the time on his slide is puberty and the hormone is testosterone."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermatogenesis terms, spermatogonia types and meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-terms","mh-meiosis","mh-dark-a","mh-2s-3","mh-seq-cells","mh-num-spg","mh-tf-puberty"]}},{"id":"mh-dark-a","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A small cell on the basement membrane of a seminiferous tubule has an oval to flattened nucleus with a peripheral nucleolus and a nuclear vacuole. What is it?","choices":["Dark type A spermatogonium, the reserve stem cell","Pale type A spermatogonium, the progenitor cell","Type B spermatogonium, about to make primary spermatocytes","Sertoli cell, a support cell that no longer divides"],"answer":0,"ex":"Dark type A (reserve stem cells: nuclear vacuole, peripheral nucleolus) → more dark A and pale A. Pale type A (progenitors: no vacuole, central nucleolus, light euchromatic nucleus) → more pale A and type B. Type B → primary spermatocytes.","src":"slides","why":["Oval to flattened nucleus, peripheral nucleolus and a nuclear vacuole: his description of dark type A, the reserve stem cells.","Pale type A cells lack the nuclear vacuole and have a central nucleolus in a lighter, euchromatin-rich nucleus.","Type B cells are the ones that differentiate into primary spermatocytes; the vacuole marks dark type A.","Sertoli cells are tall columnar cells reaching the lumen with an ovoid nucleus, not small basal cells."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermatogenesis terms, spermatogonia types and meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-terms","mh-meiosis","mh-dark-a","mh-2s-3","mh-seq-cells","mh-num-spg","mh-tf-puberty"]}},{"id":"mh-2s-3","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Type B spermatogonia differentiate into primary spermatocytes, which move straight into the adluminal compartment.\n2. Pale type A spermatogonia are the reserve stem cells of the seminiferous tubule.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Statement 1 is his type B row. Statement 2 swaps the two type A cells: dark type A are the reserve stem cells; pale type A are the progenitors that make more pale A and type B.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: dark type A are the reserve stem cells; pale type A are the progenitor cells that make more pale A and type B.","Statement 1 is true, not false: type B cells become primary spermatocytes, which immediately move into the adluminal compartment.","Statement 1 is true: type B cells become primary spermatocytes, which immediately move into the adluminal compartment. Statement 2 is false: dark type A are the reserve stem cells; pale type A are the progenitor cells that make more pale A and type B.","Statement 1 is true, not false: type B cells become primary spermatocytes, which immediately move into the adluminal compartment. Statement 2 is false, not true: dark type A are the reserve stem cells; pale type A are the progenitor cells that make more pale A and type B."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermatogenesis terms, spermatogonia types and meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-terms","mh-meiosis","mh-dark-a","mh-2s-3","mh-seq-cells","mh-num-spg","mh-tf-puberty"]}},{"id":"mh-seq-cells","lec":"mhisto","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the germ cells in order, from the progenitor spermatogonium to the cell that undergoes spermiogenesis.","steps":["Pale type A spermatogonium","Type B spermatogonium","Primary spermatocyte","Secondary spermatocyte","Spermatid"],"ex":"Pale type A (progenitor) → type B → primary spermatocyte (moves into the adluminal compartment; diploid, enters meiosis) → secondary spermatocyte → haploid spermatid, which spermiogenesis turns into a spermatozoon. Dark type A cells sit upstream as the reserve.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermatogenesis terms, spermatogonia types and meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-terms","mh-meiosis","mh-dark-a","mh-2s-3","mh-seq-cells","mh-num-spg","mh-tf-puberty"]}},{"id":"mh-organelles","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"During spermiogenesis, the spermatid rearranges its organelles. Which pairing is right?","choices":["The Golgi apparatus forms the acrosome; the mitochondria gather in the middle piece","The mitochondria form the acrosome; the Golgi apparatus gathers in the middle piece","The Golgi apparatus forms the acrosome; the mitochondria are shed with the excess cytoplasm","The nucleus forms the acrosome; the mitochondria wrap the condensed nucleus in the head"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spermiogenesis: the nucleus elongates and condenses into the head, the Golgi forms the acrosome over it, the mitochondria line up in the middle piece, a flagellum forms, and much of the cytoplasm is discarded.","src":"slides","why":["The Golgi migrates to the top of the nucleus to make the acrosome, and the mitochondria localize in the middle piece.","That reverses them: the Golgi caps the nucleus as the acrosome, and the mitochondria go to the middle piece.","The Golgi half is right, but the mitochondria are kept and placed in the middle piece; what is shed is excess cytoplasm.","The nucleus condenses into the bulk of the head; the acrosome comes from the Golgi and the mitochondria sit in the middle piece."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermiogenesis and spermiation; testis organization and temperature","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-organelles","mh-spermiation","mh-tf-head","mh-lobules","mh-stages","mh-tf-temp","mh-case-temp"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-head","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: During spermiogenesis, the spermatid's nucleus elongates, condenses and is reshaped into the main bulk of the sperm head.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The nucleus becomes the head, the Golgi the acrosome on top of it, the mitochondria the middle piece; a flagellum forms and excess cytoplasm is discarded.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["That is his slide's line on the nucleus: it elongates, condenses and becomes the main bulk of the head.","Nothing is changed here; the nucleus really does become the bulk of the head, with the acrosome capping it."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermiogenesis and spermiation; testis organization and temperature","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-organelles","mh-spermiation","mh-tf-head","mh-lobules","mh-stages","mh-tf-temp","mh-case-temp"]}},{"id":"mh-spermiation","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"What is spermiation?","choices":["The release of sperm from the Sertoli cell; the excess cytoplasm stays behind","The transformation of spermatids into spermatozoa in the seminiferous tubule","The conditioning of sperm in the female tract that lets them fertilize an ovum","The 2 to 12 days in which sperm gain forward motility in the epididymis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spermiation is the last step in the tubule: the finished sperm lets go of the Sertoli cell and its excess cytoplasm stays behind. Don't mix it up with spermiogenesis (the remodeling before it) or capacitation (in the female tract).","src":"slides","why":["His slide: spermiation is the release of the sperm from the Sertoli cell, and the excess cytoplasm remains.","That is spermiogenesis, the remodeling that comes before release.","That is capacitation, which happens much later, after ejaculation.","That is epididymal maturation, after the sperm have left the testis."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermiogenesis and spermiation; testis organization and temperature","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-organelles","mh-spermiation","mh-tf-head","mh-lobules","mh-stages","mh-tf-temp","mh-case-temp"]}},{"id":"mh-seq-ducts","lec":"mhisto","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the path of sperm leaving the testis in order, starting where they are made.","steps":["Seminiferous tubule","Tubuli recti (straight tubules)","Rete testis, in the mediastinum","Efferent ductules","Duct of the epididymis"],"ex":"Tubuli recti lead out of the seminiferous tubules into the rete testis (a cuboidal-lined network in the mediastinum); 10 to 12 efferent ductules carry sperm on to the epididymis. His overview slide calls the straight ducts the rete testis; his duct slide separates them.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct path out of the testis and the vas deferens (vasectomy)","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-seq-ducts","mh-num-ducts","mh-tf-efferent","mh-ejac-duct","mh-case-vas-1","mh-case-vas-3"]}},{"id":"mh-efferent","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A duct's lining alternates between patches of non-ciliated simple cuboidal cells and patches of ciliated simple columnar cells. What does each cell type do?","choices":["The cuboidal cells absorb fluid from the tubules; the ciliated cells move sperm toward the epididymis","The cuboidal cells move the sperm toward the epididymis; the ciliated cells absorb fluid from the tubules","The cuboidal cells secrete glycerophosphocholine; the ciliated cells digest residual bodies","The cuboidal cells make androgen-binding protein; the ciliated cells release sperm at spermiation"],"answer":0,"ex":"Alternating short cuboidal and tall ciliated columnar patches = efferent ductules, the 10 to 12 ducts from the rete testis to the epididymis. Their cilia are true, motile cilia, unlike the stereocilia of the epididymis.","src":"slides","why":["These are the efferent ductules: the non-ciliated cells absorb fluid from the seminiferous tubules and the ciliated cells sweep sperm toward the epididymis.","That swaps the jobs: the cilia do the moving, and the non-ciliated cuboidal cells do the absorbing.","Those are jobs of the epididymis's columnar cells, further down the duct system.","ABP and spermiation belong to Sertoli cells, inside the seminiferous tubules."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct linings: rete testis, efferent ductules (cilia), epididymis and vas (stereocilia)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-efferent","mh-cilia-stereo","mh-epid-id","mh-x-pseudo","mh-2s-2","mh-2s-5","mh-case-vas-2"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-efferent","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: About 10 to 12 efferent ductules carry sperm from the rete testis to the epididymis.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Rete testis → 10 to 12 efferent ductules → epididymis. Their ciliated columnar cells sweep the spermatozoa toward the epididymis.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His intratesticular duct table gives 10 to 12 ductuli efferentes, running from the rete testis to the epididymis.","Nothing is changed: the count (10 to 12) and both ends (rete testis, epididymis) match his table."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct path out of the testis and the vas deferens (vasectomy)","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-seq-ducts","mh-num-ducts","mh-tf-efferent","mh-ejac-duct","mh-case-vas-1","mh-case-vas-3"]}},{"id":"mh-num-ducts","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the intratesticular ducts are true?\n1. Tubuli recti (straight tubules) lead out of the seminiferous tubules.\n2. The rete testis lies in the mediastinum and is lined by cuboidal epithelium.\n3. The efferent ductules run from the rete testis to the epididymis.\n4. The ciliated cells of the efferent ductules absorb fluid coming from the seminiferous tubules.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":1,"ex":"Seminiferous tubules → tubuli recti → rete testis (cuboidal, in the mediastinum) → efferent ductules (non-ciliated cuboidal cells absorb fluid; ciliated columnar cells move sperm) → epididymis.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 3, which is true: the 10 to 12 efferent ductules connect the rete testis to the epididymis.","Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: the non-ciliated cuboidal cells absorb the fluid; the ciliated cells move sperm toward the epididymis.","It leaves out 2, which is true: it is a network of sinusoid-like channels in the mediastinum, lined by cuboidal epithelium.","It includes 4, which is false: the non-ciliated cuboidal cells absorb the fluid; the ciliated cells move sperm toward the epididymis. It leaves out 1, which is true: they are the ducts that lead out of the seminiferous tubules, into the rete testis."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct path out of the testis and the vas deferens (vasectomy)","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-seq-ducts","mh-num-ducts","mh-tf-efferent","mh-ejac-duct","mh-case-vas-1","mh-case-vas-3"]}},{"id":"mh-x-pseudo","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"According to Dr. Herr's slides, each of these is lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium EXCEPT:","choices":["Rete testis","Duct of the epididymis","Vas deferens","Seminal vesicle"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pseudostratified columnar: epididymis and vas deferens (both with stereocilia) and the seminal vesicle. Cuboidal: rete testis. Alternating cuboidal and ciliated columnar: efferent ductules. Simple or pseudostratified columnar: prostate.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["The rete testis is a network of channels in the mediastinum lined by cuboidal epithelium.","The epididymis is pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia, plus basal cells.","The vas is pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia, under a thick muscular wall.","The seminal vesicle's lumen is lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct linings: rete testis, efferent ductules (cilia), epididymis and vas (stereocilia)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-efferent","mh-cilia-stereo","mh-epid-id","mh-x-pseudo","mh-2s-2","mh-2s-5","mh-case-vas-2"]}},{"id":"mh-2s-2","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The rete testis is a network of channels lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium.\n2. The duct of the epididymis is about 15 cm long.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Both are false. The rete testis is lined by cuboidal epithelium, and the epididymis is a highly coiled duct about 4 to 6 m long; 15 cm is the length of the seminal vesicles.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the rete testis is lined by cuboidal epithelium; pseudostratified columnar is the lining of the epididymis and vas deferens. Statement 2 is false, not true: the epididymis is highly coiled and about 4 to 6 m long; about 15 cm is the length of the seminal vesicles.","Statement 1 is false: the rete testis is lined by cuboidal epithelium; pseudostratified columnar is the lining of the epididymis and vas deferens. Statement 2 is false: the epididymis is highly coiled and about 4 to 6 m long; about 15 cm is the length of the seminal vesicles.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the rete testis is lined by cuboidal epithelium; pseudostratified columnar is the lining of the epididymis and vas deferens.","Statement 2 is false, not true: the epididymis is highly coiled and about 4 to 6 m long; about 15 cm is the length of the seminal vesicles."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct linings: rete testis, efferent ductules (cilia), epididymis and vas (stereocilia)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-efferent","mh-cilia-stereo","mh-epid-id","mh-x-pseudo","mh-2s-2","mh-2s-5","mh-case-vas-2"]}},{"id":"mh-epid-id","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A section shows one duct cut many times. It is lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium with long stereocilia, and its lumen is packed with sperm. Which duct is it?","choices":["Duct of the epididymis","Ductus (vas) deferens","Ductuli efferentes (efferent ductules)","Seminal vesicle"],"answer":0,"ex":"Coiled duct + pseudostratified columnar + stereocilia + sperm in the lumen = epididymis. Same lining but straight with thick muscle = vas deferens.","src":"slides","why":["Highly coiled (about 4 to 6 m), so one section catches it many times; pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia; it stores sperm.","The vas has the same lining but is a straight tube with a thick muscular wall, so you would see one lumen, not many coils.","The efferent ductules alternate non-ciliated cuboidal and ciliated columnar cells; they have motile cilia, not stereocilia.","The seminal vesicle is pseudostratified columnar too, but it is a gland with yellow lipochrome pigment that adds fluid, not a duct that stores sperm."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct linings: rete testis, efferent ductules (cilia), epididymis and vas (stereocilia)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-efferent","mh-cilia-stereo","mh-epid-id","mh-x-pseudo","mh-2s-2","mh-2s-5","mh-case-vas-2"]}},{"id":"mh-cilia-stereo","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement correctly contrasts the apical surfaces of the efferent ductules and the epididymis?","choices":["Efferent ductules: motile cilia that sweep sperm. Epididymis: stereocilia, nonmotile microvilli","Efferent ductules: stereocilia that absorb fluid. Epididymis: motile cilia that sweep sperm along","Both have motile cilia, but the epididymis's cilia are longer and beat toward the vas deferens","Both have stereocilia only; sperm are swept along by the cilia of the rete testis instead"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cilia (motile) = efferent ductules. Stereocilia (long nonmotile microvilli that absorb) = epididymis and vas deferens. In the epididymis, sperm are moved by peristalsis toward the storage region in the tail.","src":"slides","why":["True cilia move sperm in the efferent ductules; the epididymis's stereocilia are really long microvilli that do not move.","That swaps them: the efferent ductules are the ciliated ones, and the stereocilia belong to the epididymis (and vas deferens).","The epididymis has no motile cilia; its stereocilia are nonmotile, and peristalsis moves its sperm.","The rete testis is lined by cuboidal epithelium; the motile cilia in this system are in the efferent ductules."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct linings: rete testis, efferent ductules (cilia), epididymis and vas (stereocilia)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-efferent","mh-cilia-stereo","mh-epid-id","mh-x-pseudo","mh-2s-2","mh-2s-5","mh-case-vas-2"]}},{"id":"mh-2s-5","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The columnar cells of the epididymis absorb fluid from the lumen.\n2. In the epididymis, the columnar cells are the precursors of the basal cells.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Statement 1 is true: the columnar cells, with stereocilia on their apical surface, absorb fluid from the lumen. Statement 2 is false: it is the other way round; the basal cells are the precursors of the columnar cells.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: it is the other way round, since the basal cells are the precursors of the columnar cells.","Statement 1 is true, not false: his slide gives them stereocilia on the apical surface and says they absorb fluid from the lumen.","Statement 1 is true: his slide gives them stereocilia on the apical surface and says they absorb fluid from the lumen. Statement 2 is false: it is the other way round, since the basal cells are the precursors of the columnar cells.","Statement 1 is true, not false: his slide gives them stereocilia on the apical surface and says they absorb fluid from the lumen. Statement 2 is false, not true: it is the other way round, since the basal cells are the precursors of the columnar cells."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct linings: rete testis, efferent ductules (cilia), epididymis and vas (stereocilia)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-efferent","mh-cilia-stereo","mh-epid-id","mh-x-pseudo","mh-2s-2","mh-2s-5","mh-case-vas-2"]}},{"id":"mh-x-epid","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of these is one of the epididymis's main functions EXCEPT:","choices":["Capacitating sperm so they can fertilize an ovum","Maturing sperm over 2 to 12 days","Storing sperm until ejaculation","Moving sperm by peristalsis to the storage region in the tail"],"answer":0,"ex":"The epididymis's three jobs: maturation (2 to 12 days), storage until ejaculation, and transport by peristalsis to the storage region in the tail. Capacitation is not one of them.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Capacitation happens in the female tract, and the epididymis's glycerophosphocholine inhibits it.","Maturation (chromatin, membrane charge, new surface proteins, forward motility) is one of its three functions.","Storage until ejaculation is another of its three functions.","Transport by peristalsis to the tail is its third function."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"The epididymis: three jobs, sperm maturation and capacitation","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-epid","mh-num-epid","mh-gpc","mh-motility","mh-tf-epid74"]}},{"id":"mh-num-epid","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about sperm maturing in the epididymis are true?\n1. Sperm chromatin is stabilized in the epididymis.\n2. The acrosome forms from the Golgi apparatus while sperm mature in the epididymis.\n3. Sperm acquire new surface proteins in the epididymis.\n4. Sperm acquire forward motility in the epididymis.","choices":["1, 3 and 4","1 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4","3 and 4 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Epididymal maturation (2 to 12 days): chromatin stabilized, plasma membrane surface charge changed, new surface proteins, forward motility. The acrosome was already made during spermiogenesis in the tubule.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: the acrosome forms earlier, during spermiogenesis in the seminiferous tubule.","It leaves out 3, which is true: new surface proteins are one of the maturation changes, along with a change in membrane surface charge.","It includes 2, which is false: the acrosome forms earlier, during spermiogenesis in the seminiferous tubule. It leaves out 1, which is true: stabilization of chromatin is the first maturation change on his list.","It leaves out 1, which is true: stabilization of chromatin is the first maturation change on his list."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"The epididymis: three jobs, sperm maturation and capacitation","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-epid","mh-num-epid","mh-gpc","mh-motility","mh-tf-epid74"]}},{"id":"mh-gpc","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Columnar cells of the epididymis secrete glycerophosphocholine. What does it do?","choices":["It inhibits capacitation, so sperm can't yet be conditioned to fertilize","It is the main energy source for sperm, stored with them in the tail of the epididymis","It breaks down the proteins of the semen so the ejaculate liquefies after ejaculation","It lubricates the urethra just before the emission of semen, as a slippery mucus"],"answer":0,"ex":"Capacitation happens in the female tract. While sperm wait in the epididymis, its columnar cells secrete glycerophosphocholine, which holds capacitation back; the same cells absorb fluid through their stereocilia.","src":"slides","why":["Glycerophosphocholine inhibits capacitation, which is meant to happen later, in the female tract.","The main energy source for sperm is fructose from the seminal vesicles.","Liquefying semen is the prostate's job, with its proteolytic enzymes such as fibrinolysin.","Lubrication before emission is the bulbourethral glands' mucus."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"The epididymis: three jobs, sperm maturation and capacitation","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-epid","mh-num-epid","mh-gpc","mh-motility","mh-tf-epid74"]}},{"id":"mh-motility","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A researcher collects sperm from the efferent ductules and from the tail of the epididymis. Which difference best fits Dr. Herr's slides?","choices":["Sperm from the epididymis have gained forward motility; sperm from the efferent ductules have not","Sperm from the efferent ductules have gained forward motility; sperm from the epididymis lose it in storage","Sperm from both sites are already capacitated and ready to fertilize an ovum","Sperm from the efferent ductules still lack an acrosome, which forms in the epididymis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sperm leave the testis unfinished. In the epididymis (2 to 12 days) their chromatin stabilizes, their membrane surface charge changes, and they gain new surface proteins and forward motility. In the efferent ductules they are carried by cilia. Capacitation waits for the female tract.","src":"slides","why":["Forward motility is one of the changes sperm acquire during their 2 to 12 days of maturation in the epididymis, after the efferent ductules.","The efferent ductules come before the epididymis, and motility is gained in the epididymis; storage there does not take it away.","Capacitation happens only in the female tract, and the epididymis actively inhibits it.","The acrosome forms from the Golgi during spermiogenesis, inside the seminiferous tubule, before sperm leave the testis."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"The epididymis: three jobs, sperm maturation and capacitation","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-epid","mh-num-epid","mh-gpc","mh-motility","mh-tf-epid74"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-epid74","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: On Dr. Herr's slides, sperm spend about 74 days maturing in the epididymis.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"74 days is spermatogenesis, in the testis. Maturation in the epididymis takes 2 to 12 days.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The 74 days on his slide is spermatogenesis; epididymal maturation takes 2 to 12 days.","Epididymal maturation takes 2 to 12 days; about 74 days is how long spermatogenesis takes in the tubules."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"The epididymis: three jobs, sperm maturation and capacitation","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-epid","mh-num-epid","mh-gpc","mh-motility","mh-tf-epid74"]}},{"id":"mh-ejac-duct","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which part of the excretory duct system collects the fluid of the seminal vesicles on its way to the prostatic urethra?","choices":["Ejaculatory duct","Vas deferens","Duct of the epididymis","Penile urethra"],"answer":0,"ex":"Excretory path: epididymis → vas deferens (straight, thick muscular wall) → ejaculatory duct (collects the seminal vesicle fluid; runs through the prostate) → prostatic urethra → penile urethra (bulbourethral glands empty here).","src":"slides","why":["His slide says the ejaculatory duct collects fluids from the glands (seminal vesicles and prostate); his prostate figure shows it passing through the prostate to the urethra.","The vas brings sperm from the epididymis and leads on to the ejaculatory duct; it does not collect the seminal vesicle fluid itself.","The epididymis is upstream, at the testis; it matures and stores sperm.","The penile urethra is downstream and is where the bulbourethral glands empty."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct path out of the testis and the vas deferens (vasectomy)","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-seq-ducts","mh-num-ducts","mh-tf-efferent","mh-ejac-duct","mh-case-vas-1","mh-case-vas-3"]}},{"id":"mh-case-vas-1","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"His semen volume is still close to the average ejaculate of about 3 mL. What best explains this?","choices":["About 70% of semen comes from the seminal vesicles, which join the tract downstream of the cut","The cut vas deferens heals open within weeks, so the testis is still adding most of the fluid volume","The epididymis now secretes extra fluid to replace the sperm that can no longer pass","The bulbourethral glands supply most of the semen and empty into the penile urethra"],"answer":0,"ex":"A vasectomy cuts the vas deferens in the spermatic cord. Everything that joins downstream (the seminal vesicles through the ejaculatory duct, the prostate, the bulbourethral glands) still empties, so the volume and the fructose stay; only the sperm from the testis are gone.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","38-year-old man, 12 weeks after a bilateral vasectomy"],["Semen volume","2.9 mL"],["Sperm","None seen"],["Fructose","Present"]],"pcase":"mhisto-case1","why":["The seminal vesicles make about 70% of the volume and drain into the ejaculatory duct, beyond the cut vas deferens, so only the sperm are missing.","His sperm count is zero, so nothing is coming through the vas, and the testis is not the main source of volume anyway.","The epididymis absorbs fluid rather than supplying the semen's volume, and it is upstream of the cut.","The bulbourethral glands do empty into the penile urethra, but their mucus is a small lubricating share, not most of the semen."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct path out of the testis and the vas deferens (vasectomy)","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-seq-ducts","mh-num-ducts","mh-tf-efferent","mh-ejac-duct","mh-case-vas-1","mh-case-vas-3"]}},{"id":"mh-case-vas-2","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"The surgeon sent the two removed segments to pathology to confirm they were vas deferens. Which description should the pathologist see?","choices":["A straight tube with a thick muscular wall, lined by pseudostratified columnar cells with stereocilia","A highly coiled duct, cut many times in one section, lined by pseudostratified columnar cells with stereocilia","A duct whose lining alternates between ciliated simple columnar and non-ciliated simple cuboidal cells","Coiled tubular glands with a fibroelastic lamina propria and yellow lipochrome pigment in their cells"],"answer":0,"ex":"Epididymis and vas deferens share a pseudostratified columnar lining with stereocilia. Tell them apart by shape and wall: coiled and cut many times (epididymis) versus a straight tube with a thick muscular wall (vas).","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","38-year-old man, 12 weeks after a bilateral vasectomy"],["Semen volume","2.9 mL"],["Sperm","None seen"],["Fructose","Present"]],"pcase":"mhisto-case1","why":["That is his vas deferens: straight, thick muscular wall, and a pseudostratified columnar lining with stereocilia.","Same lining, but highly coiled and cut many times is the epididymis, not the vas.","Alternating ciliated columnar and cuboidal patches are the efferent ductules, inside the testis.","Lipochrome pigment and a fibroelastic lamina propria describe the seminal vesicle."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct linings: rete testis, efferent ductules (cilia), epididymis and vas (stereocilia)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-efferent","mh-cilia-stereo","mh-epid-id","mh-x-pseudo","mh-2s-2","mh-2s-5","mh-case-vas-2"]}},{"id":"mh-case-vas-3","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"To cut only the vas deferens, the surgeon separates it from the other contents of the spermatic cord. Which structures, shown beside the vas in the cord on Dr. Herr's slide, are left intact?","choices":["The testicular artery and the pampiniform plexus","The ejaculatory ducts and the prostatic utricle","The rete testis and the 10 to 12 efferent ductules","The helicine arteries and the deep (efferent) veins"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spermatic cord on his slide: vas deferens + testicular artery + pampiniform plexus. That is where the vas is reached for a vasectomy.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","38-year-old man, 12 weeks after a bilateral vasectomy"],["Semen volume","2.9 mL"],["Sperm","None seen"],["Fructose","Present"]],"pcase":"mhisto-case1","why":["Dr. Herr's spermatic cord slide labels the vas deferens, the testicular artery and the pampiniform plexus, so those two run with it.","Those are labels on his prostate figure, where the ejaculatory ducts pass through the gland.","Those are intratesticular ducts, inside the testis, upstream of the epididymis.","Those are the vessels of the penis's erectile tissue."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Duct path out of the testis and the vas deferens (vasectomy)","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-seq-ducts","mh-num-ducts","mh-tf-efferent","mh-ejac-duct","mh-case-vas-1","mh-case-vas-3"]}},{"id":"mh-case-t-1","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains his low LH?","choices":["Testosterone lowers LH secretion by negative feedback","Inhibin from his Sertoli cells is blocking the release of LH","Androgen-binding protein carries testosterone to the pituitary to block LH","His Leydig cells are making anti-Müllerian hormone, which blocks LH"],"answer":0,"ex":"Testosterone lowers LH secretion by negative feedback, so testosterone from outside switches LH off. Inhibin is the brake on FSH, not LH.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","29-year-old man at a dental visit; his health history lists testosterone injections he takes for bodybuilding (not prescribed)"],["Fertility work-up","Low sperm count"],["LH","Low"],["Blood testosterone","High"]],"pcase":"mhisto-case2","why":["Release of LH is inhibited by a feedback loop in which testosterone lowers LH secretion, and he is injecting a lot of testosterone.","Inhibin blocks FSH release, not LH.","ABP works inside the seminiferous tubules, keeping testosterone concentrated there; it does not act on the pituitary.","AMH is a Sertoli cell product that blocks the Müllerian duct in the embryo; Leydig cells make testosterone."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"LH vs FSH: Leydig and testosterone vs Sertoli, ABP and inhibin","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-lh","mh-abp","mh-2s-1","mh-mm-gonadotropin","mh-case-t-1","mh-case-t-2"]}},{"id":"mh-case-t-2","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"With his LH suppressed, which testicular cells get less stimulation, and why does that matter for his sperm count?","choices":["Leydig cells, which carry LH receptors and make testosterone for sperm development","Sertoli cells, which carry LH receptors and make androgen-binding protein for the tubules","Peritubular myoid cells, which carry LH receptors and secrete testosterone into the tubules","Dark type A spermatogonia, which carry LH receptors and divide only while LH is high"],"answer":0,"ex":"LH → Leydig cells → testosterone → sperm development. His blood testosterone is high, but sperm development depends on the testosterone his own Leydig cells make inside the testis, which androgen-binding protein holds at a high concentration in the tubules. Injected testosterone lowers LH by feedback, so those cells lose their stimulus.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","29-year-old man at a dental visit; his health history lists testosterone injections he takes for bodybuilding (not prescribed)"],["Fertility work-up","Low sperm count"],["LH","Low"],["Blood testosterone","High"]],"pcase":"mhisto-case2","why":["LH binds Leydig cell receptors to start testosterone production, and that testosterone stimulates sperm development; with LH low, the Leydig cells are no longer driven.","Sertoli cells respond to FSH, not LH; ABP is their FSH-driven product.","Myoid cells sit in the tubule wall and do not make testosterone; the Leydig cells do.","Spermatogonia have no LH receptors on his slides; testosterone brings them into the cell cycle at puberty."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"LH vs FSH: Leydig and testosterone vs Sertoli, ABP and inhibin","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-lh","mh-abp","mh-2s-1","mh-mm-gonadotropin","mh-case-t-1","mh-case-t-2"]}},{"id":"mh-case-temp","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains the poor sperm production in his right testis?","choices":["It sits at body temperature, too warm for normal spermatogenesis","Its Sertoli cells die at body temperature, so the germ cells lose their support","Its Leydig cells need FSH, which cannot reach a testis in the abdomen","It has no spermatogonia, since they enter the cell cycle only once the testis descends"],"answer":0,"ex":"The testes descend into the scrotum during fetal development because normal spermatogenesis needs a temperature a few degrees below the body's 37°C (about 35°C on Dr. Herr's slide). A testis left in the abdomen is too warm for normal spermatogenesis, while its heat-resistant Sertoli cells survive.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","24-year-old man"],["Exam","Right testis never descended and lies in the abdomen; left testis is in the scrotum"],["Right testis biopsy","Few developing germ cells; Sertoli cells present"]],"why":["The testes descend into the scrotum because spermatogenesis needs a lower temperature (about 35°C on Dr. Herr's slide, 34°C in Dr. Madison's lecture); an abdominal testis is at body temperature, 37°C.","Sertoli cells are resistant to heat, and the biopsy shows they are still there.","Leydig cells respond to LH, not FSH, and hormones reach the testis through the blood wherever it lies.","Spermatogonia enter the cell cycle at puberty under testosterone; descent is a fetal event, and the problem here is temperature."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Spermiogenesis and spermiation; testis organization and temperature","basis":"slides","ids":["mh-organelles","mh-spermiation","mh-tf-head","mh-lobules","mh-stages","mh-tf-temp","mh-case-temp"]}},{"id":"mh-case-rad","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which cells line his tubules, and why did they survive the treatment?","choices":["Sertoli cells, which do not divide in the mature testis and resist radiation and toxic agents","Leydig cells, which line the tubules and are described as resistant to heat and radiation","Primary spermatocytes, which the blood-testis barrier shielded from the radiation and drugs","Dark type A spermatogonia, reserve stem cells that stretch from the basement membrane to the lumen"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sertoli cells do not divide in the mature testis and are resistant to heat, ionizing radiation and various toxic agents, so after treatment they can be nearly all that is left lining the tubules.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","45-year-old man seen for infertility; treated with chemotherapy and pelvic radiation for lymphoma at age 30"],["Semen analysis","No sperm"],["Testicular biopsy","Tubules lined almost only by tall columnar cells that reach from the basement membrane to the lumen, with ovoid nuclei; almost no germ cells"]],"why":["Tall columnar cells from basement membrane to lumen with ovoid nuclei are Sertoli cells, and his slide says they don't divide and resist heat, ionizing radiation and toxic agents.","Leydig cells sit outside the tubules, in the interstitium; the resistance on his slide belongs to the Sertoli cell.","The barrier protects gametes from the immune system, not from radiation, and the biopsy shows almost no germ cells.","Spermatogonia are small cells at the base of the tubule; only Sertoli cells reach the lumen."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cells: shape, the blood-testis barrier and its two compartments","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sertoli-id","mh-btb","mh-btb-why","mh-mm-adluminal","mh-num-sertoli","mh-case-rad","mh-tf-sertoli-divide"]}},{"id":"mh-match-numbers","lec":"mhisto","type":"match","stem":"Match each item to its number on Dr. Herr's slides.","pairs":[["One full round of spermatogenesis","About 74 days"],["Sperm maturation in the epididymis","2 to 12 days"],["Sperm formed each day","About 200 million"],["Seminal vesicles' share of semen volume","About 70%"],["Sperm in an ejaculate that are abnormal or immotile","About 25%"]],"ex":"74 days in the tubules, then 2 to 12 days in the epididymis; about 200 million sperm a day; the seminal vesicles give about 70% of semen volume; about 25% of ejaculated sperm are abnormal and/or immotile.","src":"slides"},{"id":"mh-sv-fructose","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which gland supplies most of the semen's volume along with the sperm's main energy source?","choices":["Seminal vesicles: about 70% of the volume, rich in fructose","Prostate: about 70% of the volume, rich in citric acid","Bulbourethral glands: about 70% of the volume, rich in galactose","Seminal vesicles: about 25% of the volume, rich in sialic acid"],"answer":0,"ex":"Seminal vesicles: about 70% of the volume; fructose (energy), prostaglandins, proteins and the yellow lipochrome pigment. Prostate: citric acid, acid phosphatase, fibrinolysin, PSA. Bulbourethral glands: galactose and sialic acid mucus.","src":"slides","why":["Seminal vesicle secretion makes up about 70% of semen volume, and its fructose is the main energy source for sperm.","The prostate is the main source of citric acid and acid phosphatase, but it is not most of the volume.","Bulbourethral mucus (galactose, sialic acid) lubricates before emission; it is a small share.","Right gland, wrong details: it is 70% of the volume, and sialic acid belongs to the bulbourethral glands."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Accessory glands: what each looks like and what each adds to semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sv-fructose","mh-sv-id","mh-bu-id","mh-bu-timing","mh-x-prostate","mh-num-glands","mh-2s-4","mh-case-stain"]}},{"id":"mh-sv-id","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Coiled tubular glands about 15 cm long are lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium over a fibroelastic lamina propria, with inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle. Which gland is it?","choices":["Seminal vesicle","Prostate gland","Bulbourethral gland","Duct of the epididymis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Seminal vesicle on a slide: coiled glands, pseudostratified columnar lining, fibroelastic lamina propria, two smooth muscle layers (inner circular, outer longitudinal) and yellow lipochrome pigment, which fluoresces under UV light.","src":"slides","why":["Every feature is on his seminal vesicle slide: coiled tubular glands about 15 cm long, pseudostratified columnar lining, fibroelastic lamina propria, inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle.","The prostate is 30 to 50 compound tubuloalveolar glands in a fibromuscular stroma, often with concretions in the lumens.","The bulbourethral gland makes mucus and has skeletal muscle between its secretory units.","The epididymis is a single coiled duct, 4 to 6 m long, with stereocilia, that stores sperm; it is not a gland with two smooth muscle layers."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Accessory glands: what each looks like and what each adds to semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sv-fructose","mh-sv-id","mh-bu-id","mh-bu-timing","mh-x-prostate","mh-num-glands","mh-2s-4","mh-case-stain"]}},{"id":"mh-bu-id","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A gland's mucus-secreting units are separated by bundles of skeletal muscle, and its duct empties into the penile urethra. Which gland is it?","choices":["Bulbourethral gland","Prostate gland","Seminal vesicle","Vas deferens"],"answer":0,"ex":"Muscle type tells them apart: skeletal muscle between secretory units = bulbourethral (Cowper's) gland; smooth muscle in a fibromuscular stroma = prostate; inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle = seminal vesicle.","src":"slides","why":["Skeletal muscle between the secretory units and a thick, slippery mucus emptied into the penile urethra are his bulbourethral (Cowper's) gland.","The prostate's stroma is fibromuscular, with smooth muscle, and it opens into the prostatic urethra.","The seminal vesicle has smooth muscle (inner circular, outer longitudinal), and its fluid is collected by the ejaculatory duct.","The vas deferens is a duct with a thick smooth muscle wall and a stereociliated lining, not a mucous gland."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Accessory glands: what each looks like and what each adds to semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sv-fructose","mh-sv-id","mh-bu-id","mh-bu-timing","mh-x-prostate","mh-num-glands","mh-2s-4","mh-case-stain"]}},{"id":"mh-bu-timing","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which secretion enters the urethra before the emission of semen and serves mainly to lubricate?","choices":["Bulbourethral mucus, rich in galactose and sialic acid","Prostatic fluid, rich in citric acid and acid phosphatase","Seminal vesicle fluid, rich in fructose and prostaglandins","Epididymal fluid, rich in glycerophosphocholine"],"answer":0,"ex":"Bulbourethral (Cowper's) mucus comes first, lubricating ahead of emission. Then comes the ejaculate, whose first portion is prostatic fluid, which reduces urethral acidity and helps dilute the semen.","src":"slides","why":["Bulbourethral secretion has a lubricating function and precedes the emission of semen.","Prostatic fluid is the first portion of the ejaculate itself, not a secretion that comes before emission.","Seminal vesicle fluid is the bulk of the semen (about 70%), part of the ejaculate.","Glycerophosphocholine inhibits capacitation in the epididymis; it does not lubricate the urethra."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Accessory glands: what each looks like and what each adds to semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sv-fructose","mh-sv-id","mh-bu-id","mh-bu-timing","mh-x-prostate","mh-num-glands","mh-2s-4","mh-case-stain"]}},{"id":"mh-2s-4","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Smooth muscle is interspersed between the secretory units of the bulbourethral gland.\n2. Seminal vesicle secretion is rich in fructose, the main energy source for sperm.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Statement 1 is false: the muscle between the bulbourethral gland's secretory units is skeletal muscle (smooth muscle in a fibromuscular stroma is the prostate). Statement 2 is true: the seminal vesicles' fructose is the sperm's main energy source.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the muscle between the bulbourethral gland's secretory units is skeletal muscle; the prostate is the gland with a fibromuscular stroma.","Statement 2 is true, not false: fructose from the seminal vesicles is the main energy source for sperm.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the muscle between the bulbourethral gland's secretory units is skeletal muscle; the prostate is the gland with a fibromuscular stroma. Statement 2 is true, not false: fructose from the seminal vesicles is the main energy source for sperm.","Statement 1 is false: the muscle between the bulbourethral gland's secretory units is skeletal muscle; the prostate is the gland with a fibromuscular stroma. Statement 2 is true: fructose from the seminal vesicles is the main energy source for sperm."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Accessory glands: what each looks like and what each adds to semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sv-fructose","mh-sv-id","mh-bu-id","mh-bu-timing","mh-x-prostate","mh-num-glands","mh-2s-4","mh-case-stain"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-prostate-first","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Prostatic fluid is the first portion of the ejaculate; it reduces the acidity of the urethra and helps dilute the semen.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prostatic fluid: the first portion of the ejaculate, reduces urethral acidity, helps dilute (liquefy) semen and provides a medium for sperm.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His prostate slide lists exactly these: first portion of the ejaculate, reduces the urethra's acidity, helps dilute semen.","Nothing is changed; the lubricating secretion that comes before the ejaculate is the bulbourethral glands', but the first portion of the ejaculate is prostatic."]},{"id":"mh-tf-prostate-sets","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The prostate's 30 to 50 compound tubuloalveolar glands are arranged in three sets: mucosal, submucosal and main.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prostate: 30 to 50 compound tubuloalveolar glands in three sets (mucosal, submucosal, main), simple or pseudostratified columnar epithelium, fibromuscular stroma.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His prostate slide gives 30 to 50 compound tubuloalveolar glands in three sets: mucosal, submucosal and main.","Nothing is changed: 30 to 50 glands, compound tubuloalveolar, in mucosal, submucosal and main sets."]},{"id":"mh-x-prostate","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of these is a product of the prostate EXCEPT:","choices":["Fructose","Citric acid","Fibrinolysin","Prostate-specific antigen"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prostate: citric acid and acid phosphatase (main source), fibrinolysin and other proteolytic enzymes, prostaglandins, amylase and PSA. Fructose is the seminal vesicles'.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Fructose comes from the seminal vesicles and is the sperm's main energy source.","The prostate is the main source of citric acid in semen.","Fibrinolysin is the proteinase his slide names among the prostate's products.","PSA is a prostatic product and a clinically important tumor marker."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Accessory glands: what each looks like and what each adds to semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sv-fructose","mh-sv-id","mh-bu-id","mh-bu-timing","mh-x-prostate","mh-num-glands","mh-2s-4","mh-case-stain"]}},{"id":"mh-num-glands","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"On Dr. Herr's slides, which statements about the accessory glands are true?\n1. The prostate is the main source of citric acid and acid phosphatase in semen.\n2. Prostate cells contain a yellowish lipochrome pigment that fluoresces under UV light.\n3. The bulbourethral glands empty their secretion into the prostatic urethra.\n4. The prostate makes prostate-specific antigen, a clinically important tumor marker.","choices":["1 only","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","1 and 4 only"],"answer":3,"ex":"Prostate: citric acid, acid phosphatase, PSA. Lipochrome pigment: seminal vesicles. Bulbourethral glands: empty into the penile urethra.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: PSA comes from the prostate and is a clinically important tumor marker.","It includes 2, which is false: the lipochrome pigment is in the seminal vesicle cells, and it is what helps identify semen stains.","It includes 3, which is false: they empty into the penile urethra.","Statements 1 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: the lipochrome pigment is in the seminal vesicle cells, and it is what helps identify semen stains. Statement 3 is false: they empty into the penile urethra."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Accessory glands: what each looks like and what each adds to semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sv-fructose","mh-sv-id","mh-bu-id","mh-bu-timing","mh-x-prostate","mh-num-glands","mh-2s-4","mh-case-stain"]}},{"id":"mh-match-secretions","lec":"mhisto","type":"match","stem":"Match each source to the product Dr. Herr's slides give it.","pairs":[["Seminal vesicles","Fructose, the main energy source for sperm"],["Prostate","Citric acid, acid phosphatase and PSA"],["Bulbourethral glands","Galactose and sialic acid in a slippery mucus"],["Epididymis","Glycerophosphocholine, which inhibits capacitation"],["Sertoli cells","Androgen-binding protein and inhibin"]],"ex":"Seminal vesicles: fructose (plus prostaglandins, proteins, lipochrome). Prostate: citric acid, acid phosphatase, fibrinolysin, PSA. Bulbourethral glands: galactose and sialic acid mucus. Epididymis: glycerophosphocholine. Sertoli cells: ABP, inhibin and AMH.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Sertoli cell functions (ABP, AMH, inhibin, phagocytosis) and Leydig cells","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-x-sertoli","mh-amh","mh-tf-leydig","mh-wall","mh-match-secretions"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-pg","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Prostaglandins in semen influence sperm transit only within the male reproductive ducts.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Semen's prostaglandins (from the seminal vesicles and prostate) influence sperm transit in both the male and the female reproductive ducts.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His semen slide says the prostaglandins influence sperm transit in both the male and the female reproductive ducts.","They act in the female reproductive ducts too, not only the male ones."]},{"id":"mh-case-psa-1","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which feature of the gland cells that make PSA fits their job?","choices":["Abundant RER, Golgi and secretory granules for packaging and secreting proteins","Abundant smooth ER and lipid droplets, the machinery for making steroid hormones","Long nonmotile stereocilia on the apical surface for absorbing fluid from the lumen","A yellowish lipochrome pigment that fluoresces when the cells are placed under UV light"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prostate epithelium (simple or pseudostratified columnar, in a fibromuscular stroma) is built to secrete protein: RER, Golgi, secretory granules. Its products include PSA, a clinically important tumor marker.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","67-year-old man at a dental recall visit"],["Recent blood test","PSA raised (8.1 ng/mL)"],["Prostate biopsy","Glands lined by simple to pseudostratified columnar epithelium in a fibromuscular stroma; calcified bodies in many gland lumens"]],"pcase":"mhisto-case3","why":["His prostate slide lists RER, Golgi and secretory granules, the organelles of a protein-secreting cell, and PSA is a protein.","Smooth ER and lipid are the kit of a steroid-making cell; prostate cells package and secrete proteins and enzymes.","Stereocilia belong to the epididymis and vas deferens.","Lipochrome pigment marks the seminal vesicle."]},{"id":"mh-case-psa-2","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"What are the calcified bodies in his gland lumens, and what do they tell you?","choices":["Corpora amylacea: calcified glycoproteins that increase with age, of unknown significance","Corpora amylacea: calcified PSA deposits that prove the raised PSA comes from cancer","Clumps of yellow lipochrome pigment carried into the prostate gland in seminal vesicle fluid","Residual bodies of cytoplasm shed during spermiogenesis, carried down from the testis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Prostatic concretions (corpora amylacea): calcified glycoproteins in the gland lumens that increase with age; their significance is unknown. On his slide they are an age-related finding, not a diagnosis.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","67-year-old man at a dental recall visit"],["Recent blood test","PSA raised (8.1 ng/mL)"],["Prostate biopsy","Glands lined by simple to pseudostratified columnar epithelium in a fibromuscular stroma; calcified bodies in many gland lumens"]],"pcase":"mhisto-case3","why":["Prostatic concretions (corpora amylacea) are calcified glycoproteins that increase with age; Dr. Herr's slide says their significance is unknown.","The slide calls them calcified glycoproteins of unknown significance; PSA is secreted by the gland cells, and the concretions prove nothing about cancer.","The lipochrome pigment sits inside seminal vesicle cells; it does not form calcified bodies in prostate lumens.","Shed cytoplasm is phagocytosed by Sertoli cells or taken up by epididymal cells; it does not calcify in the prostate."]},{"id":"mh-case-stain","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which glands do the two findings point to?","choices":["UV fluorescence: seminal vesicle lipochrome; acid phosphatase: the prostate","UV fluorescence: prostatic concretions; acid phosphatase: the seminal vesicles","UV fluorescence: bulbourethral sialic acid; acid phosphatase: the epididymis","UV fluorescence: seminal vesicle fructose; acid phosphatase: the bulbourethral glands"],"answer":0,"ex":"Two forensic clues from his slides: the seminal vesicles' yellow lipochrome pigment fluoresces under UV light, which helps identify semen stains, and the prostate is the main source of acid phosphatase in semen.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Evidence","Dried stain on clothing, sent to a crime lab"],["Under UV light","The stain fluoresces"],["Acid phosphatase","High"]],"why":["The yellowish lipochrome pigment of seminal vesicle cells fluoresces under UV light (his forensic point), and the prostate is the main source of acid phosphatase in semen.","That swaps the glands: the fluorescent pigment is the seminal vesicles', and acid phosphatase is the prostate's.","Sialic acid marks bulbourethral mucus, which is not the fluorescent part, and acid phosphatase comes from the prostate.","Fructose is the seminal vesicles' energy supply, but it is the lipochrome pigment that fluoresces; acid phosphatase is prostatic."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Accessory glands: what each looks like and what each adds to semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-sv-fructose","mh-sv-id","mh-bu-id","mh-bu-timing","mh-x-prostate","mh-num-glands","mh-2s-4","mh-case-stain"]}},{"id":"mh-erection","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which set of changes produces penile erection?","choices":["Arterial and trabecular smooth muscle relaxes, sinusoids fill, veins are pressed against the tunica albuginea","Arterial and trabecular smooth muscle contracts and squeezes blood into the sinusoids against the tunica albuginea","The helicine arteries constrict while the deep veins dilate, so the blood pools in the corpus spongiosum instead","Skeletal muscle around the bulbourethral glands contracts and pumps blood into the cavernous spaces of the penis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Relax → fill → trap. Relaxed arterial smooth muscle lets the helicine arteries fill the cavernous sinusoids; relaxed trabeculae let the sinusoids expand; the swelling tissue presses the draining veins against the tough tunica albuginea, so the blood stays in.","src":"slides","why":["That is his summary: relaxation of trabecular and arterial smooth muscle, more filling of the cavernous sinusoids, and venous outflow compressed against the tunica albuginea.","Contraction is the trap: erection comes from relaxation of that smooth muscle, which lets blood in.","The helicine arteries must open to fill the cavernous spaces, and the veins are compressed, not dilated.","Erection is a matter of vascular and trabecular smooth muscle tone; the skeletal muscle on his slides surrounds bulbourethral secretory units."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Erection: relaxation, filling, venous compression","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-erection","mh-2s-6","mh-venous","mh-tf-cavernosum"]}},{"id":"mh-2s-6","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. During erection, the nutritive arteries open into the cavernous spaces and fill them with blood.\n2. During erection, venous outflow is compressed against the tunica albuginea.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Statement 1 is false: on his figure the helicine arteries, branches of the deep artery, fill the cavernous spaces; the nutritive arteries supply the capillary and venular network inside the trabeculae. Statement 2 is true: the filled sinusoids press the draining veins against the tunica albuginea.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: on his figure the helicine arteries fill the cavernous spaces; the nutritive arteries supply the capillary network inside the trabeculae.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the filling sinusoids press the draining veins against the tunica albuginea, so blood stays in.","Statement 1 is false, not true: on his figure the helicine arteries fill the cavernous spaces; the nutritive arteries supply the capillary network inside the trabeculae. Statement 2 is true, not false: the filling sinusoids press the draining veins against the tunica albuginea, so blood stays in.","Statement 1 is false: on his figure the helicine arteries fill the cavernous spaces; the nutritive arteries supply the capillary network inside the trabeculae. Statement 2 is true: the filling sinusoids press the draining veins against the tunica albuginea, so blood stays in."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Erection: relaxation, filling, venous compression","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-erection","mh-2s-6","mh-venous","mh-tf-cavernosum"]}},{"id":"mh-venous","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A man's imaging shows normal arterial inflow and normal filling of the cavernous spaces, yet the blood is not held in and the erection fades within moments. Which step of erection is failing?","choices":["Compression of venous outflow against the tunica albuginea","Relaxation of the smooth muscle in the trabeculae and arteries","Filling of the cavernous sinusoids by the helicine arteries","Supply of the trabeculae by the nutritive arteries"],"answer":0,"ex":"Erection needs all three steps: relaxation (more inflow), filling of the sinusoids, and compression of the efferent veins against the tunica albuginea (outflow blocked). Normal inflow with blood still escaping points to the last step.","src":"hub","why":["Inflow and filling are working, so the failing step is the last one: trapping the blood by pressing the veins against the tunica albuginea.","Inflow and filling are normal, which means the smooth muscle is relaxing; the problem is on the outflow side.","The imaging shows the cavernous spaces filling normally.","Nutritive arteries feed the capillary network inside the trabeculae; they do not hold blood in the sinusoids."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Erection: relaxation, filling, venous compression","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-erection","mh-2s-6","mh-venous","mh-tf-cavernosum"]}},{"id":"mh-tf-cavernosum","lec":"mhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The penile urethra runs through the corpus cavernosum.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"On his erectile tissue figure the penile urethra runs in the corpus spongiosum; the corpus cavernosum is the separate erectile tissue wrapped in the tunica albuginea.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His erectile tissue figure places the penile urethra in the corpus spongiosum, not the corpus cavernosum.","The urethra runs in the corpus spongiosum; the corpus cavernosum is a separate column of erectile tissue."],"likely":{"lec":"mhisto","t":"Erection: relaxation, filling, venous compression","basis":"objectives","ids":["mh-erection","mh-2s-6","mh-venous","mh-tf-cavernosum"]}},{"id":"fh-organ-jobs","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which organ conducts sperm up toward the oviducts, receives the zygote for implantation at the blastula stage, and is the muscular sac that holds and expels the fetus?","choices":["The uterus","The uterine tube","The cervix","The vagina"],"answer":0,"ex":"Herr's overview table: ovaries make gametes and steroids, the oviducts conduct ova to the uterus, the uterus receives the zygote at the blastula stage and conducts sperm up to the oviducts, the cervix opens the uterus into the vagina, and the vagina receives the sperm.","src":"slides","why":["Implantation, conducting sperm up to the oviducts and holding and expelling the fetus are all the uterus's jobs.","The tube conducts the ovum to the uterus and is usually where fertilization happens, but implantation is in the uterus.","The cervix is the opening of the uterus into the vagina, not the site of implantation.","The vagina is the canal that receives the sperm; it plays no part in implantation."]},{"id":"fh-steroids","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pairing matches an ovarian steroid class with its action?","choices":["Progestogens: prepare the internal sex organs for pregnancy by promoting secretory changes in the endometrium","Estrogens: prepare the internal sex organs for pregnancy by promoting the secretory changes in the endometrium","Progestogens: promote the growth and maturation of the internal and the external sex organs","Estrogens: regulate milk ejection from the alveoli and lactiferous sinuses of the lactating breast"],"answer":0,"ex":"The ovary's steroids split the work: estrogens promote growth and maturation of the internal and external sex organs, and progestogens prepare them for pregnancy by promoting secretory changes in the endometrium.","src":"slides","why":["Progestogens drive the secretory changes that ready the endometrium for a pregnancy.","That is the progestogens' job; estrogens promote the growth and maturation of the sex organs.","Growth and maturation of the internal and external sex organs is the estrogens' job.","Milk ejection is regulated by oxytocin, not by an ovarian steroid."]},{"id":"fh-arrest-1","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"In a resting adult ovary, every follicle holds an oocyte arrested at which stage?","choices":["Prophase of meiosis I, as a primary oocyte","Metaphase of meiosis II, as a secondary oocyte","Metaphase of meiosis I, as a primary oocyte","Interphase before meiosis, as an oogonium"],"answer":0,"ex":"Marked with three asterisks on his slide: all follicles in the ovary contain a primary oocyte arrested in prophase of meiosis I. The LH surge makes it finish meiosis I shortly before ovulation; the secondary oocyte then arrests at metaphase II until fertilization.","src":"slides","why":["All follicles hold a primary oocyte held in prophase I until an LH surge restarts it.","Metaphase II is the second arrest, reached only after the LH surge, just before ovulation.","The first arrest is in prophase I, not metaphase I.","Oogonia stop dividing by mitosis in fetal life; by birth they have become primary oocytes that entered meiosis."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"The two meiotic arrests: prophase I until the LH surge, metaphase II until fertilization","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-arrest-1","fh-arrest-2","fh-lh-meiosis","fh-tf-meiosis2","fh-seq-meiosis","fh-polar","fh-ex-secondary"]}},{"id":"fh-arrest-2","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A secondary oocyte is ovulated but never meets a sperm, and it dies about a day later. Where was its meiosis halted when it died?","choices":["At metaphase of meiosis II, because only fertilization lets it finish","At prophase of meiosis I, because ovulation alone never restarts meiosis","At telophase of meiosis II, just after the second polar body formed","Nowhere: meiosis II ended at ovulation, so it died as a mature ovum"],"answer":0,"ex":"Ovulation releases a secondary oocyte arrested at metaphase II. Only upon fertilization does it complete the second meiotic division, forming the ovum and a second polar body; unfertilized, it stays viable about 24 hours.","src":"slides","why":["The secondary oocyte waits at metaphase II, and only fertilization completes the second division.","The LH surge already restarted meiosis and finished meiosis I before ovulation.","The second polar body forms only when fertilization completes meiosis II.","It never becomes an ovum without fertilization; meiosis II stays unfinished."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"The two meiotic arrests: prophase I until the LH surge, metaphase II until fertilization","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-arrest-1","fh-arrest-2","fh-lh-meiosis","fh-tf-meiosis2","fh-seq-meiosis","fh-polar","fh-ex-secondary"]}},{"id":"fh-lh-meiosis","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"What restarts the arrested meiosis of the primary oocyte in the dominant follicle?","choices":["The LH surge, which releases meiosis-inducing factor shortly before ovulation","Oocyte maturation inhibitor, secreted into the antrum by the surrounding granulosa cells","Sperm entry at fertilization, which completes the first meiotic division","FSH acting on the granulosa cells as the antrum first begins to form"],"answer":0,"ex":"The LH surge causes release of a meiosis-inducing factor that signals the primary oocyte to complete meiosis I, giving a secondary oocyte and the first polar body. Granulosa cells' OMI is the brake; LH is the trigger.","src":"slides","why":["LH causes release of meiosis-inducing factor, and the primary oocyte finishes meiosis I.","OMI, as its name says, holds maturation back; it doesn't restart meiosis.","Fertilization completes the second meiotic division; the first is already done by ovulation.","FSH drives granulosa cells to make liquor folliculi; the trigger for meiosis is the LH surge."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"The two meiotic arrests: prophase I until the LH surge, metaphase II until fertilization","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-arrest-1","fh-arrest-2","fh-lh-meiosis","fh-tf-meiosis2","fh-seq-meiosis","fh-polar","fh-ex-secondary"]}},{"id":"fh-polar","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"When the primary oocyte completes meiosis I, why is the result one large secondary oocyte and one tiny polar body instead of two equal cells?","choices":["Cytokinesis is unequal: one daughter keeps most of the cytoplasm and the other gets a small rim","The chromosomes divide unequally, so the polar body is left with fewer chromosomes than the oocyte","The polar body is a granulosa cell of the corona radiata that has fused with the oocyte's membrane","The second daughter becomes atretic at once and shrinks by apoptosis to a small remnant"],"answer":0,"ex":"Meiosis I in the oocyte ends with unequal cytokinesis: the secondary oocyte keeps most of the cytoplasm and the first polar body gets only a small ring of it. That keeps the egg's large store of cytoplasm in one cell.","src":"slides","why":["The cytoplasm, not the DNA, is split unevenly, so one cell stays large.","Each daughter of meiosis I gets a full haploid set; the uneven part is the cytoplasm.","A polar body is the oocyte's own sister cell from the division, not a granulosa cell.","Atresia is the death of whole follicles that fail to ovulate, not how a polar body forms."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"The two meiotic arrests: prophase I until the LH surge, metaphase II until fertilization","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-arrest-1","fh-arrest-2","fh-lh-meiosis","fh-tf-meiosis2","fh-seq-meiosis","fh-polar","fh-ex-secondary"]}},{"id":"fh-seq-meiosis","lec":"fhisto","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the events of oogenesis in order, from the fetus to fertilization.","steps":["Oogonia divide by mitosis in the fetal ovary","Primary oocytes arrest in prophase of meiosis I","An LH surge makes one primary oocyte finish meiosis I, giving the first polar body","The secondary oocyte arrests at metaphase of meiosis II and is ovulated","Fertilization completes meiosis II, giving the ovum and a second polar body"],"ex":"Mitosis of oogonia stops by the 5th fetal month; the primary oocytes sit in prophase I for years; the LH surge finishes meiosis I just before ovulation; the secondary oocyte waits at metaphase II; only fertilization finishes meiosis II.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"The two meiotic arrests: prophase I until the LH surge, metaphase II until fertilization","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-arrest-1","fh-arrest-2","fh-lh-meiosis","fh-tf-meiosis2","fh-seq-meiosis","fh-polar","fh-ex-secondary"]}},{"id":"fh-match-counts","lec":"fhisto","type":"match","stem":"Match each point in a woman's life to the number of oocytes at that point.","pairs":[["5th fetal month, when oogonia stop dividing","About 5 to 7 million primary oocytes"],["Birth","About 1 to 2 million primary oocytes"],["Menarche","About 400,000 oocytes"],["The whole reproductive span","About 450 oocytes ovulated"]],"ex":"The numbers fall at every step: about 5 to 7 million by the 5th fetal month, 1 to 2 million that gain follicular cells and survive to birth, about 400,000 at menarche, and only about 450 ovulated over 30 to 40 years.","src":"slides"},{"id":"fh-tf-12","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Each month about 12 follicles begin to develop, and usually only one dominant follicle ovulates.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"About 12 follicles start each month; one dominant follicle typically ovulates a secondary oocyte and the rest become atretic (die by apoptosis).","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["About 12 start, one ovulates, and the rest undergo atresia.","That is the monthly pattern: a dozen or so recruited, one dominant follicle ovulates."]},{"id":"fh-tf-meiosis2","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: When fertilization completes meiosis II, the oocyte gives off its first polar body and becomes the ovum.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The first polar body comes from meiosis I, when the LH surge makes the primary oocyte divide shortly before ovulation. Fertilization completes meiosis II, forming the ovum and a second polar body.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The polar body made at fertilization is the second one; the first came from meiosis I, before ovulation.","Meiosis I already made the first polar body before ovulation; completing meiosis II at fertilization gives the second."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"The two meiotic arrests: prophase I until the LH surge, metaphase II until fertilization","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-arrest-1","fh-arrest-2","fh-lh-meiosis","fh-tf-meiosis2","fh-seq-meiosis","fh-polar","fh-ex-secondary"]}},{"id":"fh-primordial-id","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A section of ovarian cortex shows a spherical oocyte about 25 micrometers across with an asymmetric nucleus and vesicular chromatin, completely surrounded by one layer of flattened cells joined by desmosomes. What is this structure?","choices":["A primordial follicle","An early primary follicle","A late primary follicle","A secondary follicle"],"answer":0,"ex":"The primordial follicle is the most primitive: a primary oocyte (about 25 micrometers, arrested in prophase I) wrapped by a single layer of squamous follicular cells attached to each other by desmosomes. Cuboidal cells mean it has become a primary follicle.","src":"slides","why":["One layer of squamous follicular cells around a roughly 25 micrometer primary oocyte is the primordial follicle.","A unilaminar primary follicle also has one layer, but its cells are cuboidal and the oocyte is much larger.","A multilaminar follicle has several layers of granulosa cells and a theca forming outside.","A secondary follicle has many granulosa cell layers and fluid collecting into an antrum."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Identifying the five follicle stages histologically","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-primordial-id","fh-unilaminar-id","fh-antral-id","fh-graafian-id","fh-num-follicles","fh-seq-follicle","fh-2s-1","fh-corona"]}},{"id":"fh-unilaminar-id","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Around an enlarging oocyte, the follicular cells have become cuboidal but still form only one layer, and a thin zona pellucida is starting to appear. Which stage is this?","choices":["An early primary follicle","A primordial follicle","A late primary follicle","A secondary follicle"],"answer":0,"ex":"In the early primary follicle the follicular cells turn cuboidal (and are now called granulosa cells), the oocyte grows large with abundant RER, Golgi and mitochondria, and the zona pellucida begins to form. One layer is unilaminar; more than one is multilaminar.","src":"slides","why":["One layer of cuboidal cells with a zona pellucida beginning to form is the unilaminar primary follicle.","A primordial follicle's single layer is squamous, and it has no zona pellucida yet.","A multilaminar follicle has more than one layer of granulosa cells.","A secondary follicle is defined by liquor folliculi, which this follicle doesn't have."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Identifying the five follicle stages histologically","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-primordial-id","fh-unilaminar-id","fh-antral-id","fh-graafian-id","fh-num-follicles","fh-seq-follicle","fh-2s-1","fh-corona"]}},{"id":"fh-antral-id","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which feature distinguishes a secondary follicle from a late primary follicle?","choices":["Liquor folliculi collecting among the granulosa cells","A zona pellucida that fully surrounds the oocyte","Granulosa cells stacked in several layers","A theca folliculi forming outside the granulosa cells' basal lamina"],"answer":0,"ex":"Secondary follicles are distinguished from primary follicles by liquor folliculi, which granulosa cells make under FSH and which merges into one chamber, the antrum. The zona, multiple layers and theca are already present in the late primary follicle.","src":"slides","why":["The presence of liquor folliculi is what defines the secondary (antral) follicle.","The zona pellucida already surrounds the oocyte in the late primary follicle.","Several layers of granulosa cells are what make a late primary follicle multilaminar.","The theca folliculi starts organizing in the late primary follicle."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Identifying the five follicle stages histologically","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-primordial-id","fh-unilaminar-id","fh-antral-id","fh-graafian-id","fh-num-follicles","fh-seq-follicle","fh-2s-1","fh-corona"]}},{"id":"fh-graafian-id","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which description fits a mature Graafian follicle?","choices":["About 20 mm across, bulging on the ovary's surface, with the oocyte held on the cumulus oophorus","About 25 micrometers across, with one layer of squamous follicular cells and no zona pellucida yet","Several layers of granulosa cells and a new theca folliculi, but no fluid collecting yet","A collapsed, folded wall of lutein cells making progesterone after the oocyte has left"],"answer":0,"ex":"The Graafian follicle is about 20 millimeters across and makes a bulge on the ovary's surface; its theca layers are more prominent, and the oocyte adheres to the follicle wall through the cumulus oophorus. Growth from primordial to this stage takes many months.","src":"slides","why":["The Graafian follicle is large enough to bulge from the ovary, and the cumulus holds the oocyte to its wall.","That is the primordial follicle, the smallest and most primitive stage.","That is the late primary (multilaminar) follicle, before liquor folliculi appears.","That is the corpus luteum, which forms from the follicle after ovulation."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Identifying the five follicle stages histologically","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-primordial-id","fh-unilaminar-id","fh-antral-id","fh-graafian-id","fh-num-follicles","fh-seq-follicle","fh-2s-1","fh-corona"]}},{"id":"fh-corona","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"In a secondary follicle, the single layer of granulosa cells that stays wrapped around the oocyte, and leaves with it at ovulation, is the:","choices":["Corona radiata","Cumulus oophorus","Theca interna","Zona pellucida"],"answer":0,"ex":"A single layer of granulosa cells around the oocyte is the corona radiata; the cumulus oophorus is the stalk of granulosa cells that holds the oocyte to the wall. At ovulation the secondary oocyte leaves with the corona radiata cells.","src":"slides","why":["The corona radiata is the one layer of granulosa cells hugging the oocyte, released with it at ovulation.","The cumulus is the mound of granulosa cells that attaches the oocyte to the follicle wall, not the layer around it.","The theca interna lies outside the basal lamina of the granulosa cells, far from the oocyte.","The zona is the glycoprotein coat between the oocyte and the corona, not a layer of cells."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Identifying the five follicle stages histologically","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-primordial-id","fh-unilaminar-id","fh-antral-id","fh-graafian-id","fh-num-follicles","fh-seq-follicle","fh-2s-1","fh-corona"]}},{"id":"fh-zp-contact","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Once the zona pellucida separates the oocyte from its granulosa cells, how do the two stay in contact?","choices":["Oocyte microvilli and granulosa cell filopodia reach into the zona and touch","Tight junctions between the oocyte and granulosa cells seal the zona shut","Desmosomes anchor each granulosa cell to the oocyte straight through the zona","Capillaries of the theca interna pass through the zona to reach the oocyte"],"answer":0,"ex":"The zona pellucida (glycoproteins ZP1, ZP2 and ZP3, made by the follicular cells) sits between the oocyte and the granulosa cells. Microvilli from the oocyte and filopodia from the granulosa cells penetrate it and touch each other.","src":"slides","why":["Processes from both cells penetrate the zona pellucida and make contact.","Granulosa cells lack tight junctions; that is why there is no blood-follicle barrier.","Desmosomes join the squamous follicular cells of a primordial follicle to each other.","The theca's capillaries stay outside the basal lamina; no vessels enter the granulosa layer."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Granulosa versus theca: junctions, zona, liquor folliculi, LH and FSH","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-gap-barrier","fh-2s-2","fh-2s-3","fh-mm-liquor","fh-zp-contact"]}},{"id":"fh-gap-barrier","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Granulosa cells of a primary follicle are linked by extensive gap junctions but no tight junctions. What follows from that, compared with the seminiferous tubule of L13?","choices":["There is no blood-follicle barrier, unlike the blood-testis barrier of tight junctions between Sertoli cells","There is a blood-follicle barrier like the testis's, because extensive gap junctions seal the spaces between the cells","The oocyte is shielded from the immune system by the theca externa, which takes the place of a barrier","The granulosa cells can't pass small molecules to each other, so the oocyte must be fed by the theca interna"],"answer":0,"ex":"Primary follicles develop extensive gap junctions between granulosa cells but no tight junctions, so there is no blood-follicle barrier. In the male, tight junctions between Sertoli cells split the tubule into basal and adluminal compartments: the blood-testis barrier.","src":"slides","why":["Without tight junctions there is no barrier; the testis has one because Sertoli cells are sealed by tight junctions.","Gap junctions are channels for exchange between cells; sealing takes tight junctions.","The theca externa is fibrous tissue and smooth muscle; the lecture gives it no barrier role.","Gap junctions are exactly what lets granulosa cells share small molecules."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Granulosa versus theca: junctions, zona, liquor folliculi, LH and FSH","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-gap-barrier","fh-2s-2","fh-2s-3","fh-mm-liquor","fh-zp-contact"]}},{"id":"fh-mm-liquor","lec":"fhisto","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these are in liquor folliculi, the fluid of the antral follicle? (Select all that apply.)","choices":["Glycosaminoglycans and proteoglycans","Steroid-binding proteins with progesterone and estradiol","Oocyte maturation inhibitor (OMI)","Glycogen released from desquamating epithelial cells","Lactose and caseins"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Under FSH, granulosa cells make liquor folliculi: GAGs, proteoglycans, steroid-binding proteins and steroids (progesterone, estradiol), plus OMI secreted into the antrum. Glycogen is the vagina's; lactose and caseins are milk's.","src":"slides","why":["Liquor folliculi contains GAGs and proteoglycans.","Liquor folliculi carries steroid-binding proteins and steroids such as progesterone and estradiol.","Granulosa cells secrete OMI into the antrum.","Glycogen from shed cells belongs to the vaginal epithelium, where Lactobacillus turns it into lactic acid.","Lactose and caseins are components of milk."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Granulosa versus theca: junctions, zona, liquor folliculi, LH and FSH","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-gap-barrier","fh-2s-2","fh-2s-3","fh-mm-liquor","fh-zp-contact"]}},{"id":"fh-tf-months","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Growth from a primordial follicle to a mature Graafian follicle takes about two weeks, the length of one follicular phase.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"His slide flags it: growth from primordial to mature follicle takes many months. About 12 follicles develop each month and one ovulates, but each has been growing far longer than one cycle.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Two weeks is far too short; the whole process takes many months.","It takes many months, not two weeks."]},{"id":"fh-2s-1","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The primary oocyte of a primordial follicle is about 25 micrometers across.\n2. The follicular cells of a primordial follicle are attached to each other by desmosomes.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"A primordial follicle is a primary oocyte of about 25 micrometers completely wrapped by one layer of squamous follicular cells, which are attached to each other by desmosomes. In the early primary follicle the oocyte grows to about 100 to 150 micrometers, and the granulosa cells develop extensive gap junctions.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: the primordial oocyte is a spherical cell about 25 micrometers across. Statement 2 is true: the squamous follicular cells completely surround the oocyte and are attached to each other by desmosomes.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the primordial oocyte is a spherical cell about 25 micrometers across. Statement 2 is true, not false: the squamous follicular cells completely surround the oocyte and are attached to each other by desmosomes.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the squamous follicular cells completely surround the oocyte and are attached to each other by desmosomes.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the primordial oocyte is a spherical cell about 25 micrometers across."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Identifying the five follicle stages histologically","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-primordial-id","fh-unilaminar-id","fh-antral-id","fh-graafian-id","fh-num-follicles","fh-seq-follicle","fh-2s-1","fh-corona"]}},{"id":"fh-2s-2","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The theca externa is richly vascularized, and its cells make androgens in response to LH.\n2. The theca folliculi organizes outside the basal lamina of the granulosa cells.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Stromal cells organize into the theca folliculi outside the granulosa cells' basal lamina. The theca interna is the richly vascular layer with LH receptors that makes androgens (estrogen precursors); the theca externa is mostly fibrous tissue and smooth muscle.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: that describes the theca interna; the theca externa is mostly fibrous connective tissue and smooth muscle cells.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the theca folliculi forms from stromal cells external to the basal lamina of the granulosa cells.","Statement 1 is false, not true: that describes the theca interna; the theca externa is mostly fibrous connective tissue and smooth muscle cells. Statement 2 is true, not false: the theca folliculi forms from stromal cells external to the basal lamina of the granulosa cells.","Statement 1 is false: that describes the theca interna; the theca externa is mostly fibrous connective tissue and smooth muscle cells. Statement 2 is true: the theca folliculi forms from stromal cells external to the basal lamina of the granulosa cells."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Granulosa versus theca: junctions, zona, liquor folliculi, LH and FSH","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-gap-barrier","fh-2s-2","fh-2s-3","fh-mm-liquor","fh-zp-contact"]}},{"id":"fh-2s-3","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Granulosa cells secrete oocyte maturation inhibitor into the antrum.\n2. The cumulus oophorus attaches the oocyte to the wall of the follicle.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Granulosa cells do both jobs here: they secrete OMI (and the rest of liquor folliculi) into the antrum, and they form the cumulus oophorus that anchors the oocyte to the follicle wall.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: OMI is secreted into the antrum by the granulosa cells. Statement 2 is true: the oocyte adheres to the follicle wall through the cumulus oophorus, formed by granulosa cells.","Statement 1 is true, not false: OMI is secreted into the antrum by the granulosa cells. Statement 2 is true, not false: the oocyte adheres to the follicle wall through the cumulus oophorus, formed by granulosa cells.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the oocyte adheres to the follicle wall through the cumulus oophorus, formed by granulosa cells.","Statement 1 is true, not false: OMI is secreted into the antrum by the granulosa cells."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Granulosa versus theca: junctions, zona, liquor folliculi, LH and FSH","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-gap-barrier","fh-2s-2","fh-2s-3","fh-mm-liquor","fh-zp-contact"]}},{"id":"fh-num-follicles","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about follicle stages are true?\n1. A late primary follicle already has a single fluid-filled antrum.\n2. An early primary follicle has one layer of cuboidal granulosa cells.\n3. A primordial follicle has one layer of squamous follicular cells.\n4. A secondary follicle has a corona radiata around its oocyte.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","2, 3 and 4","3 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"Squamous cells (primordial) turn cuboidal (early primary), then multiply into many layers (late primary). Fluid, liquor folliculi, is what makes a follicle secondary, and by then a corona radiata wraps the oocyte.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: liquor folliculi and the antrum are what define the secondary follicle; the late primary follicle has none yet.","It includes 1, which is false: liquor folliculi and the antrum are what define the secondary follicle; the late primary follicle has none yet. It leaves out 3, which is true: the primordial follicle's single layer of follicular cells is squamous.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: liquor folliculi and the antrum are what define the secondary follicle; the late primary follicle has none yet.","It leaves out 2, which is true: the unilaminar primary follicle has one layer of cuboidal granulosa cells."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Identifying the five follicle stages histologically","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-primordial-id","fh-unilaminar-id","fh-antral-id","fh-graafian-id","fh-num-follicles","fh-seq-follicle","fh-2s-1","fh-corona"]}},{"id":"fh-seq-follicle","lec":"fhisto","type":"sequence","stem":"Put these views of a growing follicle in the order they develop.","steps":["A primary oocyte wrapped by one layer of flat follicular cells","One layer of cuboidal granulosa cells, with a zona pellucida starting to form","Several layers of granulosa cells, with theca cells organizing outside the basal lamina","Pools of liquor folliculi merging into a single antrum","A follicle about 20 millimeters across that bulges from the ovary's surface"],"ex":"Primordial, early primary (unilaminar), late primary (multilaminar), secondary (antral), mature Graafian: the cells go from squamous to cuboidal, then multiply, then make fluid, and the follicle finally bulges from the ovary.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Identifying the five follicle stages histologically","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-primordial-id","fh-unilaminar-id","fh-antral-id","fh-graafian-id","fh-num-follicles","fh-seq-follicle","fh-2s-1","fh-corona"]}},{"id":"fh-ex-secondary","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"All of the following describe a secondary (antral) follicle EXCEPT:","choices":["Its oocyte has already completed meiosis I","Liquor folliculi accumulates and forms a single antrum","A cumulus oophorus forms from granulosa cells","Cortical granules are evident under the oocyte's plasma membrane"],"answer":0,"ex":"The secondary follicle has liquor folliculi and an antrum, a cumulus oophorus, a corona radiata and visible cortical granules, but its oocyte is still a primary oocyte arrested in prophase I. Meiosis I is completed only after the LH surge, shortly before ovulation.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["This is the exception: the oocyte in a secondary follicle is still a primary oocyte in prophase I; meiosis I finishes only after the LH surge.","Liquor folliculi merging into one antrum is the hallmark of this stage.","The cumulus oophorus forms in the secondary follicle.","Cortical granules under the oocyte's membrane are listed for this stage."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"The two meiotic arrests: prophase I until the LH surge, metaphase II until fertilization","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-arrest-1","fh-arrest-2","fh-lh-meiosis","fh-tf-meiosis2","fh-seq-meiosis","fh-polar","fh-ex-secondary"]}},{"id":"fh-ovulation-trigger","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Estrogen from a Graafian follicle raises the blood estrogen level near mid-cycle. What happens next?","choices":["LH production rises sharply, the LH surge","LH production falls as estrogen feeds back","Progesterone rises and the spiral arteries constrict","The corpus luteum forms before rupture"],"answer":0,"ex":"Estrogen released from the Graafian follicle raises blood estrogen, which increases LH production (the LH surge). The surge then swells the follicle and restarts meiosis; L16 covers the feedback behind it.","src":"slides","why":["Rising estrogen from the Graafian follicle increases LH production: the LH surge.","At this point estrogen drives LH up, not down; that is what sets off ovulation.","Spiral artery constriction follows a drop in progesterone at the end of the cycle.","The corpus luteum forms from the follicle only after ovulation."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Ovulation: the LH surge, rupture, and the corpus luteum's lutein cells and fate","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-ovulation-trigger","fh-mm-lh","fh-rupture","fh-where-released","fh-lutein-estrogen","fh-cl-fate","fh-ex-postov","fh-2s-5"]}},{"id":"fh-mm-lh","lec":"fhisto","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these are effects of LH on the Graafian follicle and its oocyte around ovulation? (Select all that apply.)","choices":["More blood flow to the follicle and leakier theca interna capillaries, causing local edema","Release of meiosis-inducing factor, so the primary oocyte finishes meiosis I","Conversion of the collapsed follicle into a corpus luteum after ovulation","Completion of meiosis II, giving the ovum and a second polar body","Secretion of oocyte maturation inhibitor into the antrum"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"The LH surge raises blood flow and theca interna capillary permeability (local edema), releases meiosis-inducing factor so meiosis I finishes, and after rupture converts the follicle into a corpus luteum. Meiosis II waits for fertilization.","src":"slides","why":["LH increases blood flow and theca interna capillary permeability, so the follicle swells with edema.","LH causes release of meiosis-inducing factor and meiosis I is completed.","After the follicle collapses, LH converts it into the corpus luteum.","Meiosis II is completed only upon fertilization, not by LH.","OMI comes from granulosa cells and holds the arrest; LH ends it."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Ovulation: the LH surge, rupture, and the corpus luteum's lutein cells and fate","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-ovulation-trigger","fh-mm-lh","fh-rupture","fh-where-released","fh-lutein-estrogen","fh-cl-fate","fh-ex-postov","fh-2s-5"]}},{"id":"fh-rupture","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"What makes the Graafian follicle finally rupture through the surface of the ovary?","choices":["Hyaluronic acid and water swell the antrum until the follicle and tunica albuginea give way","The new corpus luteum forms first and pushes the oocyte out through the ovarian capsule","Peg cells of the infundibulum secrete enzymes that digest a hole through the ovary's capsule","Spiral arteries constrict, and the ischemic outer cortex of the ovary breaks down"],"answer":0,"ex":"Granulosa cells keep producing GAGs and hyaluronic acid (fluid, mostly water) into the antrum, so it swells. The follicle moves to the outer cortex, pushes against the tunica albuginea, and the walls of both the ovary and the follicle rupture.","src":"slides","why":["Granulosa cells pour GAGs and hyaluronic acid into the antrum; the swollen follicle presses on the capsule until both walls tear.","The corpus luteum forms from the follicle after it ruptures, not before.","Peg cells line the uterine tube and secrete nutrient fluid for the ovum.","Spiral artery constriction and ischemia describe menstruation in the endometrium."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Ovulation: the LH surge, rupture, and the corpus luteum's lutein cells and fate","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-ovulation-trigger","fh-mm-lh","fh-rupture","fh-where-released","fh-lutein-estrogen","fh-cl-fate","fh-ex-postov","fh-2s-5"]}},{"id":"fh-where-released","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Where does the secondary oocyte go at ovulation?","choices":["Into the peritoneum near the infundibulum, and from there it enters the tube","Directly into the ampulla of the tube, which is fused to the surface of the ovary","Into the ovarian medulla, where it waits among the vessels for sperm","Into the uterine cavity through the intramural part of the tube"],"answer":0,"ex":"The secondary oocyte and the cells of the corona radiata are released into the peritoneum near the opening of the oviduct, the fimbriated infundibulum, and enter the duct.","src":"slides","why":["The oocyte and corona cells are released into the peritoneum near the tube's opening, the infundibulum, and enter it.","The ampulla is the tube's broadest segment and usual fertilization site, but it isn't fused to the ovary.","The medulla is the ovary's central vascular connective tissue; the oocyte leaves the ovary entirely.","The intramural region is where the tube passes into the uterus, at the far end from the ovary."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Ovulation: the LH surge, rupture, and the corpus luteum's lutein cells and fate","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-ovulation-trigger","fh-mm-lh","fh-rupture","fh-where-released","fh-lutein-estrogen","fh-cl-fate","fh-ex-postov","fh-2s-5"]}},{"id":"fh-lutein-estrogen","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A corpus luteum makes both progesterone and estrogen. Which of its cells account for the estrogen, and which follicle layer did they come from?","choices":["Granulosa-lutein cells, from the granulosa cells","Theca-lutein cells, from the enlarged theca interna cells","Theca-lutein cells, from the theca externa","Fibroblasts, from the ovarian stroma"],"answer":0,"ex":"After ovulation, granulosa cells become granulosa-lutein cells (progesterone and estrogen) and theca interna cells enlarge into theca-lutein cells (progesterone). Together they are the corpus luteum.","src":"slides","why":["Granulosa cells of the old follicle become granulosa-lutein cells, which make progesterone and estrogen.","Theca-lutein cells do come from the theca interna, but his slide lists them as making progesterone.","Theca-lutein cells come from the theca interna, and they make progesterone.","Fibroblasts fill an old corpus luteum and make collagen, turning it into a corpus albicans."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Ovulation: the LH surge, rupture, and the corpus luteum's lutein cells and fate","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-ovulation-trigger","fh-mm-lh","fh-rupture","fh-where-released","fh-lutein-estrogen","fh-cl-fate","fh-ex-postov","fh-2s-5"]}},{"id":"fh-tf-thecalutein","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Theca-lutein cells of the corpus luteum develop from the theca externa.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Theca-lutein cells come from the theca interna, the vascular, LH-responsive layer, whose cells enlarge after ovulation and make progesterone. The theca externa is fibrous tissue and smooth muscle.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["They come from the theca interna, not the theca externa.","The theca interna, not the theca externa, enlarges into theca-lutein cells."]},{"id":"fh-2s-5","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Granulosa-lutein cells make both progesterone and estrogen.\n2. After ovulation, FSH converts the collapsed follicle into the corpus luteum.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"After ovulation the Graafian follicle collapses and folds, and LH converts it into the corpus luteum: granulosa cells become granulosa-lutein cells (progesterone and estrogen), and theca interna cells become theca-lutein cells (progesterone).","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: it is LH, not FSH, that converts the collapsed follicle into the corpus luteum.","Statement 1 is true, not false: granulosa-lutein cells, from the follicle's granulosa cells, make progesterone and estrogen.","Statement 1 is true: granulosa-lutein cells, from the follicle's granulosa cells, make progesterone and estrogen. Statement 2 is false: it is LH, not FSH, that converts the collapsed follicle into the corpus luteum.","Statement 1 is true, not false: granulosa-lutein cells, from the follicle's granulosa cells, make progesterone and estrogen. Statement 2 is false, not true: it is LH, not FSH, that converts the collapsed follicle into the corpus luteum."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Ovulation: the LH surge, rupture, and the corpus luteum's lutein cells and fate","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-ovulation-trigger","fh-mm-lh","fh-rupture","fh-where-released","fh-lutein-estrogen","fh-cl-fate","fh-ex-postov","fh-2s-5"]}},{"id":"fh-cl-fate","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"If no pregnancy occurs, what becomes of the corpus luteum?","choices":["It degenerates and fills with collagen-secreting fibroblasts, becoming a corpus albicans","A hormone from the placenta keeps it alive, so it goes on secreting progesterone for weeks","It turns back into a primordial follicle, ready to grow again in the next cycle","It ruptures again later in the luteal phase and releases a second secondary oocyte"],"answer":0,"ex":"No pregnancy: the corpus luteum degenerates, and the old structure fills with fibroblasts that secrete collagen, becoming the corpus albicans. Pregnancy: a placental hormone maintains it.","src":"slides","why":["Without pregnancy it degenerates, and fibroblasts lay down collagen to form the corpus albicans.","Placental support of the corpus luteum happens only if pregnancy occurs.","Follicles only move forward or die; a corpus luteum never becomes a follicle again.","One follicle releases one oocyte; the corpus luteum is what's left after that."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Ovulation: the LH surge, rupture, and the corpus luteum's lutein cells and fate","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-ovulation-trigger","fh-mm-lh","fh-rupture","fh-where-released","fh-lutein-estrogen","fh-cl-fate","fh-ex-postov","fh-2s-5"]}},{"id":"fh-ex-postov","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"After ovulation, all of the following happen EXCEPT:","choices":["The released secondary oocyte completes meiosis II whether or not it is fertilized","The emptied Graafian follicle collapses and becomes folded","Granulosa cells differentiate into granulosa-lutein cells that make progesterone and estrogen","Theca interna cells enlarge into progesterone-making theca-lutein cells"],"answer":0,"ex":"After ovulation the follicle collapses and folds, LH converts it into the corpus luteum, granulosa cells become granulosa-lutein cells and theca interna cells become theca-lutein cells. The oocyte has left at metaphase II, and only fertilization would finish its meiosis II; unfertilized, it lives about 24 hours.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["This is the exception: the ovulated oocyte stays at metaphase II, and only fertilization completes meiosis II.","The follicle collapses and folds once the oocyte leaves.","Granulosa cells of the Graafian follicle become hormone-making granulosa-lutein cells.","Theca interna cells enlarge and become theca-lutein cells."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Ovulation: the LH surge, rupture, and the corpus luteum's lutein cells and fate","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-ovulation-trigger","fh-mm-lh","fh-rupture","fh-where-released","fh-lutein-estrogen","fh-cl-fate","fh-ex-postov","fh-2s-5"]}},{"id":"fh-phase-days","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"In a 28-day cycle, which day ranges match the three uterine phases?","choices":["Menstrual 1-5, proliferative 5-14, secretory 15-28","Menstrual 1-7, proliferative 7-21, secretory 21-28","Menstrual 1-5, proliferative 5-21, secretory 21-28","Menstrual 1-3, proliferative 3-10, secretory 10-28"],"answer":0,"ex":"Menstrual phase days 1 to 5, proliferative (follicular) phase 5 to 14, secretory (luteal) phase 15 to 28. Ovulation sits between the last two.","src":"slides","why":["Menstruation days 1 to 5, proliferative (follicular) 5 to 14, secretory (luteal) 15 to 28.","This leaves only a week for the secretory phase; it runs from day 15 to 28.","Ovulation falls about day 14, so the proliferative phase ends then, not at day 21.","Menstruation lasts about days 1 to 5, and the proliferative phase runs to day 14."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Lining up the ovarian and uterine cycles on a given day","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-day10","fh-day22","fh-phase-days","fh-num-cycle"]}},{"id":"fh-day10","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"On day 10 of a regular 28-day cycle, which pairing of ovary and endometrium would you expect?","choices":["A growing antral follicle making estrogen; a proliferative endometrium rebuilding from the basalis","A corpus luteum making progesterone; a secretory endometrium with coiled glands full of secretion","A degenerating corpus luteum; a menstrual endometrium shedding its functionalis","A Graafian follicle that has just ruptured; an endometrium with only its basalis left"],"answer":0,"ex":"The ovarian follicular phase lines up with the uterine proliferative phase (days 5 to 14): growing follicles make estrogen, and the basalis's glands and connective tissue proliferate to rebuild the functionalis.","src":"slides","why":["Day 10 is the follicular phase in the ovary and the proliferative phase in the uterus.","That is the luteal and secretory phase, days 15 to 28.","A failing corpus luteum and shedding of the functionalis belong to days 1 to 5.","Rupture comes at about day 14, and a basalis-only endometrium is the end of menstruation."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Lining up the ovarian and uterine cycles on a given day","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-day10","fh-day22","fh-phase-days","fh-num-cycle"]}},{"id":"fh-day22","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"On day 22 of a regular 28-day cycle, with no pregnancy, which description best fits the ovary and the endometrium?","choices":["A corpus luteum secreting progesterone; a thick functionalis with coiled glands that secrete","A Graafian follicle bulging on the surface; straight glands in a thin, regrowing functionalis","Only a corpus albicans; spiral arteries constricting and the functionalis separating from the basalis","Several early primary follicles and no corpus luteum; an endometrium made only of its basalis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Days 15 to 28 are the secretory (luteal) phase: the corpus luteum makes progesterone, which promotes the secretory changes in the endometrium that prepare it for implantation.","src":"slides","why":["Day 22 is the luteal phase: the corpus luteum's progesterone drives secretory changes in the endometrium.","Those are late follicular and proliferative findings, before ovulation.","The functionalis separates only after progesterone drops, at the start of the next cycle.","A basalis-only endometrium is the end of menstruation, days 1 to 5."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Lining up the ovarian and uterine cycles on a given day","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-day10","fh-day22","fh-phase-days","fh-num-cycle"]}},{"id":"fh-num-cycle","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the uterine cycle are true?\n1. The stratum functionalis is shed during the menstrual phase.\n2. Glands and connective tissue of the basalis rebuild the functionalis in the proliferative phase.\n3. The secretory phase depends on progesterone from the corpus luteum.\n4. The proliferative phase lines up with the ovary's luteal phase.","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":0,"ex":"The functionalis is shed (menstrual), regrown from the basalis (proliferative, which is the ovary's follicular phase), then made secretory by the corpus luteum's progesterone (secretory, the luteal phase).","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1, 2 and 3 are true. Statement 4 is false: the proliferative phase is also called the follicular phase; it is the secretory phase that matches the luteal phase.","It includes 4, which is false: the proliferative phase is also called the follicular phase; it is the secretory phase that matches the luteal phase.","It leaves out 2, which is true: the basalis's glands and connective tissue proliferate to make a new functionalis.","It includes 4, which is false: the proliferative phase is also called the follicular phase; it is the secretory phase that matches the luteal phase. It leaves out 1, which is true: the functionalis separates from the basalis and is shed in menstruation."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Lining up the ovarian and uterine cycles on a given day","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-day10","fh-day22","fh-phase-days","fh-num-cycle"]}},{"id":"fh-menses-cause","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"What sets off menstruation?","choices":["A drop in progesterone constricts the spiral arteries, so the functionalis becomes ischemic and separates","A drop in estrogen dilates the spiral arteries, so the functionalis floods with blood and detaches","The LH surge raises capillary permeability in the endometrium, so edema lifts off the functionalis","A rise in progesterone makes the glands of the basalis over-secrete until the entire endometrium sloughs off"],"answer":0,"ex":"Marked with three asterisks: the drop in progesterone causes constriction of the spiral arteries, leading to local ischemia in the functional layer and its separation from the basal layer. The basalis stays to rebuild it.","src":"slides","why":["Flagged on his slide: falling progesterone, spiral artery constriction, local ischemia, separation of the functionalis.","The trigger is a progesterone drop, and the arteries constrict rather than dilate.","LH-driven edema happens in the theca interna of the Graafian follicle, before ovulation.","Rising progesterone builds the secretory endometrium; only the functionalis is ever shed."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Menstruation: progesterone drop, spiral artery constriction, functionalis shed, basalis regrows it","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-menses-cause","fh-tf-basalis","fh-tf-helical","fh-num-cycle","fh-ex-endometrium"]}},{"id":"fh-tf-basalis","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The stratum basalis is shed with each menstrual period and regrown from the stratum functionalis.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It is the other way around: the functionalis is shed, and the basalis, a deep narrow layer that stays, regrows it from its glands and connective tissue.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The layers are swapped: the functionalis is shed, and the basalis regrows it.","The basalis stays put; its glands and connective tissue rebuild the shed functionalis."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Menstruation: progesterone drop, spiral artery constriction, functionalis shed, basalis regrows it","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-menses-cause","fh-tf-basalis","fh-tf-helical","fh-num-cycle","fh-ex-endometrium"]}},{"id":"fh-tf-helical","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The stratum functionalis is vascularized by many helical (spiral) arteries.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The functionalis is vascularized by many helical arteries and undergoes extreme changes in the cycle; their constriction when progesterone falls starts menstruation.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Helical arteries supply the functionalis, and their constriction triggers menstruation.","The functionalis is the layer with the many helical arteries."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Menstruation: progesterone drop, spiral artery constriction, functionalis shed, basalis regrows it","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-menses-cause","fh-tf-basalis","fh-tf-helical","fh-num-cycle","fh-ex-endometrium"]}},{"id":"fh-ampulla","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Fertilization usually takes place in which part of the uterine tube?","choices":["The ampulla, the broadest segment of the tube","The infundibulum, the funnel covered by fimbriae","The isthmus, the narrow region next to the uterus","The intramural region, inside the uterine wall"],"answer":0,"ex":"Four regions from the ovary inward: infundibulum (funnel with fimbriae), ampulla (broadest, usual site of fertilization), isthmus (narrow, next to the uterus) and the intramural part in the uterine wall.","src":"slides","why":["The ampulla is the broadest segment and usually the site of fertilization.","The infundibulum catches the ovulated oocyte; fertilization is usually further along.","The isthmus is the narrow part between the ampulla and the uterus.","The intramural region is where the tube passes through the uterine wall."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Uterine tube: ampulla, capacitation, 24-hour viability, ciliary direction","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-ampulla","fh-capacitation","fh-tf-24h","fh-tf-cilia","fh-2s-4"]}},{"id":"fh-peg","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"In the epithelium of the uterine tube, what do the non-ciliated peg cells do?","choices":["Secrete a fluid that supplies nutrients to the ovum","Beat in waves that carry the ovum toward the uterus","Store glycogen that bacteria turn into lactic acid","Secrete a thick mucus plug against sperm in pregnancy"],"answer":0,"ex":"The tube's simple columnar epithelium has two cell types: ciliated cells, whose cilia beat toward the uterus, and non-ciliated peg cells, which secrete a nutrient fluid for the ovum.","src":"slides","why":["Peg cells secrete the fluid that nourishes the ovum.","Moving the ovum is the ciliated cells' job; peg cells are the non-ciliated ones.","Glycogen that Lactobacillus turns into lactic acid comes from the vaginal epithelium.","The pregnancy plug comes from the cervical glands."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Linings of the uterine tube, endometrium, cervix and vagina","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-match-epith","fh-id-tube","fh-id-vagina","fh-ex-tube","fh-tz","fh-num-vagina","fh-peg","fh-2s-6"]}},{"id":"fh-tf-cilia","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The cilia of the uterine tube's epithelium beat toward the ovary.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The wave of the tube's cilia is directed toward the uterus, carrying the ovum (or embryo) along.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The cilia beat toward the uterus, not the ovary.","The ciliary wave runs toward the uterus."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Uterine tube: ampulla, capacitation, 24-hour viability, ciliary direction","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-ampulla","fh-capacitation","fh-tf-24h","fh-tf-cilia","fh-2s-4"]}},{"id":"fh-tf-24h","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: An ovulated oocyte that isn't fertilized remains viable for about 24 hours.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"His slide flags it: the oocyte remains viable for about 24 hours if not fertilized, so fertilization has to happen soon after ovulation, usually in the ampulla.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["About a day: that's the oocyte's window for fertilization.","The slide gives about 24 hours of viability."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Uterine tube: ampulla, capacitation, 24-hour viability, ciliary direction","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-ampulla","fh-capacitation","fh-tf-24h","fh-tf-cilia","fh-2s-4"]}},{"id":"fh-capacitation","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Sperm arriving from the male tract can't yet fertilize an oocyte. Which part of the female tract produces factors that promote their activation, capacitation?","choices":["The folded mucosa lining the uterine tube","The watery secretions of the cervical glands at ovulation","The glycogen-rich stratified squamous lining of the vagina","The granulosa cells of the corona radiata around the oocyte"],"answer":0,"ex":"The uterine tube's mucosa provides nutrients and protection for the oocyte and sperm and produces factors that promote sperm activation, capacitation. In L13, the epididymis's glycerophosphocholine inhibits capacitation, so it happens only in the female tract.","src":"slides","why":["The tube's mucosa nourishes and protects the oocyte and sperm and makes factors that promote capacitation.","Watery cervical fluid at ovulation helps sperm migrate, but capacitation factors come from the tube's mucosa.","Vaginal glycogen feeds Lactobacillus, which keeps the surface acidic; it doesn't capacitate sperm.","The corona radiata leaves with the oocyte, but the lecture places capacitation in the tube's mucosa."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Uterine tube: ampulla, capacitation, 24-hour viability, ciliary direction","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-ampulla","fh-capacitation","fh-tf-24h","fh-tf-cilia","fh-2s-4"]}},{"id":"fh-id-tube","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A section shows longitudinal mucosal folds lined by simple columnar epithelium of ciliated cells mixed with non-ciliated secretory cells, a thin, poorly defined inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle layer, and a serosa. Where is it from?","choices":["The uterine tube","The endometrium of the uterus","The canal of the cervix","The ductus epididymis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Uterine tube: longitudinal mucosal folds in all regions; simple columnar epithelium of ciliated cells and peg cells; a poorly defined inner circular and outer longitudinal muscularis; and a simple squamous serosa outside.","src":"slides","why":["Folds, ciliated cells with peg cells, a poorly defined two-layer muscularis and a serosa are the uterine tube.","The endometrium has branched glands in a lamina propria over a thick three-layer myometrium, not folds and a thin muscularis.","The cervical canal is lined by mucus-secreting columnar cells forming glandular crypts.","The epididymis (L13) is lined by pseudostratified columnar cells with stereocilia, not cilia and peg cells."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Linings of the uterine tube, endometrium, cervix and vagina","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-match-epith","fh-id-tube","fh-id-vagina","fh-ex-tube","fh-tz","fh-num-vagina","fh-peg","fh-2s-6"]}},{"id":"fh-ex-tube","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"All of the following describe the uterine tube EXCEPT:","choices":["Its mucosal folds are found only in the ampulla","Its ciliated simple columnar cells beat toward the uterus","Its non-ciliated peg cells secrete a nutrient fluid","Its muscularis has poorly defined inner circular and outer longitudinal layers"],"answer":0,"ex":"The tube's mucosa has longitudinal folds in all regions, lined by ciliated simple columnar cells and peg cells; its muscularis is a poorly defined inner circular and outer longitudinal layer; a simple squamous serosa covers it.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["This is the exception: the longitudinal mucosal folds are present in all regions of the tube.","The cilia's wave is directed toward the uterus.","Peg cells secrete fluid that provides nutrients to the ovum.","The muscularis is described exactly that way."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Linings of the uterine tube, endometrium, cervix and vagina","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-match-epith","fh-id-tube","fh-id-vagina","fh-ex-tube","fh-tz","fh-num-vagina","fh-peg","fh-2s-6"]}},{"id":"fh-2s-4","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The infundibulum is the narrow region of the uterine tube between the ampulla and the uterus.\n2. The middle layer of the myometrium is longitudinal smooth muscle.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"From the ovary inward, the tube runs infundibulum (a funnel covered by fimbriae), ampulla (broadest), isthmus (narrow, next to the uterus) and the intramural part. The myometrium is inner longitudinal, middle circular (with the large vessels of the stratum vasculare) and outer longitudinal.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: that is the isthmus; the infundibulum is the funnel-shaped segment covered by fimbriae, at the ovary's end. Statement 2 is false, not true: the middle layer is circular smooth muscle carrying large blood vessels, the stratum vasculare.","Statement 1 is false: that is the isthmus; the infundibulum is the funnel-shaped segment covered by fimbriae, at the ovary's end. Statement 2 is false: the middle layer is circular smooth muscle carrying large blood vessels, the stratum vasculare.","Statement 1 is false, not true: that is the isthmus; the infundibulum is the funnel-shaped segment covered by fimbriae, at the ovary's end.","Statement 2 is false, not true: the middle layer is circular smooth muscle carrying large blood vessels, the stratum vasculare."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Uterine tube: ampulla, capacitation, 24-hour viability, ciliary direction","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-ampulla","fh-capacitation","fh-tf-24h","fh-tf-cilia","fh-2s-4"]}},{"id":"fh-ex-endometrium","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"All of the following are features of the endometrium EXCEPT:","choices":["A middle circular layer of smooth muscle with large blood vessels","Simple columnar epithelium with ciliated and nonciliated cells","Simple branched glands in a lamina propria that reach toward the myometrium","A functionalis vascularized by many helical arteries"],"answer":0,"ex":"The endometrium is the mucosa: simple columnar epithelium (ciliated and nonciliated cells) over a lamina propria with simple branched glands, split into functionalis (helical arteries) and basalis. The vessel-rich circular stratum vasculare belongs to the myometrium.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["This is the exception: that is the stratum vasculare, the middle layer of the myometrium.","The endometrial epithelium is simple columnar with ciliated and nonciliated cells.","The lamina propria has many simple branched glands extending down to the myometrium.","The functionalis is supplied by many helical arteries."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Menstruation: progesterone drop, spiral artery constriction, functionalis shed, basalis regrows it","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-menses-cause","fh-tf-basalis","fh-tf-helical","fh-num-cycle","fh-ex-endometrium"]}},{"id":"fh-tf-myoestrogen","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The size and number of myometrial smooth muscle cells are related to the estrogen level.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The myometrium's cells track estrogen: their size and number are related to the estrogen level, and in pregnancy they show both hypertrophy and hyperplasia.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Estrogen governs how big and how many the myometrial cells are.","The lecture ties myometrial cell size and number to estrogen."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Pregnancy and the tract: corpus luteum kept, myometrium grows, cervical plug","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-pb-cl","fh-pb-myo","fh-cervix-mucus","fh-mm-preg","fh-tf-myoestrogen"]}},{"id":"fh-cervix-mucus","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"How do cervical gland secretions differ at ovulation and in pregnancy?","choices":["Watery at ovulation to help sperm migrate; viscous in pregnancy, forming a plug against microbes and sperm","Viscous at ovulation, to trap sperm in the crypts; watery in pregnancy, to flush microorganisms back out of the canal","Watery at ovulation and in pregnancy alike, because the crypts keep secreting the same fluid all the time","They don't change, because the only part of the tract with cyclic changes is the vaginal epithelium"],"answer":0,"ex":"At ovulation the cervical glands secrete a watery fluid that helps sperm migrate. In pregnancy the secretions are more viscous and form a plug that keeps microorganisms and sperm out of the uterus.","src":"slides","why":["Watery fluid eases sperm through at ovulation; thick mucus seals the canal in pregnancy.","The pattern is the reverse: watery at ovulation, viscous in pregnancy.","Cervical secretions change: they turn viscous and plug the canal in pregnancy.","The cervical glands change across the cycle and in pregnancy; the vagina's epithelium changes too."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Pregnancy and the tract: corpus luteum kept, myometrium grows, cervical plug","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-pb-cl","fh-pb-myo","fh-cervix-mucus","fh-mm-preg","fh-tf-myoestrogen"]}},{"id":"fh-tz","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"In a section through the cervix, where does the lining change abruptly from simple columnar to stratified squamous epithelium?","choices":["At the transformation zone, where the cervix projects into the vagina","At the intramural region, where the uterine tube passes into the uterus","At the border between the endometrium's functionalis and its basalis","At the internal opening, where the canal meets the body of the uterus"],"answer":0,"ex":"The cervical canal is lined by mucus-secreting simple columnar cells that form glandular crypts. The region projecting into the vagina is covered by stratified squamous epithelium, and the abrupt change between them is the transformation zone.","src":"slides","why":["The part that projects into the vagina is stratified squamous, and the abrupt change is the transformation zone.","The intramural region is part of the uterine tube, lined by its simple columnar epithelium.","Functionalis and basalis are two layers under the same simple columnar epithelium.","The lining stays columnar there; the abrupt switch is where the cervix meets the vagina."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Linings of the uterine tube, endometrium, cervix and vagina","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-match-epith","fh-id-tube","fh-id-vagina","fh-ex-tube","fh-tz","fh-num-vagina","fh-peg","fh-2s-6"]}},{"id":"fh-id-vagina","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"A biopsy shows stratified squamous epithelium whose cells are full of glycogen, over a vascular lamina propria crowded with lymphocytes and neutrophils, with no glands in the wall. Where was it taken?","choices":["The vagina","The canal of the cervix","The endometrium in the secretory phase","The ampulla of the uterine tube"],"answer":0,"ex":"The vaginal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium over a highly vascular lamina propria with many lymphocytes and neutrophils; the wall has no glands, and the epithelial cells store glycogen that they release as they desquamate.","src":"slides","why":["Stratified squamous, glycogen-rich cells, a leukocyte-rich lamina propria and no glands are the vagina.","The cervical canal is lined by mucus-secreting simple columnar cells in glandular crypts.","The endometrium is simple columnar with branched glands, the opposite of a gland-free wall.","The ampulla is lined by ciliated columnar cells and peg cells on mucosal folds."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Linings of the uterine tube, endometrium, cervix and vagina","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-match-epith","fh-id-tube","fh-id-vagina","fh-ex-tube","fh-tz","fh-num-vagina","fh-peg","fh-2s-6"]}},{"id":"fh-num-vagina","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the vagina are true?\n1. Its mucosa is simple columnar epithelium over a lamina propria.\n2. Its wall is devoid of glands.\n3. Its lamina propria contains many lymphocytes and neutrophils.\n4. Its muscular layer is mainly longitudinal bundles of smooth muscle.","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"The vagina has three layers: a mucosa of stratified squamous epithelium over a vascular lamina propria rich in lymphocytes and neutrophils, a muscular layer of mainly longitudinal smooth muscle, and a dense connective tissue adventitia. There are no glands; the ovulation mucus is cervical.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: the vaginal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium, not simple columnar. It leaves out 4, which is true: the muscular layer is mainly longitudinal smooth muscle bundles.","It includes 1, which is false: the vaginal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium, not simple columnar.","It leaves out 4, which is true: the muscular layer is mainly longitudinal smooth muscle bundles.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: the vaginal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium, not simple columnar."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Linings of the uterine tube, endometrium, cervix and vagina","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-match-epith","fh-id-tube","fh-id-vagina","fh-ex-tube","fh-tz","fh-num-vagina","fh-peg","fh-2s-6"]}},{"id":"fh-ovulation-mucus","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Where does the mucus associated with ovulation come from?","choices":["The glands of the cervix","Glands in the vaginal mucosa","Peg cells of the uterine tube","The vestibular glands within the labia"],"answer":0,"ex":"The vagina has no glands; its mucosa makes local secretions and the vestibular glands in the labia add more, but the mucus associated with ovulation comes from the glands of the cervix.","src":"slides","why":["The mucus associated with ovulation comes from the cervical glands.","The vaginal wall is devoid of glands.","Peg cells make nutrient fluid for the ovum inside the tube.","Vestibular glands add local secretions, but the ovulation mucus is cervical."]},{"id":"fh-mammary-structure","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"How is the mammary gland organized?","choices":["15 to 20 lobes of tubuloalveolar glands radiating from the nipple, separated by fat and collagenous tissue","30 to 50 compound tubuloalveolar glands arranged in three sets, called the mucosal, submucosal and main glands","15 to 20 lobes of simple branched glands that extend down to a three-layered smooth muscle wall","A single coiled tubular gland about 15 cm long, lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium"],"answer":0,"ex":"The mammary gland is 15 to 20 lobes of tubuloalveolar glands radiating out from the nipple, separated by adipose and collagenous connective tissue. It is an accessory organ, related developmentally and functionally to the reproductive system.","src":"slides","why":["That is the mammary gland's layout: 15 to 20 lobes of tubuloalveolar glands set in fat and collagen.","That is the prostate from L13, not the mammary gland.","Simple branched glands reaching smooth muscle describe the endometrium over the myometrium.","That describes a seminal vesicle (L13)."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Milk production (prolactin) versus ejection (oxytocin), and colostrum","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-pb-letdown","fh-pb-colostrum","fh-num-milk","fh-ex-milk","fh-mammary-structure","fh-tf-lactating"]}},{"id":"fh-tf-lactating","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: During lactation, the terminal portions of the ducts branch, grow and develop secretory alveolar units.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"A resting gland is built like a lactating one but smaller; in lactation the terminal ducts branch and grow and form secretory alveolar units.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Lactation adds secretory alveoli at the ends of the branching ducts.","That is how a resting gland becomes a lactating one."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Milk production (prolactin) versus ejection (oxytocin), and colostrum","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-pb-letdown","fh-pb-colostrum","fh-num-milk","fh-ex-milk","fh-mammary-structure","fh-tf-lactating"]}},{"id":"fh-ex-milk","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Human milk contains all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Fructose, as its main carbohydrate","Lactose, as its main carbohydrate","Caseins, as its main proteins","IgA, as its antibody"],"answer":0,"ex":"Milk is lactose (carbohydrate), caseins (protein), lipids, IgA, minerals and electrolytes. Fructose is the seminal vesicles' energy source for sperm.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["This is the exception: milk's carbohydrate is lactose; fructose is the sugar in seminal vesicle fluid (L13).","Lactose is milk's carbohydrate.","Caseins are the milk proteins listed.","Milk is rich in antibodies, and IgA is the one listed."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Milk production (prolactin) versus ejection (oxytocin), and colostrum","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-pb-letdown","fh-pb-colostrum","fh-num-milk","fh-ex-milk","fh-mammary-structure","fh-tf-lactating"]}},{"id":"fh-num-milk","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the mammary gland and milk are true?\n1. A resting, nonsecreting mammary gland is built like a lactating gland but is smaller.\n2. Oxytocin regulates milk ejection.\n3. Colostrum is made for about 3 to 4 weeks after birth.\n4. IgA is a component of milk.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"A resting gland has the same structure as a lactating one, only smaller. Ejection is regulated by oxytocin (synthesis by prolactin), milk carries IgA, and colostrum, rich in protein and IgA, is made for 3 to 4 days after birth.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: milk is rich in antibodies, IgA among them.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: colostrum lasts about 3 to 4 days after birth, not weeks.","It includes 3, which is false: colostrum lasts about 3 to 4 days after birth, not weeks. It leaves out 2, which is true: oxytocin regulates milk ejection.","It leaves out 1, which is true: the resting gland is structurally similar to the lactating gland, only smaller."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Milk production (prolactin) versus ejection (oxytocin), and colostrum","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-pb-letdown","fh-pb-colostrum","fh-num-milk","fh-ex-milk","fh-mammary-structure","fh-tf-lactating"]}},{"id":"fh-2s-6","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The fundus is the broad part of the uterus where the oviducts enter.\n2. Vaginal tissue is rich in elastic fibers and leukocytes but has few sensory nerves.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"The uterus has three regions: the body (the broad part the oviducts enter), the fundus (the rounded region above the openings of the oviducts) and the cervix. Vaginal tissue is rich in elastic fibers and leukocytes and has few sensory nerves; most sensation comes from the external genitalia.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: that is the body; the fundus is the rounded region above the openings of the oviducts.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the vaginal wall is rich in elastic fibers and leukocytes, and most sensation comes from the external genitalia instead.","Statement 1 is false, not true: that is the body; the fundus is the rounded region above the openings of the oviducts. Statement 2 is true, not false: the vaginal wall is rich in elastic fibers and leukocytes, and most sensation comes from the external genitalia instead.","Statement 1 is false: that is the body; the fundus is the rounded region above the openings of the oviducts. Statement 2 is true: the vaginal wall is rich in elastic fibers and leukocytes, and most sensation comes from the external genitalia instead."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Linings of the uterine tube, endometrium, cervix and vagina","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-match-epith","fh-id-tube","fh-id-vagina","fh-ex-tube","fh-tz","fh-num-vagina","fh-peg","fh-2s-6"]}},{"id":"fh-match-epith","lec":"fhisto","type":"match","stem":"Match each site to its lining.","pairs":[["Uterine tube","Simple columnar: ciliated cells and secretory peg cells"],["Endometrium","Simple columnar over a lamina propria of simple branched glands"],["Cervical canal","Mucus-secreting simple columnar cells forming glandular crypts"],["Vagina","Stratified squamous cells that store glycogen"]],"ex":"Three simple columnar linings, told apart by their extra features (peg cells in the tube, branched glands in the endometrium, mucous crypts in the cervical canal), and one stratified squamous lining, the vagina's, which also covers the part of the cervix in the vagina.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Linings of the uterine tube, endometrium, cervix and vagina","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-match-epith","fh-id-tube","fh-id-vagina","fh-ex-tube","fh-tz","fh-num-vagina","fh-peg","fh-2s-6"]}},{"id":"fh-mm-preg","lec":"fhisto","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these changes happen in the female reproductive organs during pregnancy? (Select all that apply.)","choices":["A placental hormone helps maintain the corpus luteum","Myometrial smooth muscle cells undergo hypertrophy and hyperplasia","Cervical secretions become viscous and form a plug","The functionalis is shed each month as the spiral arteries constrict","The cervical glands secrete a watery fluid that helps sperm migrate"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Pregnancy: a placental hormone keeps the corpus luteum (and its progesterone) going, the myometrium grows by hypertrophy and hyperplasia, and the cervix's secretions thicken into a plug.","src":"slides","why":["If pregnancy occurs, the placenta makes a hormone that helps maintain the corpus luteum.","The cells grow larger and more numerous in pregnancy.","The plug keeps microorganisms and sperm out of the uterus.","Shedding follows a progesterone drop, which a maintained corpus luteum prevents.","Watery cervical fluid is the ovulation pattern; pregnancy makes it viscous."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Pregnancy and the tract: corpus luteum kept, myometrium grows, cervical plug","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-pb-cl","fh-pb-myo","fh-cervix-mucus","fh-mm-preg","fh-tf-myoestrogen"]}},{"id":"fh-pb-colostrum","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"She asks whether the milk she's making now is weaker than the milk that will come later. What best describes it?","choices":["It's colostrum, with more protein and IgA than later milk","It's colostrum, with less protein and IgA than later milk","It's mature milk; colostrum appears only from the third week on","It's mostly lactose and water, with antibodies only from the first month"],"answer":0,"ex":"Two days after delivery she is making colostrum, produced for 3 to 4 days after birth, with increased concentrations of protein and IgA. Far from weaker, it is the most antibody-rich milk.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","29-year-old woman at a dental recall visit"],["History","Gave birth 2 days ago; breastfeeding"],["Question","Is my early milk weaker than later milk?"]],"pcase":"fhisto-case1","why":["Colostrum, made for 3 to 4 days after birth, has increased protein and IgA.","Colostrum has more protein and IgA, not less.","Colostrum comes first, for 3 to 4 days after birth.","Milk carries IgA from the start, and colostrum is especially rich in it."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Milk production (prolactin) versus ejection (oxytocin), and colostrum","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-pb-letdown","fh-pb-colostrum","fh-num-milk","fh-ex-milk","fh-mammary-structure","fh-tf-lactating"]}},{"id":"fh-pb-letdown","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"While she nurses in the waiting room, her milk lets down as soon as the baby begins to suckle. Which hormones are at work?","choices":["Oxytocin ejects the milk; prolactin keeps up its synthesis","Prolactin ejects the milk; oxytocin keeps up its synthesis","Estrogen ejects the milk; progesterone keeps up its synthesis","Oxytocin ejects the milk; progesterone keeps up its synthesis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Milk synthesis (production) is regulated by prolactin; milk secretion, the ejection or let-down, is regulated by oxytocin.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","29-year-old woman at a dental recall visit"],["History","Gave birth 2 days ago; breastfeeding"],["Observation","Milk lets down as soon as the baby suckles"]],"pcase":"fhisto-case1","why":["Ejection is oxytocin's; synthesis and production are prolactin's.","The roles are swapped: prolactin for synthesis, oxytocin for ejection.","Estrogen and progesterone act on the gland in pregnancy, but synthesis is regulated by prolactin and ejection by oxytocin.","Ejection is right, but milk synthesis is regulated by prolactin."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Milk production (prolactin) versus ejection (oxytocin), and colostrum","basis":"objectives","ids":["fh-pb-letdown","fh-pb-colostrum","fh-num-milk","fh-ex-milk","fh-mammary-structure","fh-tf-lactating"]}},{"id":"fh-pb-cl","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Without a pregnancy, her corpus luteum would have degenerated weeks ago. Why is it still active?","choices":["A hormone from the placenta helps maintain it","Her LH has kept surging every few days since ovulation","Its fibroblasts have taken over making progesterone","Granulosa cells keep secreting oocyte maturation inhibitor into it"],"answer":0,"ex":"If pregnancy does not occur, the corpus luteum degenerates and becomes a corpus albicans. If it does, the placenta produces a hormone that helps maintain the corpus luteum and its progesterone.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","27-year-old woman, 9 weeks pregnant, here for a dental cleaning"],["Cycles before pregnancy","Regular, 28 days"],["Physician's note","Corpus luteum still active"]],"pcase":"fhisto-case2","why":["If pregnancy occurs, the placenta produces a hormone that helps maintain the corpus luteum.","One LH surge triggers ovulation; the lecture credits the placenta with keeping the corpus luteum.","Fibroblasts lay down collagen as a corpus luteum dies, forming a corpus albicans.","OMI is a follicle's antral secretion; it plays no part in keeping a corpus luteum alive."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Pregnancy and the tract: corpus luteum kept, myometrium grows, cervical plug","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-pb-cl","fh-pb-myo","fh-cervix-mucus","fh-mm-preg","fh-tf-myoestrogen"]}},{"id":"fh-pb-myo","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Her uterus is enlarging as the pregnancy goes on. How do the myometrial smooth muscle cells account for this?","choices":["They grow larger (hypertrophy) and also more numerous (hyperplasia)","They only grow larger, since smooth muscle cells in the uterus can't divide","They only grow more numerous, while each of the cells stays the same size","They are replaced by collagen from fibroblasts, as in a corpus albicans"],"answer":0,"ex":"The size and number of myometrial muscle cells are related to estrogen, and in pregnancy they show both hypertrophy (bigger cells) and hyperplasia (more cells).","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","27-year-old woman, 9 weeks pregnant, here for a dental cleaning"],["Cycles before pregnancy","Regular, 28 days"],["Exam","Uterus enlarging as expected"]],"pcase":"fhisto-case2","why":["In pregnancy the myometrial cells show both hypertrophy and hyperplasia.","They do divide: hyperplasia, an increase in cell number, happens alongside hypertrophy.","The cells also enlarge: hypertrophy happens alongside hyperplasia.","Collagen replacement describes the old corpus luteum, not the growing myometrium."],"likely":{"lec":"fhisto","t":"Pregnancy and the tract: corpus luteum kept, myometrium grows, cervical plug","basis":"slides","ids":["fh-pb-cl","fh-pb-myo","fh-cervix-mucus","fh-mm-preg","fh-tf-myoestrogen"]}},{"id":"fh-pb-abx","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"What best explains why her antibiotic course could lead to this?","choices":["Killing Lactobacillus cuts the lactic acid made from glycogen that keeps the vaginal surface acidic","The antibiotic stops the vaginal glands from secreting the mucus that normally flushes out pathogens","The antibiotic kills the lymphocytes and neutrophils that crowd the vaginal lamina propria","The antibiotic keeps the vaginal epithelium from storing glycogen, so it stops desquamating"],"answer":0,"ex":"Vaginal epithelial cells store glycogen and release it as they desquamate; Lactobacillus species break it down to lactic acid, which keeps the surface acidic and inhibits pathogens. An antibiotic that kills the lactobacilli removes that acid barrier.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman, 6 days after a tooth extraction"],["Medication","Amoxicillin for a dental infection, day 6 of 7"],["Complaint","Vaginal itching and discharge; her physician diagnosed a yeast infection"]],"why":["Lactobacillus turns epithelial glycogen into lactic acid; the acid inhibits pathogens, so losing it lets yeast grow.","The vaginal wall has no glands; the lecture's defense is the acidic surface.","Antibiotics act on bacteria, not on her white cells; the acid barrier is what's lost.","The epithelium still stores glycogen; what's missing is the bacteria that turn it into acid."]},{"id":"fh-pb-meno","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"What best describes her ovaries now?","choices":["They have stopped both their reproductive and their endocrine functions","They still release an oocyte each month but have stopped making steroids","They still make estrogen and progesterone but no longer release oocytes","Their oogonia have begun dividing by mitosis again to restock the follicles"],"answer":0,"ex":"Menopause, usually between 45 and 55 years of age, is when the ovaries cease their reproductive and endocrine functions: no more ovulation and no more ovarian estrogen and progesterone.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","53-year-old woman, new dental patient"],["History","No menstrual period for the past 14 months"],["Medications","None"]],"why":["At menopause the ovaries cease both gamete production and steroid production.","Menopause ends ovulation as well as hormone production.","Menopause ends the ovaries' endocrine function too.","Oogonia stop dividing by the 5th fetal month and never start again."]},{"id":"fh-num-ovary","lec":"fhisto","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the ovary are true?\n1. Its surface epithelium is continuous with the mesothelium.\n2. Its surface epithelium, the germinal epithelium, gives rise to the oogonia.\n3. Its medulla is loose connective tissue rich in blood vessels and nerves.\n4. Its developing follicles lie in the medulla.","choices":["1 only","1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4"],"answer":2,"ex":"The ovary's surface layer is continuous with the mesothelium and, despite the name germinal epithelium, houses no germ cells and makes no gametes. The medulla is vascular loose connective tissue; the follicles are in the cortex.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 3, which is true: the medulla is loose connective tissue rich in blood vessels and nerves.","It includes 2, which is false: the name persists, but the germinal epithelium neither houses germ cells nor gives rise to gametes.","Statements 1 and 3 are true. Statement 2 is false: the name persists, but the germinal epithelium neither houses germ cells nor gives rise to gametes. Statement 4 is false: the developing follicles are in the cortex, the peripheral part of the ovary.","It includes 4, which is false: the developing follicles are in the cortex, the peripheral part of the ovary."]},{"id":"mp-4g-gonadal","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"A person with androgen insensitivity syndrome is XY, has testes and has female external genitalia. Using the lecture's 4 G's of sexual differentiation, which layers are male in this person?","choices":["Genetic and gonadal sex only","Gonadal and phenotypic sex only","Genetic and phenotypic sex only","Genetic, gonadal and phenotypic sex"],"answer":0,"ex":"The 4 G's: genetic sex (XY or XX), gonadal sex (testes or ovaries), phenotypic or genital sex (penis or vagina) and gender (identity, a person's internal sense of their own gender, which can't be read from these findings). The disorders in this lecture are where the layers don't line up: in androgen insensitivity the genetic and gonadal layers are male and the phenotypic layer is female.","src":"slides","why":["XY is the genetic layer and the testes are the gonadal layer; the external genitalia, the phenotypic layer, are female.","The testes make gonadal sex male, but phenotypic (genital) sex is the external genitalia, which are female here; XY makes genetic sex male too.","XY makes genetic sex male, but phenotypic sex is the external genitalia, which are female here; the testes make gonadal sex male.","Phenotypic (genital) sex is the external genitalia, and in androgen insensitivity they are female."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: AMH, testosterone and the Wolffian ducts, DHT and the external genitalia","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-amh","mp-wolffian","mp-dht-targets","mp-fetal-leydig","mp-num-6","mp-4g-gonadal","mp-tf-1"]}},{"id":"mp-amh","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"In a male fetus, which hormone, from which cells, makes the ducts that would otherwise form the female internal structures regress?","choices":["Anti-Müllerian hormone, from the Sertoli cells","Anti-Müllerian hormone, from the Leydig cells","Testosterone, from the Leydig cells","Dihydrotestosterone, from 5-alpha-reductase in peripheral tissues"],"answer":0,"ex":"Three signals build a male: AMH from Sertoli cells removes the Müllerian ducts, testosterone from Leydig cells (stimulated by hCG and LH) builds the internal male structures from the Wolffian ducts, and DHT, made from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase, forms the external genitalia and the prostate.","src":"slides","why":["Sertoli cells secrete AMH, and AMH makes the Müllerian ducts, the female internal ducts, regress.","AMH is a Sertoli cell product; the Leydig cells make testosterone.","Testosterone drives the Wolffian ducts to form the internal male structures; it does not remove the Müllerian ducts.","DHT forms the penis, scrotum and prostate; it plays no part in Müllerian regression."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: AMH, testosterone and the Wolffian ducts, DHT and the external genitalia","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-amh","mp-wolffian","mp-dht-targets","mp-fetal-leydig","mp-num-6","mp-4g-gonadal","mp-tf-1"]}},{"id":"mp-wolffian","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which hormone, acting in the male fetus, drives the Wolffian ducts to form the internal male reproductive structures?","choices":["Testosterone made by the Leydig cells","Dihydrotestosterone made in peripheral tissues","Anti-Müllerian hormone made by the Sertoli cells","Estrogen made from testosterone by aromatase"],"answer":0,"ex":"Testosterone acts directly on the Wolffian ducts. That is why the Wolffian ducts develop in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency (testosterone is made and its receptor works) but regress in androgen insensitivity syndrome (no working androgen receptor).","src":"slides","why":["Testosterone itself acts on the Wolffian ducts to build the internal male structures.","DHT forms the external structures (penis and scrotum) and the prostate, not the Wolffian-derived internal structures.","AMH removes the Müllerian ducts; it does not build the Wolffian ducts.","Aromatase turns testosterone into estradiol; estrogen does not drive Wolffian development."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: AMH, testosterone and the Wolffian ducts, DHT and the external genitalia","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-amh","mp-wolffian","mp-dht-targets","mp-fetal-leydig","mp-num-6","mp-4g-gonadal","mp-tf-1"]}},{"id":"mp-dht-targets","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which structures depend mainly on dihydrotestosterone (DHT), rather than on testosterone itself, as they form in the male fetus?","choices":["The penis, scrotum and prostate","The internal structures formed from the Wolffian ducts","The testes, which form because of the Y chromosome","The Müllerian ducts, which DHT makes regress"],"answer":0,"ex":"Some tissues respond to testosterone and others are more sensitive to DHT. The Wolffian ducts are testosterone tissue; the penis, scrotum and prostate are DHT tissue, which is why a missing 5-alpha-reductase leaves female external genitalia but normal Wolffian structures.","src":"slides","why":["Testosterone is converted peripherally to DHT by 5-alpha-reductase, and DHT forms the penis and scrotum, along with the prostate.","The Wolffian ducts respond to testosterone itself, which is why they still form when DHT is missing.","The testes form because of the Y chromosome; they are the source of the hormones, not a DHT target.","The Müllerian ducts regress because of AMH from the Sertoli cells, not DHT."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: AMH, testosterone and the Wolffian ducts, DHT and the external genitalia","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-amh","mp-wolffian","mp-dht-targets","mp-fetal-leydig","mp-num-6","mp-4g-gonadal","mp-tf-1"]}},{"id":"mp-ais-cause","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"What causes androgen insensitivity syndrome (testicular feminization)?","choices":["An X-linked deficiency of the androgen receptor","A deficiency of 5-alpha-reductase that leaves DHT low","Absent Sertoli cells, so no anti-Müllerian hormone is made","A missing Y chromosome, so the testes never form"],"answer":0,"ex":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome is an X-linked androgen receptor deficiency. The person is XY with testes, AMH removes the Müllerian ducts, but neither testosterone nor DHT can act, so the Wolffian ducts regress and the genitalia and secondary sex characteristics follow the default female pathway.","src":"slides","why":["AIS is an X-linked androgen receptor deficiency: testosterone is made, but its target cells can't respond to it.","That is 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, in which testosterone still acts and the Wolffian ducts develop.","In AIS the Sertoli cells make AMH, so the Müllerian ducts regress; with no AMH there would be a uterus.","People with AIS are XY and have testes; the Y chromosome is present."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome vs 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ais-cause","mp-cs-ais-1","mp-cs-ais-2","mp-cs-5ard-1","mp-cs-5ard-2","mp-mm-both","mp-2s-1"]}},{"id":"mp-btb","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Sertoli cells form tight junctions with one another to create the blood-testis barrier. What is the main purpose of this barrier?","choices":["It keeps the immune system from recognizing and attacking the haploid germ cells","It keeps testosterone in the tubule by binding it to androgen-binding protein","It cools the testis by passing heat from the testicular arteries to the veins","It releases mature spermatids into the lumen as extra cytoplasm is removed"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sertoli cells join by tight junctions to form the blood-testis barrier, which prevents immune recognition of and action against the haploid germ cells. Their other jobs (nourishing sperm, secreting fluid and androgen-binding protein, spermiation) are separate functions of the same cell.","src":"slides","why":["The barrier keeps haploid cells out of reach of immune recognition and attack.","Holding testosterone in the testis is the job of androgen-binding protein, a Sertoli secretion, not the tight junctions.","Cooling comes from the intertwined arteries and veins and from the scrotum, not from the Sertoli barrier.","That is spermiation, another Sertoli job, but not what the tight junctions are for."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sertoli vs Leydig cell functions (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-x-sertoli","mp-num-2","mp-btb","mp-abp","mp-lh-signal","mp-tf-8","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-abp","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Why do Sertoli cells secrete androgen-binding protein (ABP)?","choices":["To keep testosterone levels in the testis high enough to support spermatogenesis","To carry testosterone through the blood to distant tissues such as muscle","To convert testosterone into estradiol inside the seminiferous tubules","To feed testosterone back to the hypothalamus so that GnRH secretion falls"],"answer":0,"ex":"FSH turns on ABP in Sertoli cells, and ABP keeps intratesticular testosterone higher than in the blood. Testosterone is necessary for spermatogenesis, so this local supply matters. It also depends on LH driving the Leydig cells, which is why a man injecting testosterone (which shuts LH off) can have high blood levels and still make few sperm.","src":"slides","why":["ABP holds testosterone in the testis, so levels there are higher than in the blood, and spermatogenesis needs that.","ABP works locally: the lecture describes it as keeping testosterone high inside the testis, not as a blood carrier.","Turning testosterone into estradiol is aromatase's job, not ABP's.","Serum testosterone does inhibit GnRH, but that is feedback on the brain, not what ABP is for."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sertoli vs Leydig cell functions (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-x-sertoli","mp-num-2","mp-btb","mp-abp","mp-lh-signal","mp-tf-8","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-spermiation","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"A drug stops Sertoli cells from stripping the excess cytoplasm off mature spermatids and releasing them into the tubule lumen. Which process is blocked?","choices":["Spermiation","Meiosis of the spermatocytes","Maturation in the epididymis","Emission and ejaculation"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spermiation takes place over several days at the inner edge of the seminiferous epithelium: the spermatid is reshaped, its excess cytoplasm is removed with Sertoli help, and it is released into the lumen. Full maturation still happens later, in the epididymis.","src":"slides","why":["Spermiation is the Sertoli step that removes the spermatids' excess cytoplasm and releases them into the lumen.","Meiosis comes earlier: two divisions turn one spermatocyte into the four spermatids that spermiation later releases.","Maturation comes after release, in the epididymis, where sperm gain motility and forward motility proteins.","Emission moves sperm and seminal fluid into the urethra under sympathetic control, long after spermiation."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Spermatogenesis, spermiation and maturation in the epididymis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-meiosis","mp-num-3","mp-spermiation","mp-epid-time","mp-mm-epid","mp-2s-4","mp-tf-3","mp-acrosome"]}},{"id":"mp-cold","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"A man wades into a cold lake. What happens to his testes and scrotum, and why?","choices":["The testes rise and the scrotum tightens, conserving heat","The testes descend and the scrotum relaxes, letting air circulate","The testes descend, since spermatogenesis works best near core body temperature","Nothing changes, because the scrotum responds only to sexual stimulation"],"answer":0,"ex":"The cremaster and dartos muscles keep the testes near 34 degrees Celsius: testes descend to get cooler and rise to get warmer. A contracted scrotum conserves heat; a relaxed, elongated one lets air circulate and cool it.","src":"slides","why":["Testes rise to get warmer, and the contracted scrotum conserves heat, keeping the testes near 34 degrees Celsius.","Descent and a smooth, relaxed scrotum are the cooling response to warmth, the opposite of what cold triggers.","Spermatogenesis is optimal at about 34 degrees Celsius, a few degrees below core temperature, and cold makes the testes rise.","The scrotum contracts with cold, exercise or sexual stimulation and relaxes when warm."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Temperature control of spermatogenesis","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-cold","mp-tf-4","mp-tf-7"]}},{"id":"mp-meiosis","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"One spermatocyte completes both meiotic divisions. What does it produce?","choices":["Four spermatids, each with 23 unpaired chromosomes","Two spermatids, each with 23 unpaired chromosomes","Four spermatids, each with 46 paired chromosomes","Four spermatids, each with 22 autosomes plus both an X and a Y"],"answer":0,"ex":"Spermatocytes go through two consecutive meiotic divisions, and no new chromosomes are formed in the second. One spermatocyte gives four spermatids with 23 unpaired chromosomes (22 autosomes and an X or a Y), which then mature for about two months into spermatozoa.","src":"slides","why":["Two meiotic divisions turn one spermatocyte into four spermatids, each with 22 autosomes plus an X or a Y.","Two consecutive divisions give four cells, not two.","Meiosis halves the chromosome number: spermatids are haploid, with 23 unpaired chromosomes.","Each spermatid carries one sex chromosome, either an X or a Y, never both."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Spermatogenesis, spermiation and maturation in the epididymis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-meiosis","mp-num-3","mp-spermiation","mp-epid-time","mp-mm-epid","mp-2s-4","mp-tf-3","mp-acrosome"]}},{"id":"mp-epid-time","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Sperm taken straight from the seminiferous tubules cannot swim or fertilize an ovum. Where do they gain these abilities?","choices":["In the epididymis, starting about 18 to 24 hours after they arrive","In the seminiferous tubule, as Sertoli cells release them by spermiation","In the prostatic urethra, when they meet fluid that neutralizes acidity","In the vas deferens, during emission under sympathetic control"],"answer":0,"ex":"Full sperm maturation occurs in the epididymis, not the testis. Epididymal fluid (hormones, enzymes and nutrients) lets the sperm gain motility, lose cytoplasm and acquire forward motility proteins and acrosomal stabilizing factors, and sperm can be stored there for months.","src":"slides","why":["Sperm enter the epididymis non-motile and begin to gain fertilizing capability after about 18 to 24 hours there.","Spermiation releases spermatids into the lumen, but those sperm still cannot swim or fertilize.","Prostatic fluid helps motility at ejaculation by neutralizing acidity, but maturation happens earlier, in the epididymis.","The vas deferens carries sperm to the urethra during ejaculation; it is not where they mature."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Spermatogenesis, spermiation and maturation in the epididymis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-meiosis","mp-num-3","mp-spermiation","mp-epid-time","mp-mm-epid","mp-2s-4","mp-tf-3","mp-acrosome"]}},{"id":"mp-acrosome","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"A sperm has a normal head, mitochondria and tail but no acrosome. Which step would it most likely fail at?","choices":["Entering the ovum, since it lacks hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes","Swimming, since the acrosome supplies the energy for the tail","Carrying its chromosomes, since the acrosome holds the nuclear material","Gaining forward motility in the epididymis, since the acrosome makes those proteins"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sperm parts: the head (nuclear material, fertilizes the ovum), the acrosome on top (formed from the Golgi, with hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes that are important for entry into the ovum), mitochondria around the spiral body, and a cilia-like tail whose flagellar movement gives motility.","src":"slides","why":["The acrosome, formed from the Golgi, holds hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes, which are important for the sperm's entry into the ovum.","Motility comes from the tail's flagellar movement, with mitochondria around the spiral body; the acrosome is the enzyme cap on the head.","The nuclear material is the head itself, which fertilizes the ovum; the acrosome is the cap on top of it.","Sperm acquire forward motility proteins from the epididymis's fluids as they mature; the acrosome does not make them."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Spermatogenesis, spermiation and maturation in the epididymis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-meiosis","mp-num-3","mp-spermiation","mp-epid-time","mp-mm-epid","mp-2s-4","mp-tf-3","mp-acrosome"]}},{"id":"mp-rate-limit","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the rate-limiting step in making testosterone from cholesterol in the Leydig cell?","choices":["The P450 side-chain cleavage enzyme, a desmolase","Aromatase, also called estrogen synthase","5-alpha-reductase in peripheral tissues","Conversion of adrenal androgens into testosterone"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cholesterol is the precursor for both testosterone and estrogen, and the P450 side-chain cleavage enzyme (a desmolase) is the rate-limiting step. LH speeds the pathway by turning on enzymes like P450scc; aromatase and 5-alpha-reductase then act on the finished testosterone.","src":"slides","why":["Side-chain cleavage of cholesterol by P450scc, a desmolase, is the rate-limiting step.","Aromatase acts after testosterone is made, converting it to estradiol.","5-alpha-reductase converts finished testosterone to DHT, outside the testis.","Leydig cells can convert adrenal androgens to testosterone, but that is a late step, not the rate-limiting one."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Testosterone synthesis and metabolism: desmolase, aromatase, 5-alpha-reductase","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-rate-limit","mp-aromatase","mp-2s-6","mp-2s-2","mp-x-tprod","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-aromatase","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"What does aromatase do?","choices":["Converts testosterone to estradiol and androstenedione to estrone","Converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone in peripheral tissues","Cleaves the side chain of cholesterol, the rate-limiting step","Converts estradiol to testosterone and estrone to androstenedione"],"answer":0,"ex":"Aromatase turns androgens into estrogens: androstenedione to estrone and testosterone to estradiol. In the male fetus and newborn it is in the Sertoli cells (FSH); from puberty on it is in the Leydig cells (LH).","src":"slides","why":["Aromatase (estrogen synthase) catalyzes the last steps of estrogen synthesis from androgens.","That is 5-alpha-reductase, which makes DHT for the external genitalia and prostate.","That is the P450 side-chain cleavage enzyme, a desmolase, at the start of the pathway.","This is the reverse of aromatase: it turns androgens into estrogens, not estrogens into androgens."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Testosterone synthesis and metabolism: desmolase, aromatase, 5-alpha-reductase","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-rate-limit","mp-aromatase","mp-2s-6","mp-2s-2","mp-x-tprod","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-lh-signal","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"LH binds its receptor on a Leydig cell. What does the signal that follows mainly do?","choices":["Activates PKA, which turns on enzymes such as P450scc that build testosterone","Activates PKA, which turns on androgen-binding protein and inhibin","Activates guanylate cyclase, which raises cGMP and relaxes smooth muscle","Triggers the release of anti-Müllerian hormone, which removes the Müllerian ducts"],"answer":0,"ex":"Two cells, two gonadotropins, one second messenger: both the LH receptor (Leydig) and the FSH receptor (Sertoli) activate PKA. LH turns on steroid enzymes such as P450scc; FSH turns on aromatase, ABP and inhibin.","src":"slides","why":["LH receptor signaling activates PKA and the expression of enzymes like P450scc that convert cholesterol to testosterone.","Those are genes FSH turns on in the Sertoli cells, the other half of the two-cell model.","That is the nitric oxide pathway of erection in the corpus cavernosum.","AMH comes from the fetal Sertoli cells, not from LH acting on Leydig cells."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sertoli vs Leydig cell functions (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-x-sertoli","mp-num-2","mp-btb","mp-abp","mp-lh-signal","mp-tf-8","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-gnrh-cont","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"A man is given GnRH continuously instead of in pulses. What happens?","choices":["LH secretion falls, so testosterone falls","LH secretion rises, so testosterone rises","LH secretion rises, but testosterone falls because Leydig cells stop responding","FSH falls because of inhibin, while LH and testosterone stay normal"],"answer":0,"ex":"GnRH and LH secretion must be pulsatile to maintain normal testosterone production. Continuous GnRH decreases LH secretion and therefore testosterone, the opposite of what more GnRH might suggest.","src":"slides","why":["GnRH must be pulsatile; continuous GnRH lowers LH secretion, and with less LH, testosterone production drops.","More GnRH does not mean more LH: the pituitary needs pulses, and continuous exposure lowers LH.","The change is at the pituitary: LH itself falls with continuous GnRH.","Inhibin acts on FSH only; continuous GnRH lowers LH and, through it, testosterone."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis: LH on Leydig cells, FSH on Sertoli cells, testosterone and inhibin feedback, pulsatile GnRH","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-match-axis","mp-num-1","mp-2s-3","mp-gnrh-cont","mp-x-tprod","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-pit-pattern","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"A man's serum testosterone is low, and his LH and FSH are low as well. Where is the problem most likely?","choices":["Above the testis, in the pituitary or hypothalamus","In the testes, whose failing Leydig cells send less testosterone back","In the tubules alone, whose damaged Sertoli cells make less inhibin","Outside the axis, from injected testosterone suppressing GnRH and LH"],"answer":0,"ex":"Read the pattern with the feedback rules: LH drives testosterone, and testosterone and inhibin hold LH and FSH down. When the pituitary (or the GnRH above it) fails, everything downstream falls; when the testis fails, LH and FSH rise because the brake is gone; injected testosterone gives low LH and FSH with a high testosterone.","src":"hub","why":["Low LH and FSH with a low testosterone means the testes aren't being stimulated: without LH the Leydig cells make little testosterone.","Failing testes lower testosterone, which takes the brake off GnRH and LH, so LH and FSH would be high, not low.","Low inhibin raises FSH on its own; it would not lower LH, FSH and testosterone together.","Injected testosterone does lower LH and FSH, but his blood testosterone would then be high, not low."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Reading LH, FSH and testosterone: failing testes, failing pituitary, injected testosterone","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-cs-steroid-1","mp-cs-steroid-2","mp-cs-primary","mp-pit-pattern","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-sv-volume","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Both of a man's seminal vesicles are removed. Which change in his ejaculate would you expect?","choices":["Its volume falls by half or more, and it loses most of its fructose","It stays coagulated, because the seminal vesicles make the PSA that prevents clotting","It contains no sperm, because the seminal vesicles are where sperm are stored","It loses the early lubricating mucus that flushes the urethra before ejaculation"],"answer":0,"ex":"Semen is sperm plus seminal fluid, and most of the fluid, an estimated 50 to 80 percent, comes from the seminal vesicles (Dr. Herr's histology lecture says about 70 percent). Their fructose is the sperm's energy source until fertilization. The prostate adds a thin, milky fluid and PSA, and the bulbourethral glands add lubricating mucus early on.","src":"slides","why":["Seminal vesicle fluid is an estimated 50 to 80 percent of semen and is its source of fructose, so both drop.","PSA is a prostate product; the seminal vesicles add fibrinogen, a clotting factor, instead.","Sperm are stored in the epididymis and reach the urethra through the vas deferens, so they are still there.","That mucus comes from the bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands, which are untouched."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The accessory glands and semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-sv-volume","mp-x-sv","mp-psa","mp-match-glands","mp-num-4","mp-seq-route","mp-x-ais"]}},{"id":"mp-psa","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"What does prostate-specific antigen (PSA) do in semen?","choices":["It is a serine protease that cuts proteins and prevents seminal coagulation","It supplies fructose, the energy source that keeps sperm alive until fertilization","It lubricates and flushes the urethra early in sexual excitation","It keeps the female tract from treating the sperm as invaders"],"answer":0,"ex":"PSA is a serine protease from the prostate that breaks proteins into smaller peptides and so prevents seminal coagulation, helping sperm reach the egg. The prostate's thin, milky fluid also neutralizes acidity, which enhances sperm motility and fertility.","src":"slides","why":["PSA, made by the prostate's columnar epithelium, breaks proteins into smaller peptides and so prevents seminal coagulation.","Fructose comes from the seminal vesicles, not from PSA.","That is the clear, thick mucus of the bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands.","His notes give that role to the seminal vesicles' prostaglandins."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The accessory glands and semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-sv-volume","mp-x-sv","mp-psa","mp-match-glands","mp-num-4","mp-seq-route","mp-x-ais"]}},{"id":"mp-ans","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pairing of the stages of the male sexual act with their autonomic control is correct?","choices":["Erection and lubrication: parasympathetic; emission and ejaculation: sympathetic","Erection and lubrication: sympathetic; emission and ejaculation: parasympathetic","Erection: parasympathetic; lubrication, emission and ejaculation: sympathetic","Erection: sympathetic; lubrication: parasympathetic; ejaculation: sympathetic"],"answer":0,"ex":"The slide's mnemonic is \"Point and Shoot\": erection (point) and lubrication are parasympathetic, and emission and ejaculation (shoot) are sympathetic.","src":"slides","why":["\"Point and shoot\": point (erection, with lubrication) is parasympathetic; shoot (emission and ejaculation) is sympathetic.","This swaps the two divisions: erection and lubrication are parasympathetic, emission and ejaculation sympathetic.","Lubrication is a parasympathetic function, like erection.","Erection is driven largely by the parasympathetic system, through acetylcholine and nitric oxide."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Autonomic control of erection, emission and ejaculation (\"point and shoot\") and sildenafil","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ans","mp-erect-nt","mp-seq-erection","mp-cs-pde5-1","mp-cs-pde5-2","mp-num-5","mp-2s-5","mp-tf-5"]}},{"id":"mp-erect-nt","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons in the erectile tissue start an erection by releasing which neurotransmitters?","choices":["Acetylcholine and nitric oxide","Norepinephrine and nitric oxide","Acetylcholine and cyclic GMP","Norepinephrine and prostaglandins"],"answer":0,"ex":"Erection is largely parasympathetic: postganglionic neurons release acetylcholine and nitric oxide, the penile vessels dilate, the cavernous tissue engorges, and the engorged tissue compresses the draining veins.","src":"slides","why":["These two dilate the penile blood vessels, so the cavernous tissue fills with blood.","Norepinephrine belongs to the sympathetic side, which drives emission and ejaculation, not erection.","cGMP is the second messenger made inside the smooth muscle after NO activates guanylate cyclase; nerves don't release it.","Prostaglandins in semen come from the seminal vesicles, and norepinephrine is sympathetic."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Autonomic control of erection, emission and ejaculation (\"point and shoot\") and sildenafil","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ans","mp-erect-nt","mp-seq-erection","mp-cs-pde5-1","mp-cs-pde5-2","mp-num-5","mp-2s-5","mp-tf-5"]}},{"id":"mp-fetal-leydig","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"In the male fetus, what stimulates the Leydig cells to make testosterone?","choices":["hCG and LH","FSH and inhibin","Anti-Müllerian hormone","Dihydrotestosterone"],"answer":0,"ex":"In the fetus, hCG and LH act on the Leydig cells, which make the testosterone that builds the Wolffian structures. LH keeps this role for life: it is the Leydig cell's signal in the adult as well.","src":"slides","why":["The development slide shows hCG and LH driving the fetal Leydig cells to make testosterone.","FSH acts on Sertoli cells, and inhibin suppresses FSH; neither drives Leydig testosterone.","AMH comes from the Sertoli cells and makes the Müllerian ducts regress.","DHT is made from testosterone; it is a product of the pathway, not the signal that starts it."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: AMH, testosterone and the Wolffian ducts, DHT and the external genitalia","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-amh","mp-wolffian","mp-dht-targets","mp-fetal-leydig","mp-num-6","mp-4g-gonadal","mp-tf-1"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-steroid-1","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Testosterone's negative feedback acts on GnRH and LH, and inhibin is the hormone that holds FSH down. Why is his FSH low too?","choices":["Less GnRH, which drives FSH as well as LH","Extra inhibin from his Sertoli cells, holding FSH down","Failed Sertoli cells that no longer respond to FSH","GnRH reaching his pituitary continuously, not in pulses"],"answer":0,"ex":"Testosterone from any source acts as negative feedback: it inhibits GnRH from the hypothalamus and LH from the pituitary. GnRH drives both LH and FSH, so with less GnRH, FSH falls too. A high testosterone with low LH and FSH means the testosterone is coming from outside the axis.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old man at a dental visit"],["Medications","Weekly testosterone injections for the past 14 months, from a men's health clinic"],["History","He and his partner have been trying to conceive for a year"],["Serum testosterone","1,240 ng/dL (typical adult male range about 300-1,000)"],["LH","0.3 IU/L (typical adult male range about 1.5-9)"],["FSH","0.4 IU/L (typical adult male range about 1.5-12)"],["Semen analysis","Very low sperm count"]],"pcase":"mphys-case1","why":["Testosterone inhibits GnRH from the hypothalamus, and GnRH drives both LH and FSH, so both fall.","Inhibin comes from Sertoli cells activated by FSH; with his FSH this low, they have no stimulus to make extra.","Failing Sertoli cells would make less inhibin, so FSH would rise, not fall.","Continuous GnRH lowers LH, but nothing here makes his GnRH continuous: the injected testosterone suppresses it."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Reading LH, FSH and testosterone: failing testes, failing pituitary, injected testosterone","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-cs-steroid-1","mp-cs-steroid-2","mp-cs-primary","mp-pit-pattern","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-steroid-2","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"His blood testosterone is high, yet his sperm count is very low. What best explains this?","choices":["Without LH and FSH, his testes lose their high local testosterone and FSH stimulus","Injected testosterone cannot cross the blood-testis barrier, so the tubules get none","His high testosterone is turned into DHT in the testis, and DHT stops spermatogenesis","Spermatogenesis needs FSH alone, so testosterone has no bearing on his sperm count"],"answer":0,"ex":"Testosterone in the testis is normally higher than in the blood, kept there by LH-driven Leydig production and Sertoli androgen-binding protein. Injected testosterone shuts off LH and FSH, so the testis loses both its local testosterone and its FSH stimulus, and sperm production falls even while blood levels are high.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old man at a dental visit"],["Medications","Weekly testosterone injections for the past 14 months, from a men's health clinic"],["History","He and his partner have been trying to conceive for a year"],["Serum testosterone","1,240 ng/dL (typical adult male range about 300-1,000)"],["LH","0.3 IU/L (typical adult male range about 1.5-9)"],["FSH","0.4 IU/L (typical adult male range about 1.5-12)"],["Semen analysis","Very low sperm count"]],"pcase":"mphys-case1","why":["Spermatogenesis needs FSH acting on Sertoli cells and the high intratesticular testosterone that LH and ABP maintain; suppressing LH and FSH removes both.","The blood-testis barrier shields haploid cells from the immune system; it is not why his sperm count fell.","5-alpha-reductase makes DHT mainly in peripheral tissues, and the lecture gives DHT no role in stopping sperm production.","Testosterone is necessary for spermatogenesis, which is why ABP keeps it high inside the testis."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Reading LH, FSH and testosterone: failing testes, failing pituitary, injected testosterone","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-cs-steroid-1","mp-cs-steroid-2","mp-cs-primary","mp-pit-pattern","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-ais-1","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which condition best explains her findings?","choices":["Androgen insensitivity syndrome","5-alpha-reductase deficiency","Failure of the Sertoli cells to make anti-Müllerian hormone","Absence of the Y chromosome, so no testes formed"],"answer":0,"ex":"The deciding findings: no uterus (AMH worked) and no internal male ducts (testosterone could not act), with female genitalia and female secondary sex characteristics. Testosterone is made, but without a working androgen receptor neither it nor DHT can act.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","17-year-old raised as a girl, seen because she has never had a menstrual period"],["Exam","Female external genitalia; breast development normal for her age"],["Karyotype","46,XY"],["Imaging","No uterus; a testis in each groin; no internal male ducts"],["Serum testosterone","In the normal adult male range"]],"pcase":"mphys-case2","why":["XY with testes, no uterus, no internal male ducts, and female genitalia and breasts fit an X-linked androgen receptor deficiency.","In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency the Wolffian ducts develop, but she has no internal male ducts.","Without AMH the Müllerian ducts would persist and she would have a uterus.","She is 46,XY and has testes."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome vs 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ais-cause","mp-cs-ais-1","mp-cs-ais-2","mp-cs-5ard-1","mp-cs-5ard-2","mp-mm-both","mp-2s-1"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-ais-2","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does she have no uterus?","choices":["Her testes' Sertoli cells made AMH, which made the Müllerian ducts regress","Her testosterone acted on the Müllerian ducts and made them regress","Her Wolffian ducts regressed, and the uterus forms from the Wolffian ducts","DHT made by 5-alpha-reductase removed her Müllerian ducts"],"answer":0,"ex":"In androgen insensitivity both duct systems are lost, for different reasons: the Müllerian ducts regress because the testes' Sertoli cells still make AMH, and the Wolffian ducts regress because androgens can't act on them.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","17-year-old raised as a girl, seen because she has never had a menstrual period"],["Exam","Female external genitalia; breast development normal for her age"],["Karyotype","46,XY"],["Imaging","No uterus; a testis in each groin; no internal male ducts"],["Serum testosterone","In the normal adult male range"]],"pcase":"mphys-case2","why":["AMH needs no androgen receptor, so the Müllerian ducts regressed even though androgens could not act.","Testosterone does not remove the Müllerian ducts, and in AIS it cannot act at all.","The uterus comes from the Müllerian ducts; the Wolffian ducts would have formed internal male structures.","DHT forms the penis, scrotum and prostate, and in AIS it cannot act; AMH removes the Müllerian ducts."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome vs 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ais-cause","mp-cs-ais-1","mp-cs-ais-2","mp-cs-5ard-1","mp-cs-5ard-2","mp-mm-both","mp-2s-1"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-5ard-1","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which defect best explains her findings?","choices":["5-alpha-reductase deficiency","Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Absent anti-Müllerian hormone","Absent Leydig cell testosterone"],"answer":0,"ex":"XY, testes, internal male ducts present, no uterus and female genitalia point to a missing DHT step. Children with 5-alpha-reductase deficiency are often raised as girls and may develop male secondary sex characteristics at puberty, when testosterone rises.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","15-year-old raised as a girl"],["History","Over the past year her voice has deepened and her muscle mass has increased"],["Karyotype","46,XY"],["Imaging","No uterus; a testis in each groin; internal male ducts present"],["Genitalia","Female-appearing at birth"]],"pcase":"mphys-case3","why":["Testosterone works (Wolffian ducts, male changes at puberty), but no DHT was made, so the external genitalia stayed female.","In AIS the Wolffian ducts regress and puberty brings no male changes, because androgens cannot act.","Without AMH the Müllerian ducts would persist and she would have a uterus.","Without testosterone the Wolffian ducts would not form and puberty would bring no male changes."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome vs 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ais-cause","mp-cs-ais-1","mp-cs-ais-2","mp-cs-5ard-1","mp-cs-5ard-2","mp-mm-both","mp-2s-1"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-5ard-2","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which pair of lab results would you expect?","choices":["Testosterone in the male range; DHT low","Testosterone low; DHT low","Testosterone in the male range; DHT in the male range","Testosterone low; DHT in the male range"],"answer":0,"ex":"The enzyme sits between the two hormones: testosterone is made normally, and DHT, which needs 5-alpha-reductase, is deficient or absent. Testosterone tissues (Wolffian ducts, pubertal changes) develop; DHT tissues (penis, scrotum, prostate) don't.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","15-year-old raised as a girl"],["History","Over the past year her voice has deepened and her muscle mass has increased"],["Karyotype","46,XY"],["Imaging","No uterus; a testis in each groin; internal male ducts present"],["Genitalia","Female-appearing at birth"]],"pcase":"mphys-case3","why":["5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT, so testosterone is made but DHT is deficient.","Low testosterone would also have stopped the Wolffian ducts and the male changes at puberty.","Normal DHT would have formed male external genitalia in the fetus.","DHT is made from testosterone by the missing enzyme, so it cannot be normal here."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome vs 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ais-cause","mp-cs-ais-1","mp-cs-ais-2","mp-cs-5ard-1","mp-cs-5ard-2","mp-mm-both","mp-2s-1"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-inhibin","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains his high FSH with a normal LH?","choices":["Less inhibin from the damaged Sertoli cells, which no longer holds FSH down","Less testosterone from the Leydig cells, which removes the negative feedback on LH","Continuous rather than pulsatile GnRH secretion from the hypothalamus","Too much androgen-binding protein reaching the pituitary"],"answer":0,"ex":"Each testis cell feeds back on its own gonadotropin: Leydig cell testosterone on GnRH and LH, Sertoli cell inhibin on FSH. Damage to the tubules with healthy Leydig cells takes away inhibin alone, so FSH rises while LH and testosterone stay normal.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","32-year-old man evaluated for infertility"],["Testis biopsy","Damaged seminiferous tubules; Leydig cells look normal"],["FSH","26 IU/L (typical adult male range about 1.5-12)"],["LH","5.1 IU/L (typical adult male range about 1.5-9)"],["Serum testosterone","560 ng/dL (typical adult male range about 300-1,000)"],["Semen analysis","Very low sperm count"]],"why":["Sertoli cells secrete inhibin, which inhibits FSH; with the tubules damaged, FSH rises on its own.","His Leydig cells and testosterone are normal, and a fall in testosterone would raise LH too.","Continuous GnRH would lower LH and testosterone, not raise FSH alone.","ABP works inside the testis to keep testosterone high; it is not the hormone that holds FSH down."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Reading LH, FSH and testosterone: failing testes, failing pituitary, injected testosterone","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-cs-steroid-1","mp-cs-steroid-2","mp-cs-primary","mp-pit-pattern","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-primary","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Where is the problem most likely, and why are his LH and FSH high?","choices":["In the testes: their low output removes negative feedback, so LH and FSH rise","In the pituitary: it overproduces LH and FSH, which shuts testosterone down","In the hypothalamus: it secretes GnRH continuously, which raises LH and FSH","Outside the axis: he is injecting testosterone, which raises LH and FSH"],"answer":0,"ex":"When serum testosterone falls, GnRH and LH secretion rise; when inhibin falls, FSH rises. Low testosterone with high LH and FSH therefore means the testes themselves are failing, while low testosterone with low LH and FSH points above the testis.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","44-year-old man evaluated for low testosterone"],["Serum testosterone","110 ng/dL (typical adult male range about 300-1,000)"],["LH","31 IU/L (typical adult male range about 1.5-9)"],["FSH","38 IU/L (typical adult male range about 1.5-12)"]],"why":["Low testosterone (and inhibin) from failing testes takes the brake off GnRH, LH and FSH.","LH stimulates Leydig cells to make testosterone; extra LH would raise testosterone, not lower it.","Continuous GnRH lowers LH and testosterone; it would not produce a high LH.","Injected testosterone suppresses GnRH and LH, and his testosterone is low, not high."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Reading LH, FSH and testosterone: failing testes, failing pituitary, injected testosterone","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-cs-steroid-1","mp-cs-steroid-2","mp-cs-primary","mp-pit-pattern","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-pde5-1","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"How does sildenafil work?","choices":["It inhibits PDE5, so cGMP made in response to nitric oxide is broken down slowly","It releases nitric oxide into the corpus cavernosum, even without arousal","It activates guanylate cyclase directly, so the smooth muscle makes more cGMP","It blocks sympathetic nerves so that emission and ejaculation are delayed"],"answer":0,"ex":"Nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase, raising cGMP, which relaxes the smooth muscle of the corpus cavernosum so blood flows in. PDE5 breaks cGMP down; sildenafil inhibits PDE5, so the cGMP signal lasts longer.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","61-year-old man updating his medical history before a dental extraction"],["Medications","Sildenafil, taken as needed"],["He asks","\"How does this pill actually work?\""]],"pcase":"mphys-case4","why":["PDE5 degrades cGMP in the corpus cavernosum; blocking it lets cGMP build up and relax the smooth muscle.","Sildenafil releases no NO and has no direct relaxant effect; the NO comes from nerves during sexual stimulation.","Guanylate cyclase is activated by NO; sildenafil acts on the breakdown of cGMP, not its production.","Sildenafil acts on an enzyme in the smooth muscle, not on the sympathetic nerves that drive emission."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Autonomic control of erection, emission and ejaculation (\"point and shoot\") and sildenafil","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ans","mp-erect-nt","mp-seq-erection","mp-cs-pde5-1","mp-cs-pde5-2","mp-num-5","mp-2s-5","mp-tf-5"]}},{"id":"mp-cs-pde5-2","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"He adds that the pill does nothing unless he is sexually aroused. What best explains this?","choices":["It has no direct relaxant effect; it only boosts cGMP after arousal releases NO","It must first be converted into nitric oxide, a step that happens only during arousal","Erection is sympathetic, and the drug works only while sympathetic tone is high","PDE5 appears only during arousal, so before that the drug has nothing to block"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sildenafil has no direct relaxant effect on the corpus cavernosum. It works only when sexual stimulation releases nitric oxide locally; then the blocked PDE5 lets cGMP rise, the smooth muscle relaxes and blood flows in.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","61-year-old man updating his medical history before a dental extraction"],["Medications","Sildenafil, taken as needed"],["He asks","\"How does this pill actually work?\""]],"pcase":"mphys-case4","why":["Sildenafil enhances the NO signal by blocking cGMP breakdown, so with no NO from sexual stimulation there is no cGMP to protect.","Sildenafil is not converted to NO; it inhibits PDE5, the enzyme that degrades cGMP.","Erection is driven largely by the parasympathetic system; the sympathetic system drives emission and ejaculation.","The limit is the missing NO and cGMP signal, not the enzyme: PDE5 is what breaks cGMP down in the corpus cavernosum."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Autonomic control of erection, emission and ejaculation (\"point and shoot\") and sildenafil","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ans","mp-erect-nt","mp-seq-erection","mp-cs-pde5-1","mp-cs-pde5-2","mp-num-5","mp-2s-5","mp-tf-5"]}},{"id":"mp-x-sertoli","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is a function of Sertoli cells EXCEPT:","choices":["Secreting the fructose that fuels sperm after ejaculation","Secreting aqueous fluid into the tubule lumen to aid sperm transport","Secreting androgen-binding protein to keep testicular testosterone high","Secreting inhibin, which inhibits FSH secretion"],"answer":0,"ex":"Sertoli cells are the FSH cell: they nourish sperm inside the tubule, form the blood-testis barrier, secrete tubular fluid, ABP and inhibin, phagocytose defective sperm, carry out spermiation and, in the fetus, make AMH. The fructose in semen comes from the seminal vesicles, at ejaculation.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Fructose is a seminal vesicle secretion that joins the sperm at ejaculation; Sertoli cells nourish sperm inside the tubule.","Sertoli cells secrete fluid into the lumen of the seminiferous tubule, which helps move the sperm along.","ABP is a Sertoli secretion that keeps intratesticular testosterone high.","Sertoli cells secrete inhibin, the negative feedback on FSH."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sertoli vs Leydig cell functions (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-x-sertoli","mp-num-2","mp-btb","mp-abp","mp-lh-signal","mp-tf-8","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-x-sv","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Seminal vesicle secretions contain each of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Prostate-specific antigen","Fructose","Prostaglandins","Fibrinogen"],"answer":0,"ex":"Seminal vesicles: fructose, citric acid and other nutrients, prostaglandins and fibrinogen, in an alkaline fluid that is 50 to 80 percent of semen. Prostate: thin, milky fluid with citrate, phosphate, calcium, a clotting enzyme, profibrinolysin and PSA.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["PSA is a serine protease made by the prostate, which keeps semen from coagulating.","Fructose is the seminal vesicles' energy source for the sperm.","Seminal vesicles secrete prostaglandins; his notes say they keep the vagina from treating sperm as invaders.","Seminal vesicles secrete fibrinogen, one of the clotting factors his notes say help keep sperm alive longer."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The accessory glands and semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-sv-volume","mp-x-sv","mp-psa","mp-match-glands","mp-num-4","mp-seq-route","mp-x-ais"]}},{"id":"mp-x-ais","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following helps protect sperm from acid EXCEPT:","choices":["Prostate-specific antigen breaking proteins into smaller peptides","The alkaline fluid of the seminal vesicles","The thin, milky fluid of the prostate","The mucus of the bulbourethral glands"],"answer":0,"ex":"Three secretions guard sperm against acid: the alkaline seminal vesicle fluid (for the acidic vagina), prostatic fluid (neutralizes the other fluids, which enhances motility and fertility) and bulbourethral mucus (neutralizes the acidic urethra). PSA does a different job: it breaks proteins into smaller peptides and prevents seminal coagulation.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["PSA is a serine protease that prevents seminal coagulation; the lecture gives it no role against acid.","The slide notes say the seminal vesicles' alkaline fluid helps keep sperm alive in the acidic vagina.","Prostatic fluid neutralizes the acidity of the other fluids, which greatly enhances sperm motility and fertility.","Bulbourethral mucus neutralizes the acidic pH of the urethra, as well as lubricating it."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The accessory glands and semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-sv-volume","mp-x-sv","mp-psa","mp-match-glands","mp-num-4","mp-seq-route","mp-x-ais"]}},{"id":"mp-x-tprod","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is needed for normal testosterone production by the testes EXCEPT:","choices":["Continuous, steady GnRH secretion from the hypothalamus","LH acting on receptors on the Leydig cells","Cholesterol as the starting material","The P450 side-chain cleavage enzyme (a desmolase)"],"answer":0,"ex":"Normal testosterone needs pulsatile GnRH, LH on the Leydig cells, cholesterol and the rate-limiting side-chain cleavage enzyme. Steady, continuous GnRH does the opposite: it lowers LH and testosterone.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["GnRH must be pulsatile; continuous GnRH lowers LH and therefore testosterone.","LH is the signal for Leydig cells to make testosterone.","Testosterone is synthesized from cholesterol.","This rate-limiting enzyme starts the conversion of cholesterol to testosterone."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Testosterone synthesis and metabolism: desmolase, aromatase, 5-alpha-reductase","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-rate-limit","mp-aromatase","mp-2s-6","mp-2s-2","mp-x-tprod","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-mm-epid","lec":"mphys","type":"multi","stem":"Which changes happen to sperm as they mature in the epididymis? Select all that apply.","choices":["They gain motility","They acquire forward motility proteins and acrosomal stabilizing factors","They lose cytoplasm","They complete the second meiotic division","They take up fructose from the seminal vesicles"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"In the epididymis, fluids with hormones, enzymes and nutrients let sperm gain motility, lose cytoplasm and acquire forward motility proteins and acrosomal stabilizing factors. Meiosis is long finished by then, and seminal vesicle fluid joins only at ejaculation.","src":"slides","why":["Sperm arrive in the epididymis non-motile and gain motility there.","Epididymal fluids help sperm acquire these as they mature.","Sperm keep losing cytoplasm as they mature in the epididymis.","Both meiotic divisions finish in the seminiferous tubule, before spermatids form.","Seminal vesicle fluid joins the sperm only at ejaculation, not in the epididymis."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Spermatogenesis, spermiation and maturation in the epididymis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-meiosis","mp-num-3","mp-spermiation","mp-epid-time","mp-mm-epid","mp-2s-4","mp-tf-3","mp-acrosome"]}},{"id":"mp-mm-both","lec":"mphys","type":"multi","stem":"Which findings occur in BOTH androgen insensitivity syndrome and 5-alpha-reductase deficiency? Select all that apply.","choices":["An XY karyotype","Regressed Müllerian ducts","Female external genitalia","Developed Wolffian ducts","Male secondary sex characteristics at puberty"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Both are XY with testes, regressed Müllerian ducts and female external genitalia. They differ in whether testosterone itself can act: in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency it can (Wolffian ducts, male changes at puberty); in AIS it can't.","src":"slides","why":["Both conditions occur in XY individuals with testes.","In both, the testes' Sertoli cells make AMH, so the Müllerian ducts regress.","In both, DHT can't act on the external genitalia: no receptor in AIS, no DHT in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency.","Only in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, where testosterone still acts; in AIS the Wolffian ducts regress.","Only in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, when testosterone rises; AIS has female secondary sex characteristics."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome vs 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ais-cause","mp-cs-ais-1","mp-cs-ais-2","mp-cs-5ard-1","mp-cs-5ard-2","mp-mm-both","mp-2s-1"]}},{"id":"mp-match-axis","lec":"mphys","type":"match","stem":"Match each hormone with its role in the hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis.","pairs":[["GnRH","Released in pulses by the hypothalamus; drives LH and FSH release"],["LH","Acts on Leydig cells to stimulate testosterone production"],["FSH","Acts on Sertoli cells to stimulate spermatogenesis"],["Inhibin","Secreted by Sertoli cells; inhibits FSH secretion"],["Testosterone","Inhibits GnRH and LH secretion by negative feedback"]],"ex":"GnRH (pulsatile) drives LH and FSH. LH acts on Leydig cells (testosterone), FSH on Sertoli cells (spermatogenesis). Testosterone feeds back on GnRH and LH, and Sertoli inhibin feeds back on FSH.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis: LH on Leydig cells, FSH on Sertoli cells, testosterone and inhibin feedback, pulsatile GnRH","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-match-axis","mp-num-1","mp-2s-3","mp-gnrh-cont","mp-x-tprod","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-match-glands","lec":"mphys","type":"match","stem":"Match each structure with what it adds to the semen or the sperm.","pairs":[["Seminal vesicles","Fructose, prostaglandins and fibrinogen; 50 to 80 percent of the volume"],["Prostate","Thin, milky fluid with citrate, calcium, a clotting enzyme and profibrinolysin"],["Bulbourethral glands","Mucus that lubricates and neutralizes the acidic urethra"],["Epididymis","Fluids that give sperm motility and forward motility proteins"]],"ex":"Seminal vesicles give most of the volume and the fructose; the prostate gives a thin, milky, acid-neutralizing fluid (and PSA); Cowper's glands give lubricating mucus early on; the epididymis matures the sperm before any of these fluids join.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The accessory glands and semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-sv-volume","mp-x-sv","mp-psa","mp-match-glands","mp-num-4","mp-seq-route","mp-x-ais"]}},{"id":"mp-seq-erection","lec":"mphys","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the steps of an erection in order, from sexual stimulation to a firm erection.","steps":["Sexual stimulation activates parasympathetic nerves to the erectile tissue","Postganglionic neurons release acetylcholine and nitric oxide","Nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase, raising cGMP","Smooth muscle relaxes and the cavernous tissue fills with blood","The engorged tissue compresses the veins that drain it"],"ex":"Parasympathetic nerves release ACh and NO; NO activates guanylate cyclase and cGMP rises; the smooth muscle of the corpus cavernosum relaxes, blood flows in, and the engorged tissue squeezes the draining veins. Sildenafil acts at the cGMP step, by blocking PDE5.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Autonomic control of erection, emission and ejaculation (\"point and shoot\") and sildenafil","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ans","mp-erect-nt","mp-seq-erection","mp-cs-pde5-1","mp-cs-pde5-2","mp-num-5","mp-2s-5","mp-tf-5"]}},{"id":"mp-seq-route","lec":"mphys","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the places sperm pass through in order, from where they are made to where they mix with the seminal fluids.","steps":["Seminiferous tubules, supported by Sertoli cells","Epididymis, where they mature and are stored","Vas deferens, during ejaculation under sympathetic control","Urethra, where they mix with the seminal fluids"],"ex":"Sperm form in the seminiferous tubules, mature and are stored (for months) in the epididymis, and at ejaculation are moved by the sympathetic nervous system through the vas deferens into the urethra, where the seminal fluids join them.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The accessory glands and semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-sv-volume","mp-x-sv","mp-psa","mp-match-glands","mp-num-4","mp-seq-route","mp-x-ais"]}},{"id":"mp-2s-1","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. In androgen insensitivity syndrome, the Müllerian ducts persist and form a uterus.\n2. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, the Wolffian ducts develop because testosterone still acts.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Müllerian regression depends on AMH from Sertoli cells, which works in both conditions, so neither has a uterus. The Wolffian ducts need testosterone itself: they regress in AIS (no receptor) but develop in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency (only DHT is missing).","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the Müllerian ducts regress in AIS, because the Sertoli cells still make AMH, which needs no androgen receptor, so there is no uterus.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the Wolffian ducts respond to testosterone itself, which is made and works normally when only 5-alpha-reductase is missing.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the Müllerian ducts regress in AIS, because the Sertoli cells still make AMH, which needs no androgen receptor, so there is no uterus. Statement 2 is true, not false: the Wolffian ducts respond to testosterone itself, which is made and works normally when only 5-alpha-reductase is missing.","Statement 1 is false: the Müllerian ducts regress in AIS, because the Sertoli cells still make AMH, which needs no androgen receptor, so there is no uterus. Statement 2 is true: the Wolffian ducts respond to testosterone itself, which is made and works normally when only 5-alpha-reductase is missing."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome vs 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ais-cause","mp-cs-ais-1","mp-cs-ais-2","mp-cs-5ard-1","mp-cs-5ard-2","mp-mm-both","mp-2s-1"]}},{"id":"mp-2s-2","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. In the male fetus and newborn, aromatase activity resides in the Sertoli cells, under FSH.\n2. In the pubertal and adult male, aromatase activity resides mainly in the Sertoli cells, under FSH.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Aromatase moves with age: Sertoli cells (FSH) in the fetus and newborn, Leydig cells (LH) from puberty on. Either way it turns testosterone into estradiol and androstenedione into estrone.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: from puberty on, aromatase activity resides in the Leydig cells, driven by LH.","Statement 1 is true, not false: before puberty, aromatase is a Sertoli cell enzyme driven by FSH.","Statement 1 is true: before puberty, aromatase is a Sertoli cell enzyme driven by FSH. Statement 2 is false: from puberty on, aromatase activity resides in the Leydig cells, driven by LH.","Statement 1 is true, not false: before puberty, aromatase is a Sertoli cell enzyme driven by FSH. Statement 2 is false, not true: from puberty on, aromatase activity resides in the Leydig cells, driven by LH."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Testosterone synthesis and metabolism: desmolase, aromatase, 5-alpha-reductase","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-rate-limit","mp-aromatase","mp-2s-6","mp-2s-2","mp-x-tprod","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-2s-3","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Sertoli cells secrete inhibin, which inhibits FSH secretion by the pituitary.\n2. LH acts on Leydig cells to stimulate testosterone production.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"The two-cell, two-gonadotropin model: FSH acts on Sertoli cells (spermatogenesis, ABP, inhibin) and LH on Leydig cells (testosterone). Each cell feeds back on its own gonadotropin: inhibin on FSH, testosterone on GnRH and LH.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: inhibin from Sertoli cells is the negative feedback on FSH. Statement 2 is true: LH receptors on Leydig cells drive testosterone synthesis from cholesterol.","Statement 1 is true, not false: inhibin from Sertoli cells is the negative feedback on FSH. Statement 2 is true, not false: LH receptors on Leydig cells drive testosterone synthesis from cholesterol.","Statement 2 is true, not false: LH receptors on Leydig cells drive testosterone synthesis from cholesterol.","Statement 1 is true, not false: inhibin from Sertoli cells is the negative feedback on FSH."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis: LH on Leydig cells, FSH on Sertoli cells, testosterone and inhibin feedback, pulsatile GnRH","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-match-axis","mp-num-1","mp-2s-3","mp-gnrh-cont","mp-x-tprod","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-2s-4","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Sperm leaving the seminiferous tubules can already swim forward and fertilize an ovum.\n2. Sperm are stored mainly in the seminal vesicles until ejaculation.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Sperm leave the tubules non-motile and gain motility and fertilizing ability in the epididymis, starting 18 to 24 hours after they arrive. The epididymis is also where they are stored, for up to several months; the seminal vesicles add fluid at ejaculation.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: sperm are non-motile when they leave the tubules and mature in the epididymis. Statement 2 is false, not true: sperm are stored in the epididymis, for several months; the seminal vesicles secrete fluid rather than store sperm.","Statement 1 is false: sperm are non-motile when they leave the tubules and mature in the epididymis. Statement 2 is false: sperm are stored in the epididymis, for several months; the seminal vesicles secrete fluid rather than store sperm.","Statement 1 is false, not true: sperm are non-motile when they leave the tubules and mature in the epididymis.","Statement 2 is false, not true: sperm are stored in the epididymis, for several months; the seminal vesicles secrete fluid rather than store sperm."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Spermatogenesis, spermiation and maturation in the epididymis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-meiosis","mp-num-3","mp-spermiation","mp-epid-time","mp-mm-epid","mp-2s-4","mp-tf-3","mp-acrosome"]}},{"id":"mp-2s-5","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Erection is driven largely by the parasympathetic nervous system.\n2. Sildenafil works by activating guanylate cyclase in the corpus cavernosum.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Parasympathetic nerves release acetylcholine and nitric oxide; NO activates guanylate cyclase, raising cGMP. Sildenafil acts one step later: it inhibits PDE5, the enzyme that degrades cGMP.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase; sildenafil inhibits PDE5, the enzyme that breaks down cGMP.","Statement 1 is true, not false: postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release acetylcholine and nitric oxide, which dilate the penile vessels.","Statement 1 is true: postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release acetylcholine and nitric oxide, which dilate the penile vessels. Statement 2 is false: nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase; sildenafil inhibits PDE5, the enzyme that breaks down cGMP.","Statement 1 is true, not false: postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release acetylcholine and nitric oxide, which dilate the penile vessels. Statement 2 is false, not true: nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase; sildenafil inhibits PDE5, the enzyme that breaks down cGMP."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Autonomic control of erection, emission and ejaculation (\"point and shoot\") and sildenafil","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ans","mp-erect-nt","mp-seq-erection","mp-cs-pde5-1","mp-cs-pde5-2","mp-num-5","mp-2s-5","mp-tf-5"]}},{"id":"mp-2s-6","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The rate-limiting step in making testosterone is catalyzed by 5-alpha-reductase.\n2. Aromatase converts testosterone to estradiol.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"The P450 side-chain cleavage enzyme (a desmolase) is the rate-limiting first step from cholesterol. After testosterone is made, 5-alpha-reductase turns it into DHT and aromatase turns it into estradiol.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the rate-limiting step is the P450 side-chain cleavage enzyme (a desmolase) at the start of the pathway; 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT afterward.","Statement 2 is true, not false: aromatase (estrogen synthase) turns testosterone into estradiol and androstenedione into estrone.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the rate-limiting step is the P450 side-chain cleavage enzyme (a desmolase) at the start of the pathway; 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT afterward. Statement 2 is true, not false: aromatase (estrogen synthase) turns testosterone into estradiol and androstenedione into estrone.","Statement 1 is false: the rate-limiting step is the P450 side-chain cleavage enzyme (a desmolase) at the start of the pathway; 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT afterward. Statement 2 is true: aromatase (estrogen synthase) turns testosterone into estradiol and androstenedione into estrone."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Testosterone synthesis and metabolism: desmolase, aromatase, 5-alpha-reductase","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-rate-limit","mp-aromatase","mp-2s-6","mp-2s-2","mp-x-tprod","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-tf-1","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, the prostate forms normally, because the prostate responds to testosterone itself.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The prostate is a DHT tissue, like the penis and scrotum: 5-alpha-reductase converts testosterone to DHT, and DHT promotes their formation. The Wolffian ducts are the structures that respond to testosterone itself, which is why they still develop in this deficiency.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The prostate depends on DHT, which is missing here; the Wolffian ducts are the tissue that responds to testosterone itself.","The prostate is a DHT tissue, along with the penis and scrotum, so its formation needs the missing enzyme."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: AMH, testosterone and the Wolffian ducts, DHT and the external genitalia","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-amh","mp-wolffian","mp-dht-targets","mp-fetal-leydig","mp-num-6","mp-4g-gonadal","mp-tf-1"]}},{"id":"mp-tf-2","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, a person raised as a girl may develop male secondary sex characteristics at puberty.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Only the DHT step is missing. At puberty testosterone rises, and testosterone acts on its own, so male secondary sex characteristics may appear.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Testosterone rises at puberty and acts without DHT, so male secondary sex characteristics can appear.","It can happen: only DHT is missing, and the pubertal rise in testosterone still acts."]},{"id":"mp-tf-3","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: On Dr. Madison's slides, spermatids mature for about two weeks to become spermatozoa.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"His spermatogenesis slide gives about two months for spermatids to mature into spermatozoa; full maturation then continues in the epididymis. Dr. Herr's histology lecture gives a different measure, about 74 days for one whole round of spermatogenesis.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His slide gives two months, not two weeks, for spermatids to mature into spermatozoa.","On his slide, spermatids mature for about two months, not two weeks."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Spermatogenesis, spermiation and maturation in the epididymis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-meiosis","mp-num-3","mp-spermiation","mp-epid-time","mp-mm-epid","mp-2s-4","mp-tf-3","mp-acrosome"]}},{"id":"mp-tf-4","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: On Dr. Madison's slides, spermatogenesis is optimal at about 34 degrees Celsius, a few degrees below core body temperature.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"His temperature slide gives 34 degrees Celsius as the optimum; the scrotum, the cremaster and dartos muscles and the heat exchange between arteries and veins keep the testes there. Dr. Herr's histology lecture gives about 35 degrees; both put it a few degrees below core temperature.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["34 degrees Celsius is the optimum on his slide, a few degrees below core body temperature.","The statement matches his slide: spermatogenesis is optimal at about 34 degrees Celsius."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Temperature control of spermatogenesis","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-cold","mp-tf-4","mp-tf-7"]}},{"id":"mp-tf-5","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Lubrication during the male sexual act is a sympathetic function.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Lubrication is parasympathetic, like erection. Only emission and ejaculation are sympathetic (\"point and shoot\").","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Lubrication is parasympathetic; the sympathetic system drives emission and ejaculation.","Lubrication is a parasympathetic function, along with erection."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Autonomic control of erection, emission and ejaculation (\"point and shoot\") and sildenafil","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ans","mp-erect-nt","mp-seq-erection","mp-cs-pde5-1","mp-cs-pde5-2","mp-num-5","mp-2s-5","mp-tf-5"]}},{"id":"mp-tf-6","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: During erection, the engorged cavernous tissue compresses the veins that drain blood from the penis.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Vasodilation fills the cavernous tissue with blood, and the engorged tissue compresses the venous vessels that drain it, so the blood stays in.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Engorgement compresses the draining veins, which keeps blood in the erectile tissue.","Compression of the draining veins by the engorged tissue is part of how an erection is kept."]},{"id":"mp-tf-7","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Where the testicular arteries and veins intertwine, heat passes from the veins to the arteries.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Heat passes from the arteries to the veins. Arterial blood is cooled before it reaches the testis, which helps keep it near 34 degrees Celsius.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Heat passes the other way, from the warm arteries to the veins, so blood is cooled before it reaches the testis.","The exchange runs from artery to vein, cooling the blood that flows into the testis."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Temperature control of spermatogenesis","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-cold","mp-tf-4","mp-tf-7"]}},{"id":"mp-tf-8","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model, the FSH-driven Sertoli cell is the one that phagocytoses defective sperm.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The two-cell slide lists the Sertoli cell's FSH-driven jobs, among them sperm nutrition, androgen-binding protein and phagocytosis of defective sperm. The LH-driven Leydig cell makes testosterone.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Phagocytosing defective sperm is on the two-cell slide's list of Sertoli (FSH) jobs.","It is a Sertoli job, under FSH; the Leydig cell's job, under LH, is making testosterone."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sertoli vs Leydig cell functions (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-x-sertoli","mp-num-2","mp-btb","mp-abp","mp-lh-signal","mp-tf-8","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-tf-9","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The gradual decline in testosterone as men age is called late-onset hypogonadism.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Testosterone declines slowly over many years in men; this is late-onset hypogonadism, or age-related low testosterone, and its consequences aren't always clear.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Late-onset hypogonadism (age-related low testosterone) is the name his notes give it.","That gradual fall is late-onset hypogonadism, also called age-related low testosterone."]},{"id":"mp-tf-10","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Leydig cells can convert adrenal androgens into testosterone.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Leydig cells make testosterone from cholesterol in response to LH, and they also express enzymes that convert the adrenal androgens to testosterone.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["His testosterone synthesis slide says Leydig cells express enzymes that convert adrenal androgens to testosterone.","They can: besides building testosterone from cholesterol, Leydig cells convert adrenal androgens to testosterone."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Testosterone synthesis and metabolism: desmolase, aromatase, 5-alpha-reductase","basis":"slides","ids":["mp-rate-limit","mp-aromatase","mp-2s-6","mp-2s-2","mp-x-tprod","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-num-1","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis are true?\n1. GnRH must be released in pulses to keep testosterone production normal.\n2. Inhibin from the Sertoli cells inhibits LH secretion.\n3. Testosterone inhibits both GnRH and LH secretion.\n4. When serum testosterone falls, GnRH and LH secretion fall too.","choices":["1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","1 and 4 only","2 and 3 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pulsatile GnRH drives LH and FSH; testosterone feeds back on GnRH and LH, and Sertoli inhibin on FSH. When testosterone falls, GnRH and LH rise, which holds testosterone in a narrow range.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1 and 3 are true. Statement 2 is false: inhibin from the Sertoli cells inhibits FSH, not LH. Statement 4 is false: when testosterone falls, GnRH and LH rise, which brings testosterone back up.","It includes 4, which is false: when testosterone falls, GnRH and LH rise, which brings testosterone back up.","It includes 4, which is false: when testosterone falls, GnRH and LH rise, which brings testosterone back up. It leaves out 3, which is true: testosterone is the negative feedback on both the hypothalamus and the pituitary.","It includes 2, which is false: inhibin from the Sertoli cells inhibits FSH, not LH. It leaves out 1, which is true: continuous GnRH lowers LH and testosterone, so the pulses are required."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis: LH on Leydig cells, FSH on Sertoli cells, testosterone and inhibin feedback, pulsatile GnRH","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-match-axis","mp-num-1","mp-2s-3","mp-gnrh-cont","mp-x-tprod","mp-cs-inhibin"]}},{"id":"mp-num-2","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements describe Sertoli cells?\n1. They have LH receptors and make testosterone from cholesterol.\n2. They join by tight junctions to form the blood-testis barrier.\n3. They secrete androgen-binding protein.\n4. They release mature spermatids into the lumen and remove their excess cytoplasm.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Sertoli cells (FSH) nourish sperm, form the blood-testis barrier, secrete fluid, ABP and inhibin, and carry out spermiation. LH receptors and testosterone synthesis belong to the Leydig cells.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: LH receptors and testosterone synthesis belong to the Leydig cells; Sertoli cells respond to FSH.","It includes 1, which is false: LH receptors and testosterone synthesis belong to the Leydig cells; Sertoli cells respond to FSH. It leaves out 2, which is true: Sertoli tight junctions keep haploid cells out of reach of the immune system.","It leaves out 4, which is true: this is spermiation, a Sertoli job.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: LH receptors and testosterone synthesis belong to the Leydig cells; Sertoli cells respond to FSH."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sertoli vs Leydig cell functions (the two-cell, two-gonadotropin model)","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-x-sertoli","mp-num-2","mp-btb","mp-abp","mp-lh-signal","mp-tf-8","mp-tf-10"]}},{"id":"mp-num-3","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about spermatogenesis are true?\n1. Spermatogonia sit in the outer 2-3 layers of the seminiferous tubule.\n2. One spermatocyte divides into two spermatids.\n3. Each spermatid carries 23 unpaired chromosomes.\n4. The second meiotic division forms new copies of the chromosomes.","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","3 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"Spermatogonia in the outer layers grow into spermatocytes, which go through two meiotic divisions to make four spermatids with 23 unpaired chromosomes each. No new chromosomes are formed in the second division.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: two meiotic divisions give four spermatids from one spermatocyte.","Statements 1 and 3 are true. Statement 2 is false: two meiotic divisions give four spermatids from one spermatocyte. Statement 4 is false: no new chromosomes are formed in the second division.","It includes 4, which is false: no new chromosomes are formed in the second division.","It leaves out 1, which is true: spermatogonia, the germinal epithelium, line the outer layers of the tubule."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Spermatogenesis, spermiation and maturation in the epididymis","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-meiosis","mp-num-3","mp-spermiation","mp-epid-time","mp-mm-epid","mp-2s-4","mp-tf-3","mp-acrosome"]}},{"id":"mp-num-4","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the male accessory glands are true?\n1. The prostate supplies 50 to 80 percent of semen volume.\n2. The seminal vesicles secrete a thin, milky fluid with citrate, calcium and profibrinolysin.\n3. The bulbourethral glands secrete mucus that lubricates and neutralizes the acidic urethra.\n4. Prostate-specific antigen, a serine protease, keeps semen from coagulating.","choices":["1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4","3 only","3 and 4 only"],"answer":3,"ex":"Seminal vesicles: fructose, prostaglandins and fibrinogen, 50 to 80 percent of the volume. Prostate: thin, milky fluid with citrate, calcium, a clotting enzyme and profibrinolysin, plus PSA. Bulbourethral glands: lubricating, neutralizing mucus.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: the seminal vesicles, not the prostate, supply 50 to 80 percent of the volume.","It includes 2, which is false: that thin, milky fluid is the prostate's; the seminal vesicles secrete fructose, prostaglandins and fibrinogen.","It leaves out 4, which is true: PSA breaks proteins into smaller peptides, which prevents seminal coagulation.","Statements 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: the seminal vesicles, not the prostate, supply 50 to 80 percent of the volume. Statement 2 is false: that thin, milky fluid is the prostate's; the seminal vesicles secrete fructose, prostaglandins and fibrinogen."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"The accessory glands and semen","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-sv-volume","mp-x-sv","mp-psa","mp-match-glands","mp-num-4","mp-seq-route","mp-x-ais"]}},{"id":"mp-num-5","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about erection, ejaculation and sildenafil are true?\n1. Nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase, raising cGMP in the corpus cavernosum.\n2. PDE5 breaks down cGMP.\n3. Sildenafil relaxes the corpus cavernosum directly, even without sexual stimulation.\n4. During ejaculation, sperm move through the vas deferens under sympathetic control.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","1 and 4 only","2 and 4 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"NO activates guanylate cyclase and cGMP relaxes the smooth muscle; PDE5 breaks cGMP down; sildenafil blocks PDE5 but needs the NO that sexual stimulation releases. Ejaculation is sympathetic.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 3, which is false: sildenafil has no direct relaxant effect; it only enhances the effect of nitric oxide released during stimulation.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: sildenafil has no direct relaxant effect; it only enhances the effect of nitric oxide released during stimulation.","It leaves out 2, which is true: PDE5 is the enzyme that degrades cGMP, which is why blocking it helps.","It leaves out 1, which is true: NO's activation of guanylate cyclase is what raises cGMP and relaxes the smooth muscle."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Autonomic control of erection, emission and ejaculation (\"point and shoot\") and sildenafil","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-ans","mp-erect-nt","mp-seq-erection","mp-cs-pde5-1","mp-cs-pde5-2","mp-num-5","mp-2s-5","mp-tf-5"]}},{"id":"mp-num-6","lec":"mphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about male sexual differentiation are true?\n1. Testes are present in androgen insensitivity syndrome.\n2. Fetal Leydig cells make testosterone in response to hCG and LH.\n3. In 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, the Müllerian ducts persist.\n4. DHT forms the penis, scrotum and prostate.","choices":["1 and 2 only","1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"The Y chromosome makes testes; Sertoli AMH removes the Müllerian ducts; Leydig testosterone (driven by hCG and LH) builds the Wolffian structures; DHT from 5-alpha-reductase forms the penis, scrotum and prostate.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 4, which is true: 5-alpha-reductase turns testosterone into DHT in peripheral tissues, and DHT forms the external structures and the prostate.","It includes 3, which is false: the Müllerian ducts regress in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, because the Sertoli cells still make AMH.","Statements 1, 2 and 4 are true. Statement 3 is false: the Müllerian ducts regress in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, because the Sertoli cells still make AMH.","It leaves out 1, which is true: the Y chromosome leads to testes even when the androgen receptor is missing."],"likely":{"lec":"mphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: AMH, testosterone and the Wolffian ducts, DHT and the external genitalia","basis":"objectives","ids":["mp-amh","mp-wolffian","mp-dht-targets","mp-fetal-leydig","mp-num-6","mp-4g-gonadal","mp-tf-1"]}},{"id":"fp-default-path","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"In a fetus with no Y chromosome, which best explains why the Wolffian ducts regress?","choices":["No testes form, so there is no testosterone to make the Wolffian ducts differentiate","No testes form, so the missing anti-Müllerian hormone lets the Wolffian ducts break down","The fetal ovary secretes estrogen, which actively breaks the Wolffian ducts down","The fetal ovary secretes anti-Müllerian hormone, which acts on the Wolffian ducts"],"answer":0,"ex":"The female tract is the default pathway: with no Y chromosome there are no testes, so there is no AMH (the Müllerian ducts form) and no testosterone (the Wolffian ducts regress). Each duct's fate follows from a missing male hormone, not from anything the ovary makes.","src":"slides","why":["Testosterone from the testes is what drives Wolffian differentiation; with no testes there is none, so the ducts regress by default.","Missing AMH is why the Müllerian ducts form; it is the missing testosterone that lets the Wolffian ducts regress.","The lecture calls the female route a default pathway: no ovarian hormone has to act on the Wolffian ducts.","AMH is made by fetal testes, not ovaries, and it acts on the Müllerian ducts."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: the default pathway and the two ducts","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-default-path","fp-2s-default","fp-tf-amh"]}},{"id":"fp-2s-default","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The female reproductive tract develops by a default pathway, with no Y chromosome driving it.\n2. Anti-Müllerian hormone is made by the fetal testes.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"No Y chromosome means no testes, and the testes are the source of both male signals: with no AMH the Müllerian ducts form, and with no testosterone the Wolffian ducts regress. Nothing from the ovary is needed.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: with no Y chromosome no testes form and nothing male drives development, so the female tract is the default. Statement 2 is true: the slide says a female makes no AMH because the testicles never form; the testes are its source.","Statement 1 is true, not false: with no Y chromosome no testes form and nothing male drives development, so the female tract is the default. Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide says a female makes no AMH because the testicles never form; the testes are its source.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide says a female makes no AMH because the testicles never form; the testes are its source.","Statement 1 is true, not false: with no Y chromosome no testes form and nothing male drives development, so the female tract is the default."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: the default pathway and the two ducts","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-default-path","fp-2s-default","fp-tf-amh"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-amh","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In a female fetus, the Müllerian ducts form because no anti-Müllerian hormone is made.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"No testes means no AMH, and without AMH the Müllerian ducts form. The other half of the default pathway is the missing testosterone, which lets the Wolffian ducts regress.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["With no testes there is no AMH, so nothing removes the Müllerian ducts and they form.","It is true: the slide pairs lack of AMH with Müllerian duct formation; lack of testosterone is the Wolffian half."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Sexual differentiation: the default pathway and the two ducts","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-default-path","fp-2s-default","fp-tf-amh"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-fimbriae","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The ovaries sit in the peritoneal cavity, and the beating of the fimbriae moves the ovulated oocyte into the fallopian tube.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"No duct joins the ovary to the tube. The ovary sits in the peritoneal cavity close to the tube's opening, the beating fimbriae carry the oocyte in, and fertilization then happens in the fallopian tube.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The ovary lies free near the tube's opening, and the beating fimbriae sweep the oocyte in.","It is true: no duct joins ovary and tube, so the beating fimbriae have to move the oocyte in."]},{"id":"fp-tf-cortex","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The follicles, the functional units of the ovary, sit in the ovarian medulla.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"The follicles are in the cortex. The medulla and the hilum hold the blood and lymphatic vessels.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The follicles are in the cortex; the medulla and hilum carry the vessels.","It is false: the cortex holds the follicles, and the medulla and hilum hold the blood and lymphatic vessels."]},{"id":"fp-kndy","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"GnRH release changes with stress, inflammation, drugs, nutrition and body metabolism. According to the lecture, how do these signals reach the GnRH neurons?","choices":["KNDy neurons act as a bridge, relaying them to the GnRH neurons","GnRH neurons sense each of them directly through their own receptors","Inhibin from the ovary carries them up to the hypothalamus","The hypophyseal portal system carries them up from the pituitary"],"answer":0,"ex":"GnRH neurons answer to sex steroids, glucocorticoids, inflammation, stress, drugs, metabolism and nutrition, but only indirectly: KNDy neurons (the K is kisspeptin) bridge those signals to them. The slide's diagram feeds circadian rhythms, metabolism (leptin) and kisspeptin production into GnRH control.","src":"slides","why":["The lecture says GnRH neurons don't respond to these stimuli directly; KNDy neurons relay them.","The slide says the opposite: GnRH neurons do not respond to these stimuli directly.","Inhibin is an ovarian hormone that suppresses FSH; it does not relay stress or nutrition signals.","The portal system carries GnRH down to the anterior pituitary, not signals up to the GnRH neurons."]},{"id":"fp-cyclic","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which feature of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis makes the female reproductive system cyclical, while the male's is acyclical?","choices":["Estradiol's positive feedback, which amplifies the pituitary's LH response to GnRH","Progesterone's negative feedback, which holds GnRH, LH and FSH down after ovulation","Inhibin's suppression of FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary","KNDy neurons, which relay stress and nutrition signals to GnRH neurons"],"answer":0,"ex":"Rising estradiol from growing follicles progressively sensitizes the pituitary to GnRH, so just before ovulation GnRH sets off a burst of LH. The lecture calls this amplification positive feedback and says it is what makes the female cyclical; the male has no positive feedback and is acyclical.","src":"slides","why":["This amplification is what the lecture says makes the female cyclical; the male has no positive feedback.","Negative feedback steadies hormone levels and both sexes rely on it, so it can't explain the cycle.","Inhibin is a brake on FSH, not the switch that produces a surge and a cycle.","KNDy neurons modulate GnRH release; the lecture ties the cycle to estradiol's positive feedback instead."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Negative vs positive feedback: estradiol above 200 pg/mL triggers the LH surge; progesterone is negative only","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-cyclic","fp-tf-200","fp-x-feedback","fp-tf-prog-fb","fp-preov-2","fp-anov-2","fp-num-axis"]}},{"id":"fp-num-axis","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis are true?\n1. GnRH neurons respond directly to stress, inflammation and nutrition.\n2. FSH and LH act on the ovaries to produce estrogen and inhibin.\n3. GnRH reaches the anterior pituitary through the hypophyseal portal system.\n4. Progesterone exerts negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"GnRH goes down the portal system, FSH and LH drive the ovary's estrogen and inhibin, and progesterone feeds back negatively. GnRH neurons hear stress, inflammation and nutrition only through KNDy neurons.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: GnRH neurons respond to those signals only indirectly, through KNDy neurons.","It includes 1, which is false: GnRH neurons respond to those signals only indirectly, through KNDy neurons. It leaves out 3, which is true: GnRH travels down the hypophyseal portal system to the secretory cells of the adenohypophysis.","It leaves out 4, which is true: progesterone gives negative feedback to both the hypothalamus and the pituitary.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: GnRH neurons respond to those signals only indirectly, through KNDy neurons."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Negative vs positive feedback: estradiol above 200 pg/mL triggers the LH surge; progesterone is negative only","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-cyclic","fp-tf-200","fp-x-feedback","fp-tf-prog-fb","fp-preov-2","fp-anov-2","fp-num-axis"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-prog-fb","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Progesterone can exert both positive and negative feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Estrogen is the hormone that can do both. Progesterone exerts negative feedback only, which is how it holds GnRH, LH and FSH down in the luteal phase.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Only estrogen switches between the two; progesterone's feedback is negative.","It is false: progesterone gives negative feedback only; estrogen is the one that can do both."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Negative vs positive feedback: estradiol above 200 pg/mL triggers the LH surge; progesterone is negative only","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-cyclic","fp-tf-200","fp-x-feedback","fp-tf-prog-fb","fp-preov-2","fp-anov-2","fp-num-axis"]}},{"id":"fp-x-feedback","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"In the lecture, each of the following holds GnRH, LH or FSH down EXCEPT:","choices":["Estradiol above about 200 pg/mL at midcycle","Progesterone from the corpus luteum in the luteal phase","Estradiol from growing follicles early in the follicular phase","Inhibin from the follicle, acting on FSH"],"answer":0,"ex":"Three brakes and one accelerator: progesterone, inhibin and estradiol at ordinary levels all suppress. Only estradiol above about 200 pg/mL turns into positive feedback and drives the LH surge.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Above that level estradiol switches to positive feedback and sets off the LH surge, so it is the exception.","Progesterone gives negative feedback on GnRH, LH and FSH through the luteal phase.","Below the critical level, for most of the follicular phase, estradiol gives negative feedback on GnRH, LH and FSH.","Inhibin suppresses FSH, which is why FSH falls as the follicle nears ovulation."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Negative vs positive feedback: estradiol above 200 pg/mL triggers the LH surge; progesterone is negative only","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-cyclic","fp-tf-200","fp-x-feedback","fp-tf-prog-fb","fp-preov-2","fp-anov-2","fp-num-axis"]}},{"id":"fp-scc","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"LH speeds up the rate-limiting step of ovarian estrogen synthesis. Which enzyme does it act on, and in which cells?","choices":["P450scc, in theca cells","Aromatase, in granulosa cells","Aromatase, in theca cells","P450scc, in granulosa cells"],"answer":0,"ex":"Two cells, two gonadotropins: LH acts on theca cells, raising P450scc (the rate-limiting step) so they make testosterone; FSH acts on granulosa cells, raising aromatase so they convert that testosterone to estrogen.","src":"slides","why":["LH raises P450scc activity in theca cells, the rate-limiting step of estrogen synthesis.","That is FSH's action: it raises aromatase so granulosa cells convert testosterone to estrogen.","Theca cells make testosterone; aromatase, the converting enzyme, is in the granulosa cells.","The lecture puts LH's effect on P450scc in theca cells, the cells that carry LH receptors in the growing follicle."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Two cells, two gonadotropins: LH, theca, P450scc, testosterone; FSH, granulosa, aromatase, estrogen","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-scc","fp-fsh-block","fp-lh-block","fp-lhr-granulosa","fp-2s-twocell","fp-2s-hormones"]}},{"id":"fp-fsh-block","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"An experimental drug blocks the FSH receptors on the granulosa cells of a growing follicle. Which step of estradiol production is most directly lost?","choices":["Conversion of testosterone to estradiol by aromatase","Cholesterol side-chain cleavage by P450scc in theca cells","Testosterone production by theca cells in response to LH","Progesterone secretion by the corpus luteum after ovulation"],"answer":0,"ex":"FSH makes granulosa cells express and activate aromatase, which converts testosterone (made by theca cells under LH) into estradiol. Block FSH and the theca cells still make testosterone, but it isn't turned into estradiol.","src":"slides","why":["FSH drives aromatase in granulosa cells; without the FSH signal that conversion fails.","That step is driven by LH acting on theca cells, which this drug leaves alone.","Theca cells answer to LH, so they keep making testosterone; it just isn't converted.","This is a growing follicle before ovulation, so there is no corpus luteum yet."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Two cells, two gonadotropins: LH, theca, P450scc, testosterone; FSH, granulosa, aromatase, estrogen","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-scc","fp-fsh-block","fp-lh-block","fp-lhr-granulosa","fp-2s-twocell","fp-2s-hormones"]}},{"id":"fp-lh-block","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Early in the follicular phase, a drug blocks the LH receptors on theca cells. What happens to estradiol production, and why?","choices":["It falls: theca cells stop making the testosterone that aromatase needs","It stays normal, because FSH still drives aromatase in granulosa cells","It rises, because granulosa cells then make estrogen directly from cholesterol","It stays normal, because the corpus luteum supplies estradiol early in the cycle"],"answer":0,"ex":"Each half of the two-cell model needs the other: theca cells (LH, P450scc) make testosterone, and granulosa cells (FSH, aromatase) convert it to estradiol. Lose LH and the precursor supply stops, so estradiol falls even though aromatase is still there.","src":"slides","why":["Aromatase in granulosa cells can only convert testosterone that the LH-driven theca cells supply.","FSH keeps aromatase active, but aromatase has nothing to convert without theca testosterone.","Granulosa cells convert testosterone to estrogen; the lecture puts the cholesterol step in theca cells.","The corpus luteum forms only after ovulation, so it can't stand in during the follicular phase."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Two cells, two gonadotropins: LH, theca, P450scc, testosterone; FSH, granulosa, aromatase, estrogen","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-scc","fp-fsh-block","fp-lh-block","fp-lhr-granulosa","fp-2s-twocell","fp-2s-hormones"]}},{"id":"fp-lhr-granulosa","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"FSH falls as the dominant follicle nears ovulation. According to the lecture, what lets the follicle keep producing estrogen anyway?","choices":["Its granulosa cells begin to express LH receptors","Its theca cells begin to express aromatase","The corpus luteum takes over estrogen production","Estrone from the adrenal glands replaces estradiol"],"answer":0,"ex":"As inhibin lowers FSH, granulosa cells of the dominant follicle start expressing LH receptors, which also prepares them for the luteal phase. The lecture says that without this the dominant follicle could not keep producing estrogen.","src":"slides","why":["The lecture says that without these LH receptors the dominant follicle couldn't produce estrogen as FSH falls.","Aromatase stays in the granulosa cells; theca cells make testosterone, the precursor.","The corpus luteum forms only after the follicle ruptures, so it can't support the follicle before ovulation.","Adrenal-derived estrone is the estrogen of menopause, not what sustains the dominant follicle."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Two cells, two gonadotropins: LH, theca, P450scc, testosterone; FSH, granulosa, aromatase, estrogen","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-scc","fp-fsh-block","fp-lh-block","fp-lhr-granulosa","fp-2s-twocell","fp-2s-hormones"]}},{"id":"fp-match-steroids","lec":"fphys","type":"match","stem":"Match each steroid to the lecture's description of it.","pairs":[["17β-estradiol","The main estrogen secreted by the ovary"],["Estrone","The estrogen of menopause, made from adrenal DHEA"],["Estriol","The estrogen of pregnancy, made in the placenta from fetal 16-OH-DHEA-S"],["Testosterone","Made by theca cells, then converted to estrogen by aromatase"]],"ex":"One estrogen per stage of life: 17β-estradiol from the ovary in the reproductive years, estrone after menopause (precursor DHEA from the adrenal zona reticularis), estriol in pregnancy (precursor 16-OH-DHEA-S from the fetus, finished in the placenta). Testosterone is the theca cells' precursor.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"The three estrogens and the corpus luteum","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-match-steroids","fp-num-cl","fp-tf-cl-cells","fp-anov-1","fp-match-roles"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-estrone","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Estrone, the estrogen of menopause, is made from DHEA that comes from the adrenal zona reticularis.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"After menopause the follicles are gone, so the dominant estrogen becomes estrone, whose precursor is adrenal DHEA from the zona reticularis. Estriol, by contrast, comes from fetal 16-OH-DHEA-S in pregnancy.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Its precursor is adrenal DHEA from the zona reticularis, which is why it outlasts the follicles.","It is true: estrone's precursor is DHEA from the adrenal zona reticularis."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Menopause: atresia, lost negative feedback, high GnRH, LH and FSH, estrone, symptoms","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-meno-1","fp-meno-2","fp-num-meno","fp-x-meno","fp-tf-estrone"]}},{"id":"fp-2s-twocell","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Theca cells resemble Sertoli cells: both respond to FSH.\n2. Estrone is the main estrogen made by the ovary during the reproductive years.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Theca cells are the Leydig-like cells (LH, testosterone); granulosa cells are the Sertoli-like cells (FSH, aromatase). The ovary's main estrogen is 17β-estradiol; estrone takes over only after menopause.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: theca cells resemble Leydig cells and respond to LH; granulosa cells are the Sertoli-like cells that respond to FSH. Statement 2 is false, not true: 17β-estradiol is the ovary's main estrogen; estrone is the estrogen of menopause.","Statement 1 is false: theca cells resemble Leydig cells and respond to LH; granulosa cells are the Sertoli-like cells that respond to FSH. Statement 2 is false: 17β-estradiol is the ovary's main estrogen; estrone is the estrogen of menopause.","Statement 1 is false, not true: theca cells resemble Leydig cells and respond to LH; granulosa cells are the Sertoli-like cells that respond to FSH.","Statement 2 is false, not true: 17β-estradiol is the ovary's main estrogen; estrone is the estrogen of menopause."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Two cells, two gonadotropins: LH, theca, P450scc, testosterone; FSH, granulosa, aromatase, estrogen","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-scc","fp-fsh-block","fp-lh-block","fp-lhr-granulosa","fp-2s-twocell","fp-2s-hormones"]}},{"id":"fp-2s-hormones","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. FSH increases P450scc activity in theca cells.\n2. Estriol is made in the placenta from DHEA supplied by the mother's adrenal glands.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Each gonadotropin has its own cell and enzyme: LH with theca cells and P450scc, FSH with granulosa cells and aromatase. Each estrogen has its own source: estriol, the estrogen of pregnancy, is finished in the placenta from 16-OH-DHEA-S made by the fetus; adrenal DHEA is the precursor of estrone, the estrogen of menopause.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: it is LH that raises P450scc in theca cells; FSH raises aromatase in granulosa cells. Statement 2 is false, not true: estriol's precursor is 16-OH-DHEA-S made by the fetus; adrenal DHEA from the zona reticularis is the precursor of estrone.","Statement 1 is false: it is LH that raises P450scc in theca cells; FSH raises aromatase in granulosa cells. Statement 2 is false: estriol's precursor is 16-OH-DHEA-S made by the fetus; adrenal DHEA from the zona reticularis is the precursor of estrone.","Statement 1 is false, not true: it is LH that raises P450scc in theca cells; FSH raises aromatase in granulosa cells.","Statement 2 is false, not true: estriol's precursor is 16-OH-DHEA-S made by the fetus; adrenal DHEA from the zona reticularis is the precursor of estrone."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Two cells, two gonadotropins: LH, theca, P450scc, testosterone; FSH, granulosa, aromatase, estrogen","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-scc","fp-fsh-block","fp-lh-block","fp-lhr-granulosa","fp-2s-twocell","fp-2s-hormones"]}},{"id":"fp-2s-receptors","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Granulosa cells begin to express FSH receptors as a primordial follicle becomes a primary follicle.\n2. The theca interna develops FSH receptors once a theca forms around the growing follicle.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Receptors arrive in order: FSH receptors on granulosa cells at the primary stage, then LH receptors on the theca interna once the follicle has several granulosa layers and a theca. Those two receptor sets let the follicle answer both gonadotropins.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: the theca interna develops LH receptors, not FSH receptors.","Statement 1 is true, not false: as the oocyte enlarges and the granulosa layer proliferates, the granulosa cells start to express FSH receptors.","Statement 1 is true: as the oocyte enlarges and the granulosa layer proliferates, the granulosa cells start to express FSH receptors. Statement 2 is false: the theca interna develops LH receptors, not FSH receptors.","Statement 1 is true, not false: as the oocyte enlarges and the granulosa layer proliferates, the granulosa cells start to express FSH receptors. Statement 2 is false, not true: the theca interna develops LH receptors, not FSH receptors."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Follicle development, receptors and the oocyte's meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-2s-receptors","fp-secondary","fp-seq-follicle","fp-oocyte-gf","fp-x-graafian","fp-meiosis","fp-tf-cohort"]}},{"id":"fp-secondary","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"A growing follicle has a single layer of cuboidal granulosa cells around its oocyte. In the lecture's sequence, which change comes next?","choices":["Granulosa cells form several layers and a theca appears","Fluid collects among the granulosa cells and forms an antrum","The granulosa cells begin to express FSH receptors","The oocyte finishes its first meiotic division"],"answer":0,"ex":"Single cuboidal layer with FSH receptors, then several granulosa layers plus a theca (the theca interna gains LH receptors), then a fluid-filled antrum. Dr. Madison calls the layered stage the secondary follicle; the L14 histology lecture calls it the late primary follicle and keeps the name secondary for the antral follicle, so learn the order, not just the names.","src":"slides","why":["The two major changes after the single-layer stage are more granulosa layers and the acquisition of a theca.","The fluid-filled antrum comes later, after the layers and the theca are in place.","Granulosa cells gain FSH receptors as the single-layer follicle forms, so this has already happened.","The first meiotic division is finished only by ovulation, after the LH surge."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Follicle development, receptors and the oocyte's meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-2s-receptors","fp-secondary","fp-seq-follicle","fp-oocyte-gf","fp-x-graafian","fp-meiosis","fp-tf-cohort"]}},{"id":"fp-oocyte-gf","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"According to the lecture, what mainly drives a follicle's early growth, from a single granulosa layer to several layers with a theca?","choices":["Autocrine and paracrine growth factors made by the oocyte","The LH surge acting on LH receptors of the theca interna","Progesterone secreted by the corpus luteum after it forms","Positive feedback of estradiol once it rises above 200 pg/mL"],"answer":0,"ex":"Early follicle growth is local: growth factors from the oocyte act on the surrounding cells (autocrine and paracrine). The receptors gained along the way, FSH on granulosa cells and LH on the theca, then let the follicle respond to the pituitary.","src":"slides","why":["The slide credits an active autocrine and paracrine process driven by growth factors from the oocyte.","The LH surge comes at midcycle and triggers ovulation, long after this early growth.","The corpus luteum forms after ovulation; it doesn't drive early follicle growth.","That threshold sets off the LH surge at midcycle; it isn't what grows an early follicle."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Follicle development, receptors and the oocyte's meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-2s-receptors","fp-secondary","fp-seq-follicle","fp-oocyte-gf","fp-x-graafian","fp-meiosis","fp-tf-cohort"]}},{"id":"fp-x-graafian","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is true of the Graafian follicle EXCEPT:","choices":["It is the stage at which granulosa cells first express FSH receptors","It has an antrum filled with follicular fluid","Before ovulation it has a high expression of LH receptors","The dominant one ruptures about 24 to 36 hours after the LH surge"],"answer":0,"ex":"Graafian follicle: an antrum of follicular fluid (a plasma exudate conditioned by the oocyte and granulosa cells), many LH receptors before ovulation, and the dominant one ruptures 24 to 36 hours after the surge. FSH receptors appeared much earlier, at the single-layer primary stage.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Granulosa cells first express FSH receptors at the primary stage, so this is the exception.","The Graafian follicle has a large antrum filled with follicular fluid.","Preovulatory Graafian follicles express LH receptors heavily, so the LH surge can act on them.","The surge acts on the dominant Graafian follicle's LH receptors, and it ruptures typically 24 to 36 hours later."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Follicle development, receptors and the oocyte's meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-2s-receptors","fp-secondary","fp-seq-follicle","fp-oocyte-gf","fp-x-graafian","fp-meiosis","fp-tf-cohort"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-cohort","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: In each menstrual cycle, only a single follicle enters the growth sequence that forms an antrum.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"A few follicles enter the sequence that forms an antrum each cycle; only one of them goes on to become the dominant follicle that ovulates.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["A few follicles start the sequence each cycle; only later is one selected as the dominant follicle.","It is false: a few follicles start growing each cycle, and only one of them becomes dominant."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Follicle development, receptors and the oocyte's meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-2s-receptors","fp-secondary","fp-seq-follicle","fp-oocyte-gf","fp-x-graafian","fp-meiosis","fp-tf-cohort"]}},{"id":"fp-seq-follicle","lec":"fphys","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the stages of a follicle's life in order, from earliest to latest.","steps":["Primordial follicle, arrested until puberty","One layer of cuboidal granulosa cells, now with FSH receptors","Several granulosa layers, and a theca whose inner layer has LH receptors","A fluid-filled antrum; one such follicle becomes dominant","Corpus luteum: the ruptured follicle's remains, secreting steroid hormones"],"ex":"Primordial, then one cuboidal layer (primary), then several layers with a theca, then an antrum (Dr. Madison calls these secondary and Graafian; L14 calls them late primary and secondary), then rupture and the corpus luteum, which becomes the corpus albicans if there is no fertilization.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Follicle development, receptors and the oocyte's meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-2s-receptors","fp-secondary","fp-seq-follicle","fp-oocyte-gf","fp-x-graafian","fp-meiosis","fp-tf-cohort"]}},{"id":"fp-meiosis","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"When does the oocyte complete its first meiotic division?","choices":["By ovulation, after the LH surge lifts the block on meiosis","At puberty, when follicles first move past the primordial stage","In the fallopian tube, after the oocyte leaves the follicle","As the primary follicle's granulosa cells gain FSH receptors"],"answer":0,"ex":"The oocyte stays a primary oocyte through all of follicle growth. The LH surge triggers resumption of meiosis, the first division is complete by ovulation (a secondary oocyte), and the second is completed in the fallopian tube; the L14 histology lecture adds that it finishes only if a sperm fertilizes it.","src":"slides","why":["The LH surge removes the factors inhibiting meiosis, and the first division is complete by the time the oocyte is ovulated.","Puberty lets follicles grow, but the oocyte stays a primary oocyte until ovulation.","The tube is where the second meiotic division is completed, not the first.","Gaining FSH receptors marks the primary follicle; the oocyte's meiosis is still arrested then."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Follicle development, receptors and the oocyte's meiosis","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-2s-receptors","fp-secondary","fp-seq-follicle","fp-oocyte-gf","fp-x-graafian","fp-meiosis","fp-tf-cohort"]}},{"id":"fp-2s-meiosis","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The oocyte completes its second meiotic division before it leaves the follicle.\n2. No follicle moves beyond its first stage until puberty, so a follicle can stay arrested there for 13 to 50 years.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"The oocyte waits a long time at two points: as a primary oocyte in a follicle that can't start growing until puberty (13 to 50 years), and again until the second meiotic division is finished in the fallopian tube.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the oocyte leaves the follicle with only its first division done; the second is completed later, in the fallopian tube.","Statement 2 is true, not false: no follicle progresses past the first stage before puberty, so the arrest can last 13 to 50 years.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the oocyte leaves the follicle with only its first division done; the second is completed later, in the fallopian tube. Statement 2 is true, not false: no follicle progresses past the first stage before puberty, so the arrest can last 13 to 50 years.","Statement 1 is false: the oocyte leaves the follicle with only its first division done; the second is completed later, in the fallopian tube. Statement 2 is true: no follicle progresses past the first stage before puberty, so the arrest can last 13 to 50 years."]},{"id":"fp-num-cl","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the corpus luteum are true?\n1. It forms from the remaining elements of the ruptured follicle.\n2. If fertilization occurs, it is replaced by the corpus albicans.\n3. Its steroid hormones are needed for implantation and for maintaining a zygote.\n4. Without fertilization, it regresses over about 14 days.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","3 and 4 only"],"answer":2,"ex":"The corpus luteum is the ruptured follicle's remains and makes the steroid hormones needed for implantation. With fertilization the placenta takes over hormone production; without it the corpus luteum regresses over about 14 days into a scar, the corpus albicans.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 2, which is false: with fertilization the placenta takes over hormone production; the corpus albicans is the scar left when there is no fertilization.","It leaves out 4, which is true: without fertilization it regresses over 14 days into the corpus albicans.","Statements 1, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 2 is false: with fertilization the placenta takes over hormone production; the corpus albicans is the scar left when there is no fertilization.","It leaves out 1, which is true: the corpus luteum is what is left of the ruptured follicle."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"The three estrogens and the corpus luteum","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-match-steroids","fp-num-cl","fp-tf-cl-cells","fp-anov-1","fp-match-roles"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-cl-cells","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: The corpus luteum is made mostly of theca cells, with some granulosa cells.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It is the reverse: mostly granulosa cells with some theca cells. Those granulosa cells gained LH receptors at midcycle, in preparation for the luteal phase.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["The proportions are reversed: it is mostly granulosa cells, with some theca cells.","It is false: the corpus luteum is mostly granulosa cells, with some theca cells."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"The three estrogens and the corpus luteum","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-match-steroids","fp-num-cl","fp-tf-cl-cells","fp-anov-1","fp-match-roles"]}},{"id":"fp-day21","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"In a typical 28-day cycle, which hormone dominates on day 21?","choices":["Progesterone, secreted by the corpus luteum","17β-estradiol, secreted by the dominant follicle","LH, released by the anterior pituitary in a surge","FSH, released by the anterior pituitary under GnRH"],"answer":0,"ex":"Days 14 to 28 are the luteal (secretory) phase, driven by the corpus luteum and dominated by progesterone, which holds GnRH, LH and FSH down by negative feedback.","src":"slides","why":["Day 21 is mid-luteal, and the luteal phase is dominated by progesterone from the corpus luteum.","Estradiol dominates the follicular phase; by day 21 the dominant follicle has already ruptured.","The LH surge is at midcycle, around day 14, where it triggers ovulation.","FSH drives early follicle growth; in the luteal phase progesterone's negative feedback holds it down."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Cycle phases and their dominant hormones (day 10 estradiol, day 21 progesterone)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-day21","fp-day10","fp-num-follicular","fp-2s-luteal","fp-preov-1","fp-menses"]}},{"id":"fp-day10","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best describes the hormones on day 10 of a typical 28-day cycle?","choices":["Estradiol rising from growing follicles, with negative feedback on FSH and LH","Progesterone high from the corpus luteum, holding GnRH, LH and FSH down","Estradiol above 200 pg/mL, setting off an LH surge by positive feedback","Estrogen and progesterone both falling abruptly as the corpus luteum regresses"],"answer":0,"ex":"Day 10 is mid-follicular: growing follicles make rising estradiol, which still gives negative feedback on GnRH, LH and FSH. Only when it passes about 200 pg/mL near midcycle does it switch to positive feedback.","src":"slides","why":["Day 10 is follicular phase: estradiol climbs as follicles grow and, below the critical level, feeds back negatively.","That is the luteal phase, after ovulation, around day 21.","That switch happens just before ovulation at midcycle, not on day 10.","That abrupt loss comes at the end of the luteal phase and prompts menses."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Cycle phases and their dominant hormones (day 10 estradiol, day 21 progesterone)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-day21","fp-day10","fp-num-follicular","fp-2s-luteal","fp-preov-1","fp-menses"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-200","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Estradiol switches from negative to positive feedback once its plasma level rises above about 200 picograms per milliliter.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The lecture's critical level is greater than 200 pg/mL. Below it, estradiol suppresses GnRH, LH and FSH; above it, estradiol sensitizes the pituitary to GnRH and an LH surge follows.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["That is the lecture's critical level for the switch to positive feedback.","It is true: above about 200 pg/mL, estradiol's feedback turns positive and the LH surge follows."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Negative vs positive feedback: estradiol above 200 pg/mL triggers the LH surge; progesterone is negative only","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-cyclic","fp-tf-200","fp-x-feedback","fp-tf-prog-fb","fp-preov-2","fp-anov-2","fp-num-axis"]}},{"id":"fp-inhibin","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"According to the lecture, what suppresses FSH as the follicle nears ovulation?","choices":["Inhibin produced by the follicle","Progesterone from the corpus luteum","Kisspeptin from KNDy neurons","Estrone made from adrenal DHEA"],"answer":0,"ex":"At midcycle the slide shows estradiol's positive feedback driving LH up while inhibin from the follicle holds FSH down. That falling FSH is why the granulosa cells switch on LH receptors to keep making estrogen.","src":"slides","why":["The midcycle slide says FSH is suppressed by the production of inhibin.","The corpus luteum doesn't exist until after ovulation, so its progesterone can't be the midcycle brake.","KNDy (kisspeptin) neurons relay signals to GnRH neurons; they aren't the FSH brake the slide names.","Estrone is the estrogen of menopause; it isn't the midcycle brake on FSH."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Ovulation: LH surge mechanism and timing (24-36 hours; 14 days before menses)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-num-ovulation","fp-seq-ovulation","fp-ovtime-1","fp-ovtime-2","fp-tf-14days","fp-inhibin"]}},{"id":"fp-num-ovulation","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about ovulation are true?\n1. Preovulatory Graafian follicles have low expression of LH receptors.\n2. The LH surge triggers the oocyte to resume meiosis.\n3. After the LH surge, collagenases weaken the follicle wall and prostaglandins increase blood flow.\n4. The egg is released about 24 to 36 hours after the LH surge.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Preovulatory follicles carry many LH receptors, so the surge can act on them: it restarts meiosis, raises collagenases (which weaken the wall) and prostaglandins (which increase blood flow), and the follicle ruptures typically 24 to 36 hours later.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It includes 1, which is false: preovulatory Graafian follicles have high expression of LH receptors, which is how the surge acts on them.","It includes 1, which is false: preovulatory Graafian follicles have high expression of LH receptors, which is how the surge acts on them. It leaves out 2, which is true: the surge lifts the block on meiosis, so the first division is complete at ovulation.","It leaves out 4, which is true: the lecture puts release typically 24 to 36 hours after the surge.","Statements 2, 3 and 4 are true. Statement 1 is false: preovulatory Graafian follicles have high expression of LH receptors, which is how the surge acts on them."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Ovulation: LH surge mechanism and timing (24-36 hours; 14 days before menses)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-num-ovulation","fp-seq-ovulation","fp-ovtime-1","fp-ovtime-2","fp-tf-14days","fp-inhibin"]}},{"id":"fp-seq-ovulation","lec":"fphys","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the events leading to ovulation in order.","steps":["Growing follicles raise plasma estradiol","Estradiol passes about 200 pg/mL and its feedback turns positive","The pituitary releases a surge of LH in response to GnRH","Collagenases and prostaglandins rise and weaken the follicle wall","The dominant follicle ruptures and releases the oocyte"],"ex":"Estradiol climbs, crosses the critical level and sensitizes the pituitary to GnRH; LH surges; collagenases and prostaglandins weaken the wall; and the follicle ruptures about 24 to 36 hours after the surge.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Ovulation: LH surge mechanism and timing (24-36 hours; 14 days before menses)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-num-ovulation","fp-seq-ovulation","fp-ovtime-1","fp-ovtime-2","fp-tf-14days","fp-inhibin"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-14days","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Ovulation occurs about 14 days before the next menses.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The lecture times midcycle at 14 days prior to menses, and the corpus luteum lasts about 14 days. In a 28-day cycle that puts ovulation on day 14.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Midcycle is 14 days before menses on the slide.","It is true: ovulation comes 14 days before menses, which lands on day 14 only in a 28-day cycle."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Ovulation: LH surge mechanism and timing (24-36 hours; 14 days before menses)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-num-ovulation","fp-seq-ovulation","fp-ovtime-1","fp-ovtime-2","fp-tf-14days","fp-inhibin"]}},{"id":"fp-num-follicular","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the follicular phase are true?\n1. It is also called the proliferative phase.\n2. It is dominated by progesterone.\n3. For most of it, estradiol gives negative feedback on GnRH, LH and FSH.\n4. It ends with the regression of the corpus luteum.","choices":["1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","1 and 4 only","3 and 4 only"],"answer":0,"ex":"The follicular, or proliferative, phase precedes ovulation and is dominated by estrogen, whose feedback is negative until it passes about 200 pg/mL. It ends with ovulation; corpus luteum regression ends the luteal phase.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statements 1 and 3 are true. Statement 2 is false: the follicular phase is dominated by estrogen; progesterone dominates the luteal phase. Statement 4 is false: it ends with ovulation; regression of the corpus luteum ends the luteal phase.","It includes 4, which is false: it ends with ovulation; regression of the corpus luteum ends the luteal phase.","It includes 4, which is false: it ends with ovulation; regression of the corpus luteum ends the luteal phase. It leaves out 3, which is true: the follicular-phase slide shows estradiol's negative feedback on GnRH, LH and FSH.","It includes 4, which is false: it ends with ovulation; regression of the corpus luteum ends the luteal phase. It leaves out 1, which is true: the slide labels the follicular phase the proliferative phase, the one before ovulation."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Cycle phases and their dominant hormones (day 10 estradiol, day 21 progesterone)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-day21","fp-day10","fp-num-follicular","fp-2s-luteal","fp-preov-1","fp-menses"]}},{"id":"fp-2s-luteal","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The luteal phase is driven by the dominant Graafian follicle.\n2. The luteal phase is also called the secretory phase.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Follicular = proliferative, before ovulation, driven by the growing follicle and estrogen. Luteal = secretory, after ovulation, driven by the corpus luteum and progesterone.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: the luteal phase follows ovulation and is driven by the corpus luteum, the ruptured follicle's remains.","Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide labels days 14 to 28 the secretory phase.","Statement 1 is false, not true: the luteal phase follows ovulation and is driven by the corpus luteum, the ruptured follicle's remains. Statement 2 is true, not false: the slide labels days 14 to 28 the secretory phase.","Statement 1 is false: the luteal phase follows ovulation and is driven by the corpus luteum, the ruptured follicle's remains. Statement 2 is true: the slide labels days 14 to 28 the secretory phase."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Cycle phases and their dominant hormones (day 10 estradiol, day 21 progesterone)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-day21","fp-day10","fp-num-follicular","fp-2s-luteal","fp-preov-1","fp-menses"]}},{"id":"fp-2s-mucus","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Estrogen initiates the production of watery cervical mucus.\n2. Progesterone decreases watery cervical mucus, so the mucus becomes thick.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cervical mucus follows the dominant hormone: watery under estrogen in the follicular phase, thick under progesterone in the luteal phase.","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: estrogen starts watery cervical mucus in the follicular phase. Statement 2 is true: in the luteal phase progesterone decreases watery mucus and the mucus becomes thick.","Statement 1 is true, not false: estrogen starts watery cervical mucus in the follicular phase. Statement 2 is true, not false: in the luteal phase progesterone decreases watery mucus and the mucus becomes thick.","Statement 2 is true, not false: in the luteal phase progesterone decreases watery mucus and the mucus becomes thick.","Statement 1 is true, not false: estrogen starts watery cervical mucus in the follicular phase."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Estrogen vs progesterone on the endometrium, myometrium and cervical mucus","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-mm-estrogen","fp-mm-progesterone","fp-2s-mucus","fp-tf-myometrium","fp-endo-luteal"]}},{"id":"fp-endo-luteal","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which change in the uterus or cervix belongs to the luteal phase?","choices":["Endometrial proliferation slows; secretions and vessels rise","The lining begins to grow and the endometrial arteries lengthen","The lining and blood are sloughed after an abrupt hormone loss","Watery cervical mucus begins to be made as estrogen climbs"],"answer":0,"ex":"Estrogen builds the lining (follicular phase); progesterone slows proliferation and makes the lining secretory and well vascularized (luteal phase), ready to receive a fertilized ovum.","src":"slides","why":["Progesterone slows proliferation and raises secretions and blood supply, preparing to receive a fertilized ovum.","Initial growth and artery lengthening are estrogen's work in the follicular phase.","That is menses, at the end of the cycle, when the corpus luteum regresses.","That is a follicular-phase estrogen effect; in the luteal phase progesterone makes the mucus thick."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Estrogen vs progesterone on the endometrium, myometrium and cervical mucus","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-mm-estrogen","fp-mm-progesterone","fp-2s-mucus","fp-tf-myometrium","fp-endo-luteal"]}},{"id":"fp-menses","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Without fertilization, what prompts menses at the end of the cycle?","choices":["Corpus luteum regression and an abrupt loss of estrogen and progesterone","A second LH surge from the pituitary that ruptures the endometrial arteries","A rise in inhibin that suppresses FSH and shrinks the endometrial lining","The placenta taking over progesterone production from the corpus luteum"],"answer":0,"ex":"The corpus luteum lasts about 14 days. When it regresses into the corpus albicans, estrogen and progesterone fall abruptly and the endometrial lining and blood slough off: menses.","src":"slides","why":["As the corpus luteum regresses, estrogen and progesterone drop abruptly and the lining and blood are sloughed.","There is one LH surge, at midcycle; it triggers ovulation, not menses.","Inhibin suppresses FSH; the lecture doesn't link it to shedding the lining.","Placental takeover happens with fertilization, and it keeps the lining rather than shedding it."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Cycle phases and their dominant hormones (day 10 estradiol, day 21 progesterone)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-day21","fp-day10","fp-num-follicular","fp-2s-luteal","fp-preov-1","fp-menses"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-myometrium","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Progesterone causes the myometrium to contract.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"On the myometrium the two hormones oppose each other: estrogen causes contraction, progesterone causes relaxation.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Progesterone relaxes the myometrium; estrogen is the one that contracts it.","It is false: progesterone relaxes the myometrium, and estrogen contracts it."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Estrogen vs progesterone on the endometrium, myometrium and cervical mucus","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-mm-estrogen","fp-mm-progesterone","fp-2s-mucus","fp-tf-myometrium","fp-endo-luteal"]}},{"id":"fp-mm-estrogen","lec":"fphys","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these are effects of estrogen in the follicular phase, according to the lecture? Select all that apply.","choices":["Growth of the endometrium","Lengthening of the endometrial arteries","Production of watery cervical mucus","Relaxation of the myometrium","Increased endometrial secretions"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Estrogen (follicular phase): endometrial growth, longer endometrial arteries, watery cervical mucus, myometrial contraction. Progesterone (luteal phase): slower proliferation, more secretions and vasculature, thick mucus, myometrial relaxation.","src":"slides","why":["Estrogen builds the endometrial lining in the follicular phase.","The slide lists artery lengthening among estrogen's follicular-phase effects.","Estrogen initiates watery cervical mucus.","Estrogen contracts the myometrium; progesterone is what relaxes it.","Rising endometrial secretions are progesterone's luteal-phase effect."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Estrogen vs progesterone on the endometrium, myometrium and cervical mucus","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-mm-estrogen","fp-mm-progesterone","fp-2s-mucus","fp-tf-myometrium","fp-endo-luteal"]}},{"id":"fp-mm-progesterone","lec":"fphys","type":"multi","stem":"Which of these are actions of progesterone, according to the lecture? Select all that apply.","choices":["Relaxing the myometrium","Thickening the cervical mucus","Increasing endometrial vasculature","Positive feedback that triggers the LH surge","Starting endometrial growth after menses"],"correct":[0,1,2],"ex":"Progesterone relaxes the myometrium, thickens cervical mucus and makes the endometrium secretory and well vascularized, and it gives only negative feedback. Starting the lining's growth and the LH surge are estrogen's jobs.","src":"slides","why":["Progesterone relaxes the myometrium, the opposite of estrogen.","Progesterone decreases watery mucus, so the mucus becomes thick.","Progesterone enhances and sustains endometrial growth and increases its blood supply.","That is estradiol above about 200 pg/mL; progesterone's feedback is only negative.","Estrogen starts the growth in the follicular phase; progesterone enhances and sustains it."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Estrogen vs progesterone on the endometrium, myometrium and cervical mucus","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-mm-estrogen","fp-mm-progesterone","fp-2s-mucus","fp-tf-myometrium","fp-endo-luteal"]}},{"id":"fp-meno-1","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains her FSH and LH results?","choices":["Her follicles are gone, so estradiol and inhibin no longer suppress the pituitary","A pituitary tumor is secreting FSH and LH, and they are driving her ovaries","Estrone from her adrenal glands is exerting positive feedback on her pituitary","Progesterone from a persisting corpus luteum is stimulating the pituitary"],"answer":0,"ex":"In menopause all remaining follicles undergo atresia, granulosa cells stop making estradiol and inhibin, and with no negative feedback GnRH, LH and FSH all rise. High gonadotropins with low estradiol point to the ovary as the problem.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","52-year-old woman at a dental recall visit; no menstrual period for 14 months"],["Symptoms","Hot flashes, vaginal dryness"],["FSH","78 mIU/mL (high)"],["LH","45 mIU/mL (high)"],["Estradiol","Under 20 pg/mL (low)"],["Pregnancy test","Negative"]],"pcase":"fphys-case1","why":["Complete atresia removes the follicles' estradiol and inhibin, so negative feedback is lost and FSH and LH rise.","If the pituitary were driving working ovaries, her estradiol would be high, not low.","Estrone becomes her main estrogen, but the lecture ties the rise in FSH and LH to lost negative feedback.","She has no cycles and so no corpus luteum, and progesterone's feedback is only negative anyway."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Menopause: atresia, lost negative feedback, high GnRH, LH and FSH, estrone, symptoms","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-meno-1","fp-meno-2","fp-num-meno","fp-x-meno","fp-tf-estrone"]}},{"id":"fp-meno-2","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"According to the lecture, which change best explains her hot flashes?","choices":["Rising GnRH after estrogen's negative feedback is lost","Rising inhibin from the last follicles, which suppresses FSH","Falling FSH and LH as the pituitary stops responding to GnRH","Rising estrone from adrenal DHEA taking over from estradiol"],"answer":0,"ex":"The slide calls hot flashes vascular instability; the speaker notes tie them to GnRH, which climbs very high once the follicles stop making estrogen.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","52-year-old woman at a dental recall visit; no menstrual period for 14 months"],["Symptoms","Hot flashes, vaginal dryness"],["FSH","78 mIU/mL (high)"],["LH","45 mIU/mL (high)"],["Estradiol","Under 20 pg/mL (low)"],["Pregnancy test","Negative"]],"pcase":"fphys-case1","why":["The speaker notes say GnRH goes sky high without the follicles' estrogen, and rising GnRH can lead to hot flashes.","Granulosa cells stop making inhibin in menopause, so it falls rather than rises.","FSH and LH rise in menopause, as her labs show.","Estrone does become the dominant estrogen, but the lecture ties hot flashes to rising GnRH."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Menopause: atresia, lost negative feedback, high GnRH, LH and FSH, estrone, symptoms","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-meno-1","fp-meno-2","fp-num-meno","fp-x-meno","fp-tf-estrone"]}},{"id":"fp-num-meno","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about menopause are true?\n1. All remaining follicles undergo atresia, a form of programmed cell death.\n2. Estrone becomes the dominant estrogen.\n3. FSH and LH fall because the ovary no longer responds to them.\n4. Granulosa cells keep making inhibin.","choices":["1 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 4","2 and 3 only"],"answer":1,"ex":"Menopause is complete atresia of the remaining follicles. Granulosa cells stop making estradiol and inhibin, so with no negative feedback GnRH, LH and FSH rise, and estrone (from adrenal DHEA) becomes the dominant estrogen.","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["It leaves out 2, which is true: with the follicles gone, estrone, made from adrenal DHEA, becomes the main estrogen.","Statements 1 and 2 are true. Statement 3 is false: FSH and LH rise, because nothing gives negative feedback anymore. Statement 4 is false: granulosa cells stop making both estradiol and inhibin.","It includes 4, which is false: granulosa cells stop making both estradiol and inhibin.","It includes 3, which is false: FSH and LH rise, because nothing gives negative feedback anymore. It leaves out 1, which is true: atresia, a form of programmed cell death, takes the last follicles."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Menopause: atresia, lost negative feedback, high GnRH, LH and FSH, estrone, symptoms","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-meno-1","fp-meno-2","fp-num-meno","fp-x-meno","fp-tf-estrone"]}},{"id":"fp-x-meno","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Each of the following is a feature of menopause listed in the lecture EXCEPT:","choices":["Increased breast mass","Thinning of the vaginal epithelium","Accelerated bone loss","Decreased vaginal secretions"],"answer":0,"ex":"The menopause slide's symptoms: thinning of the vaginal epithelium, decreased vaginal secretions, decreased breast mass, accelerated bone loss and vascular instability (hot flashes), all following the decline in estradiol.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Breast mass decreases in menopause, so this is the exception.","The lecture lists thinning of the vaginal epithelium among the symptoms.","Bone loss speeds up once estradiol secretion declines.","Decreased vaginal secretions are on the lecture's list of symptoms."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Menopause: atresia, lost negative feedback, high GnRH, LH and FSH, estrone, symptoms","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-meno-1","fp-meno-2","fp-num-meno","fp-x-meno","fp-tf-estrone"]}},{"id":"fp-prl-1","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which category from the lecture's reproductive-disorders slide best fits her?","choices":["Secondary amenorrhea caused by hyperprolactinemia","Primary amenorrhea caused by a hormonal disorder","Secondary amenorrhea caused by menopause","A disorder of sexual differentiation, such as Turner syndrome"],"answer":0,"ex":"The slide sorts reproductive disorders into disorders of sexual differentiation (example: Turner syndrome), primary amenorrhea and secondary amenorrhea. Secondary amenorrhea's causes are pregnancy, lactation, menopause and hyperprolactinemia; her pregnancy test is negative, she isn't nursing, her FSH is normal and her prolactin is high.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman at a dental visit; no period for 5 months"],["History","Regular periods since age 12; not breastfeeding"],["Pregnancy test (hCG)","Negative"],["Prolactin","96 ng/mL (high)"],["FSH","5 mIU/mL (normal)"]],"pcase":"fphys-case3","why":["Her periods started and then stopped, and her prolactin is high; the slide lists hyperprolactinemia under secondary amenorrhea.","Primary amenorrhea means periods never started, and hers were regular from age 12.","At 34 with a normal FSH, menopause doesn't fit: menopause raises FSH.","Turner syndrome is the slide's example of a differentiation disorder; her history and high prolactin point elsewhere."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Amenorrhea: primary vs secondary and its causes","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-prl-1","fp-prl-2","fp-x-amen","fp-tf-amen"]}},{"id":"fp-prl-2","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Her pregnancy test and her history rule out two of the lecture's listed causes of secondary amenorrhea.\n2. Her normal FSH supports menopause as the cause of her missed periods.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Work down the slide's list of causes of secondary amenorrhea: pregnancy (her test is negative), lactation (she isn't breastfeeding), menopause (it raises FSH, and hers is normal) and hyperprolactinemia (her prolactin is high). Only the last one fits.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","34-year-old woman at a dental visit; no period for 5 months"],["History","Regular periods since age 12; not breastfeeding"],["Pregnancy test (hCG)","Negative"],["Prolactin","96 ng/mL (high)"],["FSH","5 mIU/mL (normal)"]],"pcase":"fphys-case3","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: menopause raises FSH, because the follicles' negative feedback is gone, so a normal FSH argues against it.","Statement 1 is true, not false: her negative hCG rules out pregnancy, and she isn't breastfeeding, so lactation is out too.","Statement 1 is true: her negative hCG rules out pregnancy, and she isn't breastfeeding, so lactation is out too. Statement 2 is false: menopause raises FSH, because the follicles' negative feedback is gone, so a normal FSH argues against it.","Statement 1 is true, not false: her negative hCG rules out pregnancy, and she isn't breastfeeding, so lactation is out too. Statement 2 is false, not true: menopause raises FSH, because the follicles' negative feedback is gone, so a normal FSH argues against it."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Amenorrhea: primary vs secondary and its causes","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-prl-1","fp-prl-2","fp-x-amen","fp-tf-amen"]}},{"id":"fp-x-amen","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which of the following is NOT listed in the lecture as a cause of secondary amenorrhea?","choices":["Turner syndrome","Pregnancy","Lactation","Hyperprolactinemia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Secondary amenorrhea on the slide: pregnancy, lactation, menopause and hyperprolactinemia (an increase in prolactin). Turner syndrome sits in a separate group, disorders of sexual differentiation.","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Turner syndrome is the slide's example of a disorder of sexual differentiation, not one of the causes of secondary amenorrhea.","Pregnancy is on the slide's list of causes of secondary amenorrhea.","Lactation is on the slide's list of causes of secondary amenorrhea.","An increase in prolactin is on the slide's list of causes of secondary amenorrhea."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Amenorrhea: primary vs secondary and its causes","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-prl-1","fp-prl-2","fp-x-amen","fp-tf-amen"]}},{"id":"fp-tf-amen","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Amenorrhea is the absence of menstruation, often defined as missing one or more menstrual periods.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"That is the lecture's definition. Primary amenorrhea means periods never started; secondary means periods that had started have stopped.","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["That is the definition in the lecture's notes.","It is true: the lecture defines amenorrhea as the absence of menstruation, often missing one or more periods."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Amenorrhea: primary vs secondary and its causes","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-prl-1","fp-prl-2","fp-x-amen","fp-tf-amen"]}},{"id":"fp-anov-1","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains her low day-21 progesterone?","choices":["She didn't ovulate, so no corpus luteum formed to secrete progesterone","Her corpus luteum has already regressed into the corpus albicans","Her granulosa cells lack aromatase, so they cannot make progesterone","The placenta has taken over progesterone production from the corpus luteum"],"answer":0,"ex":"Luteal progesterone comes from the corpus luteum, which forms only from a follicle that has ruptured. A low mid-luteal progesterone with no LH surge means she did not ovulate this cycle.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","30-year-old woman trying to conceive; cycles usually about 28 days"],["Home LH tests","Daily through the cycle: no surge detected"],["Estradiol (serial)","Highest value this cycle: 140 pg/mL"],["Progesterone, day 21","0.8 ng/mL (low for the luteal phase)"],["Pregnancy test","Negative"]],"pcase":"fphys-case2","why":["The corpus luteum is the ruptured follicle's remains; with no LH surge and no rupture there is none to drive a luteal phase.","The corpus luteum lasts about 14 days after ovulation, so on day 21 it should still be working.","Aromatase converts testosterone to estrogen; it isn't the step that makes progesterone.","Placental takeover needs a pregnancy, and her test is negative."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"The three estrogens and the corpus luteum","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-match-steroids","fp-num-cl","fp-tf-cl-cells","fp-anov-1","fp-match-roles"]}},{"id":"fp-anov-2","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains why she had no LH surge this cycle?","choices":["Her estradiol never passed about 200 pg/mL, so feedback never turned positive","Her progesterone stayed too low to give the positive feedback the surge needs","High inhibin from her growing follicle blocked the pituitary's LH surge","Progesterone from her corpus luteum suppressed LH before midcycle"],"answer":0,"ex":"The LH surge needs estradiol to reach a critical level (greater than 200 pg/mL) so its feedback switches to positive. Her estradiol peaked at 140, so feedback stayed negative, there was no surge, no rupture and no corpus luteum.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","30-year-old woman trying to conceive; cycles usually about 28 days"],["Home LH tests","Daily through the cycle: no surge detected"],["Estradiol (serial)","Highest value this cycle: 140 pg/mL"],["Progesterone, day 21","0.8 ng/mL (low for the luteal phase)"],["Pregnancy test","Negative"]],"pcase":"fphys-case2","why":["Below the critical level estradiol only gives negative feedback, so the pituitary never fires an LH surge.","Progesterone gives only negative feedback; the surge depends on estradiol's positive feedback.","Inhibin suppresses FSH; the lecture doesn't give it a role in blocking LH.","The corpus luteum forms only after ovulation, so it can't act before midcycle."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Negative vs positive feedback: estradiol above 200 pg/mL triggers the LH surge; progesterone is negative only","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-cyclic","fp-tf-200","fp-x-feedback","fp-tf-prog-fb","fp-preov-2","fp-anov-2","fp-num-axis"]}},{"id":"fp-ovtime-1","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Using the lecture's timing of ovulation relative to menses, on about which day of her 32-day cycles does she ovulate?","choices":["Day 18, about 14 days before her next period starts","Day 14, because ovulation falls on day 14 in every cycle","Day 16, because ovulation falls at the midpoint of any cycle","Day 20, about three days after her LH test turned positive"],"answer":0,"ex":"The lecture times ovulation at 14 days before menses, and the corpus luteum lasts about 14 days. In a 32-day cycle that is day 18, about a day after her day-17 LH surge, which fits the 24 to 36 hour lag.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","27-year-old woman with regular 32-day cycles"],["Cycle tracking","Home LH test positive on the morning of day 17"]],"pcase":"fphys-case4","why":["Ovulation is 14 days before menses, and 32 minus 14 is 18, which also fits her LH surge on day 17.","Day 14 holds only for a 28-day cycle; the fixed part is the 14 days before menses.","Halving the cycle works only for 28 days; the lecture times ovulation back from the next menses.","Release comes 24 to 36 hours after the surge, not three days."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Ovulation: LH surge mechanism and timing (24-36 hours; 14 days before menses)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-num-ovulation","fp-seq-ovulation","fp-ovtime-1","fp-ovtime-2","fp-tf-14days","fp-inhibin"]}},{"id":"fp-ovtime-2","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Her LH test turned positive on the morning of day 17. When is her follicle most likely to rupture?","choices":["About 24 to 36 hours later, around day 18","Within an hour or two, on the morning of day 17","About 14 days later, as menses begins","It already ruptured, since ovulation comes before the surge"],"answer":0,"ex":"The LH surge triggers rupture: collagenases weaken the follicle wall and prostaglandins increase blood flow, and the egg is released typically 24 to 36 hours after the surge.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","27-year-old woman with regular 32-day cycles"],["Cycle tracking","Home LH test positive on the morning of day 17"]],"pcase":"fphys-case4","why":["The lecture says the egg is released typically 24 to 36 hours after the LH surge.","The surge first has to raise collagenases and prostaglandins that weaken the wall; release follows a day or more later.","Fourteen days is the luteal phase, from ovulation to menses.","The surge comes first and triggers the rupture; ovulation follows it."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Ovulation: LH surge mechanism and timing (24-36 hours; 14 days before menses)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-num-ovulation","fp-seq-ovulation","fp-ovtime-1","fp-ovtime-2","fp-tf-14days","fp-inhibin"]}},{"id":"fp-preov-1","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which point of the cycle best matches her results?","choices":["Midcycle, just before ovulation","Early follicular phase, just after menses","Mid-luteal phase, with an active corpus luteum","After menopause, with negative feedback lost"],"answer":0,"ex":"Estradiol past the critical level (greater than 200 pg/mL), LH climbing steeply and progesterone still low is the pattern just before ovulation: the follicle is ripe and the corpus luteum hasn't formed yet.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","24-year-old woman with regular cycles; blood drawn for a research study"],["Estradiol","280 pg/mL"],["LH","52 mIU/mL, up from 9 two days earlier"],["Progesterone","0.6 ng/mL (low)"]],"pcase":"fphys-case5","why":["High estradiol, a sharply rising LH and low progesterone are the picture just before ovulation.","Early on, estradiol is still low and feeds back negatively, so LH isn't surging.","The luteal phase would show high progesterone from the corpus luteum, not 0.6 ng/mL.","Menopausal LH is high, but estradiol is low, not 280 pg/mL."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Cycle phases and their dominant hormones (day 10 estradiol, day 21 progesterone)","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-day21","fp-day10","fp-num-follicular","fp-2s-luteal","fp-preov-1","fp-menses"]}},{"id":"fp-preov-2","lec":"fphys","type":"mcq","stem":"Which best explains her rising LH?","choices":["Estradiol above about 200 pg/mL has made the pituitary more sensitive to GnRH","Progesterone has switched to positive feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary","Her follicles are gone, so negative feedback on the pituitary has been lost","Inhibin from the dominant follicle is stimulating LH release from the pituitary"],"answer":0,"ex":"Rising estradiol progressively sensitizes the pituitary to GnRH; once it passes about 200 pg/mL the result is a burst of LH. This positive feedback is what makes the female cycle, and the surge triggers ovulation.","src":"hub","pbox":[["Patient","24-year-old woman with regular cycles; blood drawn for a research study"],["Estradiol","280 pg/mL"],["LH","52 mIU/mL, up from 9 two days earlier"],["Progesterone","0.6 ng/mL (low)"]],"pcase":"fphys-case5","why":["Past the critical level estradiol's feedback turns positive: it sensitizes the pituitary, so GnRH sets off the LH surge.","Progesterone gives only negative feedback, and hers is low.","That is menopause, where estradiol is low; hers is 280 pg/mL from a working follicle.","Inhibin suppresses FSH; it does not stimulate LH."],"likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"Negative vs positive feedback: estradiol above 200 pg/mL triggers the LH surge; progesterone is negative only","basis":"objectives","ids":["fp-cyclic","fp-tf-200","fp-x-feedback","fp-tf-prog-fb","fp-preov-2","fp-anov-2","fp-num-axis"]}},{"id":"fp-match-roles","lec":"fphys","type":"match","stem":"Match each structure to its role in the lecture.","pairs":[["Theca interna","Has LH receptors and makes testosterone"],["Granulosa cells","Have FSH receptors and convert testosterone to estrogen"],["Corpus luteum","Secretes progesterone through the luteal phase"],["KNDy neurons","Relay stress and nutrition signals to GnRH neurons"],["Fimbriae","Sweep the ovulated oocyte into the fallopian tube"]],"ex":"Theca (LH, testosterone) and granulosa (FSH, aromatase) are the two cells of the two-cell model; the corpus luteum runs the luteal phase; KNDy neurons bridge outside signals to GnRH; the fimbriae carry the oocyte into the tube.","src":"slides","likely":{"lec":"fphys","t":"The three estrogens and the corpus luteum","basis":"slides","ids":["fp-match-steroids","fp-num-cl","fp-tf-cl-cells","fp-anov-1","fp-match-roles"]}},{"id":"r-h1-gq","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"A hormone binds a Gq-coupled receptor. What happens next?","choices":["Phospholipase C is activated; IP3 releases calcium from the ER","Adenylate cyclase is activated and cAMP rises inside the cell","Adenylate cyclase is inhibited and cAMP falls","The receptor moves to the nucleus and binds DNA"],"answer":0,"ex":"Gq activates phospholipase C, which splits PIP2 into IP3 and DAG. IP3, the second messenger, opens calcium channels in the endoplasmic reticulum. Dr. Herr: \"kind of commit this table to memory.\" (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"rec","why":["Gq → phospholipase C → IP3 (and DAG) → calcium from the ER.","That is Gs.","That is Gi.","That describes an intracellular steroid receptor, not a G protein."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L1 Endocrine Histology 1: Gs, Gi and Gq: what each does to cAMP, IP3 and calcium","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h1-gq","r-h1-gi","r-h1-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-h1-gi","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which G protein inhibits adenylate cyclase and lowers cAMP?","choices":["Gi","Gs","Gq","Gt"],"answer":0,"ex":"Gs activates adenylate cyclase (cAMP up); Gi inhibits it (cAMP down); Gq activates phospholipase C (IP3 up). (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"slides","why":["\"I\" for inhibitory: adenylate cyclase off, cAMP down.","Gs is stimulatory: cAMP up.","Gq works through phospholipase C and IP3.","Gt (transducin) isn't in this lecture's table; it works in the retina."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L1 Endocrine Histology 1: Gs, Gi and Gq: what each does to cAMP, IP3 and calcium","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h1-gq","r-h1-gi","r-h1-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-h1-2s-4","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Gs activates adenylate cyclase and increases cAMP.\n2. Gq activates phospholipase C and increases IP3.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Both are on the table Dr. Herr said to memorize: Gs → adenylate cyclase → cAMP up; Gi → adenylate cyclase down; Gq → phospholipase C → IP3 up. (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: Gs stimulates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP. Statement 2 is true: Gq activates phospholipase C, raising IP3 (and calcium release).","Statement 1 is true, not false: Gs stimulates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP. Statement 2 is true, not false: Gq activates phospholipase C, raising IP3 (and calcium release).","Statement 2 is true, not false: Gq activates phospholipase C, raising IP3 (and calcium release).","Statement 1 is true, not false: Gs stimulates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L1 Endocrine Histology 1: Gs, Gi and Gq: what each does to cAMP, IP3 and calcium","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h1-gq","r-h1-gi","r-h1-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-h1-acidophils","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which cells of the pars distalis are acidophils?","choices":["Somatotrophs and lactotrophs","Thyrotrophs and gonadotrophs","Corticotrophs and thyrotrophs","Stem cells and degranulated cells"],"answer":0,"ex":"Acidophils contain polypeptide hormones: somatotrophs (GH) and lactotrophs (prolactin), \"S and L.\" Basophils (glycoprotein hormones) are thyrotrophs, gonadotrophs and corticotrophs, \"T, G and C.\" (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"slides","why":["\"S and L\": GH and prolactin.","These are basophils.","These are basophils.","Those are chromophobes, with little or no hormone."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L1 Endocrine Histology 1: FLAT PiG: which pars distalis cells are acidophils and basophils","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h1-acidophils","r-h1-2s-2"]}},{"id":"r-h1-2s-2","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Basophils of the pars distalis contain glycoprotein hormones.\n2. Acidophils include thyrotrophs and gonadotrophs.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Basophils (glycoprotein hormones): thyrotrophs, gonadotrophs, corticotrophs. Acidophils (polypeptide hormones): somatotrophs and lactotrophs. (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: thyrotrophs and gonadotrophs are basophils; acidophils are somatotrophs and lactotrophs.","Statement 1 is true, not false: basophils hold glycoprotein hormones: \"T, G and C.\"","Statement 1 is true: basophils hold glycoprotein hormones: \"T, G and C.\" Statement 2 is false: thyrotrophs and gonadotrophs are basophils; acidophils are somatotrophs and lactotrophs.","Statement 1 is true, not false: basophils hold glycoprotein hormones: \"T, G and C.\" Statement 2 is false, not true: thyrotrophs and gonadotrophs are basophils; acidophils are somatotrophs and lactotrophs."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L1 Endocrine Histology 1: FLAT PiG: which pars distalis cells are acidophils and basophils","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h1-acidophils","r-h1-2s-2"]}},{"id":"r-rv-paracrine","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which of the following pairs is an example of paracrine regulation?","choices":["Somatostatin - insulin secretion","Somatostatin - GH secretion","Norepinephrine - CRH secretion","CRH - ACTH secretion","Dopamine - prolactin secretion"],"answer":0,"ex":"At the review: paracrine means signaling to neighboring cells. Delta cells and beta cells sit side by side in the islet, so somatostatin reaches the beta cells locally; the other pairs travel too far (portal blood or nerves) to count. (Exam 1 · L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary)","src":"review","why":["Delta-cell somatostatin acts on neighboring beta cells in the islet.","Hypothalamic somatostatin reaches the pituitary through the portal blood: too far for paracrine.","Norepinephrine on CRH neurons is neural signaling.","CRH reaches the corticotrophs through the portal blood.","Hypothalamic dopamine reaches the lactotrophs through the portal blood."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary: Paracrine vs neuroendocrine vs endocrine","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-paracrine","r-rv-neuroendo","r-hp-neuroendo"]}},{"id":"r-rv-neuroendo","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Release of which hormone is an example of neuroendocrine secretion?","choices":["Oxytocin","GH","Cortisol","Prolactin","ACTH"],"answer":0,"ex":"Neuroendocrine hormones are made by neurons and released into the blood: the hypothalamic releasing hormones and the posterior pituitary hormones (oxytocin and ADH), which hypothalamic neurons make and release from their axon endings. (Exam 1 · L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary)","src":"review","why":["Hypothalamic neurons make oxytocin and release it into the blood from the posterior pituitary.","GH comes from somatotrophs, anterior pituitary gland cells, not neurons.","Cortisol comes from adrenal cortex cells.","Prolactin comes from lactotrophs, anterior pituitary gland cells.","ACTH comes from corticotrophs, anterior pituitary gland cells."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary: Paracrine vs neuroendocrine vs endocrine","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-paracrine","r-rv-neuroendo","r-hp-neuroendo"]}},{"id":"r-hp-neuroendo","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"If an exam question asks about neuroendocrine hormones, Dr. Galin said it most likely means:","choices":["Releasing hormones from the hypothalamus","Hormones from the anterior pituitary","Hormones from the adrenal cortex","Paracrine signals inside the pancreatic islets"],"answer":0,"ex":"Neuroendocrine = made by the brain (CNS) and carried in the blood. The hypothalamus is part of the brain; the pituitary is not. \"If we were asking you a question about neuroendocrine hormones... most likely we are talking about releasing hormones coming from the hypothalamus.\" (Exam 1 · L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary)","src":"rec","why":["Hypothalamic releasing hormones come from the CNS.","The pituitary is outside the CNS; its hormones are endocrine.","Adrenal cortex hormones come from a peripheral gland.","Islet signaling is paracrine."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary: Paracrine vs neuroendocrine vs endocrine","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-paracrine","r-rv-neuroendo","r-hp-neuroendo"]}},{"id":"r-rv-gs","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which of the following best describes the intracellular mechanism by which a hormone using a Gαs-coupled receptor stimulates secretion of a protein hormone?","choices":["Activation of adenylate cyclase, increased cAMP, and stimulation of PKA","Activation of phospholipase C and increased intracellular calcium","Direct gene transcription via nuclear hormone receptor binding","Opening of voltage-gated sodium channels on the secretory vesicle membrane","Inhibition of tyrosine kinase activity to prevent hormone degradation"],"answer":0,"ex":"Gs activates adenylate cyclase: cAMP rises, PKA phosphorylates proteins and secretion follows. Gq is the phospholipase C / IP3 / calcium route; Gi lowers cAMP. (Exam 1 · L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary)","src":"review","why":["Gs turns on adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP and activating PKA.","Phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium release are the Gq pathway.","Nuclear receptors are for steroid and thyroid hormones, not G-protein receptors.","Secretory vesicles don't open sodium channels to release hormone.","Gs receptors don't act through tyrosine kinases."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary: Receptors: Gs, Gq and Gi; insulin is a tyrosine kinase, GH uses JAK2","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-gs","r-hp-gs-ca","r-hp-ins-vs-gh","r-hp-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-hp-gs-ca","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"According to Dr. Galin, how does a Gαs-coupled receptor lead to secretion of a peptide hormone?","choices":["cAMP and PKA rise and calcium flows in from outside the cell","Phospholipase C makes IP3, which releases calcium from the ER stores","Calcium is pumped out, hyperpolarizing the cell","The receptor's own tyrosine kinase phosphorylates IRS proteins"],"answer":0,"ex":"Gαs: adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA, and calcium channels are inserted in the cell membrane, so calcium moves from outside to inside and triggers the secretory pathway. Gαq raises calcium from intracellular stores instead. (Exam 1 · L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary)","src":"rec","why":["Gαs brings calcium in from outside the cell.","That is the Gαq route (calcium from internal stores).","Lowering calcium would stop secretion, which is closer to Gαi.","That describes the insulin receptor, not a G protein."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary: Receptors: Gs, Gq and Gi; insulin is a tyrosine kinase, GH uses JAK2","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-gs","r-hp-gs-ca","r-hp-ins-vs-gh","r-hp-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-hp-ins-vs-gh","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"How does the growth hormone receptor differ from the insulin receptor?","choices":["GH's receptor uses associated JAK; insulin's has its own kinase","GH's receptor is a G-protein-coupled receptor; insulin's is nuclear","GH's receptor has its own tyrosine kinase; insulin's uses associated JAK","Both are ion channels"],"answer":0,"ex":"Insulin receptor: intrinsic tyrosine kinase in its intracellular tails, phosphorylating insulin receptor substrates. GH receptor: tyrosine kinase-associated; JAK sits on its tails and starts JAK-STAT signaling when GH binds. (Exam 1 · L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary)","src":"rec","why":["Associated (JAK) for GH, intrinsic for insulin.","Neither is a GPCR or a nuclear receptor.","The two are swapped.","Neither is an ion channel."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary: Receptors: Gs, Gq and Gi; insulin is a tyrosine kinase, GH uses JAK2","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-gs","r-hp-gs-ca","r-hp-ins-vs-gh","r-hp-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-hp-2s-4","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Gαi activation raises intracellular calcium and triggers hormone secretion.\n2. The growth hormone receptor has intrinsic tyrosine kinase domains.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Gαi blocks both routes that raise calcium, so it inhibits secretion. The GH receptor uses associated JAK kinases; the insulin receptor is the one with intrinsic tyrosine kinase domains. (Exam 1 · L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary)","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: Gαi prevents the calcium rise, so there is no secretion. Statement 2 is false, not true: GH's receptor is tyrosine kinase-associated (JAK); insulin's is the intrinsic one.","Statement 1 is false: Gαi prevents the calcium rise, so there is no secretion. Statement 2 is false: GH's receptor is tyrosine kinase-associated (JAK); insulin's is the intrinsic one.","Statement 1 is false, not true: Gαi prevents the calcium rise, so there is no secretion.","Statement 2 is false, not true: GH's receptor is tyrosine kinase-associated (JAK); insulin's is the intrinsic one."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L2 Hypothalamus and Pituitary: Receptors: Gs, Gq and Gi; insulin is a tyrosine kinase, GH uses JAK2","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-gs","r-hp-gs-ca","r-hp-ins-vs-gh","r-hp-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-rv-gh-except","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"All of the following are true about growth hormone in humans EXCEPT:","choices":["Somatostatin withdrawal is sufficient to induce GH secretion","Much of our daily GH secretion occurs when we first enter deep sleep","Integrated daily GH secretion is highest during the pubertal growth spurt","Only human-derived GH is clinically useful","Thyroid hormones increase pituitary GH synthesis"],"answer":0,"ex":"At the review: somatostatin is a negative regulator and can suppress GH, but taking it away is not enough by itself; GH secretion needs the hypothalamus's GHRH signal. The biggest pulse comes soon after sleep begins, GH output peaks at puberty, GH is species-specific (only human GH works in people), and thyroid hormone supports GH synthesis. (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"review","fmt":"except","why":["Not true: removing somatostatin's brake isn't enough; GHRH from the hypothalamus has to drive GH.","True: the largest, most predictable pulse comes soon after falling asleep.","True: daily GH output and IGF-1 are highest during the pubertal growth spurt.","True: GH is species-specific, so only human GH works in people.","True: thyroid hormone is needed for growth and supports GH synthesis."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L3 Endocrinology of Growth: Regulation of GH secretion: GHRH (Gs) vs somatostatin (Gi), sleep, ghrelin","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-rv-gh-except","r-gr-ss-gi","r-gr-ghrh-gs","r-gr-num-1"]}},{"id":"r-gr-ss-gi","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"How does somatostatin stop GH secretion?","choices":["Through a Gi receptor: cAMP doesn't rise and the cell hyperpolarizes","Through a Gs receptor that raises cAMP and opens calcium channels in the cell","By binding GH in the blood","By destroying somatotrophs"],"answer":0,"ex":"Somatostatin binds a Gi-coupled receptor: no rise in cAMP, potassium channels activated, hyperpolarization, no GH secretion. GHRH and somatostatin are the \"yin-yang\" of GH control. (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"slides","why":["Gi, no cAMP, hyperpolarization.","A Gs receptor would raise cAMP and secretion.","It acts on the somatotroph, not on GH in blood.","It inhibits, it doesn't destroy."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L3 Endocrinology of Growth: Regulation of GH secretion: GHRH (Gs) vs somatostatin (Gi), sleep, ghrelin","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-rv-gh-except","r-gr-ss-gi","r-gr-ghrh-gs","r-gr-num-1"]}},{"id":"r-gr-ghrh-gs","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"GHRH stimulates somatotrophs through which signaling pathway?","choices":["Gαs, adenylyl cyclase and cAMP","Gi, lowering cAMP","Gq, phospholipase C and IP3 only","A nuclear receptor"],"answer":0,"ex":"GHRH binds a Gαs-coupled receptor: cAMP rises, increasing GH transcription and synthesis; cAMP also lets sodium in, depolarizing the cell, opening calcium channels and releasing preformed GH. (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"slides","why":["Gαs → cAMP → synthesis and release.","Gi is somatostatin's receptor.","The slide describes a Gs/cAMP pathway for GHRH.","GHRH is a peptide with a surface receptor."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L3 Endocrinology of Growth: Regulation of GH secretion: GHRH (Gs) vs somatostatin (Gi), sleep, ghrelin","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-rv-gh-except","r-gr-ss-gi","r-gr-ghrh-gs","r-gr-num-1"]}},{"id":"r-gr-num-1","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about GH regulation are true?\n1. GHRH raises GH through Gαs and cAMP.\n2. Ghrelin inhibits GH release.\n3. Somatostatin hyperpolarizes somatotrophs.\n4. IGF-1 exerts negative feedback on GH.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":2,"ex":"Ghrelin stimulates GH. The other three are true. (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statement 2 is false.","Leaves out 4, which is true.","Only statement 2 is false: ghrelin raises GH.","Includes 2 and leaves out 1."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L3 Endocrinology of Growth: Regulation of GH secretion: GHRH (Gs) vs somatostatin (Gi), sleep, ghrelin","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-rv-gh-except","r-gr-ss-gi","r-gr-ghrh-gs","r-gr-num-1"]}},{"id":"r-gr-r-ssfb","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the main way GH and IGF-1 feed back to limit GH secretion?","choices":["They make the hypothalamus release more somatostatin","They block GH synthesis in the liver's hepatocytes, where GH is made","They raise ghrelin, which competes with GH at its receptor","They stimulate GHRH release from the arcuate nucleus"],"answer":0,"ex":"Several negative loops (hierarchical feedback), but \"the big one is this somatostatin production\": GH and the IGFs prompt the hypothalamus to release somatostatin, which shuts the somatotrophs down through Gi. (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"rec","why":["Somatostatin release from the hypothalamus is the main brake on GH.","GH is made in the pituitary's somatotrophs, not the liver; the liver makes IGF-1.","Ghrelin raises GH; it doesn't compete at the GH receptor.","Feedback lowers GHRH; it doesn't stimulate it."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L3 Endocrinology of Growth: Regulation of GH secretion: feedback by GH and IGF-1 (they raise somatostatin)","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-gr-r-ssfb","r-gr-igf-fb","r-gr-ss-glucose","r-gr-f-igfinj"]}},{"id":"r-gr-igf-fb","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which is NOT one of the ways IGF-1 suppresses GH secretion?","choices":["Stimulating GHRH release from the hypothalamus","Acting directly on somatotrophs through its own receptor","Increasing somatostatin secretion","Suppressing GHRH release"],"answer":0,"ex":"IGF-1 feeds back directly on the pituitary (through its own receptor tyrosine kinase) and indirectly by suppressing GHRH and increasing somatostatin. GH also inhibits itself in a short loop. (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"slides","fmt":"except","why":["Stimulating GHRH would raise GH, the opposite of feedback.","IGF-1 does act directly on the pituitary.","IGF-1 does raise somatostatin.","IGF-1 does suppress GHRH."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L3 Endocrinology of Growth: Regulation of GH secretion: feedback by GH and IGF-1 (they raise somatostatin)","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-gr-r-ssfb","r-gr-igf-fb","r-gr-ss-glucose","r-gr-f-igfinj"]}},{"id":"r-gr-ss-glucose","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"High blood glucose and high free fatty acids lower GH secretion because they:","choices":["Stimulate somatostatin release","Stimulate GHRH release","Activate the GH receptor","Increase ghrelin"],"answer":0,"ex":"On the axis diagram, high glucose and FFA stimulate somatostatin, a negative regulator of GH. (GH itself raises glucose and free fatty acids, so this is feedback.) (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"slides","why":["Glucose and FFA raise somatostatin, which lowers GH.","Raising GHRH would raise GH.","Activating the GH receptor isn't how they act.","Ghrelin would raise GH."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L3 Endocrinology of Growth: Regulation of GH secretion: feedback by GH and IGF-1 (they raise somatostatin)","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-gr-r-ssfb","r-gr-igf-fb","r-gr-ss-glucose","r-gr-f-igfinj"]}},{"id":"r-gr-f-igfinj","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient is given IGF-1 injections. What happens to the patient's own GH secretion?","choices":["It falls, because IGF-1 feeds back on the pituitary and hypothalamus","It rises, because IGF-1 mimics GHRH","It stays the same, because IGF-1 only acts at the growth plate","It rises, because IGF-1 is diabetogenic like GH"],"answer":0,"ex":"IGF-1 is one of the negative feedbacks on GH: it acts on the pituitary and raises somatostatin. Extra IGF-1 therefore lowers GH. (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"focus","why":["IGF-1 suppresses GH directly at the pituitary (through its receptor tyrosine kinase) and by raising hypothalamic somatostatin.","IGF-1 is not a GHRH analog; GHRH works through Gs on somatotrophs.","IGF-1 also acts back on the pituitary and hypothalamus, not only on tissues.","It is GH, not IGF-1, that is diabetogenic; and a rise would defeat the feedback loop."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L3 Endocrinology of Growth: Regulation of GH secretion: feedback by GH and IGF-1 (they raise somatostatin)","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-gr-r-ssfb","r-gr-igf-fb","r-gr-ss-glucose","r-gr-f-igfinj"]}},{"id":"r-rv-tpo","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Thyroperoxidase functions in:","choices":["Iodination of thyroglobulin","Thyroxine's action on body cells","Hydrolysis of thyroglobulin","Release of thyroxine from thyroglobulin","Conversion of T4 to T3"],"answer":0,"ex":"At the review: thyroperoxidase, at the apical membrane facing the colloid, turns iodide into iodine, iodinates thyroglobulin's tyrosines (MIT, DIT) and couples them into T3 and T4. It has nothing to do with how T3 and T4 act; T4 to T3 is 5'-deiodinase. (Exam 1 · L4 Thyroid Physiology)","src":"review","why":["Thyroperoxidase iodinates thyroglobulin's tyrosines (and oxidizes iodide and couples MIT and DIT).","Thyroxine acts through nuclear receptors, not thyroperoxidase.","Lysosomal enzymes digest thyroglobulin after endocytosis through megalin.","Proteolysis in the lysosome frees T4 from thyroglobulin.","5'-deiodinase converts T4 to active T3."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L4 Thyroid Physiology: Synthesis, storage and release: made and stored in the colloid; NIS, pendrin, TPO, megalin","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-rv-tpo","r-th-tpo","r-th-r-nis","r-th-megalin"]}},{"id":"r-th-tpo","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which steps does thyroperoxidase carry out?","choices":["Oxidizing iodide, iodinating thyroglobulin and coupling MIT/DIT","Trapping iodide and endocytosing colloid","Converting T4 to T3 in the liver, kidney and other peripheral tissues","Cleaving T3 and T4 from thyroglobulin in lysosomes"],"answer":0,"ex":"Thyroperoxidase (needs H2O2) oxidizes iodide to iodine, adds it to thyroglobulin's tyrosines (organification) and couples MIT and DIT into T3 and T4. (Exam 1 · L4 Thyroid Physiology)","src":"slides","why":["Oxidation, organification and coupling.","Trapping is NIS; endocytosis uses megalin.","Peripheral conversion is deiodinase.","Lysosomal proteases free T3 and T4."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L4 Thyroid Physiology: Synthesis, storage and release: made and stored in the colloid; NIS, pendrin, TPO, megalin","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-rv-tpo","r-th-tpo","r-th-r-nis","r-th-megalin"]}},{"id":"r-th-r-nis","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"How does the sodium-iodide symporter move iodide against its concentration gradient?","choices":["Sodium running down its gradient drags iodide in with it","Iodide is pumped directly by ATP hydrolysis at the apical membrane","Pendrin pushes iodide in from the colloid","Thyroperoxidase pulls iodide in by oxidizing it"],"answer":0,"ex":"Extracellular sodium is high and intracellular sodium is low, so sodium moving in carries iodide along against its own gradient: secondary active transport. Follicular cells pile iodide up far above blood levels. NIS does not use ATP directly: the Na+/K+-ATPase spends the ATP that keeps the sodium gradient, so the symporter is only ultimately ATP-dependent. (Exam 1 · L4 Thyroid Physiology)","src":"rec","why":["Sodium following its electrochemical gradient carries iodide along: secondary active transport.","NIS is on the basolateral membrane and runs on the sodium gradient, not direct ATP use.","Pendrin moves iodide out of the cell into the colloid, at the apical side.","Thyroperoxidase works in the colloid, oxidizing iodide that is already there."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L4 Thyroid Physiology: Synthesis, storage and release: made and stored in the colloid; NIS, pendrin, TPO, megalin","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-rv-tpo","r-th-tpo","r-th-r-nis","r-th-megalin"]}},{"id":"r-th-megalin","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Megalin is:","choices":["The thyroglobulin receptor that allows endocytosis of colloid","The iodide transporter on the basolateral membrane","The enzyme that couples MIT and DIT into T3 and T4 in the colloid","The plasma protein that carries T4"],"answer":0,"ex":"Megalin binds thyroglobulin so the follicular cell can endocytose colloid and send it to lysosomes, where T3 and T4 are cleaved off. (Exam 1 · L4 Thyroid Physiology)","src":"slides","why":["Megalin = thyroglobulin receptor.","The basolateral iodide transporter is NIS.","Coupling is thyroperoxidase.","The plasma carrier is TBG."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L4 Thyroid Physiology: Synthesis, storage and release: made and stored in the colloid; NIS, pendrin, TPO, megalin","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-rv-tpo","r-th-tpo","r-th-r-nis","r-th-megalin"]}},{"id":"r-th-rt3","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which enzyme converts T4 to the inactive reverse T3?","choices":["5-deiodinase (inner ring)","5'-deiodinase (outer ring)","Thyroperoxidase","Pendrin"],"answer":0,"ex":"5'-deiodinase (outer ring) makes active T3; 5-deiodinase (inner ring) makes inactive reverse T3. \"Key to whether T3 or reverse T3 is formed.\" (Exam 1 · L4 Thyroid Physiology)","src":"slides","why":["Inner-ring deiodination gives reverse T3.","Outer-ring (prime) deiodination gives active T3.","Thyroperoxidase iodinates and couples; it doesn't deiodinate.","Pendrin moves iodide across the apical membrane."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L4 Thyroid Physiology: T4 to T3 conversion: 5'-deiodinase (active T3) vs 5-deiodinase (reverse T3)","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-th-rt3","r-th-2s-1","r-th-t3","r-th-r-t3"]}},{"id":"r-th-2s-1","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. 5'-deiodinase produces active T3 from T4.\n2. Reverse T3 is the most potent thyroid hormone.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Outer-ring (5') deiodination makes active T3; inner-ring (5) deiodination makes reverse T3, which is inactive. (Exam 1 · L4 Thyroid Physiology)","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: reverse T3 is inactive.","Statement 1 is true, not false: 5'-deiodinase removes an outer-ring iodine, giving active T3.","Statement 1 is true: 5'-deiodinase removes an outer-ring iodine, giving active T3. Statement 2 is false: reverse T3 is inactive.","Statement 1 is true, not false: 5'-deiodinase removes an outer-ring iodine, giving active T3. Statement 2 is false, not true: reverse T3 is inactive."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L4 Thyroid Physiology: T4 to T3 conversion: 5'-deiodinase (active T3) vs 5-deiodinase (reverse T3)","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-th-rt3","r-th-2s-1","r-th-t3","r-th-r-t3"]}},{"id":"r-th-t3","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"T3 is formed by coupling:","choices":["MIT + DIT","DIT + DIT","MIT + MIT","T4 + iodine"],"answer":0,"ex":"One iodine (MIT) plus two iodines (DIT) gives three: T3. (Exam 1 · L4 Thyroid Physiology)","src":"slides","why":["1 + 2 iodines = 3.","DIT + DIT = T4.","MIT + MIT isn't a product taught.","T4 loses an iodine to become T3; it doesn't gain one."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L4 Thyroid Physiology: T4 to T3 conversion: 5'-deiodinase (active T3) vs 5-deiodinase (reverse T3)","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-th-rt3","r-th-2s-1","r-th-t3","r-th-r-t3"]}},{"id":"r-th-r-t3","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does the thyroid release so little T3?","choices":["Its T3 is short-lived and mostly used by the gland itself","It lacks thyroperoxidase, the enzyme that couples MIT and DIT into T3","T3 stays permanently trapped on thyroglobulin in the colloid until it decays","TSH blocks the coupling of MIT and DIT"],"answer":0,"ex":"About 90% of what the gland makes is T4. The T3 it makes has a very short half-life and is readily used by the gland, so little gets out; most T3 comes from T4 in the tissues (5'-deiodinase). (Exam 1 · L4 Thyroid Physiology)","src":"rec","why":["The gland's own T3 has a short half-life and is largely used by the gland.","Thyroperoxidase does couple MIT and DIT; the gland has it.","T3 and T4 are cut off thyroglobulin in lysosomes and released.","TSH increases hormone synthesis; it doesn't block coupling."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L4 Thyroid Physiology: T4 to T3 conversion: 5'-deiodinase (active T3) vs 5-deiodinase (reverse T3)","basis":"Exam 1 focus list + hint","ids":["r-th-rt3","r-th-2s-1","r-th-t3","r-th-r-t3"]}},{"id":"r-rv-sst","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which of the following statements is NOT true of pancreatic somatostatin?","choices":["It stimulates glucagon secretion","It is stimulated by increases in blood glucose","It slows motility and nutrient absorption in the gastrointestinal tract","It suppresses insulin secretion","It is stimulated by elevated plasma amino acids and fatty acids"],"answer":0,"ex":"At the review: somatostatin is always a negative regulator; it never increases a hormone. Delta-cell somatostatin is the paracrine brake on both insulin and glucagon. It rises when fuel comes in (glucose, amino acids, fatty acids) and slows the gut, so nutrients arrive more slowly. (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"review","fmt":"except","why":["Somatostatin inhibits glucagon; it never stimulates any hormone.","Rising glucose does stimulate somatostatin, part of the brake on insulin.","In the gut it does slow motility and nutrient absorption.","It does suppress insulin: the paracrine brake, through a Gi receptor.","Fuel from a meal (amino acids, fatty acids) does stimulate it."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L5 Endocrine Pancreas: Somatostatin, the paracrine brake on insulin and glucagon","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-sst","r-pa-ss","r-pa-ss-stim"]}},{"id":"r-pa-ss","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Pancreatic somatostatin:","choices":["Inhibits both insulin and glucagon through paracrine action","Stimulates glucagon secretion to prevent hypoglycemia","Stimulates insulin secretion after meals","Speeds stomach emptying"],"answer":0,"ex":"Delta-cell somatostatin is the paracrine brake on both insulin and glucagon (Gi), keeping them in check. It also slows GI motility and absorption, extending the time over which nutrients are absorbed. (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"rec","why":["The brake on both.","It inhibits glucagon, not stimulates.","It inhibits insulin.","It slows the stomach, duodenum and gallbladder."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L5 Endocrine Pancreas: Somatostatin, the paracrine brake on insulin and glucagon","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-sst","r-pa-ss","r-pa-ss-stim"]}},{"id":"r-pa-ss-stim","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Somatostatin secretion from delta cells is stimulated by:","choices":["Increased blood glucose, amino acids and keto acids","Low blood glucose during an overnight fast, only","Epinephrine","Insulin deficiency"],"answer":0,"ex":"Somatostatin rises with increased glucose, amino acids, keto acids and several GI hormones released after food, so it is released when insulin and glucagon are being pushed up. (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"rec","why":["Nutrient signals raise it.","Low glucose isn't its trigger.","Epinephrine isn't listed as a stimulus.","That isn't the trigger listed."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L5 Endocrine Pancreas: Somatostatin, the paracrine brake on insulin and glucagon","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-sst","r-pa-ss","r-pa-ss-stim"]}},{"id":"r-rv-glucagon","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which of the following situations will result in stimulation of glucagon secretion?","choices":["High levels of plasma insulin in the absence of insulin resistance","Decreased plasma amino acid concentration","Increased somatostatin secretion within the islets","Increased plasma glucose, especially above 70 mg/dL","Decreased plasma insulin when peripheral insulin resistance occurs"],"answer":0,"ex":"At the review: it's not a direct effect. High insulin in a body that responds to it lowers blood glucose, and falling glucose is the main stimulus for glucagon. (Directly, insulin modestly inhibits glucagon.) (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"review","why":["High insulin with normal sensitivity drops blood glucose, and falling glucose stimulates glucagon.","Amino acids stimulate glucagon, so fewer of them would not.","Somatostatin is a brake on glucagon.","Rising glucose inhibits glucagon.","With insulin resistance, glucose stays high, and high glucose holds glucagon down."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L5 Endocrine Pancreas: What stimulates glucagon (falling glucose, even when insulin caused it)","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-glucagon","r-pa-glucagon-stim","r-pa-tf-5"]}},{"id":"r-pa-glucagon-stim","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which of the following stimulates glucagon secretion?","choices":["Amino acids from a protein meal","Rising blood glucose","Somatostatin","Ketones"],"answer":0,"ex":"Glucagon is stimulated by falling glucose (the main one), epinephrine, amino acids and (modestly) acetylcholine; inhibited by rising glucose, somatostatin, free fatty acids and ketones. (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"rec","why":["Protein meals raise glucagon.","Rising glucose inhibits glucagon.","Somatostatin inhibits glucagon.","Ketones feed back to inhibit glucagon."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L5 Endocrine Pancreas: What stimulates glucagon (falling glucose, even when insulin caused it)","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-glucagon","r-pa-glucagon-stim","r-pa-tf-5"]}},{"id":"r-pa-tf-5","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Free fatty acids stimulate glucagon secretion.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Free fatty acids and ketones inhibit glucagon (feedback, since glucagon produces them). They modestly stimulate insulin. (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["FFA inhibit glucagon.","They inhibit glucagon secretion."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L5 Endocrine Pancreas: What stimulates glucagon (falling glucose, even when insulin caused it)","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-glucagon","r-pa-glucagon-stim","r-pa-tf-5"]}},{"id":"r-rv-aldo-high","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"In diseases associated with aldosterone hypersecretion, which of the following would you expect?","choices":["Low plasma potassium levels","Low blood sodium levels","Hypotension","Immunosuppression","Gonadal dysfunction"],"answer":0,"ex":"Aldosterone makes the principal cells reabsorb sodium (water follows) and secrete potassium into the urine. Too much of it: potassium falls, sodium is kept and blood pressure rises. (Exam 1 · L6 Adrenal Gland)","src":"review","why":["Aldosterone secretes potassium into the urine, so too much of it drops plasma potassium.","Aldosterone keeps sodium; excess raises it, or holds it normal as water follows.","Sodium and water retention raise blood pressure; low pressure is aldosterone deficiency.","Immunosuppression is a cortisol effect.","Gonadal problems come from sex steroids, not the mineralocorticoid."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L6 Adrenal Gland: Aldosterone: keep sodium, lose potassium","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-aldo-high","r-ad-aldo-excess","r-ad-aldo-k","r-ad-angII"]}},{"id":"r-ad-aldo-excess","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which finding fits aldosterone excess?","choices":["Hypokalemia with hypertension","Hyperkalemia with hypotension","Hyponatremia with dehydration","Salt craving"],"answer":0,"ex":"Excess aldosterone: sodium retention, ECF expansion, hypertension, potassium depletion (hypokalemia) and alkalosis. Deficiency: salt craving, natriuresis, hyponatremia, acidosis, dehydration, orthostatic hypotension. (Exam 1 · L6 Adrenal Gland)","src":"rec","why":["Low K+ and high BP: the clue to primary aldosteronism.","Those fit deficiency.","Those fit deficiency.","Salt craving fits deficiency."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L6 Adrenal Gland: Aldosterone: keep sodium, lose potassium","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-aldo-high","r-ad-aldo-excess","r-ad-aldo-k","r-ad-angII"]}},{"id":"r-ad-aldo-k","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Aldosterone's effect on potassium is to:","choices":["Increase its secretion into the urine","Increase its reabsorption","Trap it inside renal cells permanently","Have no effect"],"answer":0,"ex":"More Na+/K+ pumps plus apical K+ channels move potassium into the tubular lumen: aldosterone secretes K+, which is how it fixes hyperkalemia. (Exam 1 · L6 Adrenal Gland)","src":"rec","why":["Aldosterone secretes potassium.","It promotes loss, not reabsorption.","The potassium moves out into the urine.","Potassium handling is a key effect."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L6 Adrenal Gland: Aldosterone: keep sodium, lose potassium","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-aldo-high","r-ad-aldo-excess","r-ad-aldo-k","r-ad-angII"]}},{"id":"r-ad-angII","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"What is the primary regulator of aldosterone production?","choices":["Angiotensin II","ACTH","Cortisol","Epinephrine"],"answer":0,"ex":"Angiotensin II is the primary regulator; serum potassium is the other major stimulus. ACTH has only a modest, transient effect (10-20%) and no effect on aldosterone synthase. (Exam 1 · L6 Adrenal Gland)","src":"rec","why":["Angiotensin II drives aldosterone.","ACTH's effect is modest and transient.","Cortisol isn't the regulator.","Epinephrine isn't the regulator."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L6 Adrenal Gland: Aldosterone: keep sodium, lose potassium","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-aldo-high","r-ad-aldo-excess","r-ad-aldo-k","r-ad-angII"]}},{"id":"r-rv-acth-epi","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which anterior pituitary hormone increases epinephrine secretion during stress?","choices":["ACTH","CRH","GH","GnRH"],"answer":0,"ex":"At the review: cortisol belongs to the stress response, and every releasing hormone (CRH, GnRH) comes from the hypothalamus, so those are out for \"anterior pituitary.\" ACTH raises cortisol, which flows from cortex to medulla and activates PNMT, the enzyme that makes epinephrine from norepinephrine. (Exam 1 · L6 Adrenal Gland)","src":"review","why":["ACTH raises cortisol, which reaches the medulla and turns on PNMT to make epinephrine.","CRH is a releasing hormone from the hypothalamus, not the anterior pituitary.","GH has no route to the medulla's PNMT.","GnRH is a hypothalamic releasing hormone for the gonadal axis."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L6 Adrenal Gland: Cortisol reaches the medulla and turns on PNMT","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-acth-epi","r-ad-pnmt-reg","r-ad-pnmt","r-ad-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-ad-pnmt-reg","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"What activates PNMT in the adrenal medulla?","choices":["Cortisol","Aldosterone","Angiotensin II","Insulin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cortisol, carried downstream from the cortex, activates PNMT. Sympathetic stimulation and ACTH activate the earlier enzymes (tyrosine hydroxylase, dopamine beta-hydroxylase). (Exam 1 · L6 Adrenal Gland)","src":"rec","why":["Cortisol drives PNMT.","Aldosterone isn't the regulator.","Angiotensin II acts on the glomerulosa.","Insulin isn't involved."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L6 Adrenal Gland: Cortisol reaches the medulla and turns on PNMT","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-acth-epi","r-ad-pnmt-reg","r-ad-pnmt","r-ad-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-ad-pnmt","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"About 80% of adrenal medulla output is epinephrine because chromaffin cells are rich in:","choices":["PNMT, which converts norepinephrine to epinephrine","Tyrosine hydroxylase, which converts norepinephrine to dopamine","Monoamine oxidase","Aldosterone synthase"],"answer":0,"ex":"Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase (PNMT) converts norepinephrine to epinephrine. It is highly expressed in the medulla and activated by cortisol. (Exam 1 · L6 Adrenal Gland)","src":"rec","why":["PNMT makes epinephrine.","Tyrosine hydroxylase is an early step for all catecholamines.","MAO breaks catecholamines down.","Aldosterone synthase is a cortical enzyme."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L6 Adrenal Gland: Cortisol reaches the medulla and turns on PNMT","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-acth-epi","r-ad-pnmt-reg","r-ad-pnmt","r-ad-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-ad-2s-4","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. About 80% of the catecholamine released by the adrenal medulla is norepinephrine.\n2. Cortisol is lowest in the early morning around 7 to 8 am.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"About 80% is epinephrine (PNMT). Cortisol peaks around 7-8 am and is lowest around 2-4 am. (Exam 1 · L6 Adrenal Gland)","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: about 80% is epinephrine. Statement 2 is false, not true: cortisol peaks, not dips, around 7-8 am.","Statement 1 is false: about 80% is epinephrine. Statement 2 is false: cortisol peaks, not dips, around 7-8 am.","Statement 1 is false, not true: about 80% is epinephrine.","Statement 2 is false, not true: cortisol peaks, not dips, around 7-8 am."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L6 Adrenal Gland: Cortisol reaches the medulla and turns on PNMT","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-acth-epi","r-ad-pnmt-reg","r-ad-pnmt","r-ad-2s-4"]}},{"id":"r-cs-infection","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"What did Dr. Fasinu say about giving a short corticosteroid course with an antibiotic for an infection?","choices":["Acceptable short-term; prolonged use can mask infection","It is always contraindicated while an infection is active","It makes the antibiotic work faster","It prevents candidiasis"],"answer":0,"ex":"The old rule \"never with an infection\" has relaxed: a short course (Medrol Dosepak with a Z-Pak) treats the inflammatory symptoms. Prolonged use masks infections and invites fungal ones. (Exam 1 · L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs)","src":"rec","why":["Short courses are fine; long ones mask infection.","It isn't an absolute contraindication anymore.","It doesn't speed the antibiotic.","Steroids promote candidiasis."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs: Corticosteroid side effects, masked infection first","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-cs-infection","r-cs-num-1","r-cs-electrolytes"]}},{"id":"r-cs-num-1","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which are long-term side effects of systemic glucocorticoids?\n1. Osteoporosis\n2. Hyperkalemia\n3. Impaired wound healing\n4. Masked infections","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":2,"ex":"Glucocorticoids cause hypokalemia, not hyperkalemia. The rest are on the list. (Exam 1 · L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs)","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["Hyperkalemia doesn't belong.","Masked infections belong too.","Only 2 is wrong.","Includes hyperkalemia and leaves out osteoporosis."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs: Corticosteroid side effects, masked infection first","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-cs-infection","r-cs-num-1","r-cs-electrolytes"]}},{"id":"r-cs-electrolytes","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which electrolyte disturbance is a side effect of corticosteroid therapy?","choices":["Hypokalemia with hypernatremia","Hyperkalemia with hyponatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypermagnesemia"],"answer":0,"ex":"Nutrient homeostasis problems: hypokalemia, hypernatremia and hypervolemia (salt and water retention). (Exam 1 · L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs)","src":"slides","why":["Low K+, high Na+, fluid retention.","That pattern fits aldosterone deficiency or spironolactone.","Not listed.","Not listed."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs: Corticosteroid side effects, masked infection first","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-cs-infection","r-cs-num-1","r-cs-electrolytes"]}},{"id":"r-cs-taper","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Why is a Medrol Dosepak tapered (6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 tablets) instead of stopped abruptly?","choices":["Abrupt withdrawal after HPA suppression risks adrenal insufficiency","Tapering prevents the drug from accumulating in fat","The first doses are absorbed poorly","Tapering lowers the total dose and so the risk of oral candidiasis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Exogenous steroid feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary, shutting off CRH and ACTH; the adrenal goes into \"rest mode.\" Tapering gives the axis time to restart. \"You can be asked why it is taken like this.\" (Exam 1 · L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs)","src":"rec","why":["HPA suppression → taper to avoid adrenal insufficiency.","Fat accumulation isn't the reason.","Absorption isn't the reason.","Candidiasis comes from immune suppression, not the schedule."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs: Why the Dosepak tapers (adrenal suppression)","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-cs-taper","r-cs-2s-2"]}},{"id":"r-cs-2s-2","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Exogenous glucocorticoids suppress ACTH secretion by negative feedback.\n2. Stopping a long course of glucocorticoids abruptly is safe because the adrenal resumes immediately.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Exogenous steroids shut down CRH and ACTH; the adrenal takes time to restart, so abrupt withdrawal risks adrenal insufficiency (hence tapering). (Exam 1 · L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs)","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: the adrenal needs time to restart; that is why you taper.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the drug feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary.","Statement 1 is true: the drug feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary. Statement 2 is false: the adrenal needs time to restart; that is why you taper.","Statement 1 is true, not false: the drug feeds back on the hypothalamus and pituitary. Statement 2 is false, not true: the adrenal needs time to restart; that is why you taper."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs: Why the Dosepak tapers (adrenal suppression)","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-cs-taper","r-cs-2s-2"]}},{"id":"r-td-ptu-preg","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"A woman in her first trimester has Graves disease. Which drug is preferred?","choices":["Propylthiouracil","Methimazole","Radioactive iodine","Liothyronine"],"answer":0,"ex":"Methimazole carries a higher risk of birth defects, especially in the first trimester; PTU is used then (switch back to methimazole later). Radioiodine is contraindicated in pregnancy. (Exam 1 · L8 Thyroid Drugs)","src":"rec","why":["PTU in the first trimester.","Methimazole is teratogenic in the first trimester.","Contraindicated in pregnancy.","T3 would worsen hyperthyroidism."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L8 Thyroid Drugs: Pregnancy decides methimazole vs PTU","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-td-ptu-preg","r-td-mmi-first","r-td-mmi-teratogen","r-td-num-1"]}},{"id":"r-td-mmi-first","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Methimazole is first line for most non-pregnant patients with hyperthyroidism because it:","choices":["It is once daily, with less risk of severe liver injury","It also blocks peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 in the tissues","It carries no risk of birth defects","It is given only once a week"],"answer":0,"ex":"Methimazole: once daily, lower risk of severe liver injury. PTU blocks peripheral T4→T3 conversion, has a boxed warning for liver injury, and is preferred in the first trimester. (Exam 1 · L8 Thyroid Drugs)","src":"rec","why":["Once daily and safer for the liver.","Peripheral conversion blockade is PTU's extra.","Methimazole is the more teratogenic one.","It is once daily, not weekly."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L8 Thyroid Drugs: Pregnancy decides methimazole vs PTU","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-td-ptu-preg","r-td-mmi-first","r-td-mmi-teratogen","r-td-num-1"]}},{"id":"r-td-mmi-teratogen","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statement about methimazole in pregnancy is correct?","choices":["It carries a higher risk of birth defects early in pregnancy","It is preferred throughout pregnancy","It is safer for the liver and the fetus than PTU","It is contraindicated only in the third trimester, near delivery"],"answer":0,"ex":"Methimazole is teratogenic, especially in the first trimester, so PTU is used then; methimazole can be resumed afterward. (Exam 1 · L8 Thyroid Drugs)","src":"rec","why":["Higher birth-defect risk early.","PTU is preferred in the first trimester.","Safer for the liver, but not for the fetus.","The concern is the first trimester."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L8 Thyroid Drugs: Pregnancy decides methimazole vs PTU","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-td-ptu-preg","r-td-mmi-first","r-td-mmi-teratogen","r-td-num-1"]}},{"id":"r-td-num-1","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"A patient is pregnant (first trimester) and hyperthyroid. Which statements are true?\n1. Methimazole is preferred now.\n2. PTU is preferred now.\n3. Radioactive iodine is contraindicated.\n4. A beta-blocker can be added for symptoms.","choices":["1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":3,"ex":"Methimazole is avoided in the first trimester (teratogenic), so PTU is used. The rest are true. (Exam 1 · L8 Thyroid Drugs)","src":"rec","fmt":"numbered","why":["Statement 1 is false.","Methimazole isn't preferred in the first trimester; PTU is.","Beta-blockers can be added for symptoms.","Only statement 1 is false: methimazole is avoided in the first trimester."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L8 Thyroid Drugs: Pregnancy decides methimazole vs PTU","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-td-ptu-preg","r-td-mmi-first","r-td-mmi-teratogen","r-td-num-1"]}},{"id":"r-td-levo-admin","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"How should a patient take levothyroxine?","choices":["First thing in the morning on an empty stomach","With breakfast and a glass of milk","At bedtime with a calcium supplement to protect bone","With an antacid to reduce stomach upset"],"answer":0,"ex":"Levothyroxine binds to almost anything, so the emptier the stomach the better: first thing in the morning, no food or milk. Calcium and other polyvalent metals reduce absorption. (Exam 1 · L8 Thyroid Drugs)","src":"rec","why":["Empty stomach, morning.","Food and milk reduce absorption.","Calcium binds it.","Antacids (Al, Mg) bind it."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L8 Thyroid Drugs: Levothyroxine: first thing in the morning, empty stomach","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-td-levo-admin","r-td-binders","r-td-2s-1"]}},{"id":"r-td-binders","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which drug class reduces absorption of levothyroxine?","choices":["Bile acid sequestrants","Beta-blockers","ACE inhibitors such as lisinopril","Loop diuretics"],"answer":0,"ex":"Binders lower absorption: bile acid sequestrants (cholestyramine), polyvalent metals (calcium, aluminum, magnesium) and high protein-binding drugs. (Exam 1 · L8 Thyroid Drugs)","src":"rec","why":["Sequestrants bind the drug in the gut.","Not listed as binders.","Not listed.","Not listed."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L8 Thyroid Drugs: Levothyroxine: first thing in the morning, empty stomach","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-td-levo-admin","r-td-binders","r-td-2s-1"]}},{"id":"r-td-2s-1","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Levothyroxine should be taken with food to improve absorption.\n2. Levothyroxine is about 99% protein-bound.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"Take it on an empty stomach (it binds food and minerals). It is 99% protein-bound. (Exam 1 · L8 Thyroid Drugs)","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: it should be taken on an empty stomach.","Statement 2 is true, not false: it is about 99% protein-bound.","Statement 1 is false, not true: it should be taken on an empty stomach. Statement 2 is true, not false: it is about 99% protein-bound.","Statement 1 is false: it should be taken on an empty stomach. Statement 2 is true: it is about 99% protein-bound."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L8 Thyroid Drugs: Levothyroxine: first thing in the morning, empty stomach","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-td-levo-admin","r-td-binders","r-td-2s-1"]}},{"id":"r-h2-dual","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"The adrenal medulla's \"dual blood supply\" means it receives:","choices":["Arterial blood plus venous blood from the cortex","Blood from both the renal artery and directly from the aorta","Blood from the portal vein and the hepatic artery","Lymph and blood mixed in the sinusoids"],"answer":0,"ex":"Long medullary arteries bring arterial blood; cortical sinusoids drain venous blood, rich in cortisol, into the medulla. Dr. Herr: \"So, here's the question that's going to be on your exam.\" (Exam 1 · L9 Endocrine Histology 2)","src":"rec","why":["Arterial plus cortical venous blood.","That isn't the meaning.","That describes the liver.","No mixing with lymph."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L9 Endocrine Histology 2: The medulla's dual blood supply (arterial + cortical venous)","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h2-dual","r-h2-dual-why"]}},{"id":"r-h2-dual-why","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Why does it matter that venous blood from the cortex flows through the medulla?","choices":["It carries cortisol from the cortex to the medulla's chromaffin cells","It carries aldosterone to the kidney faster","It cools the medulla","It brings LDL to the medulla for steroid synthesis"],"answer":0,"ex":"Cortical venous blood carries cortical hormones (cortisol) into the medulla; cortisol induces PNMT, which makes epinephrine (Madison's lecture ties the two together). (Exam 1 · L9 Endocrine Histology 2)","src":"slides","why":["Cortisol reaches the chromaffin cells.","Aldosterone leaves through the suprarenal vein like everything else.","No such purpose.","The medulla doesn't make steroids."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L9 Endocrine Histology 2: The medulla's dual blood supply (arterial + cortical venous)","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h2-dual","r-h2-dual-why"]}},{"id":"r-h2-spongio","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Fasciculata cells are called spongiocytes because:","choices":["They are full of lipid droplets of stored cholesterol","They are arranged around spongy bone","They phagocytose red cells","They lack a nucleus"],"answer":0,"ex":"Their lipid droplets (stored cholesterol) wash out in routine sections, leaving a pale, spongy look. Dr. Herr: the name is \"the thing to remember for the exam\" about the fasciculata. (Exam 1 · L9 Endocrine Histology 2)","src":"rec","why":["Lipid droplets.","No bone in the adrenal.","They don't phagocytose.","They have nuclei."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L9 Endocrine Histology 2: Fasciculata cells are spongiocytes (lipid droplets)","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h2-spongio","r-h2-num-2"]}},{"id":"r-h2-num-2","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which statements about the adrenal cortex and the islets are true?\n1. The zona fasciculata is the largest zone and makes glucocorticoids.\n2. The zona glomerulosa responds to angiotensin II as well as ACTH.\n3. Beta cells are the most abundant islet cell.\n4. The islets make up about 10-15% of the pancreas's volume.","choices":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2, 3 and 4","1 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4"],"answer":0,"ex":"The fasciculata is 65-80% of the cortex and makes cortisol; the glomerulosa answers to angiotensin II as well as ACTH; beta cells are 65-70% of an islet. The islets are only about 1-2% of the pancreas's volume. (Exam 1 · L9 Endocrine Histology 2)","src":"slides","fmt":"numbered","why":["Only 4 is false: the islets are about 1-2% of the volume.","Includes 4: the islets are only about 1-2% of the pancreas.","Leaves out 2: angiotensin II drives the glomerulosa too.","Includes 4 and leaves out 1: the fasciculata (65-80%) makes cortisol."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L9 Endocrine Histology 2: Fasciculata cells are spongiocytes (lipid droplets)","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h2-spongio","r-h2-num-2"]}},{"id":"r-rv-casr","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) regulates PTH secretion?","choices":["It inhibits PTH secretion when bound by calcium via activation of Gαq pathways","It stimulates PTH release in response to low calcium by activating Gαs signaling","It increases PTH gene transcription in response to high serum calcium","It senses intracellular calcium to stimulate PTH exocytosis","It activates vitamin D synthesis directly to lower calcium"],"answer":0,"ex":"At the review: \"the one contradiction in all of endocrinology.\" Usually a rise in intracellular calcium drives secretion; on the chief cell, calcium binding the CaSR (a Gq receptor) raises intracellular calcium and that blocks PTH release. It makes sense: high blood calcium doesn't need more PTH. (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"review","why":["Calcium on the CaSR activates Gq, and the rise in intracellular calcium blocks PTH secretion.","Low calcium simply leaves the CaSR quiet; it is a Gq receptor, not Gs.","High calcium turns PTH down, not up.","The CaSR senses blood (extracellular) calcium, and its signal stops exocytosis.","The CaSR controls PTH release; it doesn't make vitamin D."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation: The chief cell paradox: calcium on the CaSR stops PTH","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-casr","r-ca-casr","r-ca-r-2s-1"]}},{"id":"r-ca-casr","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"The calcium-sensing receptor on chief cells is coupled to:","choices":["Gq","Gs","Gi only","A receptor tyrosine kinase"],"answer":0,"ex":"CaSR is Gq-coupled: high extracellular calcium raises intracellular calcium from internal stores. (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"slides","why":["Gq.","Not Gs.","Not what the slides say.","It is a GPCR."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation: The chief cell paradox: calcium on the CaSR stops PTH","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-casr","r-ca-casr","r-ca-r-2s-1"]}},{"id":"r-ca-r-2s-1","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The calcium-sensing receptor on chief cells is Gs-coupled.\n2. When blood calcium is low, the receptor is quiet and PTH is secreted.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":3,"ex":"The CaSR is Gq-coupled; high calcium stimulates it and the rise in intracellular calcium blocks PTH release. (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: it is Gq-coupled (IP3, DAG, calcium from intracellular stores).","Statement 2 is true, not false: without receptor signaling, the secretory pathway moves PTH vesicles to the surface.","Statement 1 is false, not true: it is Gq-coupled (IP3, DAG, calcium from intracellular stores). Statement 2 is true, not false: without receptor signaling, the secretory pathway moves PTH vesicles to the surface.","Statement 1 is false: it is Gq-coupled (IP3, DAG, calcium from intracellular stores). Statement 2 is true: without receptor signaling, the secretory pathway moves PTH vesicles to the surface."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation: The chief cell paradox: calcium on the CaSR stops PTH","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-rv-casr","r-ca-casr","r-ca-r-2s-1"]}},{"id":"r-rv-vitd","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"In response to low serum calcium, which of the following sequences best explains how vitamin D becomes activated and where it acts to restore calcium levels?","choices":["PTH activates 1α-hydroxylase in the kidney, increasing calcitriol, which enhances calcium absorption in the intestines","Low calcium directly activates 1α-hydroxylase in the kidney, producing calcitonin to increase bone resorption","Low calcium stimulates vitamin D receptors in the bone to release stored calcium","PTH inhibits vitamin D activation to promote renal calcium reabsorption","Vitamin D acts on the kidney to increase calcium reabsorption via 1α-hydroxylase activity"],"answer":0,"ex":"Low calcium releases PTH; PTH turns on 1α-hydroxylase in the proximal tubule, making calcitriol (1,25-(OH)2-D3), whose main job is calcium absorption in the gut (calbindin). (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"review","why":["PTH turns on renal 1α-hydroxylase; the calcitriol it makes raises calcium absorption in the gut.","PTH, not calcium directly, turns on 1α-hydroxylase, and calcitonin comes from C cells and lowers calcium.","Low calcium is sensed by the parathyroid's CaSR; bone release is PTH's job.","PTH activates vitamin D; it doesn't inhibit it.","1α-hydroxylase is the kidney enzyme that activates vitamin D; calcitriol's main target is the gut."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation: PTH turns on 1α-hydroxylase; calcitriol works in the gut","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-vitd","r-ca-1alpha-on","r-ca-calcitriol-net","r-ca-2s-3"]}},{"id":"r-ca-1alpha-on","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which turns ON renal 1α-hydroxylase?","choices":["PTH","Hypercalcemia","Hyperphosphatemia","High 1,25-(OH)2-D3"],"answer":0,"ex":"On: hypocalcemia, PTH, hypophosphatemia. The others turn on 24-hydroxylase (the inactive pathway). (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"slides","why":["PTH stimulates 1α-hydroxylase.","High calcium turns on 24-hydroxylase.","High phosphate turns on 24-hydroxylase.","Calcitriol feeds back to turn on 24-hydroxylase."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation: PTH turns on 1α-hydroxylase; calcitriol works in the gut","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-vitd","r-ca-1alpha-on","r-ca-calcitriol-net","r-ca-2s-3"]}},{"id":"r-ca-calcitriol-net","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Calcitriol raises blood calcium mainly by acting on:","choices":["The intestine","Bone directly","The parathyroid","The skin"],"answer":0,"ex":"Its main action is gut absorption of calcium (and phosphate); effects on kidney and bone are weaker. (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"slides","why":["The gut.","Its bone effect is weaker (it promotes PTH's action).","It feeds back on the parathyroid, but that isn't how it raises calcium.","The skin makes the precursor."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation: PTH turns on 1α-hydroxylase; calcitriol works in the gut","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-vitd","r-ca-1alpha-on","r-ca-calcitriol-net","r-ca-2s-3"]}},{"id":"r-ca-2s-3","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. PTH stimulates renal 1α-hydroxylase.\n2. Hypophosphatemia stimulates renal 1α-hydroxylase.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hypocalcemia, PTH and hypophosphatemia all turn on 1α-hydroxylase. (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is true: PTH turns on 1α-hydroxylase. Statement 2 is true: low phosphate turns it on too.","Statement 1 is true, not false: PTH turns on 1α-hydroxylase. Statement 2 is true, not false: low phosphate turns it on too.","Statement 2 is true, not false: low phosphate turns it on too.","Statement 1 is true, not false: PTH turns on 1α-hydroxylase."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation: PTH turns on 1α-hydroxylase; calcitriol works in the gut","basis":"Exam 1 review","ids":["r-rv-vitd","r-ca-1alpha-on","r-ca-calcitriol-net","r-ca-2s-3"]}},{"id":"r-ca-2s-4","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Calcitonin is essential for calcium balance.\n2. Calcitonin secretion falls when plasma calcium rises.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Calcitonin isn't essential, and its secretion rises with plasma calcium. Statement 1 is tempting because calcitonin does lower calcium (it inhibits osteoclasts), but the lecture calls it not essential: PTH and vitamin D are the ones calcium balance depends on. (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: it isn't essential. Statement 2 is false, not true: its secretion rises with calcium.","Statement 1 is false: it isn't essential. Statement 2 is false: its secretion rises with calcium.","Statement 1 is false, not true: it isn't essential.","Statement 2 is false, not true: its secretion rises with calcium."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-cs-2s-3","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Ketoconazole blocks glucocorticoid receptors.\n2. Mitotane inhibits 11β-hydroxylase.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":1,"ex":"Ketoconazole inhibits steroidogenic enzymes; mifepristone blocks glucocorticoid receptors. Mitotane is adrenolytic; metyrapone and etomidate inhibit 11β-hydroxylase. (Exam 1 · L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs)","src":"slides","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 1 is false, not true: ketoconazole inhibits steroid synthesis; mifepristone is the receptor blocker. Statement 2 is false, not true: mitotane destroys the adrenal cortex; metyrapone and etomidate inhibit 11β-hydroxylase.","Statement 1 is false: ketoconazole inhibits steroid synthesis; mifepristone is the receptor blocker. Statement 2 is false: mitotane destroys the adrenal cortex; metyrapone and etomidate inhibit 11β-hydroxylase.","Statement 1 is false, not true: ketoconazole inhibits steroid synthesis; mifepristone is the receptor blocker.","Statement 2 is false, not true: mitotane destroys the adrenal cortex; metyrapone and etomidate inhibit 11β-hydroxylase."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-gr-r-plate","lec":"refresh","type":"sequence","stem":"Put the zones of the epiphyseal plate in order, from the epiphysis toward the shaft.","steps":["Reserve cartilage (quiescent precursors)","Proliferation","Hypertrophy","Apoptosis","Calcification and ossification"],"ex":"Reserve → proliferation → hypertrophy → apoptosis → calcification and ossification, which lengthens the bone. GH acts in the reserve zone, IGF-1 in proliferation. (Exam 1 · L3 Endocrinology of Growth)","src":"rec","likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-pa-2s-2","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. Insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase in fat.\n2. Insulin activates glucose-6-phosphatase in the liver.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Insulin keeps fat stored by inhibiting HSL. Glucose-6-phosphatase releases glucose from the liver; insulin inactivates it and glucagon activates it. (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: insulin inactivates glucose-6-phosphatase; glucagon activates it.","Statement 1 is true, not false: insulin blocks lipolysis by inhibiting HSL.","Statement 1 is true: insulin blocks lipolysis by inhibiting HSL. Statement 2 is false: insulin inactivates glucose-6-phosphatase; glucagon activates it.","Statement 1 is true, not false: insulin blocks lipolysis by inhibiting HSL. Statement 2 is false, not true: insulin inactivates glucose-6-phosphatase; glucagon activates it."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-td-inducers","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Which drugs can increase metabolism of thyroid hormone by inducing liver enzymes?","choices":["Rifampin and carbamazepine","Warfarin and theophylline","Methimazole and propylthiouracil","Amiodarone and lithium"],"answer":0,"ex":"Hepatic inducers (rifampin, carbamazepine, St. John's wort) can lower thyroid hormone levels. Methimazole and propylthiouracil also lower thyroid hormone, but by blocking its synthesis in the thyroid, not by speeding its breakdown in the liver. Warfarin and theophylline are drugs that thyroid hormone affects. (Exam 1 · L8 Thyroid Drugs)","src":"rec","why":["Enzyme inducers.","These are altered BY thyroid hormone.","These block synthesis.","These cause drug-induced thyroid disease."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-h2-r-2s-1","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Read the two statements.\n1. The middle suprarenal arteries come directly off the abdominal aorta.\n2. The inferior suprarenal arteries come off the inferior phrenic artery.","choices":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true; statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false; statement 2 is true"],"answer":2,"ex":"Superior from the inferior phrenic, middle from the aorta, inferior from the renal arteries. (Exam 1 · L9 Endocrine Histology 2)","src":"rec","fmt":"2stmt","why":["Statement 2 is false, not true: the inferior ones come from the renal arteries; the superior ones come from the inferior phrenic artery.","Statement 1 is true, not false: they branch straight off the abdominal aorta.","Statement 1 is true: they branch straight off the abdominal aorta. Statement 2 is false: the inferior ones come from the renal arteries; the superior ones come from the inferior phrenic artery.","Statement 1 is true, not false: they branch straight off the abdominal aorta. Statement 2 is false, not true: the inferior ones come from the renal arteries; the superior ones come from the inferior phrenic artery."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-ca-r-vdr","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"The vitamin D receptor in an intestinal epithelial cell is:","choices":["An intranuclear receptor that changes gene transcription","A Gq-coupled receptor on the apical membrane","A receptor tyrosine kinase on the basolateral membrane","A calcium channel that opens when vitamin D binds"],"answer":0,"ex":"Vitamin D enters the cell and binds an intranuclear receptor; the genes it turns up give the apical calcium channels, calbindin and the basolateral transporters. It is a steroid-like hormone, so its receptor is not on the membrane: the Gq-coupled receptor in this unit is the calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid chief cells. (Exam 1 · L10 Calcium and Phosphate Regulation)","src":"rec","why":["Vitamin D's receptor is intranuclear and changes transcription.","Gq-coupled is the calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid chief cells.","Receptor tyrosine kinases are insulin's type, not vitamin D's.","Vitamin D increases calcium channels; its receptor isn't one."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-pa-insulin-fat","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"In adipose tissue, insulin inhibits lipolysis by inhibiting:","choices":["Hormone-sensitive lipase","Lipoprotein lipase","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","Glycogen synthase"],"answer":0,"ex":"Insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase (which hydrolyzes stored triglycerides) while promoting glucose uptake (GLUT4), fatty acid uptake and esterification (lipoprotein lipase helps bring fatty acids in). Lipoprotein lipase is the trap: it helps fat come in for storage and insulin promotes it; hormone-sensitive lipase breaks down fat already stored, and that is the one insulin shuts off. (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"rec","why":["HSL off: stored fat stays stored.","Lipoprotein lipase helps fat uptake; insulin promotes it.","Insulin activates acetyl-CoA carboxylase (in the liver).","Glycogen synthase is about glycogen, not fat."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L5 Endocrine Pancreas: Insulin in liver, muscle and fat","basis":"Exam 1 review + hint","ids":["r-pa-insulin-fat"]}},{"id":"r-cs-mitotane","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Mitotane is described as:","choices":["An adrenolytic drug for adrenal carcinoma","A glucocorticoid receptor antagonist used in Cushing's","A synthetic mineralocorticoid","A potassium-sparing diuretic"],"answer":0,"ex":"Mitotane is adrenolytic: it destroys the adrenal cortex; used for adrenal carcinoma. (Exam 1 · L7 Hypothalamic-Pituitary Drugs)","src":"slides","why":["Adrenolytic, for adrenal carcinoma.","That is mifepristone.","That is fludrocortisone.","That is spironolactone or eplerenone."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-h2-islet-origin","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"Developmentally, islets arise when:","choices":["Cells bud off the endodermal ducts and detach","Mesoderm condenses around capillaries","Neural crest cells migrate into the pancreas from the gut wall","Acinar cells dedifferentiate in adults"],"answer":0,"ex":"Early in development, groups of cells bud from the ends of the endodermal ducts, lose their connection and form small clumps: the islets. (Exam 1 · L9 Endocrine Histology 2)","src":"slides","why":["Endodermal buds that detach.","Not mesodermal.","That is the adrenal medulla's origin.","They form in development."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-h1-tf-5","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Thyroid follicles range from about 2 to 10 mm across.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Thousands of follicles from 0.2 to 1.0 mm make up nearly the whole gland. (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Follicles are 0.2 to 1.0 mm, ten times smaller.","The slide gives 0.2 to 1.0 mm."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"Exam 1's hardest questions for the class (calcitonin, growth plates, methimazole vs PTU, Gq receptors, lipoprotein lipase, neural crest)","basis":"Exam 1 class data","ids":["r-ca-2s-4","r-cs-2s-3","r-gr-r-plate","r-pa-2s-2","r-td-inducers","r-h2-r-2s-1","r-ca-r-vdr","r-pa-insulin-fat","r-cs-mitotane","r-h2-islet-origin","r-h1-tf-5","r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-pa-tf-3","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Islet blood flows from the periphery of the islet toward its center.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"Blood flows from the center outward; beta cells are more central, alpha and delta cells more peripheral. That order is why each cell's product washes over the next: insulin from the central beta cells reaches the alpha and delta cells at the edge. (Exam 1 · L5 Endocrine Pancreas)","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Blood flows center to periphery.","It flows outward from the center."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L5 Endocrine Pancreas: Islet layout and blood flow; C-peptide","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-pa-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-h1-tf-1","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Endocrine glands are ductless and highly vascularized.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Endocrine glands lose their duct cells during development and are surrounded by an abundant fenestrated capillary bed. (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Ductless with rich capillaries: the defining features.","They are exactly that: no duct, lots of blood supply."]},{"id":"r-h1-tf-2","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Hormone-receptor signaling in the endocrine system is mostly controlled by positive feedback.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"ex":"It is almost always negative feedback to maintain homeostasis; Dr. Herr: \"there is one instance of positive that you'll talk about.\" (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"rec","fmt":"tf","why":["Positive feedback is the rare exception, not the rule.","Negative feedback is the rule; positive feedback is the rare exception."]},{"id":"r-h1-tf-3","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Pituicytes are neuroglial cells found in the pars nervosa.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"Pituicytes support and surround the axons from the hypothalamus in the pars nervosa and act as gatekeepers at the capillaries. (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["They are the supporting glia of the posterior lobe.","They are located in the pars nervosa and are neuroglial."],"likely":{"lec":"refresh","t":"L1 Endocrine Histology 1: The posterior pituitary: Herring bodies, pituicytes, ADH = vasopressin","basis":"Exam 1 hint","ids":["r-h1-tf-3"]}},{"id":"r-h1-tf-4","lec":"refresh","type":"mcq","stem":"True or false: Lactotroph numbers increase following childbirth and drop after nursing stops.","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"ex":"The number of mammotrophs (lactotrophs) increases after childbirth and drops after nursing stops. (Exam 1 · L1 Endocrine Histology 1)","src":"slides","fmt":"tf","why":["Lactotrophs expand for lactation and regress afterward.","They do change this way: up after birth, down after weaning."]}]});
